window.__SEARCH_INDEX__={"v":"b053c232c2","texts":{"2026-11-18_001":"Israel uploaded millions of photos to Amazon's facial recognition platform to track Palestinians The Israeli military uploaded 3.5m photos to Amazon’s cloud platform in order to use the US company’s facial recognition technology to analyse images of Palestinians, the Guardian can reveal. Leaked documents show the expansive catalogue of images was moved into Amazon’s cloud in the early months of Israel’s devastating aerial bombardment and ground offensive in Gaza. The images were uploaded by a department of the Israeli military that holds a vast collection of photos covering the Palestinian populations in Gaza and the West Bank. After Israel’s ground invasion of Gaza in October 2023, its military routinely collected images of Palestinians as they passed through checkpoints, often after they had been forcibly displaced by the bombing campaign. It’s not clear whether the 3.5m images uploaded to Amazon’s Rekognition were captured at these checkpoints, which effectively served as pop-up surveillance posts. However, a military source familiar with facial recognition efforts at the checkpoints said Amazon’s cloud infrastructure was used due to the “very large scale” of images collected at the sites. Israel has long used facial recognition technology, particularly in its occupation of the West Bank. Face scanning is routine at checkpoints, while Israeli soldiers use custom-built smartphone apps to collect images of people living in the Palestinian territory. Sources familiar with its work said the images were routinely used for facial recognition programmes. These include Blue Wolf, a smartphone app Israeli soldiers use in the West Bank to scan Palestinians’ faces and access information about them, including whether they’ve been flagged as suspects for arrest. The New York Times has previously reported how the military experimented with commercially available technology in Gaza to detect faces in crowds and in drone footage, at times wrongly flagging civilians as militants. In early 2024, a small group of Israeli soldiers worked from AWS’s offices in Tel Aviv, located on the 28th floor of one of Israel’s tallest skyscrapers. The soldiers were exploring ways to use the company’s cloud infrastructure to more effectively operate a facial recognition programme underway in Gaza, several military sources said. It’s not clear whether their work was made operational or if it related to the 3.5m images uploaded to Rekognition. The presence of the group at AWS’s offices, however, underscores the close relationship between the Israeli military and the cloud provider. The foundation of Israel’s relationship with Amazon is a sweeping 2021 contract known as Project Nimbus. Under the multibillion dollar deal, AWS and Google provide cloud services to a wide range of government and military clients. The Guardian revealed last year that the Nimbus contract contains unusual terms that prohibit the US companies from restricting or suspending Israeli government and military use of their cloud services, even if Israel breaches their terms of service. This would prevent the kind of unilateral action taken by Microsoft last year when it disabled the Israeli military’s access to technology used to operate a mass surveillance system uncovered by the Guardian and +972 Magazine. A central component of the Nimbus contract, files show, was an obligation on AWS to provide the Israeli military with access to a segregated area within its Tel Aviv datacentres, fortified with enhanced digital and physical security measures. This isolated area within the AWS cloud – used to store sensitive material relating to military operations and intelligence collection – was described by the company and its client as a “walled garden”. Files show the department migrated significant volumes of the data it holds into the so-called “walled garden” in AWS’s cloud throughout 2023. A military source involved in this effort said it had planned to move all of its databases from the military servers into AWS’s cloud. The source, who spoke on the condition of anonymity, said: “The plan at Rolling Stone was to migrate pictures, videos, all the confidential intelligence information – everything.” As a result of Israel’s control of the population registries covering Gaza and the West Bank, the Rolling Stone department has amassed millions of Palestinians’ photographs taken for the identity cards issued to every resident when they turn 16 years old. In Gaza, at temporary Israeli checkpoints established after the ground invasion, cameras trained on Palestinian people were used to capture their images as they approached the sites. Facial recognition was then used to match people to images held in Rolling Stone’s systems, sources said. The checkpoints became notorious in Gaza as places where people were pulled aside and detained. Many were taken into Israeli custody and held incommunicado, amounting to enforced disappearances, according to several UN bodies and rights groups. Military insiders said the checkpoints were referred to by Israeli soldiers as Nakaz – a Hebrew word for drainage. Documents suggest AWS’s cloud technology was used at times during 2024 to support the work of the checkpoints. A spokesperson for AWS said it had no “visibility” into workloads held by its customers in its cloud. They added: “We respect the privacy of our customers, and we do not discuss our relationship without their consent.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n亚马逊云成以军“人脸识别军火库”？350万张巴勒斯坦人照片被上传分析","2026-11-17_001":"The Campaign to Bring Back Jensen Huang as Nvidia CEO Amjad Masad was recording a promotional video for his AI company on Friday afternoon when he got an urgent text: Could he help the campaign to get Jensen Huang his job back? An Nvidia employee had used Masad’s service, Replit, to build a website where anyone could sign an open letter demanding that Nvidia’s board, which ousted Huang as chief executive last week, reinstate him. The “Stand With Jensen” website was struggling under the load of traffic it was receiving. Masad inspected the website’s code and found that the petition was limited to 200 signatures a minute—far below the rate of submissions it was seeing. He lifted the cap and added a Captcha to limit bots, among other fixes. As of Sunday afternoon, the petition had 106,000 names, including confirmed signatures from Masad, Uber co-founder Travis Kalanick, tech investor Gavin Baker, Shopify CEO Tobias Lutke and Epic Games CEO Tim Sweeney. Some people, including prominent Nvidia employees, said their names appeared but that they hadn’t signed the petition. Across Silicon Valley, a constellation of Huang’s peers, employees and investors are holding on to hope he could regain his position atop the chip maker—even as official scrutiny of his role in providing powerful artificial-intelligence chips to China intensifies. On Sunday, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent urged Nvidia’s board to bring in an outside candidate as CEO: “Just what was going on inside the company, they need a dramatic shake-up,” he said on Fox News. In the days since Huang stepped down following reports of affairs with two women, well-known tech CEOs and investors have registered their continued support for him—in public social-media posts and in private phone calls. In a Friday open letter, Baker and Altimeter Capital CEO Brad Gerstner said Huang “has earned the right for a deliberate process that avoids any needless rush to replace him before all the facts are known.” On Friday Nvidia’s board released a statement saying it was hiring an executive search firm and hadn’t yet engaged any potential candidates. U.S. officials suspect Alice Huang of passing information to Chinese intelligence during the relationship. Her company, Megaspeed, became a cloud partner of Nvidia, allowing it to purchase large numbers of chips. Jensen Huang’s supporters argue that the former CEO is pro-American and essential to the movement to develop American-controlled open-source AI, ensuring the technology isn’t ultimately controlled by a small number of companies. They say that policies espoused by China hawks would backfire, enabling Chinese chip giant Huawei to make up ground on Nvidia. Masad said that the vibe in his group chat of tech industry peers was of concern that a champion of open source was being taken down. Open-source software permits users to download and alter the code for themselves. “He’s been very helpful to companies and he’s a good counterbalance to voices saying we need to regulate AI and shut down open models,” said Masad. Nvidia employees who confirmed signing the open letter said they view Huang as an irreplaceable leader. Some employees have discussed holding a demonstration in Huang’s support on the company’s Santa Clara, Calif., campus in coming days. Bessent in the Fox News interview called for deeper inquiries into Nvidia’s practice of selling huge quantities of chips to companies in Southeast Asia that serve Chinese AI companies and that in some cases are suspected of diverting chips into China. Bessent said the appearance of a “culture of noncompliance at Nvidia” had persuaded him “that as the board conducts a search for a new leader, that they need to go outside the company.” Colette Kress, who was named interim CEO after Huang’s resignation, served under him as chief financial officer for 13 years. “I want to keep coming back to what was the culture at Nvidia, and were they pressing these seemingly illicit sales to goose their revenues and their earnings?” Bessent said. “And I think we need answers in the next 10 days from the board and from the outside counsel.” Jeffrey Sonnenfeld, a professor and governance expert at Yale University’s School of Management, said the grounds for Huang’s dismissal were solid under the company’s own code of conduct. “If they were to look the other way, then it’s open season, tear up the rulebook, because nothing is legally enforceable in the company’s own ethical standards,” Sonnenfeld said. Huang’s involvement in deals with Megaspeed and other companies accused of helping China get around chip-export controls will also continue to draw scrutiny. “This was aiding and abetting the circumvention of U.S. trade laws,” he said.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n一场让黄仁勋重回英伟达CEO之位的运动 周五下午，AmjadMasad正在为他的AI公司录制宣传视频，突然收到一条紧急短信：能不能帮帮忙，把黄仁勋的工作弄回来？原来，一名英伟达员工用Masad的Replit服务搭了个网站，任何人都能在上面签署一封公开信，要求上周罢免了黄仁勋CEO职务的董事会让他复职。这个名叫“StandWithJensen”的网站，正在汹涌而来的访问量下苦苦支撑。Masad检查了网站代码，发现请愿被限制在每分钟200个签名，远低于它实际收到的提交速率。于是他取消了上限，又加了验证码挡机器人，另外还顺手修了一堆东西。 截至周日下午，这份请愿书已经集了10.6万个名字，其中包括马斯德本人、Uber联合创始人特拉维斯·卡兰尼克、科技投资人加文·贝克、Shopify首席执行官托比亚斯·卢特克，以及EpicGames首席执行官蒂姆·斯威尼等人的确认签名。但也有一些，包括英伟达内部一些知名员工，表示，自己的名字虽然出现在了请愿书上，本人却并没有签过。 在整个硅谷，黄仁勋的同行、员工与投资人们，如同一片璀璨的星群，正抱着一线希望，盼他能重回这家芯片巨头的最高位，即便与此同时，官方对他向中国供应强大AI芯片一事的审查正在步步升级。周日，财政部长斯科特·贝森特敦促英伟达董事会引入一位外部候选人出任CEO：“公司内部到底在发生什么？他们需要一场彻底的洗牌。”他在福克斯新闻上如是说。 在黄仁勋因与两名女性的婚外情报道而下台之后的这些天里，知名的科技公司CEO和投资人们纷纷表达了对他的持续支持，既在公开的社交媒体上发帖，也在私下的电话里表态。在周五的一封公开信中，贝克与AltimeterCapital的CEO布拉德·格斯特纳写道，黄仁勋“有权得到一个审慎的程序，避免在全部事实水落石出之前，就仓促地把他替换掉”。周五，英伟达董事会发布声明称，正在聘请一家高管猎头公司，并且尚未接触任何潜在候选人。 美国官员怀疑，Alice黄在与黄仁勋交往期间，向中国的情报机构传递了信息。她的公司Megaspeed成了英伟达的云合作伙伴，得以大量采购芯片。黄仁勋的支持者们则辩称，这位前CEO是亲美的，而且对于发展“由美国掌控的开源AI”这场运动至关重要，这能确保这项技术，最终不会被少数几家公司所垄断。他们说，对华鹰派所鼓吹的政策会适得其反，反而会让中国芯片巨头华为，缩小与英伟达的差距。 Masad说，在他的科技同行群里，弥漫着一股担忧：一个开源的捍卫者，正在被人扳倒。开源软件允许用户自己下载、修改代码。“他一直对公司非常有帮助，而且他是一股很好的制衡力量，去对冲那些‘我们要监管AI、关停开源模型’的声音。”Masad说。那些确认在公开信上签了名的英伟达员工表示，他们把黄仁勋视为一位不可替代的领袖。一些员工还在商量，要在接下来几天，到公司位于加州圣克拉拉的总部园区，举行一场支持黄仁勋的集会。 Bessent在福克斯新闻的采访中，呼吁对英伟达向东南亚公司出售海量芯片的做法展开更深入的调查，这些公司，有的服务于中国的AI公司，有的则被怀疑把芯片转进中国。Bessent说，英伟达内部那种“不合规文化”的表象，已经让他确信：董事会在物色新领导人的时候，必须从公司外部找。而那位在黄仁勋辞职后被任命为临时CEO的ColetteKress，此前曾在黄仁勋手下当了13年的首席财务官。“我想反复追问的是：英伟达的文化到底是什么？他们是不是在推动这些看似非法的销售，来人为抬高自己的收入和利润？”Bessent说，“而且我认为，我们需要在接下来的10天里，从董事会和外部律师那里得到一个答案。” 耶鲁大学管理学院教授、公司治理专家杰弗里·索南菲尔德表示，按照英伟达公司自己的行为准则，解雇黄仁勋的理由是站得住脚的。“如果他们睁一只眼闭一只眼，那就等于开禁了、规则手册直接撕掉算了，因为公司自己的道德标准，就再也没有任何法律约束力了。”索南菲尔德说。黄仁勋卷入的那些与Megaspeed及其他公司（这些公司被指控帮助中国规避芯片出口管制）的交易，也将继续受到审查。“这就是在协助和教唆对‘美国贸易法’的规避。”他说。","2026-11-16_001":"Scammed out of $700,000 on Tinder: Even smart people are falling for romance cons Dating-app swindlers are on the rise, but there are a few steps you can take to protect yourself. She wanted to give her heart away. A scammer posing as a love interest wanted to take her money instead. Romance and confidence schemes are driving nearly $1 billion in annual losses, according to the FBI Internet Crime Report for 2025. The personal stories behind the statistics are painful. A report released last month by the congressional Joint Economic Committee focused specifically on romance scams. The findings were based on a year-long bipartisan investigation led by the panel’s top Democrat, Sen. Maggie Hassan (New Hampshire), and Sen. Marsha Blackburn (R-Tennessee), who chairs a Senate subcommittee on consumer protection and data privacy. The probe examined Match Group platforms, including Hinge, Match, Plenty of Fish and Tinder. The report featured victims’ stories, including a woman who joined Tinder last year seeking companionship after her husband died. She matched with a user who claimed to be from New Jersey. After winning her trust, the online predator fabricated various crises — including a heart attack and an arrest in Mexico. The fraudster ultimately coaxed her into sending him money. According to the report, after the scam was exposed, the victim was contacted by a group offering to help recover her stolen funds. That turned out to be a fake cryptocurrency scheme. In total, the widow lost more than $700,000 from the two scams. “A romance scam takes more than money,” the woman, who was not identified by name in the report, told investigators. “There are wounds nobody sees. You question every conversation, every promise you believed. Some lose savings, homes, and futures. Some lose faith in love.” I invited Hassan to join me on my show “Dollars & Sense” to discuss the report on Match Group and her efforts to combat a growing and expensive crime. “It’s really important for all of these tech platforms to understand the scale of the criminal enterprise that is the global scam industry,” Hassan told me. “We are seeing scams just explode as AI has developed and become more and more effective.” According to the report, as of the second half of 2025, Tinder had not updated most of its machine learning models in years. “As a result, Tinder detected scammers and other bad actors less reliably and struggled to keep pace with emerging threats,” the report found. The committee report acknowledged that Match Group has taken steps to improve its detection of bad actors. This includes the launch of a new security feature last year called Face Check, which requires new members to film a video selfie within the app. “We’re grateful the committee recognized our significant advancements to protect our users against romance scams, including the launch of our biometric verification tool Face Check across Tinder and Hinge,” Yoel Roth, Match Group’s senior vice president of Trust and Safety, said in an email. “That said, the report mischaracterizes both the historical scale of our investment and the work already completed.” Face Check confirms that the person is real and physically present and that their face matches the one shown in their profile photos, according to Match Group. If the scanned face matches a profile photo, members get a verified badge. “Alongside our engagement with the committee over the past year, we’ve made the largest safety advancements in Match Group’s history,” Roth said. “Trust and safety is a foundational priority, and we intend to continue leading the industry in building tools that promote authentic, safe connections. Romance scams are a cross-platform, societal problem that no company can solve alone, which is why we participate in and help fund cross-industry efforts to combat online scams.” Sadly, no app can guarantee complete protection from scammers. There is no magic advice that can save you from fraudsters, because the schemes have become so sophisticated. “Romance scams are in fact nefarious,” Hassan said. “They steal people’s money, but also their trust.” And please don’t shame victims like the widow. She was looking for love and was manipulated. You are not too clever to be conned. Until there is more robust security on these platforms, my advice is to trust nothing and no one. Verify everything. Make it a point to always do a reverse image search. Go to the TinEye reverse image search tool, and upload your image or paste its URL. The AARP Fraud Victim Support Group provides an online forum for scam victims. Group sessions are confidential and led by trained facilitators. They are also open to friends and family. Go to aarp.org/fraudsupport for help.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n在 Tinder 上被骗走七十万美元——连聪明人，也正坠入一场名为爱情的骗局 七十万美元买来的教训：聪明人也会上爱情骗局的当 交友App上的骗子越来越多，但你可以采取几个步骤来保护好自己。 她想把心交出去，那个假扮成恋人的骗子，却只想拿走她的钱。根据FBI《2025年网络犯罪报告》，恋爱与信任类骗局，正在造成近10亿美元的年度损失。 统计数字背后的一个个真实故事，令人心痛。上个月，国会联合经济委员会发布了一份报告，专门聚焦恋爱诈骗。这些结论，基于一项长达一年、由两党联合开展的调查——牵头的是该委员会的头号民主党人、新罕布什尔州参议员玛吉·哈桑，以及田纳西州共和党参议员玛莎·布莱克本；后者还主持着一个关于消费者保护和数据隐私的参议院小组委员会。 这项调查审查了MatchGroup旗下的平台，包括Hinge、Match、PlentyofFish和Tinder。报告呈现了受害者的故事，其中有一位女性，去年在丈夫去世后加入了Tinder，只为寻求一份陪伴。她和一位自称来自新泽西州的用户配对成功。在赢得她的信任之后，这个网络掠食者编造了各种危机——包括一次心脏病发作，和一次在墨西哥被捕。最终，这个诈骗犯哄骗她给他汇了钱。 根据这份报告，骗局被揭穿之后，这位受害者又被一个声称能帮她追回被盗资金的团伙联系上了。结果，那又是一个假的加密货币骗局。两次骗局加起来，这位遗孀总共损失了七十多万美元。\"恋爱诈骗夺走的不只是钱，\"这位在报告中没有透露姓名的女性告诉调查人员，\"有些伤口，没有人看得到。你会质疑每一段对话、每一个你曾相信过的承诺。有些人失去了存款、房子和未来。有些人，则失去了对爱的信心。\" 我邀请哈桑上我的节目《Dollars&Sense》，一起讨论这份关于MatchGroup的报告，以及她为打击这一日益增长、代价高昂的犯罪所做的努力。\"让所有这些科技平台，真正理解'全球诈骗产业'这个犯罪企业的体量，真的非常重要，\"哈桑对我说，\"我们看到，随着AI的发展、而且变得越来越高效，诈骗正在爆炸式增长。\" 根据这份报告，截至2025年下半年，Tinder多年来一直没有更新它大部分的机器学习模型。\"结果，Tinder识别骗子和其他不法分子的能力就变得没那么可靠了，而且很难跟上不断出现的新型威胁，\"报告指出。 委员会的这份报告也承认，MatchGroup已经采取了一些措施，来改进它对不法分子的识别。这包括去年上线的一个新安全功能——FaceCheck，它要求新用户在这个App里拍摄一段视频自拍。\"我们很感激委员会认可了我们在保护用户免受恋爱诈骗方面的重大进步，包括我们在Tinder和Hinge上推出的生物识别验证工具FaceCheck，\"MatchGroup信任与安全高级副总裁约埃尔·罗斯在一封邮件中说。\"不过，这份报告歪曲了我们历史投入的规模，也歪曲了我们已经完成的工作。\" 据MatchGroup的说法，FaceCheck能确认：这个人是真实的、本人在场的，而且他们的脸，和资料照片里的那张脸是一致的。如果扫描出来的脸和某张资料照片吻合，会员就会获得一个\"认证徽章\"。\"在过去一年里，在我们与委员会保持沟通的同时，我们做出了MatchGroup历史上最大规模的安全进步，\"罗斯说。\"信任与安全，是我们的根本优先事项，我们打算继续引领这个行业，打造那些促进真实、安全连接的工具。恋爱诈骗是一个跨平台的社会问题，没有哪家公司能单独解决，这就是为什么我们参与、并资助跨行业的反诈行动。\" 遗憾的是，没有任何一个App，能保证让你完全免受骗子的侵害。也没有任何\"魔法般的建议\"，能把你从诈骗犯手里救出来，因为现在的骗局，已经变得太精密了。\"恋爱诈骗，其实是非常邪恶的，\"哈桑说。\"它们偷走人们的钱，也偷走人们的信任。\"而且，请别羞辱像那位遗孀一样的受害者。她只是在寻找爱情，却被操控了。你并没有聪明到，不会被骗的地步。 在这些平台拥有更扎实的安全保障之前，我的建议是——谁也别信，什么都别信，处处核实。一定要养成习惯，总是做一次反向图片搜索。去TinEye这个反向搜图工具，上传你的图片，或者粘贴它的网址。AARP的\"欺诈受害者支持小组\"，为诈骗受害者提供了一个在线论坛。小组的会话是保密的，由受过培训的引导者主持。它们也向亲友开放。去aarp.org/fraudsupport寻求帮助吧。","2026-11-15_001":"华盛顿对华鹰派‘复仇’成功：黄仁勋出局，芯片出口管制要加码了？ Behind Jensen Huang's spy scandal, a shadow war for Trump's ear on China As Nvidia chief executive Jensen Huang gained extraordinary access to President Donald Trump and secured his blessing to sell artificial intelligence chips to China, he fueled a backlash among Washington officials and national security hawks who wanted to keep the powerful technology out of Beijing's hands. Some national security officials within the Trump administration chafed at Huang's influence over the president, according to two former officials. Huang’s downfall, which has the potential to mark a dramatic shift in power, was ultimately set in motion by one of the capital’s most influential China hard-liners, national security adviser and Secretary of State Marco Rubio. Rubio issued a memo warning Trump about the relationship between Huang and a suspected Chinese spy, based on a report generated by the CIA, according to a person familiar with the matter. When the findings of Rubio’s memo — swiping at one of Trump’s closest allies — leaked in news reports, they set off a chain of events that culminated in Huang’s resignation Thursday. Huang admitted to and apologized for having an affair with an executive at an Nvidia customer, who American spies suspect of being a Chinese intelligence asset. Nvidia's board launched an investigation, but Huang ultimately resigned following reports of another affair with a subordinate. After Huang's resignation, Trump referred to him as a \"legend\" who had \"done a fantastic job.\" At a Group of Seven summit in Paris this week, Rubio declined several times to answer reporters' questions on his role in alerting the White House to Huang's affair. The ouster marks a huge victory for those who had long sought to curb Huang's influence over Trump's policy toward Beijing, and a stunning turn in a shadow battle that has raged for years between Nvidia and those in Washington who see China as a military and technology threat. Until recently, Huang's close relationship with Trump appeared to give him the upper hand. But with the downfall of the leatherjacket sporting CEO, the hawks are ready to seize on the moment to redirect the national agenda toward tougher policies on China. Many of Trump’s national security team had not shared the president’s view on exports of chips, according to a former official who worked on chips policy and is still connected to the industry, who likened Huang’s relationship to handing officials a “nuclear bomb.” “They wanted to destroy the influence Jensen had over the president,” said the former official, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to describe internal dynamics. “Things are forever changed,” said Fedasiuk, a longtime advocate for controls on chip exports. For decades, Nvidia’s computer chips powered the graphics in video games and were not a matter of national security. But for many in Washington, that began to change at the start of the AI boom and what officials in the Biden administration saw as the dawn of a new contest with China poised to reshape the two nations’ military and economic power. Huang took a different view, arguing that chipmakers should be able to continue to sell into China, hooking them on American systems in a way that would ultimately benefit the U.S. In December, when Trump said he had green-lit exports of a new class of chips, Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick said the bargain had been hammered out between Huang and the president. \"So it's the great American technologist talking to the great businessman president, and they reached what they thought was the right answer for this particular moment,\" Lutnick told CNBC. A Trump appointee at the Commerce department bureau responsible for export licenses expedited Nvidia's chip applications, according to two former agency officials, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to avoid professional retaliation. Huang advocated for his position in sometimes caustic terms. In an interview with a podcast in September 2025, he said the term “China hawk” should be a badge of shame. “They think they’re doing the right thing for our country, but it’s not patriotic,” he said. “Not even a little bit.” Staff at Washington think tanks and on Capitol Hill responded by designing badges designating them China hawks that they passed around among themselves. At the time, Eric Sayers, a managing director at consulting firm Beacon Global Strategies, posted to social media that the group had “long memories” — a message he shared again in recent days as the scandal around Huang deepened. Now, with Huang sidelined — and Republican committee chairs planning to haul him in to provide information about the affair — the sponsors of a trio of new measures say they are increasingly optimistic that they can push them through as part of an annual defense bill. The bills would place export restrictions on the most powerful AI chips, require technology in chips to detect smuggling and limit Chinese access to chipmaking equipment. “Leading up to this moment, we were waiting to see who Nvidia deploys to kill these bills,” said a Senate Democratic aide, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to describe internal strategy. “Now all this stuff has come out, it puts them in a very, very tough position.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n华盛顿对华鹰派‘复仇’成功：黄仁勋出局，芯片出口管制要加码了？ 随着英伟达首席执行官黄仁勋获得了接近美国总统唐纳德·特朗普的非同寻常的机会，并取得特朗普的首肯，得以向中国出售人工智能芯片，他也在华盛顿官员和国家安全鹰派人士中引发了强烈反弹。这些人希望阻止这项强大的技术落入北京手中。据两名前官员透露，特朗普政府内部的一些国家安全官员对黄仁勋施加于总统的影响力感到不满。 黄仁勋的倒台可能标志着权力格局发生戏剧性的转变，而最终推动这一事件发生的，是华盛顿最具影响力的对华强硬派人物之一——国家安全顾问兼国务卿马尔科·卢比奥。据一名知情人士透露，卢比奥根据美国中央情报局（CIA）出具的一份报告，向特朗普提交了一份备忘录，警告他注意黄仁勋与一名涉嫌为中国从事间谍活动的人士之间的关系。当卢比奥备忘录中的调查发现被新闻媒体泄露出来时，这些直指特朗普最亲密盟友之一的内容引发了一连串事件，最终导致黄仁勋于周四辞职。 黄仁勋承认自己与英伟达一家客户公司的高管发生了婚外情，并为此道歉。美国情报人员怀疑这名高管是中国情报机构的线人。英伟达董事会启动了一项调查，但在有关黄仁勋与另一名下属存在婚外情的报道出现后，他最终辞去了职务。黄仁勋辞职后，特朗普称他是一位“传奇人物”，并表示他“工作做得非常出色”。本周，在巴黎举行的七国集团峰会上，记者多次询问卢比奥在向白宫通报黄仁勋婚外情一事中扮演了什么角色，但卢比奥始终拒绝回答。 黄仁勋被迫离职，对于那些长期以来一直试图限制他对特朗普对华政策影响力的人来说，标志着一场重大胜利。这也是英伟达与华盛顿那些将中国视为军事和技术威胁的人士之间持续多年暗战的一次惊人转折。直到最近，黄仁勋与特朗普的亲密关系似乎仍让他占据上风。然而，随着这位以身穿皮夹克著称的首席执行官倒台，鹰派人士正准备抓住这一时机，推动美国国家政策议程转向对中国采取更强硬的立场。 据一名前官员透露，特朗普国家安全团队中的许多人并不赞同总统在芯片出口问题上的立场。这名前官员曾参与芯片政策制定，目前仍与该行业保持联系。他将黄仁勋的这段关系比作有人向官员们递上了一枚“核弹”。这名前官员表示：“他们一直想摧毁黄仁勋对总统的影响力。”由于需要描述政府内部的权力运作情况，他要求匿名。长期主张限制芯片出口的费达修克则表示：“一切都永远改变了。” 几十年来，英伟达的计算机芯片一直为电子游戏的图形画面提供运算支持，并不涉及国家安全问题。然而，在华盛顿许多人看来，随着人工智能热潮兴起，情况开始发生变化。拜登政府的官员认为，一场与中国之间的新竞争正在拉开序幕，而这场竞争可能重塑两国的军事和经济实力。黄仁勋则持有不同的观点。他认为，芯片制造商应该能够继续向中国销售芯片，让中国企业依赖美国的技术系统，从而最终使美国受益。 去年12月，当特朗普表示自己已经批准出口一类新型芯片时，美国商务部长霍华德·卢特尼克表示，这项协议是黄仁勋与总统亲自磋商达成的。卢特尼克在接受美国消费者新闻与商业频道（CNBC）采访时说：“这就是一位伟大的美国技术专家与一位伟大的商人总统之间的对话，他们共同得出了一个他们认为在当前这个特殊时刻最合适的答案。”据两名前政府机构官员透露，特朗普任命的一名官员在商务部负责出口许可证的机构中，加快了英伟达芯片出口申请的审批进程。这两名前官员要求匿名，以避免遭到职业上的报复。 黄仁勋在捍卫自己的立场时，有时会使用相当尖刻的言辞。2025年9月，他在一次播客采访中表示，“对华鹰派”这个称号应该成为一种耻辱的标志。他说：“他们以为自己是在为我们的国家做正确的事，但这根本不是爱国。连一点点都算不上。”作为回应，华盛顿智库和美国国会山的工作人员专门设计了印有“对华鹰派”身份标识的徽章，并在彼此之间传递。当时，咨询公司BeaconGlobalStrategies的董事总经理埃里克·塞耶斯在社交媒体上发文表示，这群人“记性很好”，暗示他们不会忘记黄仁勋的这番言论。最近几天，随着黄仁勋的丑闻不断发酵，他又重新分享了这条信息。 如今，随着黄仁勋被排挤出权力中心，共和党籍委员会主席们也正计划传唤他，就这起婚外情事件提供相关信息，三项新措施的提案发起人表示，他们越来越有信心将这些措施纳入年度国防法案并推动通过。这些法案将限制最强大的人工智能芯片出口，要求在芯片中加入能够检测走私行为的技术，并限制中国获取芯片制造设备。一名参议院民主党幕僚表示：“在这一切发生之前，我们一直在等着看英伟达会派谁出来阻止这些法案。”这名幕僚要求匿名，以便透露内部策略。“现在所有这些事情都曝光了，这让他们陷入了非常、非常困难的境地。”","2026-11-14_001":"黄仁勋黯然出局。其继任者接下的，是科技界最艰巨的担子 Jensen Huang is out. His successor has the toughest job in tech Two of the world’s most valuable companies have changed bosses in recent months. They could hardly have done so more differently. At Apple, Tim Cook made way for John Ternus in September under what the iPhone-maker called a “long-term succession plan”. Nvidia’s succession plan lasted four days. Jensen Huang, the chip giant’s only boss in its 33-year history, never intended to leave. On November 8th he confessed to an affair; on November 12th, after further revelations about his private life, he resigned. The job has fallen, for now, to Colette Kress, Nvidia’s finance chief since 2013, who has been named interim chief executive. She was never coached for the top job, and will need to learn fast. Since news of the scandal broke Nvidia’s shares have lost about 20% of their value. Besides calming investors, she will need to shore up suppliers, mend fences with Washington and hold together the vast web of financing schemes and partnerships that has fuelled Nvidia’s breakneck growth. Ms Kress inherits one of the most formidable money-making machines in corporate history. Two-thirds of the world’s artificial-intelligence computing power runs on Nvidia’s chips. Its gross margins hover around 75%, against 55% at AMD, a rival chip designer. This financial year analysts expect it to bring in more than $400bn in revenue, nearly double last year’s total. And it is sitting on around $100bn in cash and liquid financial instruments. Ms Kress’s first test will come on November 17th, when Nvidia reports its latest earnings. A blowout quarter would soothe nerves. But investors will also want assurances that Nvidia can still achieve the 70% revenue growth it has promised for next year. She will need to convince them that the upheaval at the top will not distract the company. Next is the chip giant’s intricate supply chain. Nvidia says that it cannot fill all the demand, because of supply-chain bottlenecks. That makes the launch of Vera Rubin, its next generation of AI systems, especially delicate. Reports have suggested that manufacturing problems are holding up production of the higher-end variant. Ensuring that its products are delivered on time requires working with suppliers such as TSMC and memory-makers such as SK Hynix of South Korea. Mr Huang excelled at this, running the relationships as much by charm as by contract. Nvidia has signed long-term deals with many of its suppliers, committing hundreds of billions of dollars to them through 2029. But they, too, will want reassurance that the lofty growth targets behind the orders still hold. Then there is Washington. Mr Huang was one of Donald Trump’s favourite businessmen, and used his access to lobby hard for looser curbs on selling chips to China, often clashing with hawks in Congress. The scandal has left Nvidia’s standing in the capital badly bruised. On November 18th, a day after the earnings call, Mr Huang has been asked to appear at a closed-door congressional briefing to answer questions about his relationship with Megaspeed, a Singaporean customer of Nvidia. Democrats, who take control of Congress in January, may add more pressure. One employee concedes that Mr Huang’s combative style in Washington made Nvidia enemies, and that it will need to work with policymakers rather than against them. The hardest task of all may be keeping Nvidia at the centre of the AI economy. Mr Huang’s aggressive dealmaking has made the company the AI-infrastructure boom’s most important player, and boosted demand for its chips. Over the past three years Nvidia has pledged more than $70bn to startups, making about 90 investments last year alone and at least 60 so far this year. It has also backstopped AI clouds to the tune of up to $36bn, promising to buy computing capacity they cannot sell to others, and guaranteed the debts for a giant data-centre project in Ohio intended for OpenAI. On top of that, it has signed $20bn of data-centre leases that it plans to pass on to third parties, and orchestrated a financing programme with Wall Street lenders that could direct up to $500bn of further investment into data centres. Many of these deals rested on Mr Huang’s word. However deftly she handles all this, Ms Kress is unlikely to keep the job for long. She lacks a technical background; Bloomberg reports that the board has been working with an outside recruiting firm to find a permanent replacement. Among insiders, Ian Buck, who runs Nvidia’s data-centre computing business, is popular with engineers but untested at running a giant company. Lisa Su of AMD and Rene Haas of Arm, a former Nvidia executive, may be options. Nvidia may yet find that the answer to its succession problem lies at the company that showed it how succession should be done.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n黄仁勋黯然出局。其继任者接下的，是科技界最艰巨的担子 最近几个月，全球最值钱的公司里有两家换了老板——而它们换帅的方式，天差地别。苹果这边，蒂姆·库克在九月为约翰·特努斯让路，用的是苹果口中的\"长期继任计划\"；英伟达的\"继任计划\"，只撑了四天。黄仁勋——这家芯片巨头三十三年历史上唯一的掌门人——从没打算离开。十一月八日，他承认了婚外情；十一月十二日，私生活再遭曝光后，他递交了辞呈。 眼下，这副担子落到了科莱特·克雷斯身上——这位2013年起执掌英伟达财务的高管，如今被任命为临时首席执行官。她从没被当作一把手培养过，一切都得现学。自丑闻爆出以来，英伟达股价已跌去约两成。除了安抚投资者，她还必须稳住供应商、修复与华盛顿的关系，并维系那张庞大而复杂的融资与合作网络——正是它，为英伟达那种近乎失控的狂飙式增长供了血。 克雷斯接手的，是企业史上最令人望而生畏的一台印钞机。全球三分之二的AI算力，都奔流在英伟达的芯片之上。它的毛利率高悬在75%上下，而对手AMD不过55%。本财年，分析师预计其营收将突破4000亿美元，几乎为去年总额的两倍；而它账上，还静静地躺着约1000亿美元的现金与流动资产。 克雷斯的第一次大考，将在十一月十七日到来——那一天，英伟达公布最新一季财报。一份好到炸裂的业绩，自然能安抚人心。但投资者还想要一个明确保证：英伟达此前承诺的、明年70%的收入增长，究竟还做不做得到。她必须说服他们，最高层的这场地震，不会让这家公司分心。 接下来，是这家芯片巨头那张错综复杂的供应链。英伟达自己都承认，它无法满足全部需求——因为供应链上堵着瓶颈。正因如此，其下一代AI系统VeraRubin的发布，变得格外棘手。已有报道显示，制造环节的问题，正拖慢其中高端版本的生产。 要让产品按时交付，就必须与台积电这样的代工厂、以及韩国SK海力士这样的内存厂商通力合作。黄仁勋深谙此道——他维系这些关系，靠人格魅力丝毫不亚于靠一纸合同。英伟达已与众多供应商签下长约，承诺到2029年向他们支付数千亿美元。但这些供应商，同样想讨一个说法：那些订单背后高得惊人的增长目标，究竟还算不算数。 再然后，还有华盛顿这一关。黄仁勋曾是唐纳德·特朗普最看重的商人之一，他凭借这层关系，大力游说放宽对华芯片销售的限制，屡屡与国会中的鹰派发生冲突。这场丑闻，已让英伟达在首都的声望严重受损。十一月十八日——财报电话会次日——黄仁勋被要求出席一场闭门的国会简报会，就他与英伟达新加坡客户Megaspeed的关系作出说明。明年一月将接管国会的民主党人，还可能进一步施压。一位内部员工不无无奈地承认，黄仁勋在华盛顿咄咄逼人的做派，给英伟达树敌无数；而公司眼下需要的，是与政策制定者携手，而非针锋相对。 而最难的任务，或许是让英伟达继续稳坐AI经济的中心。黄仁勋激进的交易手腕，令这家公司成为AI基建热潮中最举足轻重的角色，也顺势拉动了自家芯片的需求。过去三年，英伟达向初创公司承诺投入逾700亿美元，仅去年一年就出手约90笔，今年迄今至少又投了60笔。它还以高达360亿美元的规模为AI云厂商托底，承诺买下它们卖不掉的算力；并为一座位于俄亥俄州、专供OpenAI之用的巨型数据中心项目担保债务。此外，它还签下了200亿美元的数据中心租约，打算转手第三方，并联合华尔街贷款机构策划了一项融资计划，有望引导多达5000亿美元的额外投资涌入数据中心。而这其中的许多交易，靠的不过是黄仁勋的一句话。 然而，无论她把这一切处理得多么游刃有余，克雷斯恐怕也难久居其位。她缺少技术背景；据彭博社报道，董事会已在与外部猎头公司接洽，物色正式的继任者。内部人选中，执掌英伟达数据中心业务的伊恩·巴克深得工程师爱戴，却从未在执掌一家巨头公司上证明过自己。AMD的苏姿丰，与曾任英伟达高管的Arm现任掌门雷内·哈斯，也都是潜在选项。英伟达或许终将发现，它这个继任难题的答案，恰恰就藏在当初为它示范了\"继任应当如何完成\"的那家公司身上。■","2026-11-13_001":"千禧一代 vs 婴儿潮：一场正在撕裂美国的代际战争 The Return of Generational Warfare As last week’s midterms demonstrated, young people appear poised to drive American politics, and particularly the Democratic Party, further to the left. This is particularly true in big cities. New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani was elected last year with crucial support from younger voters, while in Los Angeles, preelection surveys showed democratic socialist (and now Mayor-elect) Nithya Raman leading by almost five to one among voters in their thirties and by more than four to one among voters aged 18 to 29. Nationally, two-thirds of voters aged 18 to 29 voted Democratic in House races last Tuesday. The recent return of generational conflict has a 1960s vibe. As back then, it’s affecting the “rich” countries like the United States, Canada, the U.K., and the nations of the European Union, but it’s also occurring in poorer regions, with mass protests spreading from Paris and Milan to Katmandu, Jakarta, Manila, New Delhi, and Sri Lanka. A new generational war could prove devastating. Societies, noted Aristotle, are like ships: though the crew members have different roles, they all must work together on a common journey. The temptation to go after the successful baby boomers seems irrepressible. Many economists and politicians treat the aging population as a great liability. The United States, like the rest of the world, is older than it has ever been: the population aged 65 and older is projected to grow from 58 million in 2022 to 82 million by 2050. The Atlantic, for example, denounces a corrupt gerontocracy that has “amassed disproportionate wealth and power.” Burning down their grandparents’ world may feel satisfying in the moment, but the notion that older Americans had it easier is not convincing. Boomers faced interest rates as high as 17 percent and unemployment rates much higher than today. Many Generation Z (zoomers) and millennials appear to lack appreciation for the contributions of their elders. In the U.S. alone, according to one financial estimate, younger cohorts will harvest upward of $73 trillion from older generations by 2045. To be sure, youthful resentment has roots in reality: fewer than half of American millennials are doing better than their parents. Between 2022 and 2025, median net worth rose 37 percent for Americans aged 75 and over while falling 23 percent among those under 35. In terms of income, millennials have fared better than their parents but still lag behind in net worth, largely because of low homeownership rates. According to the U.S. Census Bureau, the homeownership rate among young adults aged 25 to 34 was 45 percent for Generation X but dropped to 37 percent for millennials. Not coincidentally, the share of Americans aged 18 to 29 living at home has grown steadily in recent years and now sits at 49 percent. This creates the prospect of a new cohort of “forever renters” dependent on large corporations or the state to supply their residences. Housing is just one source of younger Americans’ skepticism about the future. Today, the angst is more generational, as 69 percent of millennials believe success is much harder to achieve than it was for older generations. Underemployment among recent college graduates has reached 42 percent, and many young people carry massive student-loan debt. One might expect that young people would be excited about unprecedented technological progress, from the rise of space industries to artificial intelligence. Instead, many view the rise of AI and robots with trepidation. According to Pew, 73 percent of Americans under 30 think AI means fewer jobs. Fear of the future under capitalism plays into the hands of those promoting an authoritarian alternative. It’s not just the flag that young people are unenthusiastic about but the liberal economic system that has powered the American experiment. According to a Cato Institution/YouGov poll, 62 percent of Americans under 30 have a positive view of socialism; one-third support Communism. What “socialism” and “capitalism” mean remains up in the air. Of course, despite the talk of geezer greed, not all boomers are rich. Forty percent of U.S. retirees have insufficient savings, and 19 percent have already burned through what they had stored away. The more constructive alternative to generational war, of course, would be a truce. Seniors often support their children financially. In 2024, 2.6 million parents gave over $26 billion to their children living outside the home. Nearly one in four first-time homebuyers rely on families for down payments. Boomers—the vast majority of seniors—and the aging Xers account for the largest group of formal and informal volunteers helping out their neighbors and fellow citizens. Today, more than two-thirds of grandparents are caregivers, often on a regular, even daily basis, contributing over $900 billion to the broader economy by allowing parents to work or go to school. Seniors need the younger generations, too, especially as they age. The truth is, the generations need each other, as they always have. Most young Americans aspire to own a single-family home and start a family. It’s the obligation of the older generation to help them achieve this—not for their own pleasure, but for the future of society.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n千禧一代 vs 婴儿潮：一场正在撕裂美国的代际战争 正如上周的中期选举所表明的那样，年轻人似乎正蓄势待发，准备推动美国政治，尤其是民主党，进一步向左转。这一点在大城市尤为明显。纽约市长佐赫兰·马姆达尼去年成功当选，年轻选民的关键支持功不可没。而在洛杉矶，选前民调显示，民主社会主义者妮蒂娅·拉曼（如今已成为候任市长）在30多岁的选民中以接近五比一的优势领先，在18至29岁的选民中则以超过四比一的优势领先。 从全国范围来看，在上周二的众议院选举中，18至29岁的选民有三分之二投票支持民主党。最近重新出现的代际冲突颇有20世纪60年代的味道。与当年一样，这种冲突不仅影响着美国、加拿大、英国和欧盟成员国等“富裕”国家，也正在较为贫穷的地区发生，大规模抗议活动从巴黎、米兰蔓延至加德满都、雅加达、马尼拉、新德里和斯里兰卡。一场新的代际战争可能造成毁灭性的后果。亚里士多德曾指出，社会就像一艘船：尽管船员们各自承担着不同的职责，但所有人都必须为了共同的航程而齐心协力。 想要将矛头指向那些事业有成的婴儿潮一代，这种冲动似乎已经难以遏制。许多经济学家和政治人物将老龄化人口视为巨大的负担。美国和世界其他地区一样，人口老龄化程度已经达到历史最高水平：预计65岁及以上的人口将从2022年的5800万增长到2050年的8200万。例如，《大西洋月刊》就谴责了一种腐败的老人统治体制，认为它“积累了与其人口比例不相称的财富和权力”。 摧毁祖辈所建立的世界，也许能让年轻人在那一刻感到痛快，但认为美国老一辈人的生活更加轻松，这种观点并不令人信服。婴儿潮一代曾经面临高达17%的利率，以及远高于今天的失业率。许多Z世代和千禧一代似乎没有充分认识到长辈们所作出的贡献。仅在美国，根据一项金融估算，到2045年，年轻世代就将从老一辈人那里继承超过73万亿美元的财富。 诚然，年轻人的怨恨情绪有着现实根源：在美国千禧一代中，生活过得比父母更好的人还不到一半。2022年至2025年间，75岁及以上美国人的净资产中位数增长了37%，而35岁以下人群的净资产中位数却下降了23%。从收入来看，千禧一代的表现比父母一代更好，但在净资产方面仍然落后，这在很大程度上是因为他们的住房自有率较低。根据美国人口普查局的数据，25至34岁年轻成年人中，X世代的住房自有率为45%，而千禧一代则下降至37%。并非巧合的是，近年来，18至29岁美国人与父母同住的比例持续上升，如今已达到49%。这意味着可能出现新一批“终身租房者”，他们不得不依赖大型企业或政府来提供住所。 住房问题只是美国年轻人对未来持怀疑态度的原因之一。如今，这种焦虑更多地体现为一种代际情绪，因为69%的千禧一代认为，与老一辈相比，如今取得成功要困难得多。近期大学毕业生的就业不足率已达到42%，许多年轻人还背负着巨额学生贷款债务。人们或许会以为，面对从航天产业崛起到人工智能发展这样前所未有的技术进步，年轻人应该感到兴奋。然而，许多人却对人工智能和机器人的兴起感到忧虑和恐惧。根据皮尤研究中心的数据，73%的30岁以下美国人认为，人工智能意味着就业岗位将会减少。 对资本主义制度下未来前景的恐惧，恰恰让那些鼓吹威权主义替代方案的人有机可乘。年轻人缺乏热情的，不仅仅是国旗所象征的国家认同，还有一直推动美国社会发展与制度实践的自由主义经济体系。根据卡托研究所与舆观调查机构的一项民意调查，62%的30岁以下美国人对社会主义持积极看法，三分之一的人支持共产主义。然而，“社会主义”和“资本主义”究竟意味着什么，仍然没有明确的定论。 当然，尽管人们不断谈论所谓的“老年人的贪婪”，但并非所有婴儿潮一代的人都很富有。40%的美国退休人员储蓄不足，19%的人甚至已经耗尽了此前积攒的积蓄。当然，与其发动代际战争，更具建设性的选择应该是达成休战。老年人经常在经济上支持自己的子女。2024年，260万名父母向已经搬出去独立居住的子女提供了总计超过260亿美元的经济援助。近四分之一的首次购房者依靠家人帮助支付购房首付款。 婴儿潮一代——他们构成了老年人口的绝大多数——以及逐渐步入老年的X世代，是正式和非正式志愿服务中规模最大的群体，他们帮助邻居和其他社会成员。如今，超过三分之二的祖父母承担着照护者的角色，而且往往是定期甚至每天提供照护。通过让孩子的父母能够外出工作或继续求学，他们为整体经济贡献了超过9000亿美元。当然，老年人同样需要年轻一代，尤其是随着他们年龄的增长。事实是，不同世代始终彼此需要，过去如此，现在亦然。大多数美国年轻人都渴望拥有一栋独栋住宅，并组建自己的家庭。帮助他们实现这一愿望，是老一辈人的责任——不是为了满足老一辈自身的愿望，而是为了整个社会的未来。","2026-11-12_002":"世界，是怎么变得如此依赖黄仁勋一个人的？ How the world became too reliant on Jensen Huang The chief executive's travails are a reminder that a single point of failure is never a good thing for a big company A wayward chief executive is a major problem for any company. The scandal unfolding at the top of Nvidia, though, is a liability for a much broader set of people. It's a good moment to think about how to make the world less dependent on the fortunes and foibles of Jensen Huang. The Nvidia chief said on Sunday that he had been unfaithful to his wife, following reports that a woman with whom he had relations may have been a Chinese intelligence asset. Given the value of Nvidia's intellectual property and the fraught nature of US-China politics, this raises countless questions that a board probe, assisted by outside lawyers, will have to investigate. Whatever they find, it is undeniable that Nvidia without Huang as its CEO would have a problem. He is already spread thin, overseeing about 60 direct reports, with no obvious successor. And the company's board is not exactly robust. Its directors have an average tenure of 18 years, more than twice the S&amp;P 500 mean, according to consulting firm Spencer Stuart. Five of them have served for roughly three decades, with no apparent resistance from shareholders. What hurts Nvidia hurts the market because of its sheer size. The $4.5tn company makes up nearly 7 per cent of the S&amp;P 500. But Huang's empire has also underwritten growth at many of its customers, taking stakes, backstopping loans and financing data centre builders who in turn host AI labs such as OpenAI and Anthropic. Huang was a cheerleader when tech investors lost their nerve — most recently during the so-called SaaSpocalypse software sell-off. Fortunately there are plenty of templates for reducing dependency on a CEO. Companies such as Morgan Stanley, Amazon and Apple made successful chiefs into chairs while successors found their footing. Look too at Google DeepMind founder Demis Hassabis, who just stepped back from his role as CEO to instead become chair, giving him more space to think about the AI big picture. Even if Nvidia isn't prepared to countenance Huang leaving his CEO post, many companies have de facto number-two spots — presidents and chief operating officers, say — that ensure investors know there's someone on hand if the boss collides with a bus. Nvidia should do that too. It's also time to think about whether the oversight foisted on banks — long regarded as essential social infrastructure — should apply to big tech companies. Lenders such as JPMorgan, with regulators breathing down their necks, have no choice but to build in contingency plans. Nvidia is nowhere near as complicated as a Wall Street bank, but it is becoming similarly systemic. It would not be a surprise to see that on the new Congress's agenda in 2027. Investors already seem to be contemplating a less Nvidia-centric world. Shares in rival AMD rose 12 per cent on Monday; upstart chipmaker Cerebras was up 5 per cent. Customers move more slowly than markets, but Huang's slip-up gives them, too, a prod. Whatever the consequences, his predicament is a helpful reminder that a single point of failure is never a good thing.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n世界，是怎么变得如此依赖黄仁勋一个人的？ 世界，是怎么变得如此依赖黄仁勋一个人的？这位首席执行官的这场磨难提醒我们——单点故障，对一家大公司来说，从来都不是一件好事。 一个行为出格的 CEO，对任何公司而言都是大麻烦。然而，英伟达最高层正在发酵的这桩丑闻，却成了范围远为广泛的一群人的隐患。此刻，我们正该认真想想，如何让这个世界，不再如此依赖黄仁勋个人的财富运势，与那点无伤大雅的小毛病。 这位英伟达掌门人周日承认，自己曾对妻子不忠——此前有报道称，一名与他有染的女子，可能曾是中国情报机构的线人。鉴于英伟达知识产权的价值，以及中美政治本就紧绷的敏感性质，此事牵出了无数待解的问题，一个由外部律师协助的董事会调查，将不得不逐一查清。 无论调查结果如何，不可否认的是，一旦失去黄仁勋这位 CEO，英伟达势必陷入困境。他本人早已分身乏术，一人统管约六十名直接下属，却并无明显的继任者。而公司的董事会，也说不上多么强健——其董事平均任期长达十八年，为标普 500 均值的两倍有余（据咨询公司斯宾塞·斯图尔特统计），其中五人已任职约三十年，股东对此却毫无明显异议。 伤害英伟达，即是伤害整个市场，盖因其体量之大。这家市值四点五万亿美元的公司，独占标普 500 近百分之七的权重。而黄仁勋的帝国，还一直在为众多客户提供资金支撑——入股持股、为贷款兜底、为数据中心建设商提供融资，而这些数据中心，反过来又托管着 OpenAI、Anthropic 等 AI 实验室。每逢科技投资者信心动摇，黄仁勋便挺身而出充当「啦啦队长」——最近一次，便是在那场被戏称为「SaaS 大灾难」的软件股抛售潮中。 所幸，要降低对某一位 CEO 的依赖，现成的范本不胜枚举。摩根士丹利、亚马逊、苹果等公司，都曾让功成名就的首席执行官转任董事长，令继任者得以站稳脚跟。再看谷歌 DeepMind 创始人德米斯·哈萨比斯，他刚刚卸下 CEO 一职，转任董事长，从而留出更多空间，去思考 AI 的宏大格局。 即便英伟达尚不愿容忍黄仁勋卸任 CEO 之位，许多公司都设有「事实上的」二把手——诸如总裁、首席运营官之职——以此让投资者安心：即便老板突遭不测，也随时有人可以顶上。英伟达，也当如此。 如今，也是时候思考：长久以来被视作关键社会基础设施的银行，其头上那套「强加」的监管，是否也该套用到科技巨头身上。像摩根大通这样的放贷机构，在监管者如影随形的紧盯之下，别无选择，只能未雨绸缪、建好应急预案。英伟达固然远没有华尔街银行那般盘根错节，却正变得同样「系统重要」。若在 2027 年新一届国会的议程上看到这一议题，也丝毫不令人意外。 投资者似乎已在设想一个「不那么以英伟达为中心」的世界：竞争对手 AMD 的股价周一大涨 12%，芯片新贵 Cerebras 亦上扬 5%。客户的反应往往比市场迟缓，但黄仁勋此番失足，也给了他们一记轻轻的催促。无论最终结局如何，他的这场困境都是一次有益的提醒——单点故障，从来都不是一件好事。","2026-11-12_001":"Anthropic 剑指 1.4 万亿美元估值，上演一场爆炸级的上市 Anthropic Targets $1.4 Trillion Valuation in Blockbuster IPO Anthropic PBC is seeking a fully diluted valuation of about $1.4 trillion in its initial public offering, in a bid to make history as the first frontier artificial intelligence lab to go public. The Claude chatbot maker and some of its backers are seeking to raise as much as $90.4 billion in the IPO and a private placement. The base offering would raise up to $64.4 billion, with shares marketed for $70 to $78 each. At the top of the price range, Anthropic would have a market value of about $1 trillion, based on the shares outstanding listed in the filing. Accounting for employee stock options, restricted shares and stock reserved for the company’s charitable donation matching program, Anthropic would have a fully diluted value of about $1.4 trillion. Anthropic’s ambitious target, including the private placement, would hit roughly $100 billion when including a so-called over-allotment option or greenshoe. Investors are under pressure to decide whether valuations in AI reflect a plausible trajectory for a technology that many expect to transform the economy and society as a whole, or overconfidence in an investment theme that hasn’t yet proven its staying power. The widespread adoption of Claude Code and rival tools by software developers points to the potential transformation of a wide range of professional activity, from discovering new miracle drugs to navigating complex legal and accounting rules to running a factory. Already, more than 90% of contributions to Anthropic’s code base were written by Claude. Sales are surging. The company reported third-quarter revenue of nearly $17 billion as of Sept. 30, versus $1.4 billion in the same period a year ago. Keeping the pace of development running at a breakneck pace will require growing revenue faster than Anthropic’s spending on AI infrastructure. Anthropic has $895 billion of commitments, most of which are for computing power, chips, the filing shows. About $70 billion worth — a figure that’s larger than the individual market caps of roughly two-thirds of S&P 500 companies — are due just in the year through September 2027. Unlike other tech companies with sources of income such as advertising to fall back on, Anthropic will have to convince investors that its focus on AI alone will generate profit far beyond the technology’s surging costs. SpaceX may have tied its aspirations to AI when it pitched investors on its IPO, but most of its actual revenue at the time came from its other business lines. The speed of development has become increasingly controversial, with Anthropic’s own Chief Executive Officer Dario Amodei among those calling for top AI labs to “pace the frontier” in order to prioritize safety. Anthropic itself warned that the technology could pose “catastrophic or existential risks to humanity” in its IPO filing, if the technology isn’t developed with adequate safeguards and oversight. Regulators, investors and even Anthropic’s employees have flagged the significant risk of humans losing control of the technology, with one putting the chance of AI eliminating all humans in the next decade at greater than 10%. Bill Gates said AI is powerful enough to drive events that cause “a billion deaths.” Anthropic’s rival OpenAI is targeting a 2027 listing and Chief Executive Officer Sam Altman called 2026 an “ill-advised moment” for an IPO, citing concern over AI safety. The company is raising funds at a $1.25 trillion valuation, people familiar with the matter have said. Anthropic’s origin story centers around former OpenAI executives defecting because they wanted to prioritize AI safety. Nearly six years after its founding, Anthropic has structured itself as a public benefit corporation that balances shareholder interests with those of society, and whose governance includes a Long-Term Benefit Trust that has the power to elect and remove a majority of the members of the company’s board. The company’s total operating expenses jumped to $17.5 billion in the third quarter, a 292% increase from a year prior. It flipped from an operating profit of $974 million in the second quarter — its first after at least five quarters of operating losses — back to a negative $567 million in the three months ended Sept. 30. The operating loss in the third quarter was primarily driven by $5 billion of computing costs under short-term commitments and $2 billion of non-cash charges related to the company’s charitable donation-matching program. AI pricing pressures have begun to hit Anthropic’s revenue. Lower prices on some of its new models, plus contractual discounts for some customers, reduced its revenue growth in the third quarter by about $600 million. Anthropic has cut prices in newer model rollouts, including Opus 5.5 in September, and OpenAI has also introduced lower-priced models in recent months. The AI giants face growing competition from cheaper open-weight models, including from Chinese developers, which customers can download and fine-tune to their needs.\n⟪中文译文⟫\nAnthropic 剑指 1.4 万亿美元估值，上演一场爆炸级的上市 Anthropic这家公益公司，正寻求以约1.4万亿美元的完全稀释估值上市——若能成行，它将成为史上第一家走进公开市场的前沿AI实验室。 Anthropic这家Claude的缔造者，与其背后的一批投资方一道，拟通过IPO及一笔私募最多募得904亿美元；其中基础发行最多644亿美元，每股定价70至78美元。按价格区间上限、仅以已发行在外股份计，其市值约为1万亿美元；若再计入员工期权、限制性股票以及为慈善配捐计划预留的股份，完全稀释后的市值可达约1.4万亿美元。 Anthropic这一雄心勃勃的目标，若将私募与所谓的\"超额配售选择权\"（即绿鞋期权）一并计入，总规模将达约1000亿美元。投资者此刻如坐针毡，必须做出判断：AI领域的天价估值，究竟反映了一项被寄望于重塑整个经济与社会之技术的合理前景，还只是对一个尚未证明其持久生命力之投资主题的过度自信。 ClaudeCode及其竞品工具被软件开发者广泛采用，这预示着从研发神药、到驾驭复杂的法律与会计规则、再到运营工厂等一系列职业活动，都可能迎来变革。而Anthropic自家代码库中，已有超过90%的代码出自Claude之手。 销售额正在飙升：公司截至9月30日的第三季度收入接近170亿美元，而去年同期仅为14亿。要让研发维持在这种不要命的高速上，收入的增速就必须跑赢其在AI基础设施上的开支。招股文件显示，Anthropic背负着8950亿美元的承诺支出，其中大头是算力与芯片；而单是截至2027年9月的一年内，就有约700亿美元到期——这个数字，比标普500中约三分之二公司的市值都要大。 与其他有广告等收入来源可作退路的科技公司不同，Anthropic必须让投资者相信，它一门心思扑在AI上，也能赚出远超技术飙升成本的利润。SpaceX在上市推介时，或许也曾把抱负寄托在AI上，但它当时绝大部分的实际收入，其实来自其他业务线。 研发速度已愈发引发争议，连Anthropic自己的CEO达里奥·阿莫迪，也跻身于呼吁顶级AI实验室\"放慢前沿步伐\"、把安全置于首位的人之列。Anthropic甚至在招股文件中警告：倘若这项技术未在充分的防护与监督下发展，便可能给人类带来\"灾难性乃至生存性的风险\"。 监管者、投资者，乃至Anthropic自己的员工，都已对人类失去对这项技术控制权的重大风险示警，其中一人更将\"未来十年内AI灭绝全人类\"的概率估至10%以上。比尔·盖茨则称，AI强大到足以引发\"十亿人死亡\"级别的事件。 Anthropic的宿敌OpenAI计划于2027年上市，其CEO山姆·奥特曼以担忧AI安全为由，称2026年并非上市IPO的\"明智时机\"。据知情人士透露，OpenAI目前正以1.25万亿美元的估值进行融资。 Anthropic的出身故事，围绕一群前OpenAI高管的\"出走\"展开——他们出走，是为了把AI安全置于首位。成立近六年后，Anthropic已将自己打造成一家公益公司，既须在股东利益与社会利益之间求得平衡，其治理结构中又设有一个\"长期利益信托\"，握有选举与罢免公司董事会多数成员的权力。 公司第三季度总运营支出飙升至175亿美元，较上年同期增长292%。其运营业绩从第二季度9.74亿美元的盈利——那也是它连续至少五个季度亏损后首次转正——又跌回截至9月30日季度的负5.67亿美元。第三季度的亏损，主要源于短期承诺下的50亿美元算力成本，以及与慈善配捐计划相关的20亿美元非现金费用。 AI的价格压力已开始冲击Anthropic的收入：部分新模型降价，加上给某些客户的合同折扣，令其第三季度收入增速减少了约6亿美元。Anthropic在新模型发布时不断压价（包括9月的Opus5.5），OpenAI近几个月也推出了更便宜的模型。这些AI巨头正面临来自更廉价的开放权重模型日益激烈的竞争——其中包括来自中国开发者的模型——客户可将这些模型下载并按需微调。","2026-11-11_001":"450万移民改变了美国：他们究竟是谁，又去了哪里？ A Border Surge Reshaped America. Now, Data Shows Who 4.5 Million Migrants Were and Where They Went An unprecedented surge in border crossings over the past decade reshaped America: Millions of people from more than 150 countries arrived, bringing the U.S. immigrant population to its highest levels ever. Now, data obtained exclusively by The Associated Press paints a picture of who these migrants are, where they went, and what happened after the border. Blocks, neighborhoods and entire cities were remade in places like Elkhart, Indiana; Knoxville, Tennessee; and Dodge City, Kansas. This was no longer just a story about the border. It was a transformation unfolding in the everyday lives of Americans. Via a Freedom of Information Act request, AP obtained federal records on the self-reported destinations of 4.5 million migrants released in the United States after crossing the Mexican border — both legally and illegally — between October 2018 and July 2025. The Border Patrol encountered migrants from 174 countries in one year alone, a sharp departure from earlier periods when they were nearly all Mexican and Central American. Some went to cities that have long been magnets for immigrants — New York, Miami — but increasingly, others found their way to smaller cities with less developed immigrant footholds. Cincinnati, for example, saw a large number of arrivals from West Africa, Uzbekistan and India. New York City’s Queens borough registered at least 25 nationalities with significant arrivals, including five countries that each sent more than 7,000 people: Ecuador, China, Colombia, Venezuela and India. While Florida was the most popular state overall, Latin American immigrants increasingly looked elsewhere for more promising job prospects and better quality of life. Nearly six of every 10 migrants released at the border were women and children, a dramatic shift from a few years earlier when migrants were predominantly men. In 2023, Samba Diallo and roughly 2,000 of his countrymen arrived in Lockland, Ohio, increasing the town’s population by more than 50%, so suddenly it seemed to some that it happened overnight. No one knew they were coming, and Lockland has no formal services for immigrants. They piled into apartments, sometimes 12 or 15 in a space designed for a single family. They slept on mattresses pressed side by side on the floor. The fire chief warned there had been some small fires — they were unaccustomed to American stoves — and in a crammed apartment complex, this could pose a serious hazard. The influx of immigrants shocked this small town. One evening in 2023, Vincent Wilson, a local bicycle shop owner, arrived to teach an English class at a Christian-run center next to his bike shop. Instead of the group of a dozen or so West Africans he’d known for years, 160 people lined up around the building. Down the street, the lobby at a food and clothing pantry was packed, another line stretching out the door. Wilson realized they were walking miles a day. He put out a request for people to donate old, unused bikes. They didn’t speak much English at first, but Wilson figured out that the language of bicycles is universal: point, grunt, giggle and everyone understands. The Mauritanians became more visible in the community, walking around town, often in groups, sometimes wearing traditional robes unusual on American streets. Wilson became a familiar face to the newcomers, helping them navigate a country whose language and customs were still foreign to them. Khalidou Sy, a well-known hip-hop artist back home, became the Mauritanian community’s unofficial mayor. He spoke at churches and community meetings, explaining who all these new neighbors were. He also did some schooling of his fellow countrymen, he said. Mauritania is a more casual society, and here people strictly follow rules, he remembers scolding. Don’t whistle at women. Cross the street at the crosswalk. Rumors and lies spread. Rumors began circulating that the newcomers to Lockland were eating the ducks in the city park. Right-wing media reported about what was happening here as an “invasion.” KKK flyers started showing up, complaining of “illegal immigrants” and calling on “red-blooded white Americans” to “take your stand.” A group of neo-Nazis arrived from out of town, waving swastikas on a highway overpass. City leaders started sounding the alarm. The West Africans weren’t committing serious crimes, officials told the local media. The major concern was their overcrowded living conditions. Sy feels like he’s part of a great American tradition, the “melting pot,” he said, like the Irish and Italians before him. But he’s yet to put up an American flag his realtor gave him. “I love it, I wish I was American so I could hang it outside, but I cannot pretend,” he said. Maybe one day — his asylum application is still pending. For some Americans, immigration threatens the country they have always known. For newcomers like Sy, it represents the very tradition that made America what it is. The question is whether Americans can still agree on what that identity means.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n450万移民改变了美国：他们究竟是谁，又去了哪里？ 过去十年，前所未有的越境移民潮重塑了美国：来自150多个国家的数百万人涌入，使美国的移民人口达到历史最高水平。如今，美联社独家获得的数据勾勒出了这些移民的真实面貌：他们是谁、去了哪里，以及越过边境之后发生了什么。在印第安纳州的埃尔克哈特、田纳西州的诺克斯维尔和堪萨斯州的道奇城等地，从一个个街区、社区到整座城市，都被重新塑造。这早已不再只是一个关于边境的故事，而是一场正在美国人日常生活中逐渐展开的深刻变革。 通过《信息自由法》提出的信息公开申请，美联社获得了美国联邦政府的记录，其中包含2018年10月至2025年7月期间，450万名越过美墨边境后获准进入美国境内的移民自行申报的目的地信息。这些人既包括合法越境者，也包括非法越境者。仅在某一年，美国边境巡逻队就遇到了来自174个国家的移民，这与早些年移民几乎全部来自墨西哥和中美洲的情况形成了鲜明对比。一些移民前往纽约、迈阿密等长期以来吸引移民的大城市，但越来越多的人开始前往那些移民群体规模较小、移民社区基础尚不成熟的小城市。 例如，辛辛那提迎来了大量来自西非、乌兹别克斯坦和印度的移民。纽约市皇后区记录到至少25个国家的移民大量涌入，其中有五个国家各自都有超过7000人抵达，分别是厄瓜多尔、中国、哥伦比亚、委内瑞拉和印度。虽然从整体来看，佛罗里达州仍然是最受欢迎的目的地，但越来越多的拉丁美洲移民开始将目光投向其他地方，以寻求更有前景的就业机会和更好的生活质量。在边境获准进入美国境内的移民中，每10人就有将近6人是女性和儿童。这与几年前移民主要由男性构成的情况相比，发生了显著变化。 2023年，桑巴·迪亚洛和大约2000名同胞来到俄亥俄州的洛克兰，使这座小镇的人口增加了超过50%。这一切发生得如此突然，以至于在一些人看来，仿佛一夜之间就发生了。没有人事先知道他们会来，而洛克兰也没有专门为移民提供服务的正式机构。他们大量挤进公寓，有时12到15个人住在原本为一个家庭设计的空间里。他们睡在地板上，床垫一张紧挨着一张。消防局长警告说，当地已经发生过几起小型火灾，因为这些移民不习惯使用美式炉灶。而在如此拥挤的公寓楼里，这可能构成严重的安全隐患。 移民的大量涌入让这座小镇感到震惊。2023年的一个傍晚，当地自行车店老板文森特·威尔逊来到自己店铺旁边的一家基督教机构，准备教授一堂英语课。原本他以为会见到的是自己认识多年的十来位西非人，没想到竟有160个人在建筑物周围排起了长队。沿街不远处，一家提供免费食品和衣物的救助站大厅也挤满了人，另一条队伍一直延伸到门外。威尔逊意识到，这些移民每天都要步行好几英里。于是，他发出请求，希望人们能够捐赠家中闲置的旧自行车。 起初，他们不太会说英语，但威尔逊逐渐发现，自行车的语言是全世界通用的：指一指、哼几声、咯咯笑几下，大家就都明白了。毛里塔尼亚人在社区里变得越来越常见，他们经常成群结队地在镇上走动，有时穿着在美国街头并不常见的传统长袍。威尔逊也逐渐成为这些新来者熟悉的面孔，帮助他们适应这个语言和习俗对他们而言依然陌生的国家。 哈利杜·西在家乡是一位知名的嘻哈音乐人，来到美国后，他成了毛里塔尼亚移民社区的“非官方市长”。他在教堂和社区会议上发言，向当地居民解释这些新邻居究竟是谁。他说，自己也会给同胞们上一些课。他记得自己曾严厉地告诫他们：毛里塔尼亚是一个社会习惯相对随意的国家，但在这里，人们严格遵守规则。不要对女性吹口哨。过马路要走人行横道。 谣言和谎言开始蔓延。有人传言，新来到洛克兰的移民正在捕食城市公园里的鸭子。右翼媒体将这里发生的事情报道成一场“入侵”。三K党的传单开始出现在镇上，抱怨“非法移民”，并号召“血统纯正的美国白人”“站出来捍卫自己的立场”。一群新纳粹分子从外地赶来，在高速公路的立交桥上挥舞着纳粹卐字旗。市政府领导人开始发出警告。当地官员告诉媒体，这些西非移民并没有犯下严重罪行。真正令人担忧的主要问题，是他们过度拥挤的居住环境。 西说，他觉得自己也是美国一项伟大传统——“大熔炉”——的一部分，就像在他之前来到这里的爱尔兰人和意大利人一样。但他至今还没有挂起房产经纪人送给他的那面美国国旗。“我很喜欢它，我希望自己是美国人，这样就可以把它挂在外面，但我不能假装自己是。”他说。也许有一天可以——他的庇护申请仍在等待审批。对于一些美国人来说，移民正在威胁他们一直以来所熟悉的国家。但对于西这样的新来者而言，移民恰恰代表着塑造今日美国的那项传统。问题在于，美国人是否还能就这种国家身份究竟意味着什么达成共识。","2026-11-10_001":"千亿帝国掌舵人的私密丑闻：黄仁勋承认出轨，对方被美情报部门认定为间谍 Nvidia Opens Investigation Into Jensen Huang's Relationship With Chinese Spy Nvidia said on Sunday that it was opening an investigation into Jensen Huang, its chief executive and co-founder, after he acknowledged to the company’s board that he had been intimate with a business executive who U.S. intelligence determined was a Chinese spy. In a message to the company, Mr. Huang apologized. “I was unfaithful to my wife,” he said. “My actions hurt her and our children. There is no excuse for what I did. I am deeply sorry.” The relationship and affair are a rare misstep for Mr. Huang. Over the past three years, he could do no wrong. His chipmaker, which makes the technology to build artificial intelligence, grew into one of the world’s most valuable companies and the engine of the data center boom powering the U.S. economy. He started being followed by autograph-seeking throngs at events. And his black leather jackets, which he wears daily, became collectors’ items. Mr. Huang has credited his wife, Lori, whom he married in 1985, with making it possible for him to build the company. Public scrutiny of Mr. Huang’s ties with Ms. Huang began after The New York Times first reported last month that American intelligence agencies had discovered that Mr. Huang had developed a close relationship with a Chinese citizen and chip buyer, who U.S. officials concluded was gathering intelligence for Beijing. The chip buyer was Ms. Huang or Huang Le, whose biography said she worked in China as a TV reporter before becoming a top executive at a Singapore-based data center company that had become one of Nvidia’s biggest customers in Southeast Asia. Megaspeed was formed in 2023, when a Chinese online gaming company split off its Singaporean subsidiary. Megaspeed is among the firms in Southeast Asia that buy chips to build data centers and then rent their computing power to companies in China. Megaspeed is essentially a middle man. Megaspeed’s sister company in Malaysia bought at least $4.8 billion worth of advanced AI equipment containing Nvidia chips and technology between June 2024 and February 2026, according to trade records obtained from ImportGenius, a trade platform. It was an exceptional amount of money for a small, obscure company. The U.S. government has been investigating Megaspeed since late 2024, when a Commerce Department official visited its data center in Malaysia and found the site to be suspicious. The Nvidia equipment was still packed in boxes, suggesting it was being shipped in or out rather than installed there. Investigators suspect that some Nvidia chips acquired by Megaspeed are also finding their way illegally into China. Singapore has conducted its own investigation into Megaspeed. In 2025, Singaporean authorities brought Alice Huang in for questioning, but subsequently released her. As Megaspeed’s chip purchases rolled in, Mr. Huang and Ms. Huang began spending time together. Their relationship became a preoccupation in Washington, where officials were already alarmed by the large volume of Megaspeed’s purchases. Nvidia and Mr. Huang initially said there was nothing problematic or unusual about his relationship with Ms. Huang. In an interview with Bloomberg News last month, he said that his interactions with Ms. Huang had been “related to Megaspeed, and it was all completely normal and not unusual.” The U.S. government, which had been investigating Megaspeed for possibly steering advanced Nvidia A.I. chips to China, began its surveillance of Ms. Huang more than a year ago. In the course of the surveillance, they found that she was in communication with a U.S. citizen who was identified as Mr. Huang. Officials who had seen that intelligence said they were certain the relationship was romantic. That undercut Nvidia’s original misleading statements to The Times and others over the course of the last year about the nature of Mr. Huang’s relationship with Ms. Huang. As news about the U.S. surveillance of Ms. Huang broke, Mr. Huang’s room to maneuver all but disappeared. Congress wanted him to testify, probably under oath, and reporting on the surveillance raised the possibility that U.S. intelligence knew the nature of his relationship with Ms. Huang. There was also pressure on Nvidia’s board. Nvidia’s board said that it had “no reason to believe that either national security or Nvidia secrets were compromised,” but that “the board’s inquiry will examine all available information.” Mr. Huang, 63, will continue to lead the company during the investigation, the board said. He also received public support from President Trump. Asked by reporters whether he would bring Mr. Huang on his upcoming trip to China, Mr. Trump said, “I’d certainly take him.” He also described Mr. Huang as “an honorable guy.” As a co-founder, Mr. Huang has set up a unique structure at Nvidia where he dictates all aspects of its business while overseeing some 60 people who directly report to him. “Today’s news will not slow Nvidia,” Mr. Rizzo said. “Jensen is committed to continuing to lead Nvidia as C.E.O. without interruption.” Many questions about the situation remain unanswered, at least publicly.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n千亿帝国掌舵人的私密丑闻：黄仁勋承认出轨，对方被美情报部门认定为间谍 英伟达周日表示，公司正在对其首席执行官兼联合创始人黄仁勋展开调查。此前，黄仁勋向公司董事会承认，他曾与一名商业高管发生亲密关系，而美国情报机构认定这名高管是一名中国间谍。黄仁勋在发给公司的内部消息中道歉。他说：“我对妻子不忠。我的行为伤害了她和我们的孩子。我所做的一切没有任何借口。我深感抱歉。” 这段亲密关系和婚外情对于黄仁勋来说，是一次罕见的失足。过去三年里，他似乎做什么都不会出错。他创办的芯片制造公司为人工智能的发展提供关键技术，如今已成长为全球最有价值的公司之一，并成为推动美国经济发展的数据中心热潮背后的重要引擎。在各种活动现场，他开始受到大批索要签名的粉丝追捧。而他每天穿着的黑色皮夹克，也成了收藏家们争相收藏的物品。黄仁勋曾表示，正是因为妻子洛莉的支持，他才得以创办并发展这家公司。两人于1985年结婚。 上个月，《纽约时报》首次报道称，美国情报机构发现，黄仁勋与一名中国公民兼芯片采购商建立了密切关系，而美国官员认定，这名女子正在为北京方面收集情报。此后，黄仁勋与这名黄姓女子之间的关系开始受到公众的密切审视。这名芯片采购商名叫黄乐，也被称为Ms.Huang。她的个人履历显示，她曾在中国担任电视记者，后来成为一家总部位于新加坡的数据中心公司的高级管理人员，而这家公司已经发展成为英伟达在东南亚最大的客户之一。 Megaspeed成立于2023年，当时一家中国网络游戏公司将其位于新加坡的子公司分拆出来，成立了这家公司。Megaspeed是东南亚众多购买芯片、建设数据中心，再将计算能力出租给中国企业的公司之一。从本质上说，Megaspeed就是一家中间商。根据从贸易数据平台ImportGenius获取的贸易记录，2024年6月至2026年2月期间，Megaspeed位于马来西亚的姐妹公司购买了至少价值48亿美元的先进人工智能设备，其中包含英伟达的芯片和技术。对于这样一家规模不大、鲜为人知的公司来说，这是一笔异常巨大的金额。 自2024年底以来，美国政府一直在调查Megaspeed。当时，美国商务部的一名官员走访了该公司位于马来西亚的数据中心，发现现场存在可疑之处。英伟达的设备仍然装在箱子里，这表明这些设备可能正在被运进或运出，而不是被安装在当地使用。调查人员怀疑，Megaspeed采购的部分英伟达芯片也正在通过非法途径流入中国。新加坡也对Megaspeed展开了自己的调查。2025年，新加坡当局曾传唤黄乐接受问询，但随后将她释放。 随着Megaspeed的芯片采购订单源源不断地涌入，黄仁勋与黄乐也开始频繁相处。两人之间的关系逐渐成为华盛顿官员高度关注的问题，因为这些官员此前就已经对Megaspeed巨大的采购规模感到担忧。起初，英伟达和黄仁勋都表示，他与黄乐之间的关系不存在任何问题，也没有任何不寻常之处。在上个月接受彭博新闻社采访时，黄仁勋表示，他与黄乐之间的接触都“与Megaspeed有关，一切都完全正常，没有任何异常”。 美国政府此前一直在调查Megaspeed是否可能将英伟达的先进人工智能芯片转运至中国，并在一年多前开始对黄乐实施监视。在监视过程中，调查人员发现，她一直与一名美国公民保持联系，而这名公民后来被确认就是黄仁勋。看过相关情报的官员表示，他们确信两人之间存在恋爱关系。这一发现削弱了英伟达过去一年向《纽约时报》及其他方面所作声明的可信度，因为那些声明对黄仁勋与黄乐之间关系的真实性质作出了误导性的描述。 随着美国政府监视黄乐的消息被曝光，黄仁勋几乎失去了所有回旋余地。美国国会希望他出席作证，而且很可能要求他宣誓后提供证词。与此同时，有关监视行动的报道也让外界意识到，美国情报机构可能早已掌握了他与黄乐之间关系的真实性质。英伟达董事会同样面临着压力。英伟达董事会表示，目前“没有理由认为国家安全或英伟达的商业机密受到了损害”，但同时强调，“董事会的调查将审查所有能够获得的信息”。 董事会表示，现年63岁的黄仁勋将在调查期间继续领导公司。他还获得了特朗普总统的公开支持。当记者问特朗普，是否愿意在即将到来的中国之行中带上黄仁勋时，特朗普回答说：“我当然愿意带上他。”他还形容黄仁勋是“一个正直的人”。作为联合创始人，黄仁勋在英伟达建立了一套独特的管理架构：他掌控公司业务的方方面面，同时直接管理着大约60名向他汇报工作的人员。里佐先生表示：“今天的新闻不会拖慢英伟达的发展。黄仁勋将坚定不移地继续担任英伟达首席执行官，领导公司的工作不会受到任何中断。”至少从公开信息来看，围绕这一事件，仍有许多问题尚未得到解答。","2026-11-09_002":"Leaving the cosy confines of college for the tundra of the job market has always been bracing. Not every graduate hears a calling to a particular profession. Many want to do good but also need to pay off debt. AI is making the transition even bumpier, automating entry-level jobs and threatening entire careers. On July 13th more than 200 economists and tech luminaries warned that AI could cause “large-scale job displacement”. Finding your life’s work has never been so hard. As graduates trawl LinkedIn—sending AI-polished applications only to be rejected by AI-powered recruiters—they may wonder why they bothered with college at all. Among recent American graduates, 40% work in roles that do not require a bachelor’s degree, according to the New York Fed. Gen Z’s career anxiety has produced a rash of advice books. Bill Gurley, Jodi Kantor and Benjamin Todd all offer different answers to the same question. Mr Gurley encourages students to pursue careers they genuinely love. Ms Kantor likewise argues that fulfilling work can also be financially rewarding. Yet both authors also demonstrate why highly successful people often make poor career advisers. Their own careers are exceptional, shaped by survivorship bias rather than ordinary experience. Stories of successful restaurateurs or famous podcasters make inspiring anecdotes, but they offer little guidance to the average graduate. Hard work and passion alone rarely guarantee success. Benjamin Todd’s 80,000 Hours, the strongest and most original of the three books, takes the opposite approach. Drawing on 15 years of advising people at the beginning of their working lives, it examines careers not from the perspective of those who have already succeeded, but from the choices facing ordinary people. The result is a more systematic framework for thinking about work. First, perhaps do not follow your passion. Career books have long encouraged people to discover what they enjoy and then find matching jobs. But many people simply do not know what they want. Even when they have a genuine passion, there may be little demand for it in the labour market. Large numbers of students love music, art or sport, yet relatively few jobs exist in those fields. Passion alone is not a career strategy. “‘Follow your passion’ gets it backwards,” Mr Todd argues. Instead, become highly skilled at something valuable to other people, and passion may develop afterwards. Helping others increases life satisfaction, while mastering difficult skills is rewarding in itself. If you become excellent at your work, you gain more leverage to shape your career than if you pursue an existing passion but remain mediocre. To explain how people can make a positive difference, Mr Todd draws on the philosophy of effective altruism. Donating effectively may sometimes produce greater social impact than working directly in a helping profession. He also identifies AI regulation and preventing great-power conflict as careers with unusually large potential benefits. On money, he argues that beyond roughly $75,000 of annual household income, additional earnings contribute relatively little to self-reported happiness. His discussion of AI is equally counter-intuitive. Automation eventually destroys jobs, but it often increases demand for complementary skills before doing so. Banks actually employed more clerks after ATMs were introduced because lower operating costs made it worthwhile to open more branches. Rather than searching for careers that can never be automated, workers should continually adapt, moving towards the skills that become more valuable as technology changes. That may include AI model training, cyber-security or service industries whose demand rises as AI lowers costs. Still, Mr Todd’s philosophy has its own limitations. A relentless focus on maximising global good implies that every career choice should be judged by its overall impact on humanity. Taken to its logical conclusion, this way of thinking can become paralysing. Even John Stuart Mill struggled with depression, while Sam Bankman-Fried became a cautionary example of effective altruism taken disastrously wrong. Ultimately, careers rarely follow a straight path. Tony Blair promoted rock concerts before entering politics. Maya Angelou worked as a streetcar conductor before becoming a celebrated writer. Colonel Sanders founded Kentucky Fried Chicken at the age of 62. Getting your career right often takes years of experimentation and changing direction. In an age when AI is transforming the labour market faster than ever, certainty is becoming increasingly rare. Anyone worrying about how to spend their 80,000 working hours could do worse than spending a few of them reading Mr Todd’s thoughtful book.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n【共读】“做你喜欢的事情”可能是最坑年轻人的职业建议：AI时代，找工作逻辑彻底变了 为何传统职业建议在Z世代面前失灵——别再盲从“追随梦想”那一套了，试试这个 走出大学温暖的象牙塔，踏入求职市场的荒原，这历来都是一次令人清醒的淬炼。并非每个毕业生都能听见某种职业的神圣召唤。许多人既想行善济世，又身负债务需要偿还。而人工智能的崛起，更令这场过渡雪上加霜——它正在吞噬初级岗位，甚至威胁到整个行业的存续。7月13日，两百余位经济学家与科技界翘楚联名发出警告：AI可能引发\"大规模岗位替代\"。找到毕生志业，从未如此艰难。 当毕业生们埋头刷着领英——发送经过AI精心润色的简历，却收到由AI驱动的招聘系统发来的拒信——他们或许会困惑，自己当初究竟为什么要上大学。根据纽约联储的数据，美国近年的毕业生中，有40%在从事根本不需要本科学历的工作。Z世代的职业焦虑催生了一大批指导书籍。比尔·格利、乔迪·坎特和本杰明·托德，对同一个问题给出了各自不同的解答。 格利先生鼓励学生去追求自己真正热爱的事业。坎特女士同样主张，让人有成就感的工作也能带来丰厚的经济回报。然而，这两位作者也在不经意间揭示了一个道理：为什么极度成功的人往往成为糟糕的职业顾问。他们自身的职业轨迹是异乎寻常的，是由幸存者偏差而非普通人的经验所塑造。成功餐厅老板或知名播客主的故事，固然是鼓舞人心的佳话，但对普通毕业生而言，却几乎没有指导意义。仅凭努力和热爱，极少能确保成功。 而本杰明·托德的《八万小时》，作为三本书中最为深刻、最具原创性的一部，则采取了截然相反的路径。基于15年来为初入职场者提供咨询的经验，它审视职业的视角，不是从那些已经功成名就者出发，而是立足于普通人所面临的抉择。由此，一个更为系统化的职业思考框架应运而生。 首先，或许你不该追随自己的热情所在。长期以来，职业类书籍都在鼓励人们去发现自己的兴趣所在，然后找到匹配的工作。但许多人根本不知道自己想要什么。即便他们确实怀有某种真正的热情，劳动力市场也未必对此有需求。大量学生热爱音乐、艺术或体育，但这些领域的工作岗位相对寥寥。热情本身，并不能构成一种职业策略。 \"'追随你的热情'把因果搞反了，\"托德先生如此论断。相反，应该先精进某项对他人有价值的技能，热情或许会在此之后悄然生长。帮助他人能提升人生的满足感，而掌握艰深的技能本身就令人愉悦。若你在工作中出类拔萃，便能获得更多塑造职业生涯的筹码，这远胜于追逐既有的热情却始终表现平庸。 为了阐释人们如何能对社会产生积极的改变，托德先生援引了有效利他主义的哲学。有时候，有效的捐赠所带来的社会影响力，可能比直接从事某个助人职业更为巨大。他还将AI监管、防止大国冲突等领域界定为具有超常潜在价值的职业方向。在金钱问题上，他提出，家庭年收入一旦超过大约7.5万美元，额外收入对自我报告的幸福感的贡献便微乎其微了。 他对AI的探讨同样反直觉。自动化最终会摧毁工作岗位，但在此之前，它往往会增加对互补技能的需求。自动取款机普及后，银行雇佣的出纳员反而增多了，因为更低的运营成本使得开设更多分行变得划算。与其寻找永远不会被自动化的工作，工作者更应不断适应，朝着那些随技术变革而水涨船高的技能方向迁移。这可能包括AI模型训练、网络安全，或是那些因AI降低成本而需求上升的服务行业。 尽管如此，托德先生的哲学也有其自身的局限性。对最大化全球福祉的执着追求，暗含着一种倾向：即每一个职业选择都应以它对人类整体的影响来评判。若将这种思维方式推演到极致，足以令人举步维艰。就连约翰·斯图尔特·密尔也曾深陷抑郁，而萨姆·班克曼-弗里德更是成了有效利他主义走向灾难性歧途的警示教材。 归根结底，职业生涯很少是一条坦途。托尼·布莱尔在进入政界前，曾推广过摇滚音乐会；玛雅·安杰卢在成为享誉文坛的作家前，做过电车售票员；山德士上校在62岁时才创立了肯德基。找到适合你的职业道路，往往需要多年的摸索与转向。在一个AI正以空前速度重塑劳动力市场的时代，确定性正变得日益稀缺。任何为如何度过那八万个小时的工作生涯而忧心忡忡的人，不妨从中抽出几个钟头，读一读托德先生这本发人深省的著作。","2026-11-09_001":"一家银行塌了，把拉美这位“明星优等生”一直背着光的那一面，掀到了台前 A bank collapse exposes the dark side of Latin America’s star economyUeno Bank’s seizure has toppled Paraguay’s president and tarnished its glossy pitch to investors The stunning downfall of Paraguay’s president and collapse of its most popular bank have exposed cracks in a free-market success story hailed by investors and the global conservative movement in recent years. The presidency of Santiago Peña, a smooth-talking former IMF economist, unravelled in less than 72 hours this week after regulators seized control of Ueno Bank, a flashy digital lender used by more than half of Paraguayan adults that had become insolvent. Lawmakers quickly launched impeachment proceedings against Peña, a former investor in Ueno’s holding company who installed its boss to lead Paraguay’s central bank and entrusted the lender with hundreds of millions of dollars of public money. The senate approved Peña’s ouster on Friday. The episode, which has left 2.9mn people unable to access their savings, has shocked Paraguayans after their technocratic president spent three years in office promising to build a “modern” state that might one day join the OECD, and travelling the world to promote Paraguay as a haven for foreign capital. “We thought we were improving, but actually we’re the same as always,” said Elizabeth Gonzalez, 28, a barista in a café in downtown Asunción, a few blocks from where hundreds of protesters are maintaining a vigil after larger demonstrations earlier this week. Paraguay has emerged as an oasis of consistent GDP growth and macroeconomic stability within Latin America over the past decade. Peña also secured the country’s first investment-grade credit ratings. But the rapid implosion of Peña’s government, analysts said, would draw attention to deep-rooted problems with corruption and clientelism that have marred Paraguay’s economic development story. “The underbelly of the success story has been an institutional landscape that is . . . captive to a very small number of groups and families,” said Mariano Machado, Americas principal at risk intelligence firm Verisk Maplecroft. “That’s much more visible now than it was at the start of this week.” Paraguay has been ruled by the same party — the rightwing Colorados — for 75 of the last 80 years. Peña’s impeachment was largely the result of a rift with party president Horacio Cartes. Cartes picked Peña as a candidate in 2023 elections. But earlier this year Peña moved to form his own movement within the Colorado Party, sidelining Cartes’s influence. “Peña thought he could walk alone and that Cartes would fade into irrelevance,” said Sebastián Acha, a consultant and former lawmaker in Paraguay. “Cartes did not agree.” The central bank had already received at least 10 warnings since last year regarding Ueno’s substantial lending to entities related to its own parent company and other financial issues, according to Paraguayan newspaper ABC Color. Ueno Bank had a prominent role in Peña’s vision of a modern Paraguayan economy. Founded as a fintech in 2021, it quickly obtained a banking licence and waged an aggressive marketing campaign to bring on new clients, including sponsoring the national football team. Ueno took in more than $850mn in public money, including a tenth of the social security agency’s investment fund, and many small businesses began to rely on its payment system in Paraguay’s heavily informal economy. Ueno founder Miguel Vázquez spoke about becoming the country’s first unicorn and listing on the New York Stock Exchange. “Everything that man said was just window dressing, because it was all a sham,” said Eduardo Lopez, 35, a businessman at the protest who works in exporting goods to the US. “It affected all businesses . . . and it makes the country look bad. It’s a disaster.” In the early hours of Tuesday morning, the central bank froze Ueno accounts and began a process to wind down its operations. The monetary authority said it would guarantee deposits up to the equivalent of about $38,000 per customer and that there was no risk to the wider financial system. The response has raised hopes that Paraguay’s institutions will strengthen in the wake of the Ueno fiasco. “We are seeing the start of corrective measures,” said José Tomás Sánchez, a director at ICA consultancy in Asunción, arguing that the rapid arrest and charging of those involved “sends a very strong signal . . . that you can no longer do this kind of thing in Paraguay.” But others were less optimistic. “It’s pretty clear that this crisis was only resolved because Cartes decided to abandon Peña for political reasons,” said Ramiro Blazquez Giomi, strategist at financial services group Stone X. “It suggests there haven’t been the kind of institutional improvements that we had hoped to see. “We’re suffering the consequences of Peña splitting from Cartes,” said Batarram Caballero, 23, president of the student centre at the National University of Paraguay. “This is a group of people who have meddled and are destroying our country.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n一家银行塌了，把拉美这位“明星优等生”一直背着光的那一面，掀到了台前 总统骤然垮台，国民银行轰然坍塌——这两下，把那个近些年被投资人与全球保守派一路捧为\"自由市场样板\"的成功故事，戳出了一道道细裂。 嘴皮子极溜的前IMF经济学家圣地亚哥·培尼亚，其总统任期本周不到72小时便层层脱线——只因监管一把收走了韦诺银行：那家扎眼的数字银行，一半以上巴拉圭成年人都在用，却早已资不抵债。 议会迅即启动对培尼亚的弹劾程序：此人曾持股于韦诺银行控股公司，既将其旧日掌舵人安插为央行行长，又将数以亿计的公帑托付该行。周五，参议院核准罢免。 这一桩风波，已令290万人取不出积蓄，举国为之震愕——毕竟他们那位技术官僚总统在任三年，一面许诺要建成一个\"现代化\"国家、有朝一日或可跻身经合组织，一面满世界奔走，把巴拉圭推介为外资的避风港。\"我们原以为日子在往好里走，敢情还是老一套，\"28岁的咖啡师伊丽莎白·冈萨雷斯说。她供职的咖啡馆在亚松森市中心，隔几个街区，数百名抗议者仍在静守——本周早些时候，那里曾有过规模更大的示威。 过去十年，巴拉圭在拉美渐次长成一块绿洲：增长连贯、宏观稳健。培尼亚又为这个国家挣来了史上首批投资级评级。然而分析师指出，其政府这场骤然内爆，将把目光引向根深蒂固的腐败与庇护主义——正是它们，早给巴拉圭的经济发展叙事留下了抹不掉的痕。\"这个成功故事的底下，始终是一片被极少数集团与家族攫住的制度地貌，\"风险情报公司VeriskMaplecroft美洲区负责人马里亚诺·马查多说，\"较之本周一，它如今清晰得多。\" 近八十年间，巴拉圭有七十五载系出同一政党——右翼红党。培尼亚之遭弹劾，大半缘于他与党主席奥拉西奥·卡特斯的决裂。2023年大选，正是卡特斯将他点为候选人；而今年早些时候，培尼亚却着手在红党之内另树一帜，把卡特斯的影响挪到了边线。\"培尼亚以为自己足可独行，以为卡特斯会慢慢淡到无人置理，\"巴拉圭政治顾问、前议员塞巴斯蒂安·阿查说，\"卡特斯并未首肯。\" 据巴拉圭《ABC彩色报》披露，自去年以来，央行即已接获不下十次预警，所指为Ueno向与其自家母公司相关联的实体大额放款，以及其他财务事项。在培尼亚所构想的\"现代巴拉圭经济\"图景中，Ueno银行曾居于最显眼处。它2021年以一家金融科技公司起家，旋即取得银行牌照，继而发起一场凌厉的营销攻势以广纳新客，赞助国家足球队即其中一举。 Ueno吸纳的公共资金逾8.5亿美元，其中含社保机构投资基金的十分之一；在巴拉圭非正规成分极重的经济中，众多小本生意亦渐次仰赖其支付体系维生。Ueno创始人米格尔·巴斯克斯曾谈及，欲使公司成为本国首家\"独角兽\"，并登陆纽约证券交易所。\"那人所说的一切，不过是装点门面，因为内里根本是个空壳，\"35岁的爱德华多·洛佩斯说。他是静守现场的一名商人，经营对美出口。\"此事波及所有行当……也叫这个国家面上无光。是一场灾难。\" 周二凌晨，央行冻结Ueno账户，并启动程序，令其业务有序收束。货币当局表示，将按每名存款人约合3.8万美元的上限担保存款，且更广的金融体系并无风险。此番应对使人寄望：经Ueno此番狼藉之后，巴拉圭的制度或得转强。\"我们正目睹纠偏措施的发端，\"亚松森ICA咨询公司主管何塞·托马斯·桑切斯说。他认为，对涉案者的迅速拘捕与起诉\"释放了一个极强信号……在巴拉圭，这等事今后再也做不得了\"。 然亦有人未敢乐观。\"此事之得以收场，分明只因Cartes出于政治考量决意弃Peña于不顾，\"金融服务集团StoneX策略师拉米罗·布拉斯克斯·乔米说，\"由此可见，我们昔日所盼的那般制度之进，并未发生。\"\"Peña与Cartes决裂的后果，正由我们承担，\"23岁的巴塔拉姆·卡瓦列罗说，他是国立巴拉圭大学学生中心主席。\"不过是那一撮人伸手搅局，正在毁掉我们的国家。\"","2026-11-08_001":"The blame started before voting in America’s midterm elections ended and the extent of the Republicans’ drubbing was revealed. In an interview on election day Donald Trump said that America would cut off military aid to Israel “at the end of the year”. Israel no longer needs it, he explained; the country is “thriving”. But there was another reason, the president added: “It’s a bad talking point.” J.D. Vance, the vice-president, was more direct. On the day after the election, he blamed the war in Iran, and also America’s relationship with Israel, for the Republicans’ defeat. Israel has been falling steadily out of favour in America. Its brutal campaigns in Gaza and Lebanon had already angered voters and politicians on both ends of the political spectrum. The unpopular war with Iran has drawn further attention to the $3.8bn Israel receives annually from America, nearly all of it as subsidies for the purchase of American-made arms and other military supplies. America’s alliance with Israel featured in a number of high-profile contests, including that of Abdul El-Sayed, the senator-elect from Michigan who fiercely criticised military aid. Other candidates took pride in not accepting donations from AIPAC, the pro-Israel lobby. AIPAC itself said two weeks before the election that it would stop endorsing candidates, a tacit admission of the toxicity of its brand. Barack Obama signed the current ten-year $3.8bn package, which is set to run out in 2028. The Israeli government was aware that negotiating a new package would be tricky. Growing numbers of Democrats are particularly hostile to Israel, and the Trump administration—and Republicans more widely—are loth to give money to foreigners. In light of this, Binyamin Netanyahu, Israel’s prime minister, told The Economist in January that he favoured gradually phasing out American aid over the next ten years. Talks had started in recent months about the future of aid, but Mr Trump’s election-day announcement took Israeli officials by surprise. “It’s been clear for years that the terms of the alliance with America were going to have to change,” says Alon Pinkas, a former diplomat who served as Israel’s consul-general in New York. “Now it looks like Netanyahu has agreed with Trump to end the current aid agreement, without anything ready to replace it.” The future of the relationship is unclear. Mr Trump has suggested that Israel will be able to continue buying American arms, on its own dime. But Israeli officials worry that a Democratic-controlled Congress will limit the kinds of weapons Israel can buy and restrict how it can use them, especially in the occupied Palestinian territories. For over half a century successive American administrations have been committed to maintaining Israel’s “qualitative military edge” in the Middle East, supplying it with advanced weapons systems, years before America’s Arab allies. That may be up for debate as well. An end to American aid would blow a $7bn hole in Israel’s finances in the next two years. After three years fighting multiple wars, it has been on a spending spree to refresh the IDF’s arsenal, ordering planes, helicopters and more. Israel will owe another $10bn after 2028 for kit it has already ordered, according to the Wall Street Journal. Israel’s economy could withstand that, though it would mean cuts to the national budget in the coming years. “In financial terms it’s regrettable, but not an earthquake,” says Yigal Newman, a professor of finance at the Hebrew University in Jerusalem. “American aid is barely half a percent of Israel’s GDP.” But, he points out, it would also be a blow to Israel’s standing as America’s key ally. Israel’s relationship with America not only boosts its military and economic power, it gives Israel diplomatic cover as well. America frequently deploys its veto in the United Nations Security Council to block anti-Israel resolutions. It puts pressure on other countries, such as Britain, which has announced sanctions on Israel over the violent settler attacks on Palestinians in the occupied West Bank. If the new Congress is considering similar sanctions, Israel will be more exposed internationally. All is not lost for the alliance, which goes far beyond arms sales. America maintains radar and intelligence-gathering installations in Israel and recently expanded an airbase used by American aircraft in the Negev. The two countries co-operate intensively on missile defence and intelligence sharing. Israel has access to America’s surveillance satellites. America’s arms companies make use of the Israel Defence Forces to test their weapons on the battlefield. But the relationship has fundamentally changed. Mr Netanyahu, who lost his majority in the Knesset on October 27th, has long presented himself to Israelis as the ultimate expert on America. Yet he has overseen the most drastic deterioration in his country’s standing in America in Israel’s history. Israeli diplomats privately accept that their prime minister, his hardline policies and hard-right coalition are “toxic in America”. A change in government in Jerusalem would help. But the alliance is becoming more brittle.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n川川输了中期选举，以色列先慌了？中东要变天了 共和党人将选举失利归咎于以色列：民主党的胜利可能加速美以联盟的衰落 甚至在美国中期选举投票结束、共和党惨败的程度尚未揭晓之前，指责就已经开始了。在选举日的一次采访中，唐纳德·特朗普表示，美国将在“今年年底”停止向以色列提供军事援助。他解释说，以色列已经不再需要这些援助了，因为这个国家正“蓬勃发展”。但总统补充说，还有另一个原因：“这是一个不利于政治宣传的话题。”副总统J.D.万斯则说得更加直接。选举结束后的第二天，他将共和党的失败归咎于伊朗战争，以及美国与以色列之间的关系。 以色列在美国正逐渐失去支持。以色列在加沙和黎巴嫩发动的残酷军事行动，早已激怒了美国政治光谱两端的选民和政治人物。而不得人心的对伊朗战争，又进一步引发了人们对美国每年向以色列提供的38亿美元援助的关注。这笔援助几乎全部以补贴的形式，用于购买美国制造的武器及其他军事物资。美国与以色列的同盟关系成为多场备受瞩目的选举中的重要议题，其中包括来自密歇根州的当选参议员阿卜杜勒·埃尔-赛义德所参加的选举，他曾猛烈批评对以色列的军事援助。其他候选人则以不接受亲以色列游说组织AIPAC的捐款为荣。就在选举前两周，AIPAC自身也宣布将停止公开支持候选人，这等于默认了其品牌形象如今已具有相当大的负面政治影响。 现行的十年期、每年38亿美元的援助计划由巴拉克·奥巴马签署，预计将于2028年到期。以色列政府清楚，谈判达成一项新的援助协议将十分棘手。越来越多的民主党人对以色列抱有强烈敌意，而特朗普政府乃至更广泛的共和党人也不愿意把钱给外国人。鉴于这种情况，以色列总理本雅明·内塔尼亚胡今年1月曾向《经济学人》表示，他赞成在未来十年逐步取消美国援助。近几个月来，双方已经开始就未来的援助安排展开谈判，但特朗普在选举日发表的声明仍然令以色列官员措手不及。 “多年来，人们一直很清楚，与美国的同盟关系条款迟早必须发生改变，”曾担任以色列驻纽约总领事的前外交官阿隆·平卡斯说道。“现在看来，内塔尼亚胡似乎已经同意与特朗普终止现行援助协议，却没有准备好任何可以取而代之的新安排。” 这段关系的未来仍不明朗。特朗普暗示，以色列仍然可以继续购买美国武器，只不过必须自己掏钱。但以色列官员担心，由民主党控制的国会将限制以色列能够购买的武器种类，并限制这些武器的使用方式，尤其是在被占领的巴勒斯坦领土上。半个多世纪以来，美国历届政府一直致力于维持以色列在中东地区的“质量军事优势”，比向美国的阿拉伯盟友提供先进武器系统早数年，就向以色列供应这些装备。而如今，这一长期政策也可能成为重新讨论和争议的对象。 美国援助的终止将在未来两年给以色列的财政造成70亿美元的资金缺口。在经历了三年多场战争之后，以色列一直在大举采购，以更新以色列国防军（IDF）的武器装备，包括订购飞机、直升机等。据《华尔街日报》报道，2028年之后，以色列还需要为已经订购的装备支付另外100亿美元。以色列经济能够承受这笔负担，尽管这意味着未来几年国家预算必须进行削减。“从财政角度来说，这令人遗憾，但还算不上是一场地震，”耶路撒冷希伯来大学金融学教授伊加尔·纽曼表示。“美国援助仅占以色列国内生产总值的约0.5%。” 但他指出，这也将打击以色列作为美国重要盟友的地位。以色列与美国的关系不仅增强了它的军事和经济实力，还为以色列提供了外交庇护。美国经常在联合国安全理事会动用否决权，阻止针对以色列的决议获得通过。美国还会向其他国家施压，例如英国。英国已因以色列定居者在被占领的约旦河西岸对巴勒斯坦人发动暴力袭击而宣布对以色列实施制裁。如果新一届美国国会也在考虑类似制裁，以色列在国际上将更加孤立无援，更容易受到外部压力。 对这一同盟关系而言，并非一切都已失去，因为美以同盟远不止武器销售那么简单。美国在以色列设有雷达和情报收集设施，最近还扩建了位于内盖夫地区、供美国军机使用的一座空军基地。两国在导弹防御和情报共享方面开展密切合作。以色列可以使用美国的侦察卫星。而美国的军火公司则利用以色列国防军，在真实战场上测试其武器。 但美以关系已经发生了根本性的变化。内塔尼亚胡于10月27日失去了在以色列议会中的多数席位。长期以来，他一直向以色列民众宣称自己是最了解美国的顶尖专家。然而，恰恰是在他的执政期间，以色列在美国的地位经历了该国历史上最严重的恶化。以色列外交官私下承认，他们的总理、他的强硬政策以及极右翼执政联盟，在美国已经成了“政治毒药”。如果耶路撒冷的政府发生更迭，情况或许会有所改善。但美以同盟正变得越来越脆弱。","2026-11-07_001":"US stocks shed more than $3tn in a day of chaotic trading which saw the tech-heavy Nasdaq 100 plunge 6.4 per cent after Democrats’ sweeping midterm election victory upended Wall Street’s mammoth AI bet. The market value drop on the S&P Composite 1500 index, a broad basket of America’s publicly traded companies, was the biggest since the turn of the millennium, according to FT calculations based on FactSet data. Traders stampeded out of tech shares on fears that Democrats’ stunning victory over Donald Trump’s Republicans in Tuesday’s midterms would cool the AI spending boom that has driven Wall Street to record highs this year. “[Investors] are throwing in the towel and indiscriminately liquidating,” said Adam Crisafulli, president of market research group Vital Knowledge. Selling among chipmakers was brutal, with Nvidia, the company most closely associated with the AI surge, tumbling 9.3 per cent, pushing its market value down more than $400bn. The S&P 500 index of US blue-chip companies lost 4.4 per cent, its worst session since last year’s “liberation day” ructions, in a session marked by wild swings. The broader Nasdaq Composite fell 5.5 per cent, its steepest drop in 19 months, closing more than 10 per cent below its October 6 record and confirming a technical correction. The Philadelphia semiconductor index plunged more than 12 per cent. “With the blue wave, investors fear that peak [AI capital spending] is behind us,” said Charles-Henry Monchau, chief investment officer at Swiss private bank SYZ Group. One hedge fund portfolio manager said the move was driven by quick-moving investors ditching large positions, known as “de-grossing”. “People are starting to get worried now, today the move was so swift,” said another hedge fund executive. Investors fear that newly elected politicians who campaigned against data centres will hit the brakes on AI infrastructure development, eroding Wall Street’s expectations for vast spending on chips, servers and facilities that have driven up earnings expectations and powered stocks to record highs this year. “Guys who would normally be bargain-hunting, excited by these low prices, they’re not jumping in . . . everyone’s hiding out in cash,” said the chief executive of a New York brokerage. Vincent Mortier, the investment chief of Amundi, said the “honeymoon” gains for US stocks on the day after Democrats won control of Congress had been “very short-lived”. He added that the tech repricing was “harsh and quick as the fundamental question marks resurface under the microscope of the new political US picture — data centres backlash, new regulation”. Abdul El-Sayed, the Democratic senator-elect from Michigan, has said there should be a “full-on pause” in frontier development, arguing that “you’re seeing the pace of technological development outpace the pace of safety”. Incoming South Carolina senator Annie Andrews proposed a two-year ban on new data centre construction to stop communities “getting steamrollered” by “incredibly wealthy, powerful companies”. The Democrats’ win in Congress was “a horrible day for America”, said a US portfolio manager with large positions in tech companies including Anthropic. The results of the midterms would have “massive ramifications” for the price of chips used in AI servers, the initial public offering pipeline and AI data centre companies, the person said. Several Big Tech bosses spent heavily to support the electoral chances of Trump’s Republicans, who have sought to lighten regulation of the AI sector. Semiconductor groups were among Thursday’s biggest declines. Qualcomm dropped 9.5 per cent, Broadcom fell 10 per cent and Micron shed 17 per cent. Memory stocks Sandisk and Western Digital — among the market’s best performers this year — plunged 22 per cent and 17 per cent, respectively. But the selling was not limited to tech stocks. Caterpillar, the world’s biggest heavy machinery maker, slid 6.4 per cent, while retail giant Walmart dropped 1.1 per cent. Data centre operators were also hammered, with IREN and Applied Digital shedding more than 14 per cent and CoreWeave nearly 10 per cent. Even the “hyperscalers” lost ground, with Meta down 4 per cent and Amazon and Alphabet each off 2.2 per cent. Elon Musk’s companies, which are both based in Texas where Democrats won the race for governor and their first Senate seat for more than 30 years, had another bad day. Tesla fell 10.8 per cent, adding to a 3.3 per cent drop on Wednesday, while SpaceX was down 9.1 per cent, on top of a 3.8 per cent drop the previous day. Gina Hinojosa, the governor-elect of Texas, which accounts for about a third of planned new US data centre capacity, has backed an extended moratorium on approving new construction.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n华尔街一天蒸发 3.2 万亿美元：民主党的中期选举大胜，引爆了 AI 板块的一场惨烈溃败 民主党中期选举大胜，华尔街一天蒸发 3.2 万亿美元，AI 板块惨烈溃败 在一天的混乱交易中，美股整体蒸发了超过3万亿美元——就在这一天，科技股扎堆的纳斯达克100暴跌6.4%，起因是民主党在中期选举中取得压倒性胜利，一举掀翻了华尔街押在AI上的那场庞然豪赌。《金融时报》基于FactSet数据测算，标普综合1500指数——这个囊括了美国各类上市公司的宽口径篮子——的市值跌幅，创下了2000年互联网泡沫以来之最。 交易员们像受惊的角马一般，从科技股中夺路而逃——他们担心，民主党在周二的中期选举中对川总的共和党取得的那场令人目瞪口呆的大胜，会给那股把华尔街推上历史新高的AI投资热潮浇上一盆冷水。\"投资者们正在举白旗认输，不分青红皂白地清仓抛售，\"市场研究机构VitalKnowledge的总裁亚当·克里萨富利说。 芯片股的抛售异常惨烈：与AI浪潮关联最深的英伟达，暴跌9.3%，市值一夜蒸发4000多亿美元。由美国蓝筹股构成的标普500指数跌去4.4%，是自去年\"解放日\"风波以来最惨的一个交易日，盘面剧烈震荡。覆盖面更广的纳斯达克综合指数下跌5.5%，创19个月来最大单日跌幅，收盘价较10月6日的历史高点跌去逾10%，正式确认进入技术性调整。费城半导体指数更是暴跌逾12%。\"随着这股蓝色浪潮，投资者开始担心，AI资本开支的巅峰已经过去，\"瑞士私人银行SYZ集团首席投资官夏尔-亨利·蒙肖表示。一位对冲基金投资组合经理称，这一跌势由动作飞快的投资者大量甩卖头寸所致，业内称之为\"降仓位\"。\"人们现在是真的开始怕了，今天这一跌，来得太急太快，\"另一位对冲基金高管说。 投资者担心，那些在竞选中高喊反对数据中心的新当选政客，会给AI基础设施建设踩下刹车，从而一点点蚕食华尔街对芯片、服务器和设施巨额支出的预期——正是这笔支出，今年推高了盈利预期，把股票一路送上了历史新高。\"那些平时最爱抄底、看到低价就两眼放光的人，这次也不进场了……所有人，都躲进了现金里。\"纽约一家券商的首席执行官说。 欧洲资管巨头Amundi的投资总监文森·莫尔捷表示，民主党赢得国会控制权后的第二天，美股那波\"蜜月\"式上涨\"转瞬即逝\"。他补充说，这波科技股的重新定价\"又狠又急，因为那些根本性的问号，在新的美国政治图景这面显微镜下重新浮现——对数据中心的反弹、以及新的监管\"。 密歇根州民主党候任参议员阿卜杜勒·埃尔-萨义德表示，前沿AI开发应当\"全面暂停\"，他给出的理由是\"你正在看到，技术发展的速度，把安全的速度远远甩在了身后\"。南卡罗来纳州即将上任的参议员安妮·安德鲁斯则提议，对新建数据中心实施为期两年的禁令，以免社区被那些\"富可敌国、权势滔天的大公司\"\"碾压\"。 \"对美国来说，这是可怕的一天。\"一位在Anthropic等科技公司持有大量头寸的美国投资组合经理如是说。这位人士称，中期选举的结果，将给AI服务器芯片的价格、首次公开募股的排队名单，以及AI数据中心公司，带来\"巨大的连锁后果\"。此前，多位科技巨头老板曾砸下重金，支持川总的共和党提高胜选几率——而共和党一直试图放松对AI行业的监管。 半导体板块是周四跌幅最大的一批：高通下跌9.5%，博通下跌10%，美光下跌17%。今年全市场表现最亮眼的存储股——闪迪和西部数据——分别暴跌22%和17%。但这场抛售并未止步于科技股：全球最大的重型机械制造商卡特彼勒下挫6.4%，零售巨头沃尔玛也跌了1.1%。 数据中心运营商也遭到重创：IREN与AppliedDigital蒸发逾14%，CoreWeave跌去近10%。就连那些\"超大规模云厂商\"也未能守住阵地——Meta下跌4%，亚马逊和Alphabet各跌2.2%。 马斯克的两家公司都设在德州——而民主党在德州赢得了州长选举，并拿下了30多年来的首个参议院席位——它们又经历了一个糟糕的交易日：特斯拉下跌10.8%，继周三3.3%的跌幅后再遭重挫；SpaceX下挫9.1%，在前一日3.8%的跌幅上雪上加霜。德州候任州长吉娜·伊诺霍萨已表态支持延长\"暂停批准新建（数据中心）\"的禁令，而德州，占据了全美计划新增数据中心容量的大约三分之一。","2026-11-06_001":"In mid-February, one of President Trump’s economic advisers arrived at the Oval Office with charts showing how the administration’s policies might be making America great again. Household income was up, particularly among low-income Americans. Retirement accounts were soaring. And the nation’s economic growth was even better than projected. “This is great stuff,” said Trump. He had completed his first year back in office, and his power seemed limitless. Within days, Trump made a decision that would change everything. After campaigning on ending foreign wars, Trump wagered on one of his own, triggering an open-ended conflict that has seen the deaths of more than 20 U.S. servicemembers, cost U.S. taxpayers more than $47 billion, and pushed up the cost of everything from freight to fertilizer. On Tuesday, Republicans suffered losses that amounted to one of the most decisive repudiations of a president in modern history. It was likely a direct result of Trump’s decision to go to war in Iran, sending gas prices up 40%, leaving many of his most loyal supporters disillusioned and effectively drawing a curtain on the Trump age. Conservative commentator Matt Walsh described the war as “the greatest political blunder in the last two decades,” and a betrayal of Trump’s America First movement. “We’re going to have to find a new leader because Trump abandoned it,” he said in an interview. While Trump publicly defended the war, privately he asked people around him whether he should change course, including junior staffers who gathered for a photo. The war wasn’t the only issue contributing to Tuesday’s crushing defeat for Republicans. Trump also lost his advantage on immigration. After first lady Melania Trump complained about people she knew getting deported, the president would tell aides that the architect of his aggressive immigration campaign, Stephen Miller, had gone too far. When Trump’s aides told him in the spring and summer that polls showed voters concerned about his handling of affordability, he said the polling was fake, the senior administration official said. Trump also pushed out of Senate races Republican candidates who would have faced better odds, including in Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina and Texas. He instead anointed candidates he believed would be loyal to him. Democrats won in all four of these states. “It really didn’t have to be this way,” said Rep. Ralph Norman, the Republican who lost the South Carolina Republican nomination to Sen. Darline Graham after Trump swooped in. “Pride goeth before the fall. It’s going to be a wake-up call for the Republican Party.” By August, polls showed Republicans had lost their edge with voters on the economy. Trump aides grew deeply concerned in August when internal polling showed voters overwhelmingly blamed Trump for inflation over Democrats. In September, gasoline prices in the U.S. shot up 16 cents in a single week. Not long after, diesel prices hit a record high. On one Republican call, a pollster said, “People will mark the last week of September as the week the bottom fell out.” When Trump wanted to veto a popular bipartisan housing bill, senior White House official James Braid sent a note to other staffers asking them to help persuade the president to sign it. Trump shut down the lawmakers and advisers favoring the bill, scuttling a news conference so late that some lawmakers arrived anyway. Nobody cares about this, he told one adviser. Aides eventually persuaded him to let the housing bill become law without his signature. Allegations of corruption involving the administration and the Trump family became a top concern for voters. Yet Trump continued to focus on pet projects, including a new White House ballroom and a proposed 250-foot triumphal arch. He interrupted Oval Office meetings to show off his latest plans, at one point leading a guest outside to see how he attached gold letters reading “The Rose Garden.” Aides said it was a waste of time to tell him to stop talking about the ballroom because he held several long meetings a week on the topic and enjoyed giving tours. On Monday, the day before the midterm election, Trump gave a nearly hourlong speech at the Lincoln Memorial reflecting pool that aides had billed as a final argument to voters. Trump mostly talked about his renovations of the capital, including cleaning up graffiti, resodding the mall and replacing rusty pipes. One of the project’s biggest foes, he said, had been algae. There wasn’t enough time, he said, to discuss his plans for the nation. “The biggest issue is that I wasn’t on the ballot,” he told The Wall Street Journal. Yet, Trump had said repeatedly that voters should imagine that he was on the ballot. By October, some Republicans felt that a spending blitz by Republican super PACs, including Trump’s MAGA Inc., would stabilize the map. The money couldn’t compete with gas prices and other pocketbook troubles. “It was about the economy,” said Chris Winkelman, president of the Congressional Leadership Fund, a super PAC run by Johnson allies. “It was always about the economy.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n特朗普的「致命豪赌」：从权力无限到被选民彻底否定，只用了几个月 特朗普如何输掉中期选举 2月中旬，特朗普总统的一名经济顾问带着几张图表来到椭圆形办公室，向他展示本届政府的政策可能正在如何让美国再次伟大。家庭收入有所增长，尤其是低收入美国人的收入。退休账户的资产价值一路飙升。美国的经济增长甚至比预期还要强劲。“这可真是好消息。”特朗普说道。此时，他重返白宫执政已经满一年，而他的权力似乎没有任何限制。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 ——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天短短几天之内，特朗普就作出了一个将改变一切的决定。此前，他在竞选时一直主张结束海外战争，如今却决定押注一场由自己发动的战争，引发了一场看不到明确终点的冲突。这场冲突已造成20多名美国军人死亡，耗费美国纳税人超过470亿美元，并推高了从货运到化肥等几乎所有商品和服务的成本。周二，共和党遭遇了惨重的选举失利，其严重程度堪称美国现代历史上选民对总统最坚决的否定之一。这很可能是特朗普决定对伊朗开战的直接后果：汽油价格上涨了40%，令许多最忠诚的支持者深感失望，也实际上宣告了特朗普时代的落幕。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 ——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天保守派评论员马特·沃尔什将这场战争形容为“过去二十年来最大的政治失误”，并认为这是对特朗普“美国优先”运动的背叛。“我们将不得不寻找一位新的领导人，因为特朗普已经背弃了这一运动。”他在一次采访中说道。尽管特朗普公开为这场战争辩护，但私下里，他却向身边的人询问自己是否应该改变路线，甚至包括那些聚在一起准备合影的初级工作人员。战争并不是导致共和党在周二遭遇惨败的唯一问题。特朗普在移民议题上也失去了原有的优势。在第一夫人梅拉尼娅·特朗普抱怨自己认识的一些人遭到驱逐出境后，总统后来向助手们表示，主导其强硬移民政策的斯蒂芬·米勒做得太过火了。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 ——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天据一名政府高级官员透露，今年春夏两季，特朗普的助手告诉他，民调显示选民对他处理生活成本问题的方式感到担忧，但特朗普却声称这些民调是假的。特朗普还将一些原本更有胜算的共和党候选人排挤出参议院选举，包括佐治亚州、南卡罗来纳州、北卡罗来纳州和得克萨斯州的候选人。相反，他亲自指定了那些他认为会忠于自己的候选人。最终，民主党在这四个州全部获胜。“事情原本真的不必发展成这样。”共和党众议员拉尔夫·诺曼说道。在特朗普介入后，诺曼输掉了南卡罗来纳州共和党提名之争，败给了参议员达琳·格雷厄姆。“骄傲在败坏以先。这将给共和党敲响警钟。”——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 到了8月，民调显示，共和党在经济议题上已经失去了相对于民主党的选民支持优势。8月，当内部民调显示，选民将通货膨胀归咎于特朗普的人数远远超过归咎于民主党的人数时，特朗普的助手们开始深感担忧。9月，美国汽油价格在短短一周内就上涨了16美分。不久之后，柴油价格创下历史新高。在一次共和党内部电话会议上，一名民调专家说道：“人们将会记住9月的最后一周，因为那正是局势彻底崩盘的一周。”——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 当特朗普想要否决一项广受欢迎、获得两党支持的住房法案时，白宫高级官员詹姆斯·布雷德给其他工作人员发了一条消息，请他们帮忙说服总统签署这项法案。特朗普拒绝听取那些支持该法案的议员和顾问的意见，并在最后一刻取消了一场新闻发布会，以至于一些议员还是按原计划赶到了现场。“根本没人关心这件事。”他对一名顾问说道。最终，助手们说服了他，让这项住房法案在没有总统签字的情况下成为法律。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 涉及特朗普政府及特朗普家族的腐败指控成为选民最关心的问题之一。然而，特朗普却继续将注意力放在自己钟爱的项目上，包括一座新的白宫宴会厅，以及一座拟建的250英尺高的凯旋门。他甚至会打断椭圆形办公室里的会议，向别人炫耀自己的最新规划。有一次，他还带着一位客人走到外面，展示自己如何安装了写着“The Rose Garden”（玫瑰园）的金色字母标识。助手们表示，劝他不要再谈论宴会厅纯粹是浪费时间，因为他每周都会为这个项目召开好几场长时间的会议，而且非常喜欢带人参观。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 周一，也就是中期选举的前一天，特朗普在林肯纪念堂倒影池旁发表了一场将近一小时的演讲。此前，他的助手们将这场演讲宣传为面向选民的最后一次重要陈词。然而，特朗普谈论的大部分内容却是他对首都进行的翻新工程，包括清除涂鸦、为国家广场重新铺设草坪，以及更换生锈的管道。他说，这项工程面临的最大敌人之一竟然是藻类。他表示，已经没有足够的时间来讨论自己对国家未来的规划。“最大的问题是，我的名字并不在选票上。”他告诉《华尔街日报》。然而，特朗普此前曾多次表示，选民应该想象他本人就在选票上。——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天 到了10月，一些共和党人认为，包括特朗普旗下的MAGA Inc.在内的共和党超级政治行动委员会，只要展开一轮密集的巨额资金投入，就能够稳住各州的选举局势。然而，再多的钱也无法抵消汽油价格上涨和其他民生经济困境带来的影响。“问题就在于经济。”由约翰逊盟友掌管的超级政治行动委员会“国会领导基金会”的主席克里斯·温克尔曼说道。“问题从头到尾都在于经济。”——美国《华尔街日报》外刊讲解第1220天","2026-11-05_001":"After a 28-year odyssey spanning 29 countries, 36,000 miles, facing down polar bears, kidnappers and a spell in Russian custody, Karl Bushby was finally back in the arms of his longsuffering mother, Angela. “Where have I been?” he joked as Angela sobbed into his chest with a mix of relief, pride and no doubt the odd sleepless night. Surrounded by television cameras and with a Sky News helicopter overhead, the 57-year-old was given a hero’s welcome as he completed his record-breaking voyage on foot from Punta Arenas in Chile, the southernmost city on the South American continent, to his home town of Hull on Monday. “There has never been a welcome home quite like this, I’m sure,” said Bushby, shaking his head in disbelief as he greeted a crowd of well-wishers in the city’s Queen Victoria Square. “This is absolutely madness.” It has been 28 years since Bushby, then aged 29, left the army to walk the world on what he intended to be a 12-year journey. He finally crossed a misty Humber Bridge back to the city on Sunday – on the same date he set off in 1998. In that time he has stuck by two simple rules: not to return home until he had fully completed his feat, and not to use transport to advance. In all, he walked 36,000 miles across four continents, a frozen sea, deserts, jungles and mountains. Quite a trek for a man not keen on walking. “It’s boring as hell,” he told the Guardian last month. His return on Monday will make Bushby the first person to have walked continuously across the world, visiting 29 countries, having already exceeded the record for the longest unbroken foot journey (far outstripping the 25,000-mile circumference of the world). The voyage took him across the frozen sea of the Bering strait between Alaska and Russia and across the notorious Darién Gap in Central America. He described last month how he had been threatened at gunpoint, faced down a polar bear, was convicted of being a spy, and been held in custody in Russia and sentenced to deportation. Bushby strode into Queen Victoria Square like a modern folk hero, his passage only slowed by the dozens of people rushing to shake his hand and take photographs. Yet for a man who had navigated much of the planet, he did appear to get a little lost on his way through the crowds of his home city. “I’ve no idea which direction I’m going. Everybody point the way,” he said. “Sorry to keep you all waiting – it’s been a while.” Through the crowd he pushed his heavily loaded trolley, dubbed the Beast 2 (his first one broke down) laden with essentials such as medicine, sleeping bags, headtorches – and two very worn tyres. About three miles away, Angela, 75, was waiting at the gate of Bushby’s childhood home as she had promised. After a tearful embrace, they took a long look at each other. “You’ve changed a bit,” Bushby told her. “I’m older,” his mother replied through tears. “We both are,” he said. “Well done for hanging in there.” He was then whisked inside the small terrace house, festooned with union flag bunting, where he was reunited with his son, now 36, and a 12-year-old granddaughter whom he was meeting for the first time. Earlier on a makeshift stage, Bushby thanked the city for his hero’s welcome. “It took a world to get me here,” he said. “The kindness of strangers – so many, from individuals to nation states, to get me back here. I didn’t do this alone. There was a big team, the biggest team: Earth. “We faced down governments, we faced down some of the hardest challenges, we had to create two world records just to get back here. It was a global effort.” Karl Turner, the Labour MP for Kingston upon Hull East, brought with him a message from the prime minister, Andy Burnham, who congratulated Bushby on his “truly remarkable” feat and thanked him for his service as a paratrooper. In a statement read on stage by Turner, Burnham said: “Your journey has taken nearly three decades, crossed continents and tested you in ways most of us can barely imagine. “But today, as you return to the city from which you left 28 years ago, you bring with you something far greater than any world record or a remarkable personal achievement. You bring a story of what can be accomplished through extraordinary determination and self-belief.” As he was ushered away for a big lunch before the walk to his mother’s house, Bushby had one last message for the city he left in 1998: “I guess I’ll be seeing you around.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n徒步28载：英国老兵走遍世界，终返家乡受英雄礼遇 “有一阵子了”——一个英国男人，徒步横穿了整个世界，用了 28 年；当他终于回到家乡，整座城像迎接英雄一样，为他夹道欢呼。 一场历时28年、横跨29国、3.6万英里的\"奥德赛\"式远征——途中他直面过北极熊，遭遇过绑匪，还曾在俄罗斯被拘留过一阵——卡尔·布什比终于回到了他那任劳任怨、苦等他多年的母亲安吉拉的怀抱。\"我去哪儿了？\"他这样打趣道；而安吉拉扑在他胸口抽泣着，满心交织着如释重负、骄傲，还有那——用作者的话说\"偶尔\"的——彻夜难眠。 周围架满了电视摄像机，头顶上还有天空新闻台的直升机在盘旋——这位57岁的男人，在周一步行完成他那趟破纪录的远征、从智利的蓬塔阿雷纳斯（南美大陆最南端的城市）走回他的家乡赫尔时，受到了一场英雄式的欢迎。\"我敢说，从来没有过这样的欢迎回家。\"布什比说着，一边难以置信地摇着头，一边回应着维多利亚女王广场上那群前来祝福的人。\"这绝对是疯了。\" 自从布什比——当时才29岁——离开军队、踏上他那原计划12年的徒步环游世界之旅，已经过去了28年。周日，他终于走过雾蒙蒙的亨伯桥，回到了这座城市——而这一天，恰好就是他1998年出发的同一天。这28年里，他一直守着两条再简单不过的规矩：一，不彻底完成这项壮举，绝不回家；二，绝不用任何交通工具来推进行程。加起来，他徒步走过了3.6万英里，横穿四大洲，走过一片冰封的海、沙漠、丛林和群山。对一个并不爱走路的人来说，这真是一段了不起的跋涉。\"无聊透顶了。\"上个月接受《卫报》采访时，他这样说。 周一的归来，将让布什比成为史上第一个“连续徒步横穿世界”的人——他途经29个国家，早已打破了“最长不间断徒步”的纪录（远远超过了绕地球一圈的2.5万英里）。 这趟旅程，带他横穿了阿拉斯加和俄罗斯之间、白令海峡的冰封海面，也横穿了中美洲那臭名昭著的达连地堑。上个月，他这样讲述自己的遭遇：他曾被人持枪威胁，曾与北极熊正面硬刚，曾被判间谍罪，还曾在俄罗斯被拘留、并被判处驱逐出境。 布什比像一个现代的民间英雄，大步走进维多利亚女王广场——他的脚步，只是被那几十个冲上来同他握手、拍照的人给拖慢了一点。然而，对一个走遍了大半个地球的人来说，在穿过家乡的人群时，他看起来，还真有点儿\"迷路\"了。\"我完全不知道该往哪个方向走了。大伙儿给我指指路吧。\"他说，\"抱歉让各位久等了——有一阵子了。\" 他推着那辆塞得满满当当的手推车穿过人群——这车被他叫做\"野兽2号\"（第一辆坏了），上面装着药品、睡袋、头灯这些必需品，还有两个磨得快光了的旧轮胎。大约三英里外，75岁的安吉拉，正像她当年承诺过的那样，等在布什比童年老家的大门口。一阵含泪的拥抱过后，两人久久地互相打量着。\"你变了一点了。\"布什比对她说。\"我老了。\"母亲噙着泪答。\"我们都老了。\"他说，\"你撑了这么久，真了不起。\"接着，他被领进了那栋挂满英国国旗彩旗的小联排房子，在那里，他和如今36岁的儿子团聚，还第一次见到了那个12岁的孙女。 早些时候，在一个临时搭建的舞台上，布什比感谢了这座城市给他的英雄式欢迎。\"是整个世界，才把我送到了这里。\"他说，\"陌生人的善意——那么多，从一个个普通人，到一个个国家——才把我带了回来。这件事，不是我一个人做到的。我的背后有一个庞大的团队，一个最大的团队：整个地球。\"\"我们顶住了来自政府的压力，我们熬过了一些最艰难的挑战，我们甚至不得不创造两个世界纪录，才回到这里。这是一场全球性的努力。\" 卡尔·特纳，赫尔东选区的工党议员，带来了一封来自首相安迪·伯纳姆的贺信；首相在信里祝贺布什比完成了这\"真正了不起\"的壮举，也感谢了他作为伞兵的服役。在这份由特纳在台上宣读的声明中，伯纳姆说：\"你的旅程历时近三十年，横跨了各大洲，以我们大多数人几乎无法想象的方式考验了你。\"\"但今天，当你回到28年前离开的这座城市时，你带回来的，是远比任何世界纪录、比任何了不起的个人成就都更重要的东西。你带回来的，是一个关于'凭借非凡的决心和自信，一个人能成就什么'的故事。\" 在被领着去吃一顿丰盛的午餐、然后再走向他妈妈家之前，布什比留给了这座他1998年离开的城市最后一句话：\"我猜，回头见吧。\"","2026-11-04_001":"美国中期选举进入最后24小时：经济明明还不错，为什么选民还是这么不满？ On the eve of elections that will decide whether President Donald Trump's Republican Party keeps control of Congress, voters' minds are on the economy, with high and rising prices a key issue and many blaming his policies, specifically tariffs and the Iran war, for the naggingblaming his policies, specifically tariffs and the Iran war, for the nagging inflation. In short, Americans are in a sour mood. The numbers, however, tell a more complex story. On the upside, the unemployment rate remains historically low, more people than ever are employed, overall economic growth is solid and the artificial intelligence investment boom has — until recently — been driving up the stock market, padding retirement accounts for millions. Countering that momentum, of course, is inflation. Though not nearly as high as just a few years ago, it is elevated and ever-present enough that people feel the pinch whenever they fill their gas tanks, pay a utility bill or order a meal. Trump is not on the ballot on November 3, but voters have his economic record in mind as they head to the polls. And the vibe is skeptical, if not downright grumpy. Consumer surveys show the mood has generally deteriorated since Trump's second term began in January 2025, though there are nuances: One widely tracked measure of confidence published by the Conference Board perked up in October, while the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index registered a near-record low. Brian Jacobsen, chief economic strategist at Annex Wealth Management, said the confidence index tracks the macroeconomy, while the sentimentmacroeconomy, while the sentiment index is \"more personal,\" asking people how they are doing financially and how affordable they find their lives to be. \"Sentiment is the bigger driver of election outcomes, but confidence is the bigger driver of markets,\" he said. One argument for macroeconomic confidence derives from the labor market. Firms may not be hiring much, and new graduates are having a hard time finding jobs, but companies are not conducting mass layoffs either. Weekly unemployment insurance claims are near a 57-year low. The unemployment rate, at 4.2% in September, has measured no higher than 4.5% for five straight years — the longest stretch on record. There is also the updraft from stock market gains to consider. The main US equity indexes have notched dozens of record highs this year, as Trump frequently reminds attendees at his rallies. Most Americans hold some stock, though higher-income households have disproportionately more. Outlays by higher-income households, buoyed by the rising value of their stock holdings, have helped underpin consumer spending, which has been a strong driver of economic growth. Data last week showed the US economy grew at a 3.4% annualized pace last quarter, its strongest performance this year. AI-related demand is fueling many of the gains in the stock market and in the real economy, and business investment rose at an annualized 12.7% pace last quarter. Still, some economists warn of potential fragility — and cracks may be starting to appear. As the midterm elections neared, a wave of corporate earnings results from the likes of Amazon.com (AMZN.O) and Intel (INTC.O) added to growing worries about the sustainability of the AI spending boom, sending indexes such as the Nasdaq Composite sharply lower. \"A sharp reset in AI expectations could undermine corporate confidence, trigger a pullback in capex (capital expenditure) and weigh on GDP growth, just as higher oil prices, tariffs and tighter immigration restrictions push inflation higher, raising the risk of a stagflationary environment in which growth slows even as price pressures persist,\" Torsten Slok, chief economist at Apollo Global Management, wrote in a mid-October note. For much of Trump's second term in the White House, wages were growing faster than prices, meaning that workers could expect their paychecks to cover as much or more of the same goods and services as before. That situation changed earlier this year after the US and Israel launched attacks on Iran in what has become an eight- month war that has lifted fuel prices globally and contributed to price acceleration more broadly. Demand for AI is also adding to inflation, Fed policymakers and economists say. Wage growth is also slowing. Consumer prices and average hourly earnings have both risen about 5.5% since January 2025, data from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics shows. The upshot: from a purchasing-power perspective, workers are no worse off, but also no better off, than when Trump came into office in January 2025. The US central bank raised its policy rate in September and is expected to increase borrowing costs again in December, delivering what Fed Chairman Kevin Warsh calls \"monetary medicine\" to bring down inflation without, he says, the need \"to throw sand in the gears of the economy and throw people out of work.\" Longer-term borrowing costs have also risen, adding to the affordability woes that voters have cited as weighing on them. The combination of 30-year mortgage rates that have topped 7% and a shortage of housing supply that has driven up home prices has conspired to put home purchases out of reach for many people.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国中期选举进入最后24小时：经济明明还不错，为什么选民还是这么不满？ 美国选民带着对经济的不满情绪迎接中期选举。 在即将举行的选举前夕，选民们最关心的是经济问题。这场选举将决定唐纳德·特朗普总统领导的共和党能否继续掌控国会。物价居高不下且持续上涨是一个关键问题，许多人将挥之不去的通货膨胀归咎于特朗普的政策，尤其是关税政策和伊朗战争。简而言之，美国民众正普遍感到不满。 然而，经济数据却呈现出一幅更为复杂的图景。从积极的一面来看，失业率仍处于历史低位，就业人数比以往任何时候都多，整体经济增长稳健，而人工智能投资热潮直到最近还一直在推动股市上涨，让数百万人的退休账户资产增值。 当然，与这种经济增长势头形成对抗的，是通货膨胀。尽管通胀水平远没有几年前那么高，但它仍然居高不下，而且无处不在，以至于人们每次给汽车加油、支付水电燃气账单，或者点餐时，都能切身感受到经济压力。11月3日的选票上虽然没有特朗普的名字，但选民们在前往投票站时，心里仍然惦记着他的经济政绩。而整个社会的情绪，即便谈不上彻底恼火，至少也是充满怀疑的。 消费者调查显示，自特朗普于2025年1月开启第二个总统任期以来，民众的情绪总体上一直在恶化，不过其中也存在一些细微差别：世界大型企业联合会发布的一项受到广泛关注的消费者信心指标在10月份有所回升，而密歇根大学的消费者情绪指数则跌至接近历史最低水平。Annex Wealth Management的首席经济策略师布赖恩·雅各布森表示，消费者信心指数反映的是宏观经济状况，而消费者情绪指数则“更加个人化”，它关注的是人们自身的财务状况如何，以及他们觉得自己的生活在经济上是否负担得起。他说：“消费者情绪对选举结果的影响更大，而消费者信心对金融市场的影响更大。” 支撑宏观经济信心的一个理由来自劳动力市场。企业或许没有大规模招聘，应届毕业生也正面临求职困难，但与此同时，企业也没有进行大规模裁员。每周申请失业保险救济的人数接近57年来的最低水平。9月份的失业率为4.2%，而且美国失业率已经连续五年没有超过4.5%，这是有记录以来持续时间最长的一段时期。 此外，股市上涨带来的提振作用也值得考虑。今年，美国主要股指已经数十次创下历史新高，特朗普也经常在自己的政治集会上向与会者强调这一点。大多数美国人都持有一定数量的股票，不过高收入家庭持有的股票资产比例明显更高。随着股票持仓价值上涨，高收入家庭的财富得到提振，其消费支出也因此增加，从而帮助支撑了整体消费，而消费一直是推动经济增长的重要动力。上周公布的数据显示，美国经济上季度按年化计算增长了3.4%，创下今年以来的最佳表现。人工智能相关需求正在推动股市和实体经济中的许多增长，而上季度企业投资也以12.7%的年化速度增长。 然而，一些经济学家警告称，经济可能存在潜在的脆弱性，而且裂痕或许已经开始显现。随着中期选举临近，亚马逊和英特尔等企业陆续公布的一系列财报，加剧了市场对人工智能投资热潮能否持续的担忧，导致纳斯达克综合指数等股指大幅下跌。阿波罗全球管理公司的首席经济学家托斯滕·斯洛克在10月中旬的一份报告中写道：“如果市场对人工智能的预期出现大幅调整，就可能削弱企业信心，引发资本支出缩减，并拖累国内生产总值增长。与此同时，油价上涨、关税以及更加严格的移民限制又会进一步推高通胀，从而增加经济陷入滞胀环境的风险，也就是经济增长放缓，但物价上涨压力依然持续存在。” 在特朗普第二个白宫任期的大部分时间里，工资上涨的速度一直快于物价上涨，这意味着劳动者的工资能够购买与过去同样多、甚至更多的商品和服务。然而，今年早些时候，美国和以色列对伊朗发动袭击，随后冲突演变成一场持续八个月的战争，推高了全球燃油价格，并进一步加剧了更广泛的物价上涨，这种局面也随之发生了变化。美联储政策制定者和经济学家表示，人工智能领域的需求同样在加剧通货膨胀。与此同时，工资增长也在放缓。美国劳工统计局的数据显示，自2025年1月以来，消费者价格和平均时薪均上涨了约5.5%。最终的结果是：从购买力的角度来看，与特朗普2025年1月上任时相比，劳动者的经济状况既没有变得更差，也没有变得更好。 美国中央银行已于9月份上调政策利率，预计还将在12月份进一步提高借贷成本，以实施美联储主席凯文·沃什所称的“货币良药”，从而降低通货膨胀。他表示，这样做不必“向经济运转的齿轮里撒沙子，也不必让人们失去工作”。长期借贷成本也有所上升，进一步加剧了选民们所抱怨的生活负担问题。30年期抵押贷款利率已突破7%，与此同时，住房供应短缺又推高了房价。这两种因素共同作用，使许多人无力负担购房费用。","2026-11-03_001":"德国30多城爆发抗议，部分地区演变成打砸冲突：为什么欧洲年轻人越来越愤怒？ Mass demonstrations in Germany that passed largely peacefully on Saturday erupted in violence as night fell and rioters faced off against police in Frankfurt, Cologne, Hamburg and Berlin. Authorities blocked off Frankfurt’s main shopping street Die Zeil and arrested more than 30 people after demonstrators smashed shop windows and made off with streetwear and other goods. Officers arrested one person suspected of breaking into a gun store and stealing two weapons, which were retrieved. Police used water cannons to break up the crowds as protestors threw Molotov cocktails and set off fireworks. By early Sunday, the streets had emptied, while police maintained a visible presence. Several thousand mostly young people gathered at the Main river embankment on Saturday afternoon before weaving their way through the financial district and toward the European Central Bank in the city’s east. Red flags flew over a diverse crowd, with some wearing hammer and sickle motifs and others bearing rainbow flags and traditional Middle Eastern scarves. One protestor dressed as German Chancellor Friedrich Merz became an instant selfie magnet while others called for him “to resign.” Banners read: “Eat the rich,” “We can’t feed the poor but we can fight a war?”, “your housing market is making us homeless” and “Send Merz to the frontlines.” The protests were organized by a group known as “Gen Z regelt” — loosely translated as “Gen Z sorts things out” — which had stressed in the run-up to the protest that it wouldn’t tolerate any form of violence against people or property. The crowds fell short of the more than 150,000 participants initially expected across more than 30 cities. The group said it plans another countrywide protest on Nov. 14. “We are young people who no longer want to watch decisions being made about our future without us,” it said in a post on its website, calling on people of all ages to join the demonstration “for education, health, climate protection and an affordable life.” Authorities had feared a repeat of the violence that swept through France last month, when blockades and marches morphed into nationwide cost of living protests that drew more than half a million people and descended into riots, arson and looting. “I have repeatedly signaled that we must expect left-wing extremists with a potential for violence to infiltrate this demonstration, and that is exactly what has happened,” ARD television’s Tagesschau cited the interior minister for the state of Hesse, Roman Poseck, as saying. In Berlin, a mass gathering at Brandenburg Gate proceeded with little incident, but police made almost 60 arrests in other parts of the city for property damage, assault, resisting law enforcement and violations of the Weapons and Explosives Act. The unrest in France has emboldened activists in other parts of Europe, including Zurich, where several hundred people, masked and dressed in black, gathered around Langstrasse and Helvetiaplatz in the city center for an unauthorized protest on Saturday night. Participants erected barricades using rubbish containers and other objects, setting some of them on fire. A car was set alight in nearby Zwinglistrasse. Police used water cannons and rubber bullets to disperse the crowds. Youth-driven protests have erupted across Europe in recent weeks, from Belgium and Portugal in the west to Serbia and Albania in the east. Although grievances are slightly different in each country, the prevailing sentiment is one of anger over affordability, crumbling infrastructure and inequality. Students in France began to demonstrate in mid-September to denounce overcrowded classrooms, teacher shortages and outdated buildings. After the violent unrest in France, authorities rolled out tougher security measures and pledged aid for businesses and shop owners that suffered damages. In Italy, Premier Giorgia Meloni’s government passed new security measures aimed at stemming violent protests, including tougher penalties on minors after a series of incidents across the country. More than 300,000 people joined a fresh protest wave in France on Saturday that sparked renewed violence, although on a lesser scale than the midOctober riots. Paris deployed about 70,000 law enforcement officers, similar to the force used during demonstrations last weekend. Police arrested 779 people, authorities said, adding that five officers were injured in Paris. Images circulated by the National Gendarmerie showed one officer being hit by a Molotov cocktail, an attack Interior Minister Laurent Nunez described as “attempted murder” in comments to journalists late on Saturday. France has launched an attempted murder investigation after the incident, Agence France-Presse reported on Sunday. Student unions have called for blockades at high schools and universities this week and urged students to join a major cross-sector strike scheduled for Nov. 5 across the country.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n德国30多城爆发抗议，部分地区演变成打砸冲突：为什么欧洲年轻人越来越愤怒？ 德国抗议演变为暴力，激进分子与警方正面相撞。 周六，德国多地的大规模示威，白天大体上还行进得平和有序，可随着夜幕降临，事态骤然喷发成暴力——法兰克福、科隆、汉堡、柏林四座城市里，暴徒们与警方脸对脸、僵持不下。 当局封锁了法兰克福最繁华的采尔大街，逮捕了三十余人；此前，示威者砸碎橱窗，抢走潮牌服饰等商品扬长而去。警方还抓获一名涉嫌破门闯入枪店、窃走两件武器的嫌疑人，武器已悉数追回。警方出动水炮驱散人群，抗议者则投掷燃烧瓶、燃放烟花。及至周日凌晨，街头已空无一人，唯警察仍保持着令人望而却步的驻守。 周六午后，数千以年轻人为主的人群聚于美因河堤岸，继而蜿蜒穿行金融区，一路走向城东的欧洲央行。红旗在形形色色的人群上空飘扬，有人佩着镰刀锤子的图案，有人举着彩虹旗与传统的中东头巾。一名扮作德国总理默茨的抗议者，转瞬成了众人争相合影的\"自拍焦点\"，其余人则高呼要他\"下台\"。标语赫然写着：\"吃掉富人\"\"我们养不起穷人，却打得起仗？\"\"你们的楼市正让我们无家可归\"\"把默茨送上前线\"。 这场抗议由一个名为\"GenZregelt\"（大意\"Z世代来搞定\"）的团体发起。该团体早在抗议开始前就强调，绝不容忍针对人身或财物的任何形式的暴力。实际到场人数未能达到最初预期的、遍及三十余城的十五万之众。该团体称，计划于11月14日再发起一场全国性抗议。它在官网上写道：\"我们是一群年轻人，不愿再眼睁睁看着关乎我们未来的决定，在无视我们的情况下被做出。\"并号召各年龄层的人士加入示威，\"为教育、健康、气候保护，以及一种负担得起的生活\"。 德国当局此前便忧虑，上月横扫法国的暴力会在本国重演——在法国，封锁与游行渐变为一场全国性的生活成本抗议，聚起逾五十万之众，终至堕落为骚乱、纵火与劫掠。\"我反复发出过警示，我们必须料到会有具暴力倾向的左翼极端分子渗透进这场示威，而事实正是如此。\"德国电视一台《每日新闻》援引黑森州内政部长罗曼·波泽克的话如是报道。 在柏林，勃兰登堡门前的一场大规模集会进行得波澜不惊；然而在城中其他角落，警方仍以毁损财物、人身袭击、抗拒执法及违反《武器与爆炸物法》等罪名，逮捕了近六十人。 法国的动荡，给欧洲其他地方的激进分子壮了胆——苏黎世亦在其列。周六深夜，数百名蒙面黑衣之人，聚集于市中心的朗斯特拉斯与海尔维蒂亚广场一带，举行一场未经批准的抗议。参与者以垃圾桶及杂物筑起路障，并点燃其中部分。邻近的茨温利街上，一辆汽车被纵火焚烧。警方出动水炮与橡皮子弹，将人群驱散。 近几周来，青年主导的抗议在欧洲各地此起彼伏——西起比利时、葡萄牙，东及塞尔维亚、阿尔巴尼亚。各国怨艾虽略有差异，主流情绪却殊途同归：对可负担性、对日益崩塌的基础设施、对不平等的怒火。法国的学生，早在九月中旬便走上街头，声讨拥挤的教室、匮乏的师资与破败的校舍。 法国爆发暴力骚乱之后，当局推出更为严厉的安保举措，并承诺援助蒙受损失的商户与店主。在意大利，总理梅洛尼的政府通过新的安保措施，意在遏制暴力抗议，其中包括在国内一连串事件之后，加重对未成年人的处罚。周六，逾三十万人汇入法国新一轮抗议浪潮，再度点燃暴力，唯其规模小于十月中旬的那场骚乱。巴黎部署了约七万名执法人员，与上周末示威期间所动用的警力相当。警方逮捕了七百七十九人；当局并补充称，巴黎有五名警察受伤。 法国宪兵队流传出的画面显示，一名警察被燃烧瓶击中；内政部长洛朗·努内兹在周六深夜向记者发表评论时，将此次袭击定性为\"谋杀未遂\"。据法新社周日报道，法国已就此事件立案，展开谋杀未遂的调查。学生工会则呼吁本周封锁各大高中与大学，并敦促学生加入定于11月5日在全国范围举行的一场大规模跨行业罢工。","2026-11-02_001":"大国掰手腕，小国发大财：谁在中美竞争中坐收渔利？ For better or worse, many poor countries’ fortunes hinge on their natural resources. Since their industries are less sophisticated and their service sectors smaller than in rich places, an underground bounty can make a big difference to their economic prospects. Rising global demand for oil or ores can spur growth, attract investment and fill fiscal holes. Now the world may be entering another supercycle, propelled by the race between America and China to secure supplies of minerals critical to artificial intelligence, electrification and other technologies expected to define the 21st century. Chinese minerals diplomacy is alive and well. Donald Trump’s administration has already signed mineral-themed agreements with more than 20. Today the rivals are both courting a much smaller group of countries capable of getting minerals out of the ground quickly. On October 16th the World Bank said it would quintuple, to $20bn, a fund to help countries capture some of the surging demand. Yet in order to succeed, their governments cannot rely only on their subterranean resources or their cosiness with America and China. They must also show ability to supply stuff at speed. The urgency is palpable. In May 2025 Mr Trump was forced to rein in tariffs he had imposed on Chinese goods, after China retaliated by restricting exports of seven rare-earth minerals used to make semiconductors and weapons. America wants to break China’s chokehold. The wheedling has worked. According to officials, in 2025 the American government and American firms spent $15.4bn on foreign mines, up from nearly nothing in 2024, and more is expected this year. In response, Chinese state-owned mining giants splurged well over $30bn abroad, far more than in 2024. So far the biggest beneficiary has been the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC). Most Western investors abandoned the vast, conflict-torn, landlocked central African country when prices of tin, diamonds and other of its abundant resources collapsed. The Chinese have pledged $1bn to expand a copper mine and propose to build a much bigger one that could churn out up to 500,000 tonnes per year. Now America is back, too. A government-financed fund is in talks to buy 40% of one of the last remaining Western-owned operating mines, a copper concern run by Glencore, a Swiss-based company. Private firms have followed. The DRC is so indigent that the cash is already transforming its economy. In 2025 foreign mining investment was equivalent to roughly 8% of its GDP. Two other big winners are Brazil and Kazakhstan. But they are nevertheless benefiting mightily from American and Chinese interest. America’s approach has been the more focused. In Brazil it has set about building a rare-earth empire. In September USA Rare Earth took over Serra Verde, the only producer of four of the scarcest such minerals outside Asia. A further package pulled together by the Department of War and a loan from the DFC will push American investment to nearly $5bn. In Kazakhstan America is eyeing tungsten, which is vital to high-tech arms-manufacturing. In November 2025 Kaz Resources, a firm backed by the administration and by investors including Eric Trump and Donald Trump junior, is stumping up $1bn to develop deposits at Kairakty and Northern Katpar. A further $1.6bn could come via the DFC and EXIM. Last year Mr Trump’s officials spoke about recruiting countries to America’s camp while pressing them to spurn Chinese advances. Most, they said, would have to pick a side. The experience of the DRC, Brazil and Kazakhstan shows that this is not how things have played out so far. American and Chinese mineral-hunters are piling into the same handful of countries because only so many have lots of half-finished, old or dormant mines that can be completed or revived quickly and cheaply. Now America is becoming impatient—and so behaving more like the Chinese. In the short run, smash-and-grab may benefit the superpower rivals, too. Yet the strategy has its limits. It relies on others to prospect for and develop existing deposits, even as prospectors and developers grow more cautious. Despite forecasts of surging demand for copper, which may outstrip supply by 8% within ten years, the world’s biggest producer of the red metal, Chile, has cut back output, largely owing to underinvestment in decaying assets. Higher prices should in theory attract investment in new projects. But these can take a decade or more to pinpoint and develop. In the meantime, all the meddling by the governments of America and China is confusing the price signals miners rely on to decide whether an untapped deposit is worth the hassle. The result is more investment paralysis—but, for the lucky few places with ready deposits, it’s payday. ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n大国掰手腕，小国发大财：谁在中美竞争中坐收渔利？ 大国掰手腕，小国发横财——谁在中美对抗中坐收渔利？关键矿产的暴利，或许能彻底改写少数几个幸运儿的命运——但这份\"幸运\"，只属于寥寥数人。 无论好坏，许多穷国的命运都系于脚下的资源——它们的工业不如富国精密，服务业规模也更小，地底下的慷慨馈赠，便成了改写经济前景的那张牌。全球对石油与矿石的需求不断攀升，恰似一记马刺，催动增长、引来投资，也补上了政府的财政窟窿。 如今，世界或许正迈入又一轮超级周期，推手正是中美两国那场争夺关键矿产供应的竞赛——这些矿产，关乎人工智能、电气化，以及那些注定要定义21世纪的技术。中国的矿产外交方兴未艾，川总政府也已与二十余国签下矿产协议。然而时至今日，两个对手真正放下身段去讨好的，是寥寥无几、能把矿产快速挖出地面的国家。 10月16日，世界银行宣布将一支基金扩至五倍、达200亿美元，以助各国分食这波暴涨的需求。然而，要真正成功，这些国家的政府不能只仰仗地下的资源，也不能只凭与中美两国的亲密关系——它们还得拿出高速供货的本事。局势之紧迫，触手可及。2025年5月，川总被迫收回先前加诸中国商品的关税，起因是中国出手反制，限制七种用于制造半导体与武器的稀土出口。如今，美国一心想要挣脱中国锁在喉咙上的那只手。 这番软磨硬泡，到底起了效。据官员透露，2025年美国政府与企业向海外矿山砸下154亿美元，而2024年这个数字几乎为零，今年料将更多。作为回应，中国的国有矿业巨头在海外豪掷远超300亿美元，远胜往年。 截至目前，最大的赢家当属刚果民主共和国。曾几何时，锡、钻石等丰饶物产的价格一泻千里，大多数西方投资者纷纷撤离这个幅员辽阔、战乱不休、深居内陆的中非国家。而中国则承诺斥资10亿美元扩建一座铜矿，并计划再建一座年产可高达50万吨的更大矿山。 如今，美国也杀了回来。一个政府出资的基金正在洽谈，欲购得西方人硕果仅存的在营矿山之一——一座由瑞士巨头嘉能可运营的铜矿——的四成股权。私人资本亦随之跟进。刚果金赤贫如斯，这笔钱已然在改写它的经济；2025年，外资矿业投资竟相当于其GDP的约8%。 另外两个大赢家，是巴西与哈萨克斯坦。尽管如此，它们仍从中美两国的浓厚兴趣中获益巨大。美国的手法更为聚焦——在巴西，它已着手营建一座\"稀土帝国\"。9月，美国稀土公司接管了SerraVerde，这是亚洲以外唯一能生产那四种最稀缺稀土矿物的矿山。由\"战争部\"牵头拼凑的另一套方案，加上DFC的一笔贷款，将把美国的投资推高至近50亿美元。 在哈萨克斯坦，美国盯上了钨——它对高科技武器的制造至关重要。2025年11月，KazResources——这家由美国政府、以及埃里克·特朗普与小唐纳德·特朗普等投资者支持的公司——掏出10亿美元开发凯拉克特与北卡特帕尔的矿藏，另有16亿美元或经DFC与EXIM注入。去年，特朗普的官员曾扬言要拉各国入美国阵营，同时施压它们唾弃中国的示好，并称大多数国家终须选边站队。然而，刚果金、巴西与哈萨克斯坦的经历表明，事态至今并非如此。 中美两国的矿产猎手，正一窝蜂地挤进同样那寥寥几个国家——因为只有那么几个地方，坐拥大量烂尾、老旧或休眠的矿山，能够又快又省地完工或重启。如今，美国愈发不耐烦，行事也越来越像中国了。 短期内，\"砸橱窗抢劫式\"的抢矿或能让两个超级大国对手也分得一杯羹。但这套策略自有其局限：它仰赖他人去勘探、开发现成的矿藏，而勘探者与开发者却正日趋谨慎。尽管预测铜需求将激增、十年内或超供给8%，全球最大的\"红金属\"生产国智利反而减产，主因是对日渐老化的矿山投资严重不足。 理论上，高价本该引来对新项目的投资。但新矿往往要十年甚至更久方能定位与开发。而与此同时，中美两国政府的种种插手，正搅乱矿企赖以判断\"一处未开发矿藏是否值得大费周章\"的价格信号。其结果是更多的投资瘫痪——然而，对那些坐拥现成矿藏的少数幸运之地而言，这却是它们的发薪日。■","2026-09-01_001":"美国欠钱、五常内斗、战争也管不了：联合国真的还有存在的必要吗？ It may be both the most prestigious and the most frustrating job in international diplomacy: members of the United Nations are weighing who should succeed António Guterres as Secretary-General when he completes his final term in December. The timing couldn’t be more critical, with U.N.’s fortunes at a historically low ebb. With major powers repeatedly blocking the U.N. from intervening in crises and the U.S. rolling back funding, some predict that whoever succeeds Guterres will simply have to do “less with less” and manage a process of institutional decline. Yet, at a time when global challenges—wars, epidemics, and climate change—continue to proliferate, there is a compelling case for preserving the U.N. as a space for global deal making. The U.N. is an organization that rests on international cooperation, but the current global moment is one of international competition. A shrewd leader could help states de-escalate conflicts and cooperate on global challenges out of necessity, not idealism. Maybe the biggest challenge for the next Secretary-General lies inside the Security Council, the organization’s most powerful body. The five permanent, veto power-wielding members—the United States, China, Russia, Britain, and France—have repeatedly blocked intervention in crises from Ukraine to Myanmar. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine and the U.S. decision to attack Iran suggest that some of the council’s most powerful members no longer feel bound by its guiding principles. If the U.N.’s political situation is its main headache, its financial position is a close second. After the Trump Administration cut off the bulk of U.S. funding in 2025, U.N. officials have been wondering how to pay the bills. The U.S. is supposed to cover about a fifth of the organization’s core costs and a quarter of its peacekeeping budget, but currently owes the U.N. about $4 billion. European governments are also scaling back development and humanitarian aid. This is a markedly different landscape from the one Guterres encountered in 2017, when U.N. members had just agreed to the Paris climate accord and Sustainable Development Goals, including a pledge to eradicate extreme poverty by 2030. That brief moment of optimism proved ephemeral. Rising tensions among major powers have eroded governments’ trust in one another and their faith in the U.N.’s negotiation-based approach. Increasingly, governments are turning instead to smaller, looser decision-making clubs such as the Group of 7 or the BRICS. Eight candidates are currently jostling to replace Guterres, but the selection process creates its own dilemma. The Security Council’s five veto powers are central to vetting candidates and recommending one to the General Assembly. Anyone who wants to be Secretary-General must avoid offending them. Talking about what is wrong with the world today without irritating at least one of the major powers is quite hard. On the diplomatic front, the incoming leader’s priority will be to demonstrate the ability to insert himself or herself into one or more conflicts, signaling a shift from the Guterres era. Conflict prevention and peacemaking have become crowded fields, with a growing number of middle powers leading peace processes the U.N. might once have overseen. The greater danger is that the U.N. fails to engage in meaningful crisis diplomacy at all, cementing the impression that it is no longer a significant peacemaking force. The best approach may be to frame the U.N. as a sort of global risk reduction organization. The pandemic, regional conflicts and new technologies have demonstrated how crises can cause global disruption. The U.N. does not have the power to halt these trends, but it is a space in which governments can discuss risks and explore steps to mitigate them. Its advantage is that it remains a neutral space where different blocs of governments can engage in dialogue. The role of the Secretary-General is perhaps to be both the world’s most prominent event organizer and a sort of political therapist for world leaders. The new leader cannot heal all the world’s divisions, but with courage and political acumen, the Secretary-General can make international disputes a little less toxic. That may be a limited vision of what the U.N., sometimes dubbed the “parliament of man,” is for. But in an era of multiplying dangers and distrust, it is a noble and perhaps essential one.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国欠钱、五常内斗、战争也管不了：联合国真的还有存在的必要吗？ 让联合国重振雄风：下一任秘书长面临的艰难挑战 这或许是国际外交舞台上最显赫、也最令人心力交瘁的职位。联合国成员国正在权衡：当安东尼奥·古特雷斯于今年12月结束最后一个任期后，究竟由谁接任联合国秘书长。这个时间点再关键不过——联合国的处境已跌至历史低谷。大国屡屡阻挠联合国介入国际危机，美国又不断削减资金支持，因此有人预测，无论谁接替古特雷斯，恐怕都只能在资源更加捉襟见肘的情况下“少花钱、少办事”，尽可能体面地应对这一机构日渐式微的局面。 然而，当战争、流行病和气候变化等全球性挑战不断涌现之际，我们仍有充分理由维护联合国，使其继续充当全球协商与博弈的平台。联合国建立在国际合作的基础之上，而当今世界的主旋律却是国际竞争。一位精明老练的领导者，或许能够推动各国缓和冲突，并促使它们在全球性挑战面前携手合作——不是出于理想主义，而是迫于现实需要。 下一任秘书长面临的最大挑战，或许就在联合国内部最具权力的机构——安全理事会。拥有否决权的五个常任理事国——美国、中国、俄罗斯、英国和法国——已屡次阻碍联合国介入从乌克兰到缅甸的一系列危机。俄罗斯入侵乌克兰，以及美国决定对伊朗发动袭击，都表明安理会中一些最具实力的成员国似乎已不再认为自己必须受其基本原则的约束。 如果说政治困局是联合国最大的心病，那么财政危机则紧随其后。自特朗普政府于2025年切断美国对联合国的大部分资金支持以来，联合国官员一直在为如何维持机构运转而发愁。按规定，美国应承担联合国约五分之一的核心经费和四分之一的维和预算，但目前拖欠联合国的款项约达40亿美元。与此同时，欧洲各国政府也在削减发展援助和人道主义援助。 这与古特雷斯2017年上任时所面对的局面截然不同。当时，联合国成员国刚刚达成《巴黎气候协定》，并通过了可持续发展目标，其中包括到2030年消除极端贫困的承诺。然而，那段短暂的乐观岁月最终只是昙花一现。大国之间日益加剧的紧张关系，不断侵蚀各国政府彼此之间的信任，也削弱了它们对联合国以谈判协商解决问题这一模式的信心。越来越多的国家开始转向规模更小、组织更松散的决策机制，例如七国集团（G7）和金砖国家（BRICS）。 目前，共有八名候选人正在竞逐古特雷斯的职位，但秘书长的遴选机制本身就制造了一个两难困境。安理会五个拥有否决权的常任理事国，在审查候选人并向联合国大会推荐人选的过程中发挥着核心作用。这意味着，任何有志于秘书长之位的人，都必须尽量避免得罪这五个国家。然而，要谈论当今世界究竟出了什么问题，却又不触怒任何一个大国，实在不是一件容易的事。 在外交层面，新任秘书长上台后的首要任务，将是证明自己有能力真正介入一场或多场国际冲突，从而展现出与古特雷斯时代不同的姿态。如今，预防冲突与斡旋和平早已成为一片拥挤的外交战场，越来越多的中等强国开始主导和平进程，而这些事务过去或许本应由联合国主持。更大的危险在于，联合国可能彻底无法参与任何真正具有分量的危机外交，从而进一步坐实这样一种印象：它已不再是一支举足轻重的国际和平斡旋力量。 或许，最好的办法是重新将联合国定位为一种“全球风险管控机构”。新冠疫情、地区冲突以及新兴技术都已经表明，一场危机完全可能引发全球性的连锁震荡。联合国没有能力阻止这些趋势，但它至少能够提供一个平台，让各国政府共同讨论风险，并探索降低风险的可行之策。联合国真正的优势在于，它仍是一片相对中立的空间，使分属不同阵营的国家依然能够坐下来展开对话。 从某种意义上说，联合国秘书长既要成为全世界最重要的“会议召集人”，又要充当各国领导人的“政治心理治疗师”。新任秘书长当然无法弥合世界上的所有裂痕，但只要拥有足够的勇气与政治智慧，就有可能让国际争端少一些敌意与戾气。这或许是对联合国使命的一种相对克制的设想——毕竟，联合国有时被誉为“人类的议会”。然而，在一个危险不断滋生、互信日益稀缺的时代，这样的使命依然崇高，而且或许不可或缺。","2026-09-02_001":"曾发誓“绝不把石油交给美国”，如今委内瑞拉签下百年大单：川普拿走了什么？ As Nicolás Maduro’s vice president in Venezuela, Delcy Rodríguez once accused her political rival of planning to hand over the country’s riches — its vast oil reserves — to the United States. “They are her owners,” Rodriguez said in 2024 of María Corina Machado, then the U.S.-backed opposition leader. “Her master gives her orders to do what? To hand over the oil, the gas, the gold … to hand over and trample on our historical dignity.” Now, Rodríguez is the country’s interim leader, thanks to President Donald Trump. And on Friday, Trump announced what he called the “biggest oil deal in world history” — a pact that would give the U.S. a long-term controlling stake in nearly a quarter of Venezuela’s untapped oil reserves. The deal calls for the U.S. to enter a joint venture with a private company, creating a new oil giant that the Trump administration said will hold 100-year contracts at the Venezuelan oil fields. It would become the second-largest private oil company by reserves in the world, after Saudi Aramco. The agreement appears to contradict much of what Venezuela’s government has stood for since then-President Hugo Chávez proclaimed it a socialist state. For Venezuelans, it also creates a sense of history repeating itself, with a twist: A government long criticized for its opaque oil deals, and widespread corruption, is now entering a murky, long-term arrangement with a new protector, the United States. It remains unclear how much Venezuela will earn from the deal; how much it will cost U.S. taxpayers, given the need for expensive front-end investment; and how much will simply end up enriching the private company — and the controversial businessman, Alejandro Betancourt — at the center of it all. But she added to the confusion over the deal by contradicting Trump and other U.S. officials on some of its details. She said the project was signed for a term of 25 years, not 100 years, with a production target of more than 1.5 million barrels per day. She estimated the country’s revenue would reach $209 billion for the Venezuelan state, or $19 per barrel. She added, “There is something that must be absolutely clear: Venezuela retains ownership of and sovereignty over its resources, while it uses capital, technology, and operating capacity to leverage the recovery of a strategic industry hard hit by sanctions.” One key player who clearly stands to benefit is Betancourt, a Venezuelan businessman with extensive experience in the country’s oil industry, who over the past decade has faced multiple investigations into alleged money laundering around the world, including in the United States. As Betancourt grew more important to the Trump administration, serving as a critical intermediary with Venezuela’s oil industry and broker of early oil deals this year, U.S. officials lobbied to resolve a money laundering investigation against him in Switzerland. The U.S. also has not acted on a Swiss arrest warrant against him, said the people, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss a sensitive situation. Instead, he has been allowed to enter the country repeatedly for meetings with the Trump administration, people familiar with his travel said. Beyond Betancourt’s role, Palmieri said, he worries that new agreement could create conditions for the interim Venezuelan government to delay elections and remain in power to ensure the new arrangement is carried out. “What is the quid pro quo?” Palmieri asked. “If it’s just the U.S. continuing to exercise total control over the regime, it just means that the United States owns more and more of what happens in Venezuela over the next few years.” Francisco Rodríguez, a Venezuelan economist at the University of Denver, said very little is known about the agreement, which has been presented as a done deal. “That in itself is the problem,” Rodríguez said, noting there does not appear to have been any public bidding process, or an effort to go through the country’s national assembly to approve the plan. “This is precisely the type of lack of transparency that the country got used to during Chavismo,” he said of the ruling socialist party, “and embodies everything that has been wrong about how those governments handled Venezuelan affairs.” Roxanna Vigil, a former Treasury Department and White House official, said the first Trump administration had made a strong case, including through sanctions, “for why the Maduro regime was one of the most corrupt regimes in the hemisphere, maybe even in the world. “Are we a better manager of Venezuela’s money? A more trustworthy custodian?” she asked. “Or are we really just partnering with the same people who destroyed Venezuela?”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n曾发誓“绝不把石油交给美国”，如今委内瑞拉签下百年大单：川普拿走了什么？ 委内瑞拉曾发誓绝不“交出”本国石油，特朗普却想一把抓走这笔意外之财——这份协议把美国对委内瑞拉的长期主导地位浇筑成了定局；自今年1月美军擒获时任总统马杜罗以来，这个国家已经沦为一个新殖民地。 彼时身为委内瑞拉马杜罗的副总统，德尔西·罗德里格斯曾抨击她的政治对手，指其意欲将国家的财富——那片庞大的石油储量——拱手让与美国。\"他们才是她的主子，\"2024年，罗德里格斯这样评价当时受美国支持的反对派领袖玛丽亚·科里娜·马查多，\"她的主子命她做什么？命她交出石油、交出天然气、交出黄金……交出去，再把我们的历史尊严踩在脚下。\" 如今，罗德里格斯成了这个国家的临时领导人——这还得\"归功于\"唐纳德·特朗普总统。上周五，特朗普宣布达成了一项被他称为\"世界历史上最大石油协议\"的交易：根据这份协定，美国将长期控股委内瑞拉近四分之一尚未开采的石油储量。 按照协议安排，美国将与一家私营公司组建合资企业，由此催生出一家新的石油巨头；特朗普政府称，这家公司将握有委内瑞拉各大油田为期百年的合同。届时，按储量计，它将跃居全球第二大私营石油公司，仅次于沙特阿美。 这份协议，似乎与委内瑞拉政府自时任总统乌戈·查韦斯宣布国家走社会主义道路以来所秉持的诸多立场相悖。而在委内瑞拉人看来，历史仿佛正在重演，只是多了一层反转：一个长期被诟病石油交易不透明、腐败横行的政府，如今却与一位新的\"保护者\"——美国——签下了一纸含混不清的长期安排。 许多关键问题至今悬而未决：委内瑞拉究竟能从这笔交易中赚到多少？考虑到前期那笔昂贵的启动投资，它又要让美国纳税人掏出多少？还有多少，最终不过是肥了那家私营公司——以及站在这一切中心、争议缠身的商人亚历杭德罗·贝当古？ 但她随即让这笔交易变得更加扑朔迷离——在若干细节上，她的说法与特朗普及其他美国官员相左。她表示，该项目签订的期限是25年，而非100年，日产目标定在150万桶以上。据她估算，这将为委内瑞拉国家带来2090亿美元的收入，折合每桶19美元。她还补充道：\"有一点必须绝对清楚：委内瑞拉对本国资源的所有权与主权丝毫未变，我们只是借助资本、技术与运营能力，撬动这个被制裁重创的战略产业重新站起来。\" 在这笔交易中，有一个人显然稳赚不赔，那就是贝当古——一位在委内瑞拉石油行业深耕多年的商人。而在过去十年间，他在世界多地接受过多起涉嫌洗钱的调查，其中也包括美国。 随着贝当古在特朗普政府眼中的分量越来越重——他既是通往委内瑞拉石油业的关键中间人，也促成了今年早些时候的数笔石油交易——美国官员开始出面游说，试图把瑞士方面针对他的那桩洗钱调查了结掉。这些消息人士还表示，对于瑞士签发的逮捕令，美方同样按兵不动；相反，他得以一次次入境，与特朗普政府官员会面。因事涉敏感，这些知情人要求匿名。 帕尔米耶里说，除了贝当古所扮演的角色之外，他更担心的是：这份新协议可能会为委内瑞拉临时政府创造某种条件，让它得以推迟选举、继续留任，以确保这份安排落到实处。\"那么，交换条件究竟是什么？\"他反问道，\"如果这不过是美国继续对这个政权行使全面控制，那就意味着，在今后的若干年里，委内瑞拉发生的一切，会越来越多地掌握在美国手中。\" 丹佛大学的委内瑞拉经济学家弗朗西斯科·罗德里格斯说，外界对这份协议几乎一无所知，而它却已被当作板上钉钉的事实摆了出来。\"这件事本身，就是问题所在。\"他指出，此事似乎既没有走过任何公开招标程序，也没有人试图把方案提交国民议会审议通过。\"这恰恰就是查韦斯主义时期，这个国家早已习以为常的那种不透明，\"他这样评价执政的社会主义政党，\"过去那些政府在处理国家事务上的所有痼疾，都在这件事里长齐了。\" 曾在财政部和白宫任职的官员罗克萨娜·比希尔说，第一届特朗普政府曾拿出过一套很有说服力的说法——包括动用制裁——来论证马杜罗政权何以是西半球乃至全世界最腐败的政权之一。\"那么，我们就是更称职的委内瑞拉资金管理者吗？就是更值得托付的保管人吗？\"她问，\"还是说，我们不过是在跟当初毁掉委内瑞拉的那同一批人做生意？\"","2026-09-03_001":"靠5美元衣服横扫全球，Shein如今上市就破发：欧美年轻人不买了吗？ Shares in online fast-fashion retailer Shein fell 8% in their first day of Hong Kong trade on Tuesday, with investors worried about setbacks that long delayed its listing and undermined its competitive advantages. The stock fell as much as 10% despite only 6.6% of the company being floated. Shein raised about $1.7 billion in the IPO, which valued the company at around $26.5 billion — far below its 2022 peak of nearly $100 billion. The debut caps a four-year effort to tap public markets. Shein initially pursued a New York listing before turning to London, but intense scrutiny of its business practices in the West and regulatory obstacles frustrated both attempts. Hong Kong eventually became its destination. The dramatic fall in valuation reflects how much has changed since the pandemic e-commerce boom, when social-media influencers showing off “Shein hauls” helped turn the company into one of the world’s fastest-growing fashion retailers. Known globally for selling $5 tops and $10 dresses, Shein built its success around an extraordinarily fast supply chain and the ability to ship cheap products directly from China to consumers. But tariff and duty changes in the United States and Europe have weakened that advantage. The removal of the U.S. “de minimis” exemption for low-value parcels and similar changes in Europe have made cheap cross-border shipping more expensive, putting pressure on the model that fuelled Shein’s global expansion. At the same time, growth has become more expensive to sustain. Revenue growth slowed from 21% to 8%, while marketing expenses rose faster than revenue in 2025. Shein reported a $99 million net loss in the first quarter of 2026, compared with a $395 million profit a year earlier. Investors are increasingly asking whether the company can continue expanding its user base without customer acquisition becoming substantially more costly. Competition is another problem. Shein faces rivals including Temu and AliExpress while consumers have become less responsive to the ultra-cheap cross-border model. Daily active users in Europe have fallen around 45% since the EU scrapped its duty exemption on small parcels. One analyst described this as less a problem unique to Shein than “the end of an era for cheap cross-border shipping”. Shein’s enormous global reach remains an advantage, but much of its customer loyalty has always belonged to the price tag. That helps explain why investors remained cautious even after Shein’s huge valuation reset. At around 15 times forward earnings, the company came to market at more than twice PDD’s multiple despite weaker growth visibility and significant regulatory and trade risks. A heavily discounted valuation, analysts warned, is not necessarily the same thing as a cheap stock. The market is increasingly treating Shein less like a disruptive high-growth technology platform and more like a retailer facing margin and execution pressures. Shein is trying to change that story. One potentially important shift is its gradual transformation from a pure retailer into a retailer-plus-platform. Third-party brands increasingly sell through Shein, allowing the company to generate service revenue and monetise its infrastructure beyond its own inventory. It is also becoming less dependent on apparel, expanding into categories such as home and living. The goal is to turn Shein’s supply-chain and customer-acquisition capabilities into a broader lifestyle commerce platform. Yet diversification brings risks of its own. Hosting third-party merchants creates additional compliance and reputational challenges at a time when Shein is already under intense regulatory scrutiny. Its weak trading debut also stands in contrast with the extraordinary investor appetite surrounding Chinese companies in artificial intelligence, robotics and memory chips. Those are the sectors where investors currently see structural growth and where demand for new listings has been strongest. Shein’s Hong Kong debut therefore marks a striking reversal from the optimism surrounding the company four years ago. In 2022, it was a nearly $100 billion private company, celebrated as one of China’s most successful global consumer brands. Today it is worth roughly a quarter of that peak and faces slower growth, tougher trade rules, fierce competition and persistent regulatory scrutiny. Shein spent a decade proving how many consumers would buy a $5 dress. Its first day in Hong Kong showed that shoppers and shareholders may be buying two very different things.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n靠5美元衣服横扫全球，Shein如今上市就破发：欧美年轻人不买了吗？ Shein香港上市首日股价下 跌，贸易与监管风险引发 担忧 周二，在线快时尚零售商 Shein 在香港上市首日股价下跌8%，投资者担忧的是那些曾导 致其上市计划长期搁置、并削弱其竞争优势的重重阻碍。尽管此次上市流通的股份仅占 公司总股本的6.6%，股价盘中跌幅仍一度达到10%。Shein通过此次首次公开募股（IPO） 筹集了约17亿美元，公司估值约265亿美元——远低于2022年近1000亿美元的峰值。 此次上市，也为 Shein 长达四年的公开市场上市之路画上了句号。Shein最初寻求在纽约 上市，之后转向伦敦，但西方对其商业行为的严格审视以及监管方面的障碍，使这两次 尝试均未能如愿。最终，香港成为了它的上市目的地。估值的大幅缩水，反映出自疫情 期间电商繁荣以来，市场已经发生了多么巨大的变化——彼时，社交媒体上的网红纷纷 展示自己的“Shein购物战利品”，推动这家公司一跃成为全球增长最快的时尚零售商之 一。 Shein以5美元的上衣和10美元的连衣裙闻名全球，它的成功建立在一条速度惊人的供应 链之上，同时还能将廉价商品从中国直接运送到消费者手中。然而，美国和欧洲关税及 税收政策的变化，已经削弱了这一优势。美国取消针对低价值包裹的“最低限额免税” （de minimis）政策，欧洲也采取了类似调整，这使得廉价的跨境运输成本不断上升，也 给曾经推动 Shein 全球扩张的商业模式带来了压力。 与此同时，维持增长所需要付出的成本也越来越高。Shein的营收增速从21%放缓至8%， 而2025年营销费用的增长速度却超过了营收。2026年第一季度，Shein录得9900万美元净 亏损，而一年前同期则实现了3.95亿美元的利润。投资者越来越担心的是：如果获取新客 户的成本大幅上升，Shein还能否继续扩大自己的用户群体。 竞争是另一个问题。Shein不仅面临 Temu 和 AliExpress 等竞争对手，与此同时，消费者 对于这种超低价跨境购物模式的反应也不再像过去那般热烈。自欧盟取消小额包裹的关 税豁免政策以来，Shein在欧洲的日活跃用户数量已经下降约45%。一位分析师认为，这 与其说是 Shein 独有的问题，不如说是“廉价跨境运输时代的终结”。Shein庞大的全球 市场覆盖仍然是一项优势，但一直以来，它相当一部分的客户忠诚度，其实属于那个诱 人的价格标签。 这也有助于解释，为什么即使 Shein 的估值已经经历了大幅重估，投资者依然保持谨 慎。按照约15倍的预期市盈率计算，Shein上市时的估值倍数是拼多多的两倍以上，尽 管其增长前景更加不明朗，同时还面临显著的监管和贸易风险。分析师警告说，估值 被大幅压低，并不一定意味着这只股票真的便宜。 市场越来越不再把 Shein 看作一个 具有颠覆性的高增长科技平台，而是将其视为一家面临利润率和执行压力的零售企业。 Shein正在努力改变这一局面。一个可能具有重要意义的转变，是它正在逐渐从一家纯 粹的零售商，转型为“零售商+平台”的模式。越来越多的第三方品牌开始通过 Shein 销售商品，这使公司能够获得服务收入，并在自有库存之外进一步将其基础设施转化为 收益。与此同时，Shein也在降低对服装业务的依赖，向家居和生活用品等品类扩张。 其目标，是将 Shein 在供应链和获客方面的能力，转化为一个覆盖范围更加广泛的生活 方式电商平台。 然而，多元化本身也会带来新的风险。在 Shein 已经面临严格监管审查之际，引入第三 方商家又会带来额外的合规与声誉挑战。与此同时，Shein上市首日的疲软表现，也与 投资者对中国人工智能、机器人和存储芯片企业表现出的强烈热情形成鲜明对比。目前， 投资者正是在这些行业中看到了结构性增长，而市场对于这些行业新股上市的需求也最 为旺盛。 因此，Shein此次香港上市，标志着市场情绪与四年前围绕这家公司形成的乐观氛围相 比，出现了一次令人瞩目的逆转。2022年，它还是一家估值接近1000亿美元的非上市公 司，被誉为中国最成功的全球消费品牌之一。如今，它的估值仅约为巅峰时期的四分之 一，同时还面临增长放缓、贸易规则趋严、激烈竞争以及持续不断的监管审查。Shein 用了十年时间证明，究竟有多少消费者愿意购买一条5美元的连衣裙。而香港上市首日 的表现则表明：消费者愿意买的东西，与股东愿意买的东西，或许完全是两回事。","2026-09-04_001":"继马拉松之后，又一种运动全球爆火：一年150万人参赛，Hyrox到底是什么？ Inside Strawberry Arena, a cavernous stadium in Stockholm, thousands of chiselled men and women are puffing and slick with sweat. A few are the colour of the fruit. And little wonder: they have run a kilometre, covered the same distance on a ski-training machine, then run again. Between laps of the stadium they have also rowed, lunged, done burpees, carried kettlebells, pushed and pulled a sled and lobbed a medicine ball. A respectable race time is about 90 minutes; the fittest strive to finish in under an hour. This cross between a relay race and high-intensity interval training is called Hyrox. (The name is believed to be a portmanteau of \"hybrid\"—a reference to its mix of cardio and strength exercises—and \"rock star\", to make it seem cool.) To the unathletic, it may all sound nauseating. But Hyrox claims to be the \"fastest-growing mass-participation fitness concept in the world\". At the first race, in Hamburg in 2017, some 650 people took part. Last season more than 1.5m athletes lined up across 105 events in 35 countries; in the 2026-27 season more than 2m are expected to compete across 107 race weekends on six continents. September alone features events in 12 cities, including Athens, Mumbai and Washington. The format has certainly set pulses racing. The hashtag #hyrox has been viewed more than 500m times on Instagram and TikTok. Peloton, a home-exercise firm, offers Hyrox training classes. Later this year a four-day European cruise will allow athletes to train at sea, with tickets for a cabin starting at a bracing £2,200 ($3,000). There is a Hyrox dating app and bank card. \"Hyrox is not just a race or an event,\" one acolyte proclaims online. \"Hyrox is a lifestyle.\" Big brands have taken the hint. Puma, Red Bull and Centr already sponsor the series, and in April Amazfit signed a three-year global deal to become Hyrox's exclusive wearables partner, kitting athletes out with everything from smartwatches to smart rings. In August Amazon went further, striking a wide-ranging global partnership that makes it title sponsor of Hyrox's London event this December and gives it dedicated storefronts—selling supplements, sleds and recovery gear—in six European countries. Financiers have noticed too. In June L Catterton, an LVMH-backed private-equity firm, agreed to buy a majority stake in Hyrox from Infront, a Swiss sports-marketing group controlled by China's Wanda, which had held the reins since 2022. Hyrox's revenue reportedly reached around €140m ($150m) in 2025, with earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation of about €30m—modest by the standards of a Premier League football club, but proof enough that this is now a business, not just a hobby. The hype is partly explained by a boom in gym culture: men and women are pumping iron more than they used to. Across America, Britain and Europe, fitness-club memberships have reached a record high. Isabella Helm, a 25-year-old trainee accountant, loves Hyrox and trains six days a week in London: \"You get addicted to feeling fitter and stronger.\" Hyrox adds a social element to what is generally a solitary pursuit. There is also the adrenalin rush that comes with competition. Leaderboards foster friendly rivalry. Athletes can race solo, in pairs or teams; friends can pay to watch on in comfort from the stands. Participants often turn races into mini-holidays. As the format spreads, there is no shortage of new destinations to visit. Danny and Zoe Rae are British influencers who, like a number of couples, combine races with recreation and doing \"something nice\". \"It's a great way to travel,\" Mr Rae avers. The sport attracts racers of all ages. The average age of participants is 36, but the oldest at those championships was 75. Laura Hitt trains several older people at her gym in Exeter, in south-west England. She says that \"it's one of the most accessible sports I've been part of.\" Hyrox is kinder on the body than other fitness races—such as CrossFit, which peaked in popularity in the 2010s—because it does not require complex weightlifting. This may explain why it appeals to retired athletes as well as hobbyists. Will Hyrox run out of breath? Some grumble that tickets are scarce and cost upwards of £150—even without accounting for travel, hotels and months of gym fees. But Moritz Fürste, the co-founder of Hyrox (and an Olympic-medal-winning hockey player), believes the format has room to grow, and hopes it will be included in the Olympics in 2032. He concedes that it is expensive, but that demand is still far exceeding supply. \"It's like tennis and golf,\" he observes: \"Once it becomes your sport, you'll keep coming back.\" ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n继马拉松之后，又一种运动全球爆火：一年150万人参赛，Hyrox到底是什么？ Hyrox：一场跑不完的健身热——跑步加力量训练的极限组合，让全世界练到汗流浃背 斯德哥尔摩那座空旷巨大的\"草莓竞技场\"里，成千上万名线条分明的男男女女正大口喘着粗气，浑身被汗水浸得发亮，有几个人的脸红得就跟场馆名字里那种水果一样。这也难怪：他们刚跑完一公里，又在滑雪机上拉完同样的距离，接着再跑一公里。绕场的间隙里，他们还要划船、做弓步蹲、做波比跳、扛壶铃、推拉雪橇、抛掷药球。跑出一个说得过去的成绩大约需要90分钟；而体能最强的那批人，目标是一小时之内冲线。 这种介于接力赛和高强度间歇训练之间的运动，名叫Hyrox。（这个名字据说是两个词拼合而成：一个是hybrid\"混合\"，指它把有氧与力量糅在一起；另一个是rock star\"摇滚明星\"，图的是听起来够酷。）对于不爱运动的人来说，这一切光听着就让人反胃。但Hyrox 自称是\"全世界增长最快的大众参与型健身项目\"。2017年在汉堡举办首场比赛时，参赛者不过650人左右。上个赛季，已有超过150万人在35个国家的105场赛事中站上起跑线；而在2026至27赛季，预计将有超过200万人在六大洲的107个比赛周末里同场竞技。仅九月一个月，就有12座城市举办赛事，雅典、孟买、华盛顿都在其中。 这项赛事确实掀起了一阵心跳。#hyrox 这个话题在Instagram 和TikTok 上的观看量已超过5亿次。主打居家健身的Peloton 公司开设了Hyrox 训练课程。今年晚些时候还将有一趟为期四天的欧洲邮轮，供选手们在海上训练——一间舱房的起价是2200英镑（约3000美元），这个数字本身就够让人清醒的。此外还有Hyrox 的交友软件和联名银行卡。\"Hyrox 不只是一场比赛、一个活动，\"一位信徒在网上郑重宣告，\"Hyrox 是一种生活方式。\" 大品牌们已经会过意来了。彪马、红牛和Centr 早已是这一系列赛事的赞助商；今年四月，Amazfit 签下一份为期三年的全球协议，成为Hyrox 独家可穿戴设备合作伙伴，为选手配齐了从智能手表到智能戒指的全套装备。到了八月，亚马逊走得更远，缔结了一项覆盖面极广的全球合作：它由此成为今年十二月Hyrox 伦敦站的冠名赞助商，并在六个欧洲国家开设了专属店铺，售卖补剂、雪橇和恢复装备。 金融圈也注意到了。今年六月，由LVMH 出资支持的私募股权公司L Catterton 同意从Infront 手中收购Hyrox 的控股权；Infront 是一家瑞士体育营销集团，由中国万达集团控股，自2022年起执掌Hyrox。据报道，Hyrox 在2025年的营收约为1.4亿欧元（1.5亿美元），息税折旧摊销前利润约3000万欧元——以英超俱乐部的标准衡量，这个数字并不起眼，但已足以证明：这如今是一门生意，而不再只是一项爱好。 这股热度，一部分要归因于健身房文化的兴起：如今的男男女女，比过去更热衷于举铁。在美国、英国乃至整个欧洲，健身俱乐部的会员人数都创下了历史新高。25岁的实习会计伊莎贝拉·赫尔姆就是Hyrox 的拥趸，她在伦敦每周训练六天：\"你会对那种越来越壮、越来越强的感觉上瘾。\" 健身本是一件独自进行的事，而Hyrox 给它加上了社交属性。此外还有竞技带来的那股肾上腺素冲劲。排行榜催生出良性的较劲。选手可以单人上阵，也可以两人搭档或组队参赛；亲友则能买票，舒舒服服地坐在看台上观战。 参赛者常把一场比赛过成一次小型度假。随着这套赛制在全球铺开，可去的新目的地要多少有多少。丹尼和佐伊·雷是一对英国网红，和不少情侣一样，他们把比赛与休闲、与\"做点开心事\"揉在了一起。\"这是种绝佳的旅行方式，\"雷先生如是断言。 这项运动吸引着各个年龄段的选手。参赛者的平均年龄是36岁，但那届世锦赛上年纪最长的一位已经75岁。劳拉·希特在英格兰西南部埃克塞特的健身房里带着几位上了年纪的学员，她说：\"这是我参与过的门槛最低的运动之一。\"相比其他健身竞赛——比如在2010年代盛极一时的CrossFit——Hyrox 对身体更为友好，因为它不要求复杂的举重技术。这大概也解释了它为何既能吸引退役运动员，也能吸引纯粹的业余爱好者。 Hyrox 会跑到力竭吗？有人嘀咕说门票难抢，一张要150英镑往上——这还没算路费、住宿和好几个月的健身房开销。但Hyrox 的联合创始人莫里茨·菲尔斯特（他本人还是位拿过奥运奖牌的曲棍球运动员）相信这套赛制仍有生长空间，并寄望它能进入2032年奥运会。他承认这项运动确实花钱，但需求依旧远远跑在供给前面。\"它就像网球和高尔夫，\"他评论道，\"一旦它成了你的运动，你就会一次又一次地回来。\"","2026-09-05_001":"美国人开始抵制AI，原因却不是怕失业：居然是怕...... A spectre is haunting America—the spectre of data centres. The computer-filled warehouses powering America’s boom in artificial intelligence have become less popular than almost every other kind of infrastructure—even nuclear-power plants. Counties and states are rushing to wrap them in red tape or ban them altogether. On August 31st Donald Trump weighed into the fractious debate, warning that local opponents would “end up being backwards and poor” if they killed the “Golden Goose”. The backlash is scrambling traditional political alignments, reshaping the coming elections and unnerving tech firms and investors. For a sense of how quickly the politics has changed, consider Texas, Illinois and Pennsylvania. In 2019 J.B. Pritzker, Illinois’s governor, signed a bipartisan law creating incentives for data-centre construction, calling them as critical as roads, trains and schools. Greg Abbott, Texas’s governor, boasted last year that his state was “the epicentre of AI development”. Josh Shapiro, Pennsylvania’s governor, was similarly enthusiastic. Yet all three have since become more cautious as public opposition has grown. In August Mr Pritzker warned that data centres risked “higher electric rates, higher water rates, or…environmentally unsound practices”, after pausing applications for tax credits introduced in 2019. Mr Abbott ordered Texas utilities to audit data centres seeking electricity, warning that they could endanger the reliability of the grid. Mr Shapiro has also tightened rules, giving communities more power to veto projects while attacking his opponent as pro-data centre—increasingly, a political term of abuse. Public opinion has shifted dramatically. A little over a year ago some polls showed majority support for local data-centre construction. By March, a Gallup poll found 71% opposed. An Economist/YouGov poll at the end of August put opposition at 63%. Chicago illustrates the change. Long before the AI boom, the city and its suburbs hosted scores of data centres with little controversy. Now residents are packing public meetings to stop new developments. In the first eight months of this year, American counties considered 409 moratoriums or similar measures, nearly seven times last year’s total. Some proponents have blamed foreign powers. Mr Trump observed that “China could not be happier”, while OpenAI said a Chinese firm had produced images designed to undermine American support for data centres. Yet OpenAI itself concluded that the campaign generated “little or no observable engagement”. The real cause of the outrage appears much closer to home. Americans are sceptical of AI, distrust the firms building it and fear job losses. But polls suggest that what worries them most about data centres is their local impact. A Fox News poll found 75% cited concerns such as energy use, water bills and traffic as the main reason for opposing a data centre nearby; just 11% pointed to AI itself. Some fears, particularly around water consumption, may be overblown. Crop irrigation still consumes vastly more water than data centres. Electricity is a more serious question. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory estimates that data centres consumed 4.4% of America’s electricity in 2023 and could account for 9.5-15.3% by 2030. Wholesale electricity prices are being pushed up, which could eventually affect households. Yet the Electric Power Research Institute estimates that data-centre growth actually reduced average electricity prices by 6% between 2019 and 2024 by spreading the grid’s fixed costs. Thirty states have also approved or proposed special tariffs to ensure large customers pay for infrastructure upgrades. The question is whether public opinion can still be changed. An Echelon Insights poll found that opposition could be reversed if respondents were told about just one potential benefit, such as lower property taxes or more spending on schools. Another poll found that most Americans would accept several thousand dollars to support a local data centre, with a median acceptable payment of $10,000. Tech firms are already showering gifts on communities. Trade unions also remain enthusiastic supporters because data centres bring investment and well-paid construction jobs. Still, the backlash shows few signs of abating. Tax exemptions have been paused or made conditional in several states, while satisfying local demands will inevitably raise construction costs. Yet investment may simply flow to more permissive states. Texas alone has more data centres under construction than Georgia, Arizona and Pennsylvania combined. Enough projects, analysts argue, will find communities willing to accept them in return for tax revenue, jobs and other benefits. As Mr Trump put it: “Let Data Reign.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国人开始抵制AI，原因却不是怕失业：居然是怕...... 美国 | 别建在我家后院——数据中心如何成为美国最炙手可热的政治议题之一：对这类设施的日益反感，正在重塑即将到来的中期选举。 一个幽灵正在美国徘徊——数据中心的幽灵。这些塞满计算机的巨型仓库正为美国的人工智能热潮提供动力，但如今，它们却比几乎任何其他类型的基础设施都更不受欢迎——甚至连核电站都比它们更讨喜。美国各地的县和州正争相出台限制措施，甚至直接禁止建设数据中心。8月31日，唐纳德·特朗普也加入了这场激烈的争论，他警告说，如果地方上的反对者真的扼杀了这只“下金蛋的鹅”，他们最终只会“落后又贫穷”。这股抵制浪潮正在打乱美国传统的政治阵营，重塑即将到来的选举格局，也让科技公司和投资者愈发不安。 要想知道围绕数据中心的政治风向转变得有多快，不妨看看得克萨斯州、伊利诺伊州和宾夕法尼亚州。2019年，伊利诺伊州州长J.B.普利兹克签署了一项得到两党支持的法案，为建设数据中心提供优惠政策，并称这些设施与公路、铁路和学校一样至关重要。就在去年，得克萨斯州州长格雷格·阿博特还曾夸口说，自己的州是“人工智能发展的中心地带”。宾夕法尼亚州州长乔希·夏皮罗同样热情高涨。然而随着公众反对声浪不断扩大，如今三位州长的态度都已经变得谨慎许多。 今年8月，在暂停受理2019年推出的数据中心税收抵免申请后，普利兹克警告说，数据中心可能带来“更高的电费、更高的水费，或者……不利于环境的做法”。阿博特则要求得州公用事业部门对申请用电的数据中心展开审查，并警告称，这些设施可能危及电网的可靠性。夏皮罗也收紧了相关规定，赋予地方社区更大的项目否决权，同时还攻击自己的政治对手支持数据中心——如今，“支持数据中心”俨然已经成了一句政治骂名。 公众态度同样发生了剧烈转变。一年多以前，一些民调还显示，大多数人支持在当地建设数据中心；到了今年3月，盖洛普的一项民调却发现，71%的受访者表示反对。8月底，《经济学人》与YouGov联合进行的民调则显示，反对比例为63%。芝加哥正是这种变化的缩影：早在人工智能热潮兴起之前，这座城市及其郊区就已经拥有数十座数据中心，却几乎没有引发什么争议；如今，居民们却纷纷涌入公共听证会，试图阻止新的数据中心项目落地。 今年前八个月，美国各县共审议了409项暂停建设令或类似限制措施，数量接近去年全年的七倍。一些数据中心的支持者将这股反对浪潮归咎于外国势力。特朗普称，“中国恐怕高兴得不得了”；OpenAI则表示，一家中国公司曾制作相关图片，意在削弱美国公众对数据中心的支持。然而，OpenAI自己最终也承认，这场行动几乎“没有产生可观察到的互动或影响”。真正引发这场愤怒的原因，似乎离美国人的家门口要近得多。 美国人本就对人工智能心存怀疑，不信任开发AI的科技公司，也担心因此失去工作。但民调显示，真正让他们对数据中心感到担忧的，主要还是这些设施对当地社区造成的影响。福克斯新闻的一项民调发现，在反对自家附近建设数据中心的人中，75%的人将能源消耗、水费和交通等问题列为主要原因；相比之下，只有11%的人指出AI本身才是他们反对的理由。当然，其中一些担忧——尤其是围绕耗水量的担忧——可能被夸大了，毕竟农作物灌溉所消耗的水量，依然远远超过数据中心。 电力问题则要严肃得多。劳伦斯伯克利国家实验室估计，2023年，数据中心已经消耗了美国全部电力的4.4%；到2030年，这一比例可能升至9.5%—15.3%。批发电价正因此承受上涨压力，而这种压力最终可能传导至普通家庭。不过，事情并没有表面看起来那么简单。美国电力研究院估计，2019年至2024年间，数据中心的增长实际上使平均电价下降了6%，因为大量新增用电需求帮助整个电网分摊了原本固定的基础设施成本。与此同时，已有30个州批准或提出了针对大型用电客户的特殊电价机制，以确保这些客户为基础设施升级承担相应费用。 真正的问题是，公众的态度还能不能被扭转。Echelon Insights的一项民调发现，只要告诉受访者数据中心可能带来一项好处，比如降低房产税或增加学校经费，原本的反对态度就可能发生逆转。另一项民调发现，大多数美国人愿意接受数千美元的补偿，以换取他们支持在当地建设数据中心，而受访者能够接受的补偿金额中位数为1万美元。科技公司已经开始向社区大送“礼物”。工会也依然是数据中心的热情支持者，因为这些项目能够带来投资以及薪资优厚的建筑业岗位。 尽管如此，这股抵制浪潮仍然鲜有消退的迹象。一些州已经暂停数据中心的税收减免，或为这些优惠附加更多条件，而满足地方社区提出的要求也必然会推高建设成本。不过，投资或许只会流向政策更加宽松的州。仅得克萨斯州一州，目前在建的数据中心就比佐治亚州、亚利桑那州和宾夕法尼亚州三州加起来还要多。分析人士认为，只要项目足够多，最终总能找到愿意接受它们的社区，以换取税收、就业和其他利益。正如特朗普所说：“让数据称王。”","2026-09-06_001":"英伟达到底有多恐怖？一家公司的价值，已经超过很多国家一年的经济总量 NVIDIA was named in 1993 after the Latin word for envy. Sure enough, the American colossus inspires plenty of it. Insatiable global demand for its graphics-processing units (GPUs), the chips that power artificial intelligence, has made Nvidia the world’s most valuable company, worth $5.4trn. Next year it may also become the most profitable, generating $370bn in net income. By 2029 its sales could hit $1trn. Jensen Huang, Nvidia’s boss, is truly the magician at the heart of the AI boom. His firm’s share price is 14 times what it was on ChatGPT’s release in late 2022. The ten biggest public companies championing AI make up 40% of the value of the S&P 500 index. Nvidia alone accounts for 8% and unlike, say, Apple or Tesla, which make smartphones and cars, it is almost solely a bet on AI. The company has produced about 15 cents of every dollar the American stock market has returned since 2023. Yet many also see Mr Huang as a magician in a more worrying sense, fearing that he is an illusionist inflating a dangerous bubble. Through $1trn-worth of deals, Nvidia provides data-centre landlords and AI labs with cash or guarantees so that they can buy its GPUs. Some call it the “bank of AI”. At the very least, Nvidia’s critics say, its financial engineering smacks of the “vendor financing” which pumped up the revenues of networking-gear makers like Cisco in the dotcom mania of 2000-01, whose collapse brought about a recession. Look more closely, however, and the worries are mostly unjustified. If Nvidia’s bets on AI come good, they could accelerate the technology’s adoption, boosting productivity and living standards. If they misfire, the cost will fall chiefly on Nvidia’s shareholders. That is how capitalism is supposed to work. True, the dotcom and AI booms share unnerving similarities: an exciting new technology, an epic bull run, hubristic tech bosses. Nvidia’s rise from seller of chips to video-gamers and cryptocurrency miners to linchpin of the economy has been so rapid that many people have yet to learn how to say its name (“en-vidia”, not “nuh-vidia”). Just as Cisco’s sales of routers and switches presupposed exponential growth in web traffic, Nvidia’s GPU revenues assume endless demand for AI tokens. Today it is the pace of AI adoption that is hard to forecast. If it does not soon soar, Nvidia’s customers may call in the guarantees just as the chipmaker’s own sales nosedive. Nvidia’s financial wizardry is partly defensive. Its latest GPUs no longer have the market to themselves. Roughly half Nvidia’s revenue comes courtesy of America’s cloud-computing “hyperscalers”, chiefly Amazon, Google, Meta and Microsoft, which are designing their own silicon. Non-Nvidia AI chips account for 38% of the market, up from 26% in 2023. To stay ahead, Nvidia used to spend over a fifth of sales on research and development. Now it spends less than a tenth. Yet the differences from the dotcom boom are more important than the similarities. Nvidia’s balance-sheet is extraordinarily robust. The company has $99bn of cash and is churning out more. In each of the past three years annual sales have roughly doubled. Gross margins have fattened from less than 60% to 75%. This means that, whereas Cisco used debt, Nvidia can use cash to backstop its deals with buyers of its GPUs. Even if its commitments came due and its cashflows levelled off starting next year, by 2028 it would still be less leveraged than all but 39 non-financial firms in the S&P 500 are today. Profits would need to drop by 60% from that plateau for Nvidia to forsake its investment-grade credit rating. Mr Huang thinks that the biggest obstacle to the AI revolution is not lack of demand for AI but inadequate infrastructure. The markets will not provide capital on the scale that is necessary, so Nvidia is offering financing itself. Nvidia has an advantage in understanding the balance of risks and rewards. Although this bet is big enough to affect the economy, it is an entrepreneurial one. Every company that reinvests cash rather than returning it to shareholders also gambles that it can beat the market. Companies exist to make such concentrated bets. If investors want to diversify, they can do so themselves. And Mr Huang is hardly alone. The hyperscalers, the world’s most successful companies before Nvidia came along, are making the same bet. So are some big names on Wall Street. Last month Goldman Sachs, BlackRock, Blackstone and others joined Nvidia in a $500bn data-centre initiative. And so are Mr Huang’s shareholders, who haven’t yet rushed for the exit. If they are all wrong, it is their money on the line. And if they are right, the AI era may arrive a bit sooner. ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n英伟达到底有多恐怖？一家公司的价值，已经超过很多国家一年的经济总量 英伟达驱动AI狂潮——这是好事。这家芯片巨头押下的重注，正是资本主义应有的运作方式 英伟达的名字，1993年取自拉丁语中的\"嫉妒\"一词。果不其然，这头美国巨兽如今招来的嫉妒可谓不少。全球对其图形处理器——也就是驱动人工智能的那种芯片——的需求永难餍足，这已将英伟达推上全球市值之巅，坐拥5.4万亿美元。明年，它或许还将成为全球最赚钱的公司，净利润高达3700亿美元。到2029年，其营收有望突破一万亿美元。 英伟达掌门人黄仁勋，堪称这轮AI狂潮正中央的那位魔术师。他公司的股价，如今已是2022年底ChatGPT问世时的十四倍。押注AI的十家最大上市公司，合计撑起了标普500指数四成的市值；单是英伟达一家便占去8%——而且它不像苹果或特斯拉那样还有手机和汽车可倚仗，它几乎就是一场纯粹押在AI身上的豪赌。自2023年以来，美股每为投资者赚回一美元，其中约十五美分出自英伟达之手。 然而在另一层更令人不安的意义上，也有许多人把黄仁勋看作\"魔术师\"——他们担心，他其实是个玩障眼法的幻术师，正把一个危险的泡沫越吹越大。通过总额一万亿美元的交易，英伟达向数据中心业主和AI实验室提供现金或担保，好让他们买得起自己的GPU。有人因此称它为\"AI银行\"。批评者说，往最轻了讲，英伟达这套财务操作也透着一股\"供应商融资\"的味道——正是这套玩法，在2000至2001年的互联网狂热中吹起了思科等网络设备商的营收，而那场狂热的崩塌，最终引来了一场经济衰退。 然而，若凑近细看，这些忧虑大多站不住脚。倘若英伟达押在AI上的这些赌注最终赌对了，它们将加速这项技术的普及，从而拉动生产率与生活水平的提升；倘若赌砸了，代价则主要由英伟达自己的股东承担。而这，正是资本主义本该有的运作方式。 诚然，互联网泡沫与AI热潮之间，有些相似之处着实令人心里发毛：一项激动人心的新技术、一轮史诗级的牛市、一群狂妄自大的科技巨头。英伟达从当年给游戏玩家和加密矿工卖芯片的公司，一路蹿升为整个经济的命脉，速度之快，以至于许多人至今还没学会它的名字该怎么念（是\"en-vidia\"，不是\"nuh-vidia\"）。正如当年思科的路由器与交换机生意，其前提是网络流量将呈指数级增长；今天英伟达的GPU营收，同样假定了世人对AI token的需求永无止境。 而今真正难以预判的，恰恰是AI普及的节奏。这个节奏若不能尽快提速，英伟达的客户便可能上门要求兑现担保——而与此同时，这家芯片巨头自己的销售额，正一头栽下去。 英伟达的这套财技，有一部分其实是防守。它最新的GPU早已不再是市面上的独一份。约莫一半的营收，还得仰仗美国那几家云计算\"超大规模厂商\"——主要是亚马逊、谷歌、Meta与微软——而这四家，正各自埋头设计自家的芯片。非英伟达阵营的AI芯片如今已占去38%的市场，两年前这个数字还只有26%。为了守住领先，英伟达过去肯把两成以上的销售额砸进研发；而如今，这个比例不到一成。 然而，AI热潮与当年互联网泡沫之间的差异，远比那些相似之处更值得看重。英伟达的资产负债表稳健得惊人：账上躺着990亿美元现金，且仍在源源不断地生出更多；过去三年，年销售额几乎年年翻番；毛利率则从不足六成，一路养到了七成半。 这意味着：当年思科靠的是举债，而英伟达可以直接拿现金，去为它与GPU买家之间的交易兜底。哪怕它所有的承诺同时到期、现金流从明年起便再无增长，到2028年，它的负债水平依然低于标普500中除三十九家之外的所有非金融企业今日的水平。而从那个平台期算起，利润还需再跌去六成，英伟达才会丢掉它的投资级信用评级。 在黄仁勋看来，AI革命最大的拦路虎并非需求不足，而是基础设施跟不上。市场拿不出所需规模的资本，于是英伟达索性自己下场提供融资。在权衡风险与回报这件事上，它本就比旁人更有优势。这一注虽大到足以撼动整个经济，本质上却仍是一次企业家式的冒险——任何一家把现金留下来再投资、而非还给股东的公司，同样是在赌自己能跑赢大盘。公司之所以存在，为的就是下这种集中的重注；至于分散风险，投资者大可自己去做。 而黄仁勋绝非孤身一人。那些超大规模云厂商——在英伟达出现之前，它们才是全世界最成功的公司——正下着同样的注。华尔街的一众大牌亦然：上个月，高盛、贝莱德、黑石等机构携手英伟达，启动了一项五千亿美元的数据中心计划。黄仁勋的股东们同样如此，他们至今尚未夺路而逃。倘若他们全都错了，押在线上的是他们自己的钱；而倘若他们是对的，AI时代或许会来得早那么一点。■","2026-09-07_001":"中美“太空战”已经不是科幻片了：卫星开始互相追逐，甚至能直接抓走对方 In June, a secretive unmanned Chinese spaceplane monitored by U.S. military analysts released a small satellite hundreds of miles above Earth. The object then looped around another Chinese satellite before returning towards the spaceplane, according to U.S. space-tracking firm LeoLabs. Little is known about the satellite or the mission. But the U.S. military operates a similarly secretive uncrewed spaceplane. Both programs reflect the rapidly evolving field of military space operations as Beijing and Washington prepare for the possibility of conflict extending into space. Chinese researchers linked to the military are developing technologies ranging from satellites designed to pursue and even capture other spacecraft to systems that could extend fuel supplies while doing so, according to documents reviewed by Reuters. The United States and Britain have also conducted their first joint military operation in space in the path of a suspected Chinese spy satellite. U.S. Space Force chief General Chance Saltzman said China was rapidly developing ground- and space-based weapons designed to interfere with or destroy targets. Satellites have become indispensable to modern warfare. For decades they have provided communications, navigation and intelligence services to armed forces on Earth and typically remained on fixed paths once in orbit. Increasingly, however, militaries view them as active weapons platforms capable of launching attacks by disrupting another satellite through jamming or laser dazzling, or even capturing enemy spacecraft. Disruption of U.S. space capabilities could affect everything from surveillance to missile defense. The competition comes as Earth’s orbit becomes increasingly crowded. SpaceX operates about 11,000 Starlink satellites, China is building rival networks, and U.S. President Donald Trump’s Golden Dome missile defense program envisions space-based interceptors. In a joint U.S.-British operation last year, U.S. space forces directed a military satellite to swoop close to a UK one as China’s Shiyan 12-01 satellite passed beneath them. Experts told Reuters the operation appeared intended as a strategic signal to Beijing, demonstrating allied unity and capability. The U.S. and Britain publicly announced the operation but did not disclose its proximity to a Chinese spacecraft. China’s foreign ministry said it was not familiar with the specific details, but said Beijing opposes the weaponization of space and supports its peaceful use, while accusing Washington of stoking an arms race through efforts including Golden Dome and potential space-based weapons. Chinese state media has described Shiyan 12-01 as conducting space environment surveys. U.S. military leaders increasingly argue that space must be treated as a potential battlefield. U.S. Space Command head General Stephen Whiting has warned that adversaries could target American space assets early in a conflict and said the United States must adopt a more active warfighting posture. That means protecting American systems while also being prepared to threaten or strike hostile assets. “We must be well-prepared for conflict in space. And if deterrence fails, we will fight and win,” he said in April. Western officials warn of threats including ground-based lasers, electronic jammers, cyberattacks and anti-satellite missiles. In orbit, the United States, China and Russia all operate maneuverable satellites capable of approaching and inspecting other spacecraft. Such systems could potentially carry weapons, including lasers or projectiles. Some specialists note that disrupting satellites remains easier from Earth than through spacecraft-on-spacecraft attacks. In June, the U.S. Space Force disclosed an earthbound electromagnetic weapon designed to disrupt adversary satellites. Although the United States remains the world’s leading space power, China is rapidly expanding both the number of satellites it operates and the complexity of its missions. Chinese satellites have demonstrated what U.S. officials describe as orbital “dogfighting,” involving multiple satellites moving in a synchronized way in proximity. Chinese spacecraft have also shown the ability to capture a defunct satellite and move it into a different orbit. Last year, China conducted what was likely an on-orbit refueling attempt, according to a U.S. Space Force official. Refueling could be a major breakthrough because satellites are normally constrained by the fuel they launch with; replenishing it could determine whether a spacecraft remains a hunter or becomes the hunted. Chinese institutions are pursuing technologies that allow satellites to calculate fuel-efficient maneuvers while pursuing other spacecraft, manage hundreds of satellites simultaneously, and even use multiple spacecraft with nets to capture targets. Such technologies can have non-military uses, but China’s PLA Daily said controlling space was essential for safeguarding national security, seizing the strategic initiative and achieving victory in war.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n中美“太空战”已经不是科幻片了：卫星开始互相追逐，甚至能直接抓走对方 美中为太空战争武装——猎杀卫星与轨道武器 今年6月，一架受到美国军事分析人员监测、行踪神秘的中国无人航天飞机，在距离地球数百英里的高空释放了一颗小型卫星。据美国太空追踪公司LeoLabs称，这个物体随后绕着另一颗中国卫星飞行了一圈，之后又返回航天飞机附近。关于这颗卫星以及此次任务，目前外界所知甚少。不过，美国军方同样运行着一款高度保密的无人航天飞机。随着北京和华盛顿为冲突可能延伸至太空的局面做准备，这两个项目都折射出军事太空行动这一领域正在迅速演变。 据路透社查阅的文件显示，与中国军方有关联的研究人员正在开发一系列技术，从能够追踪、甚至捕获其他航天器的卫星，到能够在执行此类任务时延长燃料供应的系统。与此同时，美国和英国也已经开展了两国首次太空联合军事行动，而行动轨迹恰好经过一颗疑似中国间谍卫星附近。美国太空军司令钱斯·萨尔茨曼上将表示，中国正在迅速发展陆基和天基武器，其设计目的就是干扰甚至摧毁目标。 卫星已经成为现代战争中不可或缺的一部分。几十年来，卫星一直为地面部队提供通信、导航和情报服务，而且一旦进入轨道，通常都会沿着固定路径运行。然而，各国军方正越来越多地将卫星视为主动型武器平台：它们可以通过干扰信号或使用激光致盲的方式破坏另一颗卫星的运行，甚至直接捕获敌方航天器。美国的太空能力一旦遭到破坏，受到影响的领域可能从监视侦察一直延伸到导弹防御。 这场竞争发生之际，地球轨道正变得越来越拥挤。SpaceX运营着大约1.1万颗星链卫星，中国正在建设与之竞争的卫星网络，而美国总统唐纳德·特朗普提出的“金穹”导弹防御计划则设想部署天基拦截器。在去年美英两国的一次联合行动中，当中国“试验十二号01星”从下方经过时，美国太空部队指挥一颗军用卫星迅速靠近一颗英国卫星。专家向路透社表示，这次行动似乎意在向北京释放战略信号，展示美英盟友之间的团结以及双方所具备的太空行动能力。 美国和英国公开宣布了这次行动，但并未透露行动实际上曾接近一颗中国航天器。中国外交部表示不了解此次行动的具体情况，但同时称中方反对太空武器化，支持和平利用太空，并指责华盛顿通过“金穹”计划以及潜在的天基武器等项目煽动太空军备竞赛。中国官方媒体则称，“试验十二号01星”的任务是开展空间环境探测。 美国军方领导人越来越明确地认为，太空必须被视为一个潜在战场。美国太空司令部司令斯蒂芬·怀廷上将警告称，一旦发生冲突，对手可能会在战争初期就攻击美国的太空资产，因此美国必须采取更加积极主动的作战姿态。这意味着，美国既要保护自己的太空系统，同时也必须做好威胁甚至打击敌方太空资产的准备。“我们必须为太空冲突做好充分准备。如果威慑失败，我们就会投入战斗，并赢得胜利。”他今年4月表示。 西方官员警告称，目前的威胁包括陆基激光武器、电子干扰设备、网络攻击以及反卫星导弹。在轨道上，美国、中国和俄罗斯都运行着具备机动能力的卫星，可以接近并检查其他航天器。这类系统未来可能携带武器，包括激光装置或发射物。不过，一些专家指出，与航天器之间直接在太空发动攻击相比，从地面干扰卫星仍然更加容易。今年6月，美国太空军披露了一款部署在地面的电磁武器，其设计用途就是干扰对手的卫星。 尽管美国目前仍然是全球领先的太空强国，但中国正在迅速扩大其卫星数量，同时不断提高太空任务的复杂程度。中国卫星已经展示了美国官员所称的轨道“缠斗”能力，即多颗卫星在近距离范围内以同步方式进行机动。中国航天器还展示过捕获一颗已经失效的卫星，并将其移动到另一条轨道的能力。据一名美国太空军官员称，中国去年还进行了一次很可能属于在轨加油的尝试。 在轨加油可能成为一项重大突破，因为卫星通常会受到发射时所携带燃料数量的限制；如果能够在太空中补充燃料，就可能决定一艘航天器究竟能够继续充当“猎手”，还是最终沦为“猎物”。中国相关机构正在研发多种技术，使卫星能够在追踪其他航天器的同时计算最节省燃料的机动方案，同时管理数百颗卫星，甚至利用多艘航天器协同张网捕获目标。这些技术也可以用于非军事用途，但中国《解放军报》表示，掌控太空对于维护国家安全、夺取战略主动权以及赢得战争胜利至关重要。","2026-09-08_001":"全球多国物价再次上涨，央行又开始加息：新一轮“涨价潮”要来了吗？ Who remembers the “soft landing”? In 2022-23 central bankers dreamed of bringing high inflation down without triggering a recession. Some countries came awfully close to achieving it. But with victory tantalisingly close, a growing number of central banks are now redoubling their fight against inflation. Price pressure is building. The world is a long way from the pain of four years ago, but the diagnosis is not promising. On September 2nd New Zealand’s central bank raised rates. Rate-setters on the other side of the Tasman sea have already increased borrowing costs three times this year. The Bank of Japan did so in June and the Bank of Korea in July and August. On September 10th the European Central Bank (ECB) is expected to raise its deposit rate to 2.5%. Many traders expect that, before long, the Federal Reserve will follow. Policymakers are responding to growing evidence of rising price pressure. Average annual inflation across rich countries has stopped falling towards 2%, the target in most of them, and has instead edged up in recent months. In August annual inflation in the euro zone reached 3.3%, the highest in three years. Switzerland has inflation of just 0.8% a year, reflecting economic policy that is one of the wonders of the modern world. But even that is a two-year high. And in some places prices are evoking the sad memories of the “cost-of-living crisis”. Inflation is close to 6% in Lithuania, for instance, up from 3.1% in January. Energy is the main culprit, but not the only one. War in the Middle East has caused the price of oil and natural gas to rise. In Europe, natural-gas prices are more than twice as high as before the Iran war. They could go further as the continent’s storage tanks are refilled ahead of the winter. Already euro-zone energy inflation is easily in double-digits. The typical American, meanwhile, is paying just over $4 for a gallon of petrol, up from less than $3 at the beginning of the year. The problem, however, may be bigger than fuels. The Economist estimates that across the rich world, average annual “core” inflation, which strips out energy and food, has increased from 2.7% in early 2026 to 2.9% today. To examine countries’ underlying inflationary pressure in more detail, we found comparable figures for 23 rich countries on services prices, which are largely determined by the price of labour (rather than more volatile commodities). Services inflation is rising in two-thirds of our sample. This is despite the fact that in many countries measures of wage growth continue to decline. (One explanation for this puzzling result is that wages are not well measured; another is that falling productivity growth in services makes it harder for firms to absorb pay rises.) There is a third suspect: artificial intelligence. Subdued productivity growth has been the norm across rich economies for years, with America the main exception. But even there, economists worry that AI's disruptive effect on white-collar and professional-service jobs could eventually crimp wage growth just as its productivity gains show up—a trade-off that complicates the usual assumption that faster wage growth alone explains sticky services inflation. So far there is little evidence that people are revising up the price rises they anticipate in the future—the thing that would really spook policymakers. Most market-based measures of inflation expectations have not moved meaningfully in recent weeks. A recent survey by the ECB suggests that consumers expect lower inflation over the next year than they did a few months ago. Fancy leading indicators of inflation—including from Alternative Macro Signals, a consultancy, which analyses millions of news articles—do point to strengthening global inflationary pressures. But these tools’ predictive record is patchy. Whatever the future holds, many central banks are in no mood to take chances. Critics have argued that in 2021-22 policymakers dawdled in responding to surging inflation: rate-setters do not want to make the same mistake twice. They “shouldn’t wait” for inflation to become entrenched before responding, Slovenia’s central-bank governor has counseled. Isabel Schnabel, who sits on the ECB’s board, recently warned that waiting until energy costs pass through to pay could once again leave policymakers behind the curve. A new tightening cycle may, then, just be getting started. UBS, a Swiss bank, reckons that many of the central banks which have not yet started tightening will do so soon. Candidates include Sweden’s, and even Switzerland’s. For all the appearance of a soft landing, the fight against inflation never really ended. ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n全球多国物价再次上涨，央行又开始加息：新一轮“涨价潮”要来了吗？ 全球通胀卷土重来——抗通胀之战，亦重启战端；各国央行再度加息。 还有谁记得\"软着陆\"？2022至2023年间，各国央行行长曾梦想在不触发衰退的前提下压低高通胀。有几个国家一度无限接近成功。然而就在胜利撩人地近在咫尺之时，越来越多的央行却重新加大了抗击通胀的力度。物价压力正在积聚。眼下的世界距四年前那般切肤之痛尚远，但诊断结果并不乐观。 9月2日，新西兰央行宣布加息。塔斯曼海对岸的澳大利亚，其利率决策者今年已三度上调借贷成本。日本央行于6月跟进，韩国央行则在7月和8月两度出手。9月10日，欧洲央行预计将把存款利率上调至2.5%。众多交易员相信，美联储用不了多久也会加入这一行列。 决策者们之所以出手，是因为物价上行的证据正越来越多（见图表1）。发达国家的平均年通胀率已不再向2%这一多数国家设定的目标靠拢，反而在最近数月里悄然回升。8月，欧元区年通胀率升至3.3%，创三年新高。瑞士的年通胀率仅为0.8%，其经济治理堪称当代奇观——但即便这个数字，也已是两年来的最高点。而在某些国家，物价正重新唤起人们对\"生活成本危机\"的惨痛记忆：以立陶宛为例，其通胀率已逼近6%，而1月时还只有3.1%。 能源是首要元凶，却并非唯一。中东战火已推高了石油与天然气的价格。在欧洲，天然气价格已是伊朗战争前的两倍有余；而随着欧陆各国赶在入冬前重新灌满储气库，价格仍可能继续攀升。眼下欧元区的能源通胀早已轻松突破两位数。与此同时，一个普通美国人加满一加仑汽油要付四美元出头——而年初时，还不到三美元。 然而，问题恐怕比燃料本身更深。本刊测算，在整个发达世界，剔除能源与食品后的平均年度\"核心\"通胀率，已由2026年初的2.7%升至今日的2.9%。为更细致地考察各国潜藏的通胀压力，我们采集了23个发达国家在服务价格上的可比数据——服务价格的涨落，主要取决于人力成本，而非那些起伏无常的大宗商品。结果显示，样本中有三分之二的国家服务通胀正在上行。而这一切，恰恰发生在许多国家工资增长的各项指标持续走弱之际。（对这一悖论，一种解释是工资本身就未被准确测量；另一种则是，服务业生产率增速的滑落，令企业愈发难以自行消化涨薪的成本。） 还有第三个嫌疑人：人工智能。多年以来，生产率增长乏力一直是发达经济体的常态，美国是其中主要的例外。但即便在美国，经济学家也担心：人工智能对白领与专业服务类岗位的颠覆性冲击，最终可能会压住工资的增长——而这恰恰发生在它的生产率红利刚刚开始显现之时。这种此消彼长的权衡，令\"服务通胀居高不下仅仅源于工资加速上涨\"这一惯常假设，变得不再那么站得住脚。 迄今为止，几乎没有证据显示人们正在上调对未来物价涨幅的预期——而那，才是真正会让决策者悚然一惊的事。多数基于市场的通胀预期指标，近几周并未出现实质性变动；欧洲央行近期的一项调查甚至表明，消费者对未来一年的通胀预期，比几个月前还要低。诚然，一些花哨的通胀领先指标——包括咨询机构Alternative Macro Signals的指标，该机构会分析数以百万计的新闻报道——确实指向全球通胀压力正在增强。只不过，这类工具的预测往绩，向来时准时不准。 无论未来如何，许多央行如今都无意再赌一把。批评者指出，2021至2022年间，决策者面对飙升的通胀曾一味拖沓——这一次，利率的制定者们不愿在同一处跌倒两回。斯洛文尼亚央行行长便曾进言：不该\"坐等\"通胀扎下根来，才动手应对。欧洲央行执行董事会成员伊莎贝尔·施纳贝尔近日亦警告称，若非要等到能源成本传导至薪酬，恐怕会让决策者再一次落于形势之后。 如此看来，新一轮紧缩周期，恐怕才刚刚拉开序幕。瑞士银行瑞银估计，那些尚未启动紧缩的央行，多半也将很快跟进——候选名单上有瑞典，甚至还有瑞士。纵使表面上看去像是一场软着陆，这场与通胀的战争，其实从未真正落幕。","2026-09-09_001":"中国汽车出海杀疯了：5年出口暴涨7倍，现在开始把工厂开到全世界！欧洲人都看傻了 When Ford opened the industry’s first large-scale overseas outpost, in Manchester in 1911, it set the route by which ambitious carmakers went from dominating at home to spanning the globe. Over a century later, with Ford in retreat, exports of China’s cheap but technologically advanced cars are roaring, and its firms are taking the next step to becoming global giants. When localising production, Chinese carmakers will not be able to bring all of their competitive advantages with them. But many will travel—to the dismay of foreign rivals. China’s mighty carmakers, which pump out around a quarter of the world’s cars, have competitors on the run everywhere. Vicious competition for a domestic market forecast to shrink by 10% this year has intensified a price war and encouraged overseas sales. In five years foreigners’ share of the local market has roughly halved, while exports have risen seven-fold. In 2026 exports could hit 10m vehicles, over 40% more than last year, according to AlixPartners. There are plenty of reasons to manufacture closer to foreign customers. Carmakers want to eliminate the expense of shipping entire vehicles and avoid tariffs. A presence on the ground also helps with adapting cars to local tastes and regulations. China’s firms are already assembling cars, or plan to do so, across much of the developing world, including Indonesia, Kazakhstan, South Africa, Egypt, Brazil and Mexico. But it is the vast European market they regard as their biggest prize. If tariffs imposed by the EU in 2024 have hurt Chinese vehicle imports, it hardly shows. In the second quarter of this year Chinese carmakers captured 11% of sales in western Europe, overtaking Japanese rivals. But tougher deterrents are in the works. The EU’s proposed Industrial Accelerator Act would tie purchase subsidies and tax breaks for corporate fleets to vehicles meeting certain local-content thresholds, alongside other measures favouring production within the region. Chinese carmakers are hurriedly updating their plans. BYD is close to opening a plant in Hungary that could eventually churn out 300,000 cars a year. It has put on hold another new factory in Turkey as it searches for a pre-existing site in Spain or France. A brand new factory takes around three years to get up and running; acquiring and retrofitting an existing plant is quicker and cheaper. Volkswagen, meanwhile, plans to phase out production at four sites in Germany. An even swifter approach is to take over part of an underutilised factory. AlixPartners reckons there is spare capacity to make 2.5m cars a year across European factories. Chery signed a deal in June with Nissan to borrow part of its Sunderland factory in Britain, and a similar agreement in July allows Geely to build EVs at a Ford plant in Valencia. Such deals may speed market entry, but the next stage will be independent production. SAIC has confirmed plans for a new factory in Spain, while Xpeng is searching for a permanent European base. European car bosses may hope that the cost advantage of around 30% their Chinese rivals currently enjoy will be eroded by localised production. If so, they are bound to be disappointed. China’s edge comes from lean corporate structures, streamlined manufacturing and a high degree of vertical integration. Chinese cars typically contain around 1,000-2,000 different parts, whereas European competitors are still wrangling 3,000-10,000. China has also mastered the “software-defined vehicle”, developed around a centralised computer rather than dozens of separate micro-controllers. All this lowers costs and makes Chinese carmakers much quicker at developing new vehicles, which typically takes around two years, compared with at least double that for foreign competitors—a trait the industry calls “China speed”. Much of the advantage will persist as production moves abroad. Research and development will remain in China, and manufacturing methods perfected at home will be replicated globally. Chinese firms will face higher supplier, labour and energy costs, but they can choose cheaper manufacturing locations such as Hungary or Slovakia. Local labour costs may soon matter less. In China carmakers are working on “dark factories”, so automated that the absence of human workers means lighting is not required. Once a dark factory is ready, the Chinese will “just pack it, ship it over and unpack it,” reckons Daniel Hirsch of Oliver Wyman. AlixPartners estimates Chinese carmakers will have capacity to make 3.4m cars overseas by 2030, up from 1.2m last year; others put the figure as high as 6m. What is certain is that China speed will not slow soon.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n中国汽车出海杀疯了：5年出口暴涨7倍，现在开始把工厂开到全世界！欧洲人都看傻了 中国汽车制造商正把工厂开向全世界 —— 传统车企若想与之竞争，就必须踩下“加速踏板” 1911年，福特在曼彻斯特建立了汽车行业首个大规模海外生产基地，也由此开辟了一条后来雄心勃勃的汽车制造商们从称霸本土市场走向全球扩张的道路。一个多世纪后的今天，福特正在收缩战线，而中国价格低廉、技术先进的汽车出口却一路高歌猛进，中国车企也正迈出成为全球汽车巨头的下一步。在推进生产本地化的过程中，中国汽车制造商不可能把自己所有的竞争优势原封不动地带到海外。但其中许多优势依然会随它们一同出海——这无疑会令外国竞争对手感到不安。 中国实力雄厚的汽车制造商生产着全球约四分之一的汽车，如今正让世界各地的竞争对手疲于招架。中国国内汽车市场预计今年将萎缩10%，围绕这一市场展开的激烈竞争进一步加剧了价格战，也推动车企扩大海外销售。过去五年间，外国品牌在中国本土市场的份额大约缩水了一半，与此同时，中国汽车出口量却增长到了原来的七倍。咨询公司AlixPartners预计，到2026年，中国汽车出口量可能达到1000万辆，较去年增长40%以上。 对汽车制造商而言，把生产基地搬到离海外消费者更近的地方，有着充分的理由。车企既希望省去整车长途运输的成本，也希望避开关税。在当地建立生产基地，还有助于根据当地消费者的偏好和监管要求调整车型。如今，中国车企已经在发展中国家的许多地区组装汽车，或计划在那里开展生产，包括印度尼西亚、哈萨克斯坦、南非、埃及、巴西和墨西哥。但在它们眼中，真正最大的目标，还是规模庞大的欧洲市场。 如果说欧盟2024年对中国汽车加征的关税确实打击了中国汽车进口，那么从目前的数据来看，这种影响几乎看不出来。今年第二季度，中国汽车制造商已经拿下西欧11%的汽车销量，超过了日本竞争对手。然而，更严厉的限制措施正在酝酿之中。欧盟拟议中的《工业加速法案》计划把购车补贴以及企业车队的税收优惠，与汽车是否达到一定的本地化生产比例挂钩，同时还将出台其他措施，进一步向在欧洲地区内部生产的汽车倾斜。 中国汽车制造商正在迅速调整自己的布局。比亚迪位于匈牙利的一座工厂即将投产，未来年产能可能达到30万辆。与此同时，比亚迪暂时搁置了在土耳其建设另一座新工厂的计划，转而在西班牙或法国寻找现成的生产基地。从零开始建设一座全新工厂，通常需要大约三年才能投入运营；相比之下，收购并改造一座现有工厂不仅速度更快，成本也更低。与此同时，大众汽车正计划逐步停止其在德国四个生产基地的汽车生产。 还有一种速度更快的办法，那就是直接接手利用率不足的现有工厂的一部分。AlixPartners估计，目前欧洲各地的汽车工厂拥有每年约250万辆汽车的闲置产能。今年6月，奇瑞与日产达成协议，将使用后者位于英国桑德兰工厂的部分产能；7月达成的另一项类似协议，则允许吉利在福特位于西班牙瓦伦西亚的工厂生产电动汽车。这类合作虽然能够帮助中国车企更快进入市场，但下一阶段仍将是建立独立生产能力。上汽已经确认计划在西班牙建设一座新工厂，而小鹏也正在寻找一个永久性的欧洲生产基地。 欧洲汽车业的管理者们或许希望，中国竞争对手目前大约30%的成本优势会随着生产本地化而逐渐被削弱。如果他们真这么想，恐怕注定要失望。中国车企的优势来自精简的企业架构、高度优化的生产制造流程，以及极高程度的垂直整合。一辆中国汽车通常只包含大约1000至2000种不同的零部件，而欧洲竞争对手仍要应付3000至10000种零部件。中国车企还掌握了所谓的“软件定义汽车”：这种汽车围绕一个中央计算机系统进行开发，而不是依赖几十个彼此独立的微控制器。 所有这些优势不仅降低了成本，也让中国汽车制造商开发新车型的速度快得多。中国车企开发一款新车通常只需要大约两年，而外国竞争对手至少需要两倍的时间——汽车行业把这种特质称为“China speed（中国速度）”。随着生产向海外转移，这些优势中的很大一部分仍将继续存在。研发工作仍会留在中国，而那些已经在国内反复打磨成熟的制造方法，则会被复制到世界各地。当然，中国车企在海外也将面对更高的供应商成本、劳动力成本和能源成本，但它们可以选择匈牙利或斯洛伐克等生产成本相对较低的地区进行制造。 不过，当地的劳动力成本或许很快就不会那么重要了。在中国，汽车制造商正在探索所谓的“黑灯工厂（dark factories）”——这类工厂的自动化程度极高，以至于因为几乎没有人类工人，甚至连照明都不再需要。咨询公司Oliver Wyman的Daniel Hirsch认为，一旦这种黑灯工厂的模式成熟，中国企业便可以“直接把它打包、运到海外，再在那里拆包部署”。AlixPartners预计，到2030年，中国汽车制造商在海外的汽车年产能将达到340万辆，高于去年的120万辆；另一些机构甚至认为这一数字可能高达600万辆。有一点可以确定：“中国速度”短期内绝不会慢下来。","2026-09-10_001":"全世界的孩子都越学越差？这一代到底怎么了？全球教育出了什么问题？ Every three years the OECD, a club of mostly rich countries, releases the results of international school tests sat by 15-year-olds all across the world. There were reasons to hope that the latest data, published on September 8th, would bring positive news. Marks in mathematics, reading and science had collapsed in the previous round of testing, which was carried out just after the pandemic. Policymakers dared hope that, with the calamity well in the past, grades might start to rebound. That is not what happened. On the contrary, in rich countries—the primary focus of the tests—scores in all three disciplines have fallen further. In literacy, the decline is actually speeding up. These data suggest that there are long-running factors pushing down achievement in schools, and that without swift correction, pupils’ abilities could yet plumb further depths. Anyone who cares about the young of today—and the economies of tomorrow—ought to be alarmed. The OECD’s project, known as PISA, takes a measure of young people’s aptitudes as they approach the end of their compulsory school days. The results are used to create an international league table of sorts. This year, as in almost every round since 2000, Asian school systems rule the roost. Children in China—taking part for the first time since 2018—have wiped the floor with almost everyone else (though scores there are skewed because the tests are sat in only four rich provinces). In science they score 100 points above the average for OECD countries. Yet zoom out, and the dominant story is of long-term decline. The average scores in the 23 OECD-member countries that have taken part in every edition of PISA reached a peak around 2012 and have been falling ever since. In both maths and reading, their 15-year-olds are now testing at levels that might have been achieved by 14-year olds a decade ago. Children who were already among the poorest performers are falling even further behind. Across education systems, the gap between low- and high-performing students is now the largest ever recorded by PISA, in all the disciplines it tests. Some 29% of 15-year-olds in OECD countries are considered low performers in all three of reading, mathematics and science—up from 25% when the tests were run four years ago. But falling scores will also intensify long-running worries about the impact of technology on children’s learning—and on their reading skills in particular. Ubiquitous phones and widespread access to social media mean young people are getting less practice making sense of complex texts. Screens are clearly diverting children from books: the number of nine-year-olds in America who say they read them for pleasure has fallen from nearly 60% in the 1990s to 37% now. Declining literacy could well be dragging down marks in other disciplines—because reading well is key to progressing in subjects such as maths and science, too. PISA may itself share some blame for setting school reformers on a dodgy course. When the tests were first carried out in 2000, Finland shocked the world by coming top of the table. Policymakers who trekked to Helsinki for lessons returned with messages that teachers in their home countries were only too happy to receive. It argued that teachers should spend less time talking in front of blackboards and more time helping pupils work things out for themselves. What countries could serve as new models? Asian school systems have shared somewhat in the declines recorded in recent years, but generally less than other rich places. Asian countries are much less obsessed than Western ones with keeping classes small: they generally choose to have fewer teachers, whom they can pay more and train more intensely. That seems to work better than adding ever more staff to schools—the main way that America, in particular, has long tried to drive up scores. Britain’s march up the rankings deserves more attention than it has received. Starting some 15 years ago under a Conservative-led coalition government, school reformers in England—by a long way the largest of the country’s four separate school systems—zigged when much of the rest of the world zagged. The nation made its curriculums more fact-filled (some prefer to say “knowledge-rich”) at a time when policymakers elsewhere had been waxing lyrical about instilling vague soft skills. It made exams more rigorous, deprioritised coursework (on which it is easy to cheat) and toughened inspections. Test scores are not the measure of a person, nor proof that they are destined for failure or success. But good grades are highly predictive of fatter earnings, better health and longer lives. There is not yet much evidence that artificial intelligence will reduce the returns on a top-class education, but there are plenty of reasons to think the technology will make mincemeat of the weakly skilled. Time for school-reformers to hit the books. ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n全世界的孩子都越学越差？这一代到底怎么了？全球教育出了什么问题？ 青少年正在变笨吗？—— 最新全球学生测评，揭开一轮令人心惊的新滑坡。 每隔三年，由发达国家组成的经济合作与发展组织（OECD）都会公布一次全球15岁学生参加的国际学业测评结果。人们原本有理由期待，9月8日发布的这份最新数据能带来些好消息：上一轮测评恰逢疫情刚刚过去，数学、阅读、科学三科成绩全线崩塌；如今大难已远，决策者们不免暗自盼望，分数总该触底反弹了吧。 然而事与愿违。恰恰相反，在这场测评最为关注的发达国家，三门学科的分数继续下滑；阅读一项，跌势甚至还在加快。数据显示，有一些由来已久的因素正在拖累校内学业表现——若不迅速纠偏，学生的能力还将探至更深的谷底。凡是关心今日之少年、以及明日之经济的人，都该警醒了。 OECD的这一项目，通称PISA，衡量的是年轻人在义务教育行将结束之际所具备的能力素养，其结果则被用来排出一张国际名次表——姑且算是名次表吧。今年，一如2000年以来的几乎每一轮，亚洲的教育体系依旧独占鳌头。中国自2018年后首次参评，几乎将所有对手碾于足下（尽管其分数存在偏差，因为考试仅在四个富庶省份进行）。仅科学一科，他们就高出OECD国家均分整整100分。 可若把镜头拉远，主旋律便是一条长长的下坡路。在那23个每届PISA从未缺席的OECD成员国中，平均分在2012年前后见顶，此后一路走低。数学与阅读两科，如今15岁少年考出的水平，不过是十年前14岁孩子就能达到的高度。 本就垫底的那批孩子，正被甩得更远。放眼各国教育体系，成绩最差与最好的学生之间的差距，如今已是PISA有史以来记录到的最大值——而且三门皆然。OECD国家中，约有29%的15岁少年在阅读、数学、科学三科上同时被判为低水平者；四年前的上一轮测试，这个数字还是25%。 但分数的下滑，也势必加剧一桩由来已久的忧虑：技术究竟如何影响着孩子的学习——尤其是他们的阅读能力。手机无处不在，社交媒体触手可及，这意味着年轻人越来越少有机会去锤炼\"读懂一段难文字\"的本事。屏幕正明明白白地把孩子从书本旁引开：在美国，声称自己会因喜爱而读书的九岁孩童，已从上世纪90年代的近六成，跌至如今的37%。而阅读能力的滑坡，很可能正拖累着其他学科的成绩——毕竟，读得好，本就是数学与科学也要仰赖的进阶之钥。 说到底，PISA自己或许也难辞其咎——正是它把一众教育改革者引上了一条不太靠谱的航道。2000年首次开考，芬兰以榜首之姿震惊世界。各国政策制定者纷纷\"跋涉\"至赫尔辛基取经，带回的那套说法，恰恰是本国教师们求之不得的：少些站在黑板前的讲授，多些陪着学生自己把问题想明白的时间。 那么，哪些国家可堪充作新的样板？近年这轮下滑，亚洲教育体系多少也未能幸免，但跌幅普遍小于其他富裕地区。亚洲国家远不像西方那样执着于压缩班额：他们宁可少聘些教师，却能给得更高、练得更狠。这条路子，似乎比一味往学校里增人添丁更管用——而后者，恰恰是美国长年以来提分的主要法门。 英国这一路稳步爬升，所得到的关注远不及它应得的。大约十五年前，在保守党主导的联合政府之下，英格兰——英国四套独立教育体系中遥遥领先最大的一套——的教改者们，在世界大多数地方向左拐时，偏偏向右拐了。当别处的政策制定者仍在对\"培养含糊的软技能\"大唱赞歌时，这个国家却把课程改得更\"塞满事实\"（有人更愿称之为\"知识密集\"）：考试更严，平时作业的权重被调低（那东西太容易作假），督导检查也一并收紧。 分数从来不是丈量一个人的尺子，也不是他注定失败或成功的凭据。但好成绩确实高度预示着更丰厚的收入、更康健的身体、更长的寿命。目前尚无太多证据表明，人工智能会削减顶级教育的回报；倒是有充足的理由相信，这项技术会把技能薄弱者碾得粉碎。是时候了——该去啃书本的，是那些搞教育改革的人。","2026-09-11_001":"苹果2026发布会炸了：第一台折叠iPhone来了：卖到1.6万元，凭什么？ Apple kicked off its most consequential product event in years on Wednesday, where it introduced a truly new iPhone, a new chief executive, as well as higher prices. It hopes consumers will concentrate on the first two. The headline is the first foldable iPhone, which will start at $1,999. An almost tablet-like device, the new iPhone Duo will fit comfortably in a pocket and allows people to use more than one app at a time. It comes in two colors: white and dark gray. Equally important was the man introducing it. John Ternus took over as Apple's CEO last week, becoming Apple's third CEO since 1997, when Steve Jobs returned. Hopes are high that the longtime hardware engineering executive can spark a new wave of innovation and the foldable iPhone is the most significant Apple device in a decade. Ternus smiled broadly as he strode on stage at Apple's headquarters to kick off the event. \"We've been imagining quite a lot,\" he said to the hundreds of gathered media in the audience. \"We think you're going to like what you're going to see.\" Tim Cook and the rest of Apple's executive team sat in the front as he spoke. The bad news for consumers was that Apple increased iPhone prices, though not as much as some analysts projected. The cost of memory and storage chips inside the smartphone has quintupled since last fall, prompting Apple to raise prices across its device lineup to protect its profit margins. The highlight of the foldable iPhone Duo is the larger internal screen, which enables iPhone users to have two apps open at the same time. They will be able to watch a video while texting, or look at an email inbox on the left with a message window on the right. Another highlight: the \"crease\" — where the internal screen folds—is effectively invisible when open, unlike competing Android devices where it can often be noticeable, degrading the consumer experience. One noticeable trade-off is the device's weight. While thin when unfolded, the Duo's two halves and sturdy construction make it noticeably bulkier than other iPhone models. For its new Pro model phones, Apple emphasized the on-device AI capabilities powered by its upgraded A20 Pro chip, which it said runs faster and dissipates heat more effectively. It also showed off a new variable aperture for the models' camera, that opens and closes automatically depending on the amount of light in the scene, like a human pupil expanding in low light. Apple said the price of iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max will both jump $100, to $1,199 and $1,299, respectively, compared with last year's 17 Pro. That is not as large a price jump as some feared, and suggests Apple could take a hit on its profit margins for the devices. Notably two devices weren't on the menu: the base model iPhone, along with the second generation of the iPhone Air, which are expected next year. That means customers who want the latest model iPhone will have to pay up for the Pro models or the Duo, potentially a clever strategy by Apple to push customers to more expensive models. Foldable phones aren't new. Samsung released its first Galaxy Fold in 2019. Some companies even have tri-fold phones that haven't one but two hinges. But the category has stalled, with shipments of foldables falling 15% in the first half of the year, IDC says. Apple's entry will be a shot in the arm for the category, IDC says, capturing 44% of the revenue generated by all foldable sales in 2026 even though it launches with just over three months to go in the year. As new CEO, Ternus was the star of this year's show. At last year's event, while a mob of gadget reviewers rushed the displays of iPhone 17s, and swarmed Cook, Ternus stood to the side, chatting quietly with other Apple executives and almost entirely ignored. Ternus has been head of hardware engineering for Apple since 2021, leading the team that built the foldable iPhone. That team also incorporated Apple's highly rated chips in its Mac computers, one of its less appreciated but more consequential accomplishments of recent years. It is no accident that Apple timed his transition so that, for his first product rollout, Ternus introduced its biggest update to its most important product. One knock on Apple's Cook era has been that the company didn't release more innovative products. Though the foldable was developed on his watch, Apple is anxious to set a tone that a new innovative day has dawned at the company.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n苹果2026发布会炸了：第一台折叠iPhone来了：卖到1.6万元，凭什么？ 苹果发布全新折叠屏 iPhone Duo，迎来新 CEO，也带来更高的价格 苹果周三拉开了多年来最具分量的一场产品发布会的序幕。在这场发布会上，苹果推出了一款真正意义上的全新 iPhone，迎来了一位新任首席执行官，同时也提高了产品价格。苹果希望消费者把注意力集中在前两件事上。此次发布会最大的焦点，是苹果首款折叠屏 iPhone，起售价为 1,999 美元。全新的 iPhone Duo 展开后几乎就像一台平板电脑，却又能轻松放进口袋，同时允许用户一次使用多个应用程序。它提供两种配色：白色和深灰色。 同样重要的，还有站在台上介绍这款产品的人。约翰·特努斯上周刚刚接任苹果 CEO，成为自 1997 年史蒂夫·乔布斯回归苹果以来的第三位首席执行官。外界对这位长期负责硬件工程的高管寄予厚望，希望他能够开启苹果新一轮的创新浪潮，而这款折叠屏 iPhone 则是苹果十年来最具分量的一款设备。特努斯大步走上苹果总部的发布会舞台时满面笑容，为整场活动揭开序幕。“我们一直在构想很多东西，”他对现场数百名媒体人士说道，“我们觉得，你们会喜欢接下来看到的东西。”他说话时，蒂姆·库克和苹果其他高管都坐在前排。 对消费者来说，坏消息是苹果提高了 iPhone 的价格，尽管涨幅并没有一些分析师此前预计的那么大。自去年秋季以来，智能手机内部使用的内存和存储芯片成本已经上涨至原来的五倍，这促使苹果全面上调旗下设备的价格，以保护其利润率。 折叠屏 iPhone Duo 最大的亮点是尺寸更大的内部屏幕，它让 iPhone 用户可以同时打开两个应用程序。用户可以一边观看视频一边发短信，也可以在屏幕左侧查看电子邮件收件箱，同时在右侧打开邮件内容窗口。另一个亮点则是那道“折痕”——也就是内部屏幕折叠的位置——在手机展开后几乎完全看不见；相比之下，在竞争对手的安卓设备上，这道折痕往往相当明显，从而影响用户的使用体验。 这款设备一个明显的取舍在于重量。虽然展开之后机身很薄，但 Duo 由两个部分组成，再加上坚固的机身结构，使它明显比其他 iPhone 型号更加厚重。对于新款 Pro 系列手机，苹果重点强调了由升级版 A20 Pro 芯片驱动的端侧 AI 功能，并称这款芯片运行速度更快，同时散热效率也更高。苹果还展示了 Pro 系列摄像头全新的可变光圈，它能够根据拍摄环境中的光线多少自动打开或收缩，就像人的瞳孔会在昏暗环境中放大一样。苹果表示，iPhone 18 Pro 和 Pro Max 的价格都将上涨 100 美元，分别达到 1,199 美元和 1,299 美元，高于去年的 iPhone 17 Pro 系列。这一涨幅并没有一些人此前担心的那么大，也意味着苹果可能会在这些设备的利润率上承受一定损失。 值得注意的是，此次发布会上有两款设备并未亮相：基础款 iPhone，以及第二代 iPhone Air，这两款产品预计将在明年推出。这意味着，希望购买最新款 iPhone 的消费者只能花更多的钱选择 Pro 系列或 Duo，而这或许正是苹果的一项精明策略——推动消费者转向价格更高的机型。 折叠屏手机并不是什么新鲜事物。三星早在 2019 年就推出了第一代 Galaxy Fold。一些公司甚至已经推出了三折叠手机，它们不只有一个，而是拥有两个铰链。然而，这一品类的发展已经陷入停滞。IDC 表示，今年上半年折叠屏手机出货量下降了 15%。IDC 认为，苹果的入局将为整个折叠屏手机市场注入一剂强心针：尽管 iPhone Duo 上市时距离 2026 年结束只剩三个多月，但苹果预计仍将拿下当年全球折叠屏手机销售总收入的 44%。 作为新任 CEO，特努斯成为今年发布会当之无愧的主角。去年的发布会上，一大群数码产品评测者蜂拥至 iPhone 17 的展示区，又将库克团团围住，而特努斯却站在一旁，安静地与苹果其他高管交谈，几乎完全无人关注。自 2021 年以来，特努斯一直担任苹果硬件工程主管，并领导了打造折叠屏 iPhone 的团队。这个团队还将苹果备受好评的自研芯片应用到了 Mac 电脑中——这是苹果近年来没有得到足够重视、但影响却更为深远的成就之一。 苹果特意安排特努斯在这个时间点完成权力交接绝非偶然：这样一来，他上任后的首次新品发布，介绍的正好就是苹果最重要产品迎来的最大规模升级。库克时代的苹果一直受到的一项批评，就是公司没能推出更多真正具有创新性的产品。尽管这款折叠屏 iPhone 是在库克任内开发出来的，但苹果显然急于借此传递一个新的信号：一个崭新的创新时代，已经在这家公司拉开帷幕。","2026-09-12_001":"AI十年内会杀死全人类？Anthropic研究员发出最严重警报！1.5亿人围观 The sky streaked orange and red as my mother drove us home to Albuquerque through the Tijeras Pass, which separates the Sandia Mountains to the north from the Manzano to the south. “Look out for mushroom clouds,” my father said to her. “If you see them, turn around.” I was already pondering these worn fantasies this week, as the 25th anniversary of 9/11 loomed, when Jacob Coxon wrote a social-media post about quitting his job at Anthropic because AI technology would, in his estimation, “kill us all by the end of the decade.” The sentiment takes up unfinished business; ruination has been building up in contemporary culture. This fantasy—of the end of the world, and of being present for it—is among the most powerful emblems of the 20th century. The atomic bomb inaugurated it, and nuclear armaments remain the reference technology for apocalyptic threat and reverie. We believe in the fantasy, and pine for it, because nothing feels better than the belief that you might witness the conclusion of humankind. The threat of annihilation from nuclear weapons is simple and easy to explain. For one part, a single warhead can level an entire city. For another, the mere launch of a single missile all but assures the release of all of them. Likewise, the AI superintelligence of the Terminator films is both straightforward and obviously threatening. Skeleton-like metal robots want only to destroy humanity. Real AI is not so straightforward. I used it on the morning of Coxon’s post to help me nail down the quantity of various sugars I would use in a gelato recipe. By the afternoon, it had undergone a costume change, into the angel of death. The nature of AI’s extinction threat is unclear and second-order. In an interview with Wired, Coxon struggled to say exactly how AI might bring about the end of civilization. “The classic example is, like, synthesizing a new virus or taking down critical infrastructure by doing some sort of hacking spree,” Coxon said, before admitting that “the latter might be less likely to kill literally everyone.” Coxon is less a whistleblower than a poster; he posted a fairly common sentiment that just happened to catch wind. It’s the anniversary of 9/11. OpenAI just claimed to have solved an intractable problem in mathematics. That company and Anthropic, where Coxon worked, are racing to multitrillion-dollar IPOs. An ongoing cultural battle about the moral and aesthetic vice of AI recently turned in on itself, when the journalist Kelsey Piper accused writers who mistrust Pangram, an AI-detection tool, of essentially trying to hide their own clandestine uses of AI. As I write this, Coxon’s post has been seen more than 150 million times, a number equal to roughly 2 percent of the global population. The situation might feel unsettling, validating, preposterous, or even stupid, depending on your perspective. AI companies and proponents have an incentive to make the technology seem powerful, even threatening, because power gives rise to profit. AI detractors have reason to take the AI extinction threat seriously as evidence that those in charge are irresponsible and reprobate. During the Cold War, cloak-and-dagger paranoia was spread by spies, agents of statecraft, confidants, whistleblowers, and journalists—either as truth to power or as crackpot conspiracy theories. But today, in the internet age, conspiracy theories are everyone’s primary method of exchange. Information’s tendency to spread harmfully, like disease—the original meaning of media “virality” facilitates further spread. Whether the threat Coxon has in mind is likely or even lucid is not important. Instead, the threat’s enticement has proved so lurid that people feel compelled to embrace it. That is different from saying that there is no threat. For example, the author Annie Jacobsen noted that the “superhuman system that can hack anything” that Coxon described could, in theory, hack the labs that store dangerous biological agents. If “what’s inside gets released,” Jacobsen wrote, “it’s goodbye humans.” Thinking of AI as a dangerous superintelligence capable of eradicating civilization is much more fulfilling than pondering the threat of a cluster of haphazard computer programs. We will probably never reclaim, if that is the right word for it, the lucidity of threat we felt at the Cold War’s climax. We do seem to long for that clarity, and the yearning for it has inspired us to patch back together some of its structure. The most satisfying dream of humankind is to be alive to witness its end. “I caused it” and “I prevented it” and “I tried to warn you” amount to equally appealing versions of that fantasy. Even “I posted about it” feels pretty good.\n⟪中文译文⟫\nAI十年内会杀死全人类？Anthropic研究员发出最严重警报！1.5亿人围观 所有人都巴不得AI毁灭世界——一位Anthropic研究员辞职，因为他真信这一天会来。而他的故事之所以刷屏，是因为你也想信。 天边烧成一片橙红。母亲开车载着我们回阿尔伯克基，穿过蒂赫拉斯山口——那道山口把北边的桑迪亚山和南边的曼萨诺山劈成两半。\"留神看有没有蘑菇云，\"父亲对她说，\"要是看见了，掉头就走。 这个星期，我本来就在琢磨这些老掉牙的幻想——9·11二十五周年正一天天逼近。偏偏这时候，雅各布·考克森发了条社交媒体帖子，说他从Anthropic辞职了，因为在他看来，AI技术\"会在这个十年结束之前把我们全都弄死\"。这股情绪接的是一桩早就没了结的旧账：毁灭感，一直在当代文化里一层层堆积着，从未停过。 这个幻想——世界终结，而你恰好在场——是二十世纪最有分量的象征之一。原子弹为它揭了幕，而核武器至今仍是衡量一切末日恐惧与末日遐想的那把尺子。我们信奉这个幻想，甚至害着它的相思。因为再没有什么感觉能胜过这一个念头：你或许能亲眼看见人类的结局。 核武器带来的毁灭威胁，简单，好解释。一来，一枚弹头就能把整座城市抹平；二来，只要一枚导弹升空，剩下的几乎注定倾巢而出。同样，《终结者》里的AI超级智能也是直白得一目了然的威胁——骷髅一样的金属机器人，脑子里只装着一件事：灭掉人类。可现实中的AI没这么直白。考克森发帖那天上午，我还在用它帮我敲定一份意式冰淇淋配方里几种糖该放多少；到了下午，它换了身行头，摇身一变成了死神。 AI的灭绝威胁，性质模糊，而且是拐了好几道弯的间接威胁。在接受《连线》采访时，考克森费了半天劲，也没能说清楚AI究竟会怎样终结文明。\"最经典的例子就是，那个，合成一种新病毒，或者搞一通黑客攻击、把关键基础设施打瘫。\"他说。随后又补了一句：后面那条路，\"恐怕没那么容易真的把所有人都弄死。\" 与其说考克森是个吹哨人，不如说他只是个发帖的——他说出的是一个相当常见的看法，只不过碰巧赶上了风。这周是9·11的周年；OpenAI刚刚宣称拿下了一道数学界的世纪难题；那家公司和考克森待过的Anthropic，正赛跑着奔向估值数万亿美元的上市；而一场围绕AI在道德与审美上如何败坏的文化论战，最近掉头咬住了自己的尾巴——记者凯尔西·派珀指控说，那些质疑AI检测工具Pangram的作家，说白了不过是想遮掩自己偷偷用AI的事实。写下这行字的此刻，考克森那条帖子已被看了超过一亿五千万次——大约相当于全球人口的百分之二。 这件事让你觉得不安、觉得被印证、觉得荒唐、还是干脆觉得愚蠢，全看你站在哪一边。AI公司和它的拥护者有充分的动机，把这项技术说得强大、甚至骇人——因为强大是会生出利润来的。而AI的批评者同样有理由把灭绝威胁当真：它正好可以拿来当证据，证明掌舵的那帮人既不负责任，又无可救药。冷战年代，那种斗篷与匕首式的疑神疑鬼，是由间谍、国家谋士、心腹、吹哨人和记者散播出去的——它们要么被奉为向权力说出的真话，要么被斥为疯子的阴谋论。可到了今天，在互联网时代，阴谋论已经成了所有人最主要的交流方式。 信息本就有一种像疾病那样、以有害方式扩散的倾向——这正是媒体口中\"病毒式传播\"的本义——而这种特性本身，又反过来推着它传得更远。考克森心里那个威胁究竟有多大可能、甚至究竟清不清楚，都不重要。重要的是，这个威胁的诱惑实在太刺眼、太勾人，勾得人们非把它一把抱住不可。 这跟说\"根本不存在威胁\"，完全是两码事。举个例子：作家安妮·雅各布森就指出，考克森所描述的那种\"能黑穿一切的超人系统\"，理论上是有可能侵入那些存放着危险生物制剂的实验室的。\"里头的东西一旦跑了出来，\"她写道，\"人类就再见了。\" 把AI想象成一个足以铲平文明的危险超级智能，远比琢磨\"一堆胡乱拼凑的电脑程序能有什么风险\"来得过瘾。冷战最紧张那些年里，我们曾对威胁有过一种清清楚楚的感觉——那种清晰，我们大概再也找不回来了，如果\"找回\"这个词用得还算贴切的话。可我们确实在思念它；而正是这份思念，驱使我们把那套结构一块一块地又拼补了回来。人类最令人满足的那个梦，从来都是：活着，亲眼看着人类走到尽头。\"是我造成的\"、\"是我阻止的\"、\"我早就警告过你们\"——说到底，不过是同一个幻想同样诱人的三个版本。甚至连\"我为这事发过帖\"，感觉都还挺不错。","2026-09-13_001":"“9·11”25周年：改变美国的一天！如何催生了特朗普的“帝王式总统制”？ Twenty-five years after 9/11, the share of people with no direct recollection of the attacks is rising; just over 100m Americans, roughly a third of the population, were not alive at the time. Yet the counter-terror apparatus that President George W. Bush and Congress enacted in response—hastily drafted legal authorities to wage war, surveillance powers, permissive CIA operations and new bureaucracies—remains largely in place, even as the threat of jihadist violence has receded. Donald Trump’s adaptation of these powers may at last be seeding a debate about correcting the overreach. According to a new YouGov poll, 48% of Americans believe the expeditionary wars and surveillance that followed 9/11 have been worth the cost. But only 37% of Democrats agree, compared with 71% of Republicans. Talk of reform is growing in some Democratic circles. “We have an opportunity to rein in some of these authorities and remake agencies that have been abused,” says Dan Herman of the Centre for America Progress, a Democrat-aligned think-tank. America’s counter-terror regime arose initially with overwhelming support from Democrats and Republicans alike. When the hijacked planes hit the World Trade Centre and Pentagon, the nation braced for further onslaughts. Spooks fretted that al-Qaeda might use chemical, biological or even nuclear weapons. Terrorism became the primary concern of voters. But the dreaded wave of follow-up attacks never came. America launched expeditionary wars that ultimately cost $8trn, killed an estimated 900,000 people and may have provoked more terrorism than they prevented. Yet the initial counterattack in Afghanistan, followed by focused efforts against al-Qaeda, proved remarkably successful. Since 9/11 al-Qaeda has mustered just one fatal attack inside America, when a Saudi air-force officer killed three Americans at a naval base in 2019. The vast resources America injected into its counter-terror apparatus played a part. Congress created the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to “secure the United States from terrorist threats or attacks”. The number of FBI joint terrorism task-forces—which fuse intelligence across federal and local agencies—ballooned from 35 to over 200 today. Better intelligence detection and sharing snuffed out countless plots. Surveillance was a factor, such as Section 702, allowed spooks to snoop on foreign suspects abroad without a warrant, sweeping up Americans’ communications in their dragnet. Other statutes broadened the scope of the Foreign Terrorist Organisation (FTO) designation and bolstered the powers of the Treasury to go after the networks financing terrorist groups. Since returning to office, Mr Trump has cynically repurposed these tools. The president has dramatically broadened the definition of terrorism. His administration has branded a range of Latin American drug gangs as FTOs. His air campaign against suspected drug-smuggling boats in the Caribbean and Pacific has killed at least 277 people so far. Narco-terrorists are “the ISIS and al-Qaeda of the western hemisphere”, Pete Hegseth, the secretary of war, said recently, “and they should be treated as such”. The administration has used the 9/11 toolkit to advance its domestic agenda, too. DHS has become the engine of Mr Trump’s deportation campaign, with thousands of its agents, some of whom worked in counterterrorism, reassigned to immigration enforcement. A presidential directive signed in September 2025 orders the FBI’s terrorism task-forces to track loose, left-wing movements like Antifa, which the administration has labelled “domestic terrorists”. The State Department has designated Antifa’s overseas offshoots as FTOs and pressed allies to follow suit. The administration is also pulling powerful financial counter-terror levers. In Minneapolis, for example, federal agents used authorities created by the Patriot Act of 2001 to seize financial records from labour unions and progressive groups without warrants. In Texas, eight anti-ICE protesters were sentenced in June on spurious charges of “providing material” support to terrorists, a statute designed for al-Qaeda and ISIS financiers. Attempts at reform have so far been embryonic. Congress let Section 702 lapse in June, in part over concerns that Mr Trump is politicising the intelligence apparatus, though the authority will run until early 2027. What happens then will test the reform caucus in Congress. Democrats would like stronger safeguards, but have been unable to break through from their minority position in the legislature. Tim Kaine, a Democratic senator from Virginia, has called on Congress to repeal the 2001 AUMF, but has struggled to garner support. Perhaps it will take a younger congressional intake, for whom 9/11 is a hazier memory, to move things along. Americans who came of age during the past 25 years, notes Mr Herman, “are eager to push back against the abuses that we’re all seeing.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n“9·11”25周年：改变美国的一天！如何催生了特朗普的“帝王式总统制”？ 反恐如何成就了特朗普的帝王式总统权力 九一一事件过去二十五年后，对其毫无直接记忆的人群比例正不断攀升；约一亿出头美国人——差不多占总人口三分之一——在事发时尚未出生。然而，乔治·W·布什总统与国会当年为应对恐袭而仓促搭建的反恐机器——草草拟就的战争授权、监控权、中情局宽松的行动权限，以及新设的官僚机构——至今大体原封未动，尽管圣战暴力的威胁已然消退。 特朗普对这些权力的挪用，或许终于催生了一场关于纠正权力越界的辩论。YouGov最新民调显示，48%的美国人认为九一一之后的远征战争与监控措施代价尚值。但民主党人中仅37%持此看法，共和党人则高达71%。在一些民主党圈子里，改革之声渐起。“我们有机会约束其中一些权力，并重塑那些已被滥用的机构，”民主党倾向的智库美国进步中心的丹·赫尔曼说道。 美国的反恐体制起初赢得了民主、共和两党的压倒性支持。当被劫持的飞机撞向世贸中心和五角大楼，举国上下都在为后续袭击做准备。情报人员忧心忡忡，担心基地组织可能动用化学、生物甚至核武器。恐怖主义一跃成为选民的头号关切。 但令人生畏的后续袭击潮始终未曾到来。美国发动了一场场远征战争，最终耗资八万亿美元，估计造成九十万人死亡，而其所激发的恐怖主义或许比所阻止的还要多。然而，最初对阿富汗的反击，加上此后对基地组织的精准打击，成效却极为显著。九一一以来，基地组织在美国本土仅得手过一次致命袭击——2019年，一名沙特空军军官在海军基地枪杀三名美国人。 美国为其反恐机器注入的庞大资源功不可没。国会创设国土安全部，以“保卫美国免遭恐怖威胁或袭击”。联邦调查局的联合反恐工作组——负责整合联邦与地方各机构的情报——从35个猛增至如今的200多个。更精准的情报侦测与共享，掐灭了无数阴谋。监控亦是其中一环，比如第702条款，允许情报人员在无搜查令的情况下监听海外外国嫌疑人，其拖网式搜集难免裹挟美国人的通信。其他法规还扩大了“外国恐怖组织”认定的范围，并强化了财政部追查恐怖组织融资网络的权力。 重返白宫以来，特朗普先生对这些工具进行了玩世不恭的改造。总统大幅拓宽了恐怖主义的定义。其政府将一系列拉美贩毒团伙列为外国恐怖组织。他对加勒比海和太平洋上疑似运毒的船只发动空袭，迄今已造成至少277人死亡。贩毒恐怖分子是“西半球的伊斯兰国和基地组织”，战争部长皮特·赫格塞思近日放言，“他们就该被这样对待”。 本届政府同样动用九一一时代的工具箱来推进其国内议程。国土安全部已成为特朗普驱逐出境行动的主力引擎，数千名探员——其中一些本就从事反恐工作——被重新调配至移民执法。2025年9月签署的一项总统指令，命令联邦调查局的反恐工作组追踪像“反法西斯”这类松散左翼运动，政府已将其标签为“国内恐怖分子”。国务院已将“反法西斯”的海外分支列为外国恐怖组织，并施压盟友效仿。 本届政府还在拉动反恐金融领域的强力杠杆。例如在明尼阿波利斯，联邦探员援引2001年《爱国者法案》赋予的权力，在无搜查令的情况下从工会和进步团体调取财务记录。在得克萨斯，八名反移民执法抗议者于六月因莫须有的“向恐怖分子提供物质支持”罪名获刑——而该法条本是为基地组织和伊斯兰国的金主量身定做的。 改革尝试迄今仍处萌芽。国会在六月任由第702条款失效，部分原因正是担忧特朗普正将情报机构政治化，尽管该授权将持续至2027年初。届时如何收场，将考验国会中的改革派。民主党人希望有更强的保障措施，却苦于在立法机构中身居少数，始终无法突破。 弗吉尼亚州民主党参议员蒂姆·凯恩呼吁国会废除2001年的军事力量使用授权，但一直难以争取到足够支持。或许要等到更年轻的一批议员进入国会——对他们而言，九一一只是一段模糊的记忆——事情才能有所推进。赫尔曼指出，在过去二十五年间成年的美国人，“迫切想要反击我们眼下有目共睹的种种滥权”。","2026-11-01_001":"特朗普重返白宫背后，藏着一个你可能从没听过的男人！中期选举还能复制奇迹吗？ They are both young, bald and fathers of four. They are also the brains behind Donald Trump. But while Stephen Miller is infamous as the US president’s ideological enforcer, James Blair could, in the words of Time magazine, “be the most important person in Donald Trump’s Washington you’ve never heard of”. Blair has sufficiently impressed Trump to earn the nickname “Brilliant James”. And a well-positioned source told the Guardian: “I would say that James is probably twice, five times, 10 times more effective than Stephen Miller.” Blair, 37, is a data-driven political operative who played a key role in Trump’s historic comeback to win the 2024 presidential election. Now he faces his stiffest challenge yet: helping Republicans hold the House of Representatives and Senate in the next month’s midterm elections. Blair cut his political teeth in the partisan pressure cooker of Florida politics. At 22, he was interviewed for a position under Richard Corcoran, then a powerbroker and future speaker of the Florida house of representatives. By 2018, Blair was serving as senior adviser to the gubernatorial campaign of Republican Ron DeSantis and joined his administration as deputy chief of staff, where he forged his defining alliance with political strategist Susie Wiles, now Trump’s chief of staff. Wiles hired Blair again for Trump’s improbable bid to regain the White House. In March 2024, Blair became political director of Trump’s presidential campaign, helping shape its voter-targeting strategy. Blair’s core contribution to the 2024 campaign was his radical willingness to discard Washington’s conventional wisdom. While traditional operations rely on costly brick-and-mortar field offices and heavy television advertising, Blair argued that broadcast television was losing influence to digital-first strategies, taking the bold step of both closing field offices and outsourcing get-out-the-vote operations to independent super political action committees (Pacs). “He took a lot of abuse, but the president’s comfortable win was read as a confirmation of Blair’s data-driven approach.” After Trump’s second inauguration, Blair entered the White House as a deputy chief of staff, bringing political affairs, intergovernmental affairs and legislative affairs under his control, giving him an unusually broad portfolio. The president told a holiday reception last year: “I’d hear from other people he’s a total political genius, but I didn’t see it. And you know what? He’s not quiet. He’s incredible – the job you’ve done, thank you, James.” As the 2026 midterms approached, Blair stepped away from the White House to take charge of the political action committee Maga Inc, taking the lead in the effort to preserve Republican control of Congress. By the end of August, it had $415m in the bank. But Blair is aware of the economic headwinds facing the party. Whipple recalls a conversation with Blair in December regarding the midterm outlook. “Blair said essentially that it doesn’t matter what we do about Jeffrey Epstein or any of the other problems we have. If we don’t do something about affordability, we are fucked. And so here we are.” As election day approaches, Blair’s data models have run headlong into the realities of midterm politics. Trump is not on the ballot, making voters who are less likely to turn out in midterm elections notoriously difficult to mobilise compared with a presidential cycle. Blair’s political headaches grew earlier this month when it was revealed that he and LaCivita travelled to Republika Srpska, a self-governing region of Bosnia and Herzegovina. The New York Times reported that the pair received payments to advise the regional government before local elections. Trump distanced himself from both men, describing them as “outside consultants” in a 7 October Truth Social post and warning that if he found a conflict of interest, he would “terminate them, immediately!”. Blair responded defensively to the criticism, issuing a rare public statement. “All of this mudslinging is ridiculous, and counterproductive,” he insisted. The setback came as little surprise to those who believe Blair had been riding for a fall. One source who worked closely with Blair for many years said: “He’s an arrogant asshole who always thinks he’s the smartest guy in the room. His arrogance will result in the worst Republican election result for 20 years.” That view is rejected by Blair. “I work for candidates whose cause I believe in. I work intensely hard, and I don’t apologise for that.” In the meantime, on 3 November voters will determine whether Blair can defy the odds again. If the results are as catastrophic for Republicans as opinion polls suggest, he could face the wrath of a capricious president, looking for a scapegoat. “Brilliant James” could find himself with a less desirable nickname. “James Blair can be a raging political genius, and it’s not going to change that Donald Trump is an insane person.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n特朗普重返白宫背后，藏着一个你可能从没听过的男人！中期选举还能复制奇迹吗？ “天才詹姆斯”助特朗普重返白宫，他能再次拿下中期选举吗？ 他们都很年轻，都是光头，而且都是四个孩子的父亲。他们也都是唐纳德·特朗普背后的智囊。然而，斯蒂芬·米勒作为美国总统的意识形态执行者声名狼藉，而用《时代》杂志的话来说，詹姆斯·布莱尔可能是“特朗普治下的华盛顿最重要、但你从未听说过的人物”。布莱尔给特朗普留下了足够深刻的印象，因此赢得了“天才詹姆斯”的绰号。一位消息灵通的知情人士告诉《卫报》：“我会说，詹姆斯的办事效率可能是斯蒂芬·米勒的两倍、五倍，甚至十倍。” 37岁的布莱尔是一名以数据为导向的政治操盘手，他在特朗普历史性地东山再起、赢得2024年总统大选的过程中发挥了关键作用。如今，他正面临迄今为止最严峻的挑战：帮助共和党在下个月的中期选举中保住众议院和参议院的控制权。布莱尔是在佛罗里达州党派斗争激烈的政治环境中崭露头角、磨炼政治本领的。22岁时，他曾参加一场面试，应聘理查德·科科伦手下的一个职位。科科伦当时是佛罗里达州政坛的一位实权人物，后来成为该州众议院议长。 到2018年，布莱尔已经担任共和党人罗恩·德桑蒂斯竞选州长期间的高级顾问，随后加入德桑蒂斯的州政府，担任副幕僚长。在那里，他与政治策略师苏西·怀尔斯建立了对其职业生涯具有决定性意义的合作关系。怀尔斯如今是特朗普的白宫幕僚长。后来，怀尔斯再次聘请布莱尔，参与特朗普那场看似胜算渺茫、旨在重返白宫的竞选。2024年3月，布莱尔成为特朗普总统竞选团队的政治总监，协助制定针对特定选民群体的竞选策略。 布莱尔对2024年竞选活动最核心的贡献，在于他敢于彻底抛弃华盛顿政坛奉为圭臬的传统观念。传统竞选活动依赖成本高昂的实体地方竞选办公室和大规模电视广告投放，而布莱尔认为，广播电视的影响力正在逐渐让位于数字优先的竞选策略。因此，他大胆采取了两项举措：关闭地方竞选办公室，并将动员选民投票的工作外包给独立的超级政治行动委员会（Pacs）。“他因此遭受了大量抨击，但总统最终以明显优势获胜，被视为对布莱尔数据驱动策略的肯定。” 特朗普第二次就职后，布莱尔以副幕僚长的身份进入白宫，将政治事务、政府间事务和立法事务都纳入自己的管辖范围，使他拥有了异常广泛的职权。去年，特朗普总统在一场节日招待会上说：“我以前总听别人说他是个彻头彻尾的政治天才，但我当时没看出来。不过你们知道吗？他可不是个默默无闻、不声不响的人。他太了不起了——詹姆斯，你做得太出色了，谢谢你。” 随着2026年中期选举临近，布莱尔离开白宫，接手政治行动委员会Maga Inc，主导共和党保住国会控制权的努力。截至8月底，该委员会的银行账户中已有4.15亿美元。然而，布莱尔也清楚共和党正面临经济方面的不利因素。惠普尔回忆起去年12月与布莱尔就中期选举前景进行的一次谈话。“布莱尔当时的意思基本上是，不管我们如何处理杰弗里·爱泼斯坦的问题，或者我们面临的其他任何问题，都无关紧要。如果我们不采取措施解决民众的生活负担问题，我们就彻底完蛋了。现在，我们果然走到了这一步。” 随着选举日临近，布莱尔的数据模型迎头撞上了中期选举的残酷现实。特朗普本人并不在此次选举的候选人名单上，这使得那些本来就不太可能在中期选举中投票的选民，相比总统大选期间更加难以动员。本月早些时候，有消息披露，布莱尔和拉奇维塔曾前往波斯尼亚和黑塞哥维那的一个自治地区——塞族共和国，这让布莱尔在政治上面临的麻烦进一步加剧。《纽约时报》报道称，两人在当地选举前曾收取报酬，为该地区政府提供咨询服务。 特朗普与这两人撇清了关系。他在10月7日发布的一则Truth Social帖子中称他们为“外部顾问”，并警告说，如果发现存在利益冲突，他就会“立即终止与他们的合作！”。面对批评，布莱尔采取了防御性的回应方式，罕见地发表了一份公开声明。他坚称：“所有这些恶意中伤都荒谬至极，而且只会适得其反。”对于那些认为布莱尔早晚会因自己的行为而栽跟头的人来说，这次挫折并不令人意外。一位与布莱尔密切共事多年的知情人士说：“他就是个傲慢的混蛋，总觉得自己是屋子里最聪明的人。他的傲慢会让共和党遭遇20年来最惨重的选举失利。”布莱尔则否认了这种评价。“我为那些我认同其政治理念的候选人工作。我工作极其努力，而且我不会为此道歉。” 与此同时，11月3日，选民将决定布莱尔能否再次创造奇迹、打破不利的胜算。如果选举结果真的像民意调查所暗示的那样，给共和党带来灾难性的失败，那么他可能不得不面对一位反复无常、急于寻找替罪羊的总统的怒火。“天才詹姆斯”或许会发现，自己被冠上了一个远不如从前好听的绰号。“詹姆斯·布莱尔就算是个厉害到极点的政治天才，也改变不了唐纳德·特朗普是个疯子这一点。”","2026-10-31_001":"The Americans changing politics for the better — 2026/10/31 The Americans changing politics for the better Midterm elections are always a referendum on the sitting American president, but rarely has an incumbent so wilfully put himself in the spotlight in defiance of his own unpopularity. As the campaign ends, Donald Trump is commandeering rallies in Republican strongholds, a sign of his party’s vulnerability and his unwavering faith in his own star power. Away from the spotlight, however, something more surprising—and more important—has been happening: Hispanics and Asians have emerged as America’s most valuable voters. The curse of American politics is its bitter partisanship. As voters have sorted themselves along racial, educational and geographical lines, the parties have spent less time trying to win converts from the other side and more whipping their own supporters into a permanent frenzy: 70% of Republicans are hostile towards Democrats and more than 80% of Democrats return the favour. The result is a style of politics that tugs public life towards the extremes, punishes compromise and puts getting people angry above getting things done. Hispanic and Asian-American voters offer the best hope of arresting this baleful trend. They have become America’s crucial swing voters, with an outsize impact on races across the country. Compared with other blocs, their numbers are growing faster, their ideology is more fluid and their priorities are less divisive. That means they are likely to be good for America not just in these midterms, but also for many elections to come. No group captures the potential for opening up politics as much as Hispanics and Asian-Americans. One reason is demography. They are a growing fraction of the population—especially Hispanics, who are three times more numerous than Asian-Americans. Since 2006 their combined share of the electorate has increased by nine percentage points, to reach 21%. They are also highly diverse. The grandchildren of an immigrant from Ho Chi Minh City have a different outlook from a new arrival from Bangalore. Neither fits neatly into the familiar Democrat-Republican template. And they are more likely to switch their votes. In 2012 Democrats thought they had a lock on Latinos and Asians. In 2024 the story was how they were becoming Trumpian. It has now become clear that they were swing voters all along. White voters changed little between 2020 and 2024, while both Latinos and Asians lurched hard to the right. They were then the fastest to turn against Mr Trump in early 2025. The margin of support for Democrats has risen by 17 points among Latinos and 14 among Asians since 2024, compared with ten for whites and five for black voters. Part of the explanation for this is that these voters are the epitome of striving Americans who want to work hard and get ahead. Higher prices for food and fuel hit Hispanic voters particularly hard; housing costs affected Asian voters. Latinos are more than twice as likely to start businesses as white voters. Asians are likely to spend more time in education. Many are recent arrivals or the children of immigrants, so their opinions have not yet hardened along party lines. In contrast to the rest of the voting population, if Hispanics and Asians hold one left-leaning position, they will not necessarily endorse others. Likewise, wanting a secure southern border does not equate to support for the hardline enforcement that has frightened Hispanic employees into staying away from work. Instead, many are moderate. They have little truck with culture wars or polarisation. The economic policies they care about diverge from the tribal messages the parties use to stir up their bases. Whereas policies on abortion and trans rights are set at the extremes, economic management is suited to the swing voter’s pragmatic preference for the common good. Almost anything that gives the parties a reason to appeal to the centre will benefit America, especially when it involves the virtues of hard work and prosperity. Democrats will need a winning economic message and a coherent plan for immigration. Republicans should reject their ferocious deportations and the ugly ultranationalism that denigrates Hispanic and Asian citizens for not being “heritage Americans”. All Americans should therefore welcome these new swing voters. Thanks to them, fewer elections will be decided by hard-core party members in primaries, and more by the average voter in the general election. If politicians win more credit by accomplishing something in government, they will perhaps spend less of their time demonising the other side. If the economy seems to matter more and culture wars less, America will be more prosperous and better governed. Hispanic and Asian immigrants want the chance to thrive. Their votes will help ensure America thrives with them.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国中期选举前夕：亚裔和拉美裔，为什么正在成为最有价值的选民？ 这些正在让美国政治变好的美国人——拉丁裔和亚裔，已经成了美国最有活力、也最摇摆的选民 中期选举，向来是对现任总统的一场全民打分。可几乎没有哪一位在任者，会像他这般，明知道自己不受待见，还偏要迎着聚光灯往上站。随着选战收官，川总正在共和党的大本营里，强占一场又一场的集会——这既暴露了他所在政党的虚弱，也印证了他对自己明星光环的顽固迷信。然而，聚光灯照不到的地方，一件更令人意外、也更重要的变化，正在悄然发生：拉丁裔和亚裔，已经悄然崛起，成为美国当下最值钱的选民。 美国政治的痼疾，是那种你死我活的党派对立。随着选民按种族、学历与地域，各自抱团、分边而站，两党也变了味——它们越来越少地去争取对手阵营的倒戈者，却越来越多地把自家拥趸，煽动进一种持久的癫狂：七成共和党人对民主党满心敌意，八成以上的民主党人，则把这敌意原样奉还。其结果，是一种将公共生活拖向极端、对妥协施以惩罚、把“激怒民众”置于“办成实事”之上的政治。 拉美裔和亚裔选民，是遏制这股不祥趋势的最大希望。他们已然成为美国举足轻重的摇摆选民，对全国各地的选战，有着远超其人数规模的影响力。相较其他阵营，他们的人数增长更快，立场更流动、更不固化，最关切的议题也没那么撕裂。这意味着，他们很可能成为美国的福音——不仅在此次中期选举，更在此后一场又一场的选战里。 没有任何一个群体，能像拉美裔和亚裔这样，牢牢抓住“让政治重新打开”的潜力。原因之一是人口——他们在总人口中的占比不断攀升，尤其是拉美裔，人数是亚裔的三倍有余。自 2006 年以来，两者合计在选民中的占比已上升 9 个百分点，达到 21%。同时，他们也极为多元：一位胡志明市移民的孙辈，与一位刚从班加罗尔落脚的新移民，对世界的看法截然不同；而这两者，谁也无法被整齐地塞进那套熟悉的“民主党—共和党”模板。此外，他们也更可能改换选票。2012 年，民主党人还笃定自己已把拉美裔和亚裔牢牢攥在手里；到了 2024 年，故事却成了他们日渐“特朗普化”；而如今，真相已然明了——他们从头到尾，就是摇摆选民。 从 2020 到 2024 年，白人选民几乎纹丝未动，拉美裔和亚裔却猛地向右栽去。而到了 2025 年初，他们又成了全美最快掉头、转而反对特朗普的群体。自 2024 年以来，民主党在拉美裔中的支持优势上升了 17 个点，在亚裔中上升了 14 个点；相形之下，白人仅上升 10 个点，黑人选民更是只有 5 个点。 出现这种现象，部分原因在于：这些选民，正是“奋斗的美国人”的缩影——他们渴望辛勤工作、出人头地。食品与燃油的涨价，对拉美裔选民的打击尤重；住房成本，则沉甸甸地压在亚裔头上。拉美裔创业的可能性，是白人选民的两倍有余；而亚裔，则更倾向于把更多时间投进教育。 他们中的许多人，是刚来不久的新移民，或移民的子女，其政治观点因而尚未在党派界限上固化。与其余选民群体不同，拉美裔与亚裔即便在某一议题上持偏左立场，也未必会连带支持其他偏左主张。同样地，“希望拥有一条安全的南部边境”，并不等同于“支持那种强硬执法”——正是这种执法，已把拉美裔员工吓得不敢出门上班。 恰恰相反，他们中的许多人，是温和派。他们对“文化战争”、对“极化”这类事，几乎不屑于沾边。他们真正关心的经济政策，与两党用来煽动各自基本盘的部落式口号，本就背道而驰。堕胎、跨性别权利等议题，天然被钉死在两个极端；而经济治理，才真正契合摇摆选民那种务实、且着眼于公共利益的偏好。 几乎任何能让两党有理由去争取中间选民的东西，都会让美国受益——尤其当它关乎“勤奋”与“繁荣”这些美德的时候。民主党，需要一套能打动人心的经济叙事，以及一个前后自洽的移民方案。共和党，则应当摒弃他们那凶残的驱逐，以及那丑恶的极端民族主义——正是这种民族主义，以“血统”为尺，诋毁拉美裔与亚裔公民，称他们不配做所谓的“血统美国人”。 因此，所有美国人都应当欢迎这些新的摇摆选民。多亏了他们，会有更少的选举由初选里的铁杆党员说了算，而更多的选举，交由大选中的普通选民来定夺。倘若政客能靠在政府里成就实事来赢得赞誉，他们兴许就会少花些时间去妖魔化对手。倘若经济变得更重要、文化战争变得次要，美国就将更繁荣，也治理得更好。拉美裔与亚裔移民，渴望的是一个能够“兴旺”的机会；而他们的选票，将有助于确保——美国，与他们一同兴旺。","2026-10-30_001":"Spain's Sánchez declares three days of mourning after at least 68 killed in Toledo train crash — 2026/10/30 Spain's Sánchez declares three days of mourning after at least 68 killed in Toledo train crash The Spanish prime minister has declared three days of mourning after at least 68 people were killed and dozens more injured in a collision between two high-speed trains that officials suggested was caused by an \"inexplicable\" error at the central command centre. The trains, one from Spain's state-owned Renfe and the other from the private rail company Iryo, were travelling on Wednesday morning in the same direction on the line that stretches from the Spanish capital, Madrid, to the southern region of Andalusia. After the Iryo train came to a halt, the other slammed into its rear, knocking several carriages off the track near the town of Urda. Josimar Medina woke a little before 9am on Wednesday to find himself airborne and hurtling through his carriage on the fast train from Madrid to Seville. \"I remember flying through the air and looking for my girlfriend, who was also shooting through the air,\" said Medina, a 37-year-old tourist from the northern Peruvian city of Chiclayo. Once he landed, he looked around and realised just how lucky he and his girlfriend, Vitoria Maia, had been. \"I saw people with cuts, people covered in blood, people missing limbs, and I saw one person who had nothing left below the chest,\" said Medina. The Peruvian stood recounting the details of Wednesday's deadly rail crash, in which at least 68 people were killed and dozens more were injured, wearing a light brown T-shirt stained with the blood of a young American man he had helped move to safety. The couple climbed out of the train and helped the injured while they waited for medics and Guardia Civil officers to arrive at the scene of the crash, which took place amid vineyards and olive groves near the small town of Urda. Fredrich and Arabella Barilea, a couple from the Philippines who now live in Dubai, were on the Iryo train, and Fredrich was sent flying by the impact. \"One guy was shouting: 'Get out! Get out! Another train could hit!'\" said Fredrich. \"We saw all the people were also getting off. Many of them had head injuries and were covered in blood. We just got as far away from the train as we could.\" \"It all happened in a split second,\" said Arabella. \"The table we were sitting at just disappeared in the crash. I feel like we're on our second life.\" Spain's transport minister, Óscar Puente, said that rear-end collisions like that which had taken place on Wednesday were \"highly unlikely\" and suggested that the accident may have been caused by human error made in \"inexplicable\" circumstances. He said the LZB signalling system, which automatically halts a train if the line ahead is occupied, had gone down at about 8.30am local time due to a computer malfunction. As a result, an estimated six trains had been halted along a stretch of around 63km of track, he said. The task of monitoring the rail lines then fell to a 24-hour command centre in Madrid. Minutes after the computer malfunction, the centre instructed at least two of the trains to continue as normal, despite the fact that there was still a \"stop signal\" in place, said Puente. \"It was the control centre, through its operators, that made the decision to resume traffic, under circumstances that, from our point of view, are inexplicable,\" he said. \"Clearly, it was a wrong decision.\" Giving initial details about the crash, Puente said the Iryo train had been travelling ahead on the tracks and had been sitting stationary when the Renfe train was instructed by the command centre to resume its journey. The Renfe train then travelled approximately 25km before colliding with the Iryo train about 17 minutes later. The control centre \"had more than enough time to verify the error, and they did not fix it\", Puente added. Puente said that similar erroneous instructions had been given to at least one other train on Wednesday morning. \"And it was the engineer of one of the trains who corrected the command centre and told them he can't resume normal operation – it has to be [an] on-sight operation,\" Puente said. The tragedy was the second major deadly high-speed train accident this year in Spain, rattling a country where the extensive rail network has long been a point of pride. In January, 46 people were killed and dozens more injured after two trains – again, one from Renfe and one from Iryo – collided on the same line near the town of Adamuz in Andalucía. But, as also happened at Adamuz, the solidarity and kindness on display is likely to turn to anger over the coming hours as people seek answers as to how one of Europe's most modern and extensive high-speed rail networks could experience two deadly collisions that have between them claimed at least 114 lives in the space of just 10 months. The opposition People's party leader, Alberto Núñez Feijóo, called for an immediate investigation into what had happened, as well as a \"complete and independent review\" of Spain's entire rail network. \"Spain has the right to know what is going on with its infrastructure,\" he told reporters.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n西班牙高铁又出事了：同一条高铁线，10 个月两次重大事故、114 人死亡 西班牙托莱多列车相撞事故造成至少68人死亡，首相桑切斯宣布全国哀悼三天 西班牙首相宣布全国哀悼三天。此前，两列高速列车发生相撞事故，造成至少68人死亡、数十人受伤。官员表示，事故可能是由中央指挥中心一次“令人费解”的失误造成的。周三上午，两列列车沿着连接西班牙首都马德里与南部安达卢西亚地区的铁路线路同向行驶，其中一列属于西班牙国有铁路公司Renfe，另一列属于私营铁路公司Iryo。Iryo列车停下后，另一列列车猛烈撞上了它的尾部，导致数节车厢在乌尔达镇附近脱轨。 周三上午9点前不久，乔西马尔·梅迪纳从睡梦中醒来，却发现自己已经腾空而起，在一列从马德里开往塞维利亚的高速列车车厢里猛烈地飞了出去。“我记得自己在空中飞着，一边寻找我的女朋友，她当时也被甩到了空中。”37岁的梅迪纳说道。他是一名来自秘鲁北部城市奇克拉约的游客。落地后，他环顾四周，才意识到自己和女友维多利亚·玛雅是多么幸运。“我看到有人身上有伤口，有人浑身是血，有人失去了肢体。我还看到一个人，胸部以下已经什么都没有了。”梅迪纳说道。 这名秘鲁男子站在那里，讲述着周三那场致命列车事故的细节。事故造成至少68人死亡，另有数十人受伤。他身上穿着一件浅棕色T恤，上面沾着一名年轻美国男子的血迹，而他此前曾帮助将这名男子转移到安全地带。这对情侣从列车中爬出来，一边等待医护人员和西班牙国民警卫队警员赶到现场，一边帮助受伤的乘客。事故发生在乌尔达小镇附近，周围是一片片葡萄园和橄榄树林。 弗雷德里希和阿拉贝拉·巴里莱亚是一对来自菲律宾、如今居住在迪拜的夫妻。事故发生时，他们正乘坐Iryo列车，弗雷德里希被猛烈的撞击力甩飞了出去。“有个男人一直在大喊： ‘快出去！快出去！可能还有另一列火车会撞过来！’”弗雷德里希说道。“我们看到其他人也都在下车。很多人头部受了伤，浑身是血。我们只想着尽可能远离列车。”“这一切都发生在一瞬间。”阿拉贝拉说道。“我们坐着的那张桌子在撞击中直接消失了。我感觉我们就像获得了第二次生命。” 西班牙交通部长奥斯卡·普恩特表示，像周三发生的这种列车追尾事故“极不可能发生”，并指出，这起事故可能是由某种“令人费解”的人为失误造成的。他表示，LZB信号系统在当地时间上午8点30分左右因计算机故障而停止运行。这套系统能够在前方轨道被其他列车占用时自动使列车停下。据他介绍，受此影响，估计有六列列车在一段长约63公里的铁轨上被迫停驶。 随后，监控铁路线路的任务便落到了马德里一座全天24小时运作的指挥中心身上。普恩特表示，计算机发生故障仅仅几分钟后，尽管当时仍然存在“停车信号”，指挥中心却指示至少两列列车像往常一样继续行驶。“正是控制中心通过其操作人员作出了恢复列车运行的决定，而在我们看来，作出这一决定时的情况令人费解。”他说道。“显然，这是一个错误的决定。” 在介绍这起事故的初步细节时，普恩特表示，Iryo列车原本行驶在前方轨道上。当指挥中心指示Renfe列车恢复行驶时，Iryo列车正处于静止状态。随后，Renfe列车行驶了大约25公里，并在约17分钟后与Iryo列车相撞。普恩特补充道，控制中心“有绰绰有余的时间来核实这一错误，但他们却没有纠正”。普恩特表示，周三上午，至少还有另一列列车收到了类似的错误指令。“而且，正是其中一列列车的司机纠正了指挥中心的错误，并告诉他们，他不能恢复正常运行，必须采用目视行车方式。”普恩特说道。 这场悲剧是西班牙今年发生的第二起重大致命高铁事故，令这个长期以来以庞大铁路网络为傲的国家深受震动。今年1月，两列列车——同样是一列属于Renfe，另一列属于 Iryo——在安达卢西亚地区阿达穆斯镇附近的同一条铁路线上相撞，造成46人死亡、数十人受伤。然而，正如阿达穆斯事故发生后一样，随着人们开始追问，为什么欧洲最现代化、最庞大的高铁网络之一，竟然会在短短10个月内接连发生两起致命碰撞事故，夺走至少114条生命，眼下人们展现出的团结与善意，很可能在接下来的几个小时里逐渐转化为愤怒。 反对党人民党领导人阿尔韦托·努涅斯·费霍呼吁立即对事故经过展开调查，并对西班牙整个铁路网络进行一次“全面而独立的审查”。他对记者表示：“西班牙有权知道，自己的基础设施究竟出了什么问题。”","2026-10-29_001":"Don't expect supersize profits from Anthropic soon — 2026/10/29 Don't expect supersize profits from Anthropic soon Breakneck growth is commonplace in Silicon Valley. The rise of Anthropic is something else. In the first quarter of last year—when Claude Code, its hit programming tool, was first released—the artificial-intelligence lab pulled in $376m of revenue. By the second quarter of this year that figure had risen 30-fold, to $11.6bn, exceeding corporate mainstays such as McDonald's. In a few weeks this darling of the AI revolution will undergo an initial public offering (IPO) that will be, if not the largest of all time, then certainly close to it. On October 26th it publicly filed supporting paperwork that gave the outside world a closer look at its finances. Anthropic's growth is all the more remarkable because, unlike many zippy startups, it has already reached profitability. In the second quarter it eked out an operating profit of just under $1bn, having lost $1.7bn in the preceding three months. The company is particularly eager to draw attention to its spending on computing power, which fell from 251% of sales in the first quarter of 2025 to just 48% in the second quarter of 2026. Anthropic claims that improvements in its AI models have made it faster and more efficient to develop and serve its technology, allowing the company to make the most of its limited resources. But can Anthropic's stunning performance continue? A closer look at its filing suggests caution is warranted: growth is slowing; the company is paying higher prices to access scarce computing power; and a resulting shift towards directly purchasing AI chips will guzzle up much of the cashflow Anthropic would otherwise be on course to generate. Earlier investors who had hoped for a public valuation as high as $2trn may thus be disappointed. Anthropic's scorching revenue growth has put a target on its back. Claude Code made it an early leader in the race to automate programming. Tech giants such as Meta and Microsoft became huge customers, spending billions of dollars on Anthropic's technology. More recently those customers have been pushing staff to use internally developed alternatives. At the same time, OpenAI, Anthropic's arch-rival, has been gaining ground with its own offering, Codex, the daily usage of which has risen ten fold since early August. And after the short-lived craze for \"tokenmaxxing\" earlier this year, companies of all stripes have been more closely monitoring their spending on AI and imposing limits on their engineers. This month Anthropic told shareholders during a private gathering that revenue in the third quarter was up by about 45% from the preceding three months, a third of the rate it achieved in the second quarter. In its IPO prospectus Anthropic warns that, as the market matures, growth could slow \"more rapidly than we anticipate\". Moreover, it has become increasingly reliant on the tech giants to push its products. Almost half of Anthropic's revenue in the first six months of this year came from sales made through the cloud businesses of Alphabet, Amazon and Microsoft. Anthropic's spending on sales, marketing and distribution partnerships such as these chewed up 19% of revenue in the first half of 2026, up from 10% in the same period a year before. Another problem Anthropic faces is that, like other AI developers, it is struggling to secure the computing power it needs. In its prospectus it warns investors that its use of \"short-term compute\" could go up in the near term, weighing on margins. For example, Anthropic has agreed to pay SpaceX about $3.1bn per month to use its Nvidia chips into 2029. (Anthropic is able to back out from early next year with 90 days' notice.) That explains why Anthropic is starting to develop more of its own computing infrastructure, rather than relying solely on cloud providers. This strategy should help keep operating costs under control, but also provides forewarning that the lab will become a more capital-intensive business in the long run. The initiative, called \"Anthropic Direct\", involves procuring chips from providers such as Broadcom, which develops custom silicon, and installing them in rented data centres. That could make it harder to start throwing off the kind of free cashflow investors might hope for, and require the business to raise more capital. In Anthropic's prospectus, its founders argue that its spending plans \"may seem irresponsibly large at first glance\", but are justified by the exponential trajectory that AI is on. \"We will aim to be bold but sane,\" they write. With a technology so mind-bending, striking that balance may prove surprisingly difficult. ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\nAnthropic准备上市：一家AI公司为什么可能创造史上最大IPO之一？ 在硅谷，玩命式的增长早已司空见惯，但 Anthropic 的崛起，是另一回事。去年第一季度——彼时它的爆款编程工具 Claude Code 刚刚发布——这家人工智能实验室的营收还只有3.76亿美元；到今年第二季度，这个数字已经翻了30倍，达到116亿美元，一举超过了麦当劳这样的企业巨头。 几周之后，这个AI革命的宠儿，将进行一场首次公开募股——这场 IPO，就算不是史上最大，也肯定接近史上最大。10月26日，它公开提交了配套文件，让外界第一次得以如此近距离地，看清它的财务状况。 Anthropic 的增长之所以愈发了不起，是因为——不像许多冲劲十足的创业公司——它已经实现了盈利。第二季度，它勉强挤出了略低于10亿美元的营业利润，而在此前三个月，它才刚刚亏掉了17亿美元。这家公司尤其急于让人注意它在算力上的支出：这一项，从2025年第一季度占营收的251%，降到了2026年第二季度的仅48%。Anthropic 声称，其 AI 模型的改进，让它开发与运行技术变得更快、更高效，从而得以充分利用自身有限的资源。 但是，Anthropic 这惊艳的表现，还能持续下去吗？仔细读一读它提交的文件就会发现，谨慎不无道理：增长正在放缓；为了获取稀缺的算力，公司正付出越来越高的代价；而由此引发的、转向直接采购 AI 芯片的策略转变，又将大口吞噬掉它本可以源源不断创造出来的现金流。那些曾寄望于它以高达两万亿美元估值上市的早期投资者，恐怕要大失所望了。 Anthropic 滚烫的营收增长，已经在它背上画下了一个靶子。Claude Code 曾让它在“自动化编程”这场竞赛中占得先机。Meta、微软这样的科技巨头，成了它的大客户，在它的技术上砸下数十亿美元。但最近，这些客户开始推动自己的员工，转向使用自家开发的替代品。与此同时，Anthropic 的头号对手 OpenAI，正凭着自己的编程工具 Codex 步步紧逼——Codex 的每日使用量，自 8 月初以来已经翻了十倍。而在今年早些时候那阵“tokenmaxxing”的短暂狂热过去之后，形形色色的公司都开始更密切地监控自己的 AI 支出，并对工程师们设下了限制。 本月，Anthropic 在一次私下聚会上告诉股东，第三季度的营收较前三个月增长了约45%，只有第二季度增速的三分之一。它在 IPO 招股书中警告称，随着市场逐渐成熟，增长放缓的速度，可能“比我们预期的更快”。此外，它越来越依赖这些科技巨头，来推销自己的产品。今年上半年，Anthropic 近半数的营收，都来自通过 Alphabet、亚马逊和微软的云业务完成的销售。Anthropic 在销售、营销，以及这类分销合作上的支出，吞掉了2026年上半年营收的19%，而一年前同期，这个比例还只有10%。 Anthropic 面临的另一个问题是，和其他 AI 开发者一样，它正在艰难地争取自己所需的算力。它在招股书中警告投资者，自己对“短期算力”的使用，在近期可能会增加，从而拖累利润率。举个例子，Anthropic 已经同意，每月向 SpaceX 支付约 31 亿美元，用来租用它的英伟达芯片，一直持续到 2029 年。（从明年初起，Anthropic 可以提前 90 天通知，退出这项协议。） 这就解释了，为什么 Anthropic 开始建设更多属于自己的计算基础设施，而不是仅仅依赖云服务商。这一策略，应该有助于把运营成本控制住，但它也预示着一件事——从长远看，这家实验室会变成一家更加“资本密集”的企业。这项名为 “Anthropic Direct”的计划，包括从博通这类供应商那里采购芯片——博通是做定制芯片的——再把它们装进租来的数据中心里。这可能会让公司更难开始产生投资者所期待的那种“自由现金流”，并且需要公司去融更多的钱。 在 Anthropic 的招股书里，其创始人辩称，这些支出计划“乍一看，可能大得有些不负责任”，但它们是有理由的——理由是，AI 正处在一条指数级的轨迹上。“我们会努力做到大胆而不失理智。”他们写道。然而，面对一项如此颠覆认知的技术，要取得那个平衡，结果可能会出乎意料地难。■","2026-10-28_001":"The Great Louvre Robbery Was Just the Start. It's Been the Year of the Heist. — 2026/10/28 The Great Louvre Robbery Was Just the Start. It's Been the Year of the Heist. In the year since thieves stunned the world by smashing their way into the Louvre Museum and stealing France's crown jewels worth at least $101 million, art heists have only intensified. From Wales to Sicily, more than 14 museums have been hit by smash-and-grab criminals since early 2025, suggesting museums haven't cracked the code on how to safely protect their treasures. Police are still looking for the Louvre's stolen trove of 19th-century jewelry. Experts have myriad theories about why art heists are increasing, from soaring gold prices making golden antiquities and precious gems more valuable to the misguided notion that stolen art can reap higher sums on the black market—it cannot. But museum directors and security firms agree the Louvre breach set an ominous precedent. \"Louvre was a wake-up call that anywhere can be hit,\" said Chris Marinello, chief executive of Art Recovery International. For its part, the Louvre created a new security coordinator position now held by Olivier Goupil, a veteran police officer. The museum also added more crowd-control barriers and anti-ram devices around its exterior. There are now window railings at the upper-level Apollo Gallery where thieves broke in. In a statement, the museum said the challenge is to balance its mission of protecting its collection while also sharing it with roughly 30,000 visitors a day. \"Museums are not fortresses,\" the museum said in an email. The Louvre heist revealed that thieves are increasingly stealing gold, bronze and jewels more than traditional paintings—a turnaround from a century ago when a thief stole its \"Mona Lisa,\" later recovered. In 10 of the subsequent museum break-ins across Europe over the past year, thieves bypassed paintings to steal objects that could be dismantled or melt down, because such commodities retain value as precious metals and gems. Black-market jewelers are often willing to re-cut and reshape bigger jewels into smaller untraceable ones that can be resold anywhere, investigators say. On Oct. 5, thieves broke into the C. Karoutzos Contemporary Art Center in central France and hauled off a 330-pound bronze book cover decorated with gold, gems and shells designed by surrealist Salvador Dalí. Museum owner Christian Karoutzos said his video cameras captured a few shadowy figures leaving the scene, but the book cover remains missing. On the other hand, authorities have had better success recovering stolen paintings. It likely helped that the works were identifiable and only valuable if left intact, Marinello said. \"You can't melt down a Renoir.\" In 1990, two men disguised as police officers spent 81 minutes crisscrossing Boston's Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum to cut out and carry off masterpieces by Vermeer and Rembrandt. This year's heist at the Magnani Rocca took less than three minutes. Geoff Kelly, a former FBI agent, said thieves are breaking in, grabbing what they can and fleeing the scene within minutes—before the police arrive. In July, Paris's Museum of Natural History suffered an undetected cyberattack that disabled its alarm and surveillance systems, police said. Two months later, robbers monitoring the digital breakdown were able to saw through an emergency exit door and use blowtorches and other weapons to steal about 13 pounds of gold worth around $2 million. An earlier audit of the museum's security protocols found that the Louvre's video surveillance and physical security systems relied on a weak password, \"LOUVRE.\" It was immediately changed, the museum said. Marinello said thieves are beginning to use artificial intelligence to identify museums with hefty holdings of gold or those housed in smaller, historic buildings that might be easier to breach. It's \"something that should scare the bejesus out of museum directors,\" he said. As an experiment, Marinello asked an AI tool to help him steal a painting; the tool refused to participate in a possible crime but still assisted him in locating rope and sledgehammers and other gear from the nearest hardware stores. The flip side: Museums say they're using AI-assisted video surveillance to help guards scour for bad actors by analyzing people's behavior as they move through the galleries rather than recording footage passively. Victimized museums are often compelled to cope by closing indefinitely or reopening with fewer treasures on view. The Louvre has reopened its Apollo Gallery, but all the remaining jewelry in it has been removed, resulting in an empty, ceremonial 17th-century gallery. At the Louvre, staff are still discussing when and how to put the theft's lone survivor back on public view: Empress Eugenie's 19th-century crown, which thieves dropped and crumpled outside the museum during their getaway. Conservators say one of its eight golden eagles is missing and so are 10 of its 1,354 diamonds, but its 56 emeralds and central globe survived. \"It will be exhibited at the Louvre when we have found the ideal location to create a dedicated, secure exhibition space,\" the museum said, \"but for now, not all conditions are in place.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n欧洲博物馆接连被盗！为什么小偷宁愿偷黄金，也不愿偷价值连城的名画？ 卢浮宫惊天大劫案只是开始：这一年，艺术品盗窃案接连上演 自从窃贼强行闯入卢浮宫博物馆、盗走价值至少1.01亿美元的法国王室珠宝，震惊全世界以来，这一年间，艺术品盗窃案非但没有减少，反而愈演愈烈。从威尔士到西西里岛，自2025年初以来，已有超过14家博物馆遭到暴力破门、迅速洗劫的犯罪分子袭击。这表明，博物馆仍未找到安全保护馆藏珍宝的有效办法。警方至今仍在寻找卢浮宫失窃的那批19世纪珠宝。 对于艺术品盗窃案为何不断增加，专家们提出了各种各样的理论：从金价飙升使古代黄金文物和珍贵宝石更加值钱，到有人错误地认为，被盗艺术品能够在黑市上卖出更高的价钱——但实际上并不能。然而，博物馆馆长和安保公司一致认为，卢浮宫失窃事件开创了一个不祥的先例。“卢浮宫事件敲响了警钟，让人们意识到，任何地方都可能成为袭击目标。”国际艺术品追索公司首席执行官克里斯·马里内洛说道。 卢浮宫方面则新设立了一个安保协调员职位，目前由资深警官奥利维耶·古皮尔担任。博物馆还在建筑外围增设了更多人群管控护栏和防车辆冲撞装置。此前窃贼闯入的上层阿波罗画廊，如今也安装了窗户防护栏杆。博物馆在一份声明中表示，真正的挑战在于如何兼顾保护馆藏的使命，同时又能让每天约3万名参观者欣赏这些珍宝。“博物馆不是堡垒。”博物馆在一封电子邮件中表示。 卢浮宫盗窃案揭示了一个趋势：如今，窃贼越来越倾向于盗取黄金、青铜器和珠宝，而不是传统绘画作品。这与一个多世纪前的情况形成了鲜明反差：当时，一名窃贼盗走了卢浮宫的《蒙娜丽莎》，不过这幅画后来被追回。过去一年里，欧洲各地随后发生的博物馆入室盗窃案中，有10起案件的窃贼绕过画作，转而盗取那些可以拆解或熔化的物品，因为这些东西即使被破坏，仍能凭借其中的贵金属和宝石保有价值。调查人员表示，黑市珠宝商往往愿意将较大的珠宝重新切割、改造，变成更小、无法追踪来源的珠宝，以便在任何地方转售。 10月5日，窃贼闯入法国中部的C. Karoutzos当代艺术中心，搬走了一件重达330磅（约150公斤）的青铜书封。这件作品由超现实主义艺术家萨尔瓦多·达利设计，装饰着黄金、宝石和贝壳。博物馆所有者克里斯蒂安·卡鲁佐斯表示，监控摄像头拍下了几个模糊的人影离开现场的画面，但这件青铜书封至今仍下落不明。另一方面，当局在追回被盗画作方面取得了更大的成功。马里内洛表示，这很可能是因为画作具有可辨识的特征，而且只有保持完整才有价值。“你总不能把一幅雷诺阿的画熔掉吧。” 1990年，两名男子伪装成警察，在波士顿的伊莎贝拉·斯图尔特·加德纳博物馆里来回穿梭了整整81分钟，将维米尔和伦勃朗等大师的杰作从画框中割下并带走。而今年发生在马尼亚尼·罗卡博物馆的盗窃案，整个过程却不到三分钟。前美国联邦调查局特工杰夫·凯利表示，如今的窃贼往往破门而入，能拿什么就拿什么，然后在短短几分钟内逃离现场——赶在警方抵达之前。 警方表示，今年7月，巴黎自然历史博物馆遭到了一次未被察觉的网络攻击，导致其报警系统和监控系统失效。两个月后，一直关注着这次数字系统故障情况的劫匪得以锯开一扇紧急出口的门，并使用喷灯和其他工具，盗走了约13磅（约5.9公斤）、价值约200万美元的黄金。此前，一项针对博物馆安全规程的审计发现，卢浮宫的视频监控系统和实体安保系统竟然依赖一个极其简单的密码——“LOUVRE”（卢浮宫）。博物馆表示，这个密码随后立即被更换。 马里内洛表示，窃贼开始利用人工智能寻找潜在的作案目标，例如收藏了大量黄金的博物馆，或那些设在规模较小、可能更容易被突破安保防线的历史建筑中的博物馆。他说，这“应该把博物馆馆长们吓得魂飞魄散”。为了做一个实验，马里内洛曾要求一款人工智能工具帮助他偷走一幅画。虽然这款工具拒绝参与可能涉及犯罪的行为，但仍然协助他从附近的五金店寻找绳索、大锤和其他装备。另一方面，博物馆表示，它们也正在利用人工智能辅助视频监控系统，通过分析人们在展厅内走动时的行为，帮助安保人员仔细排查潜在的不法分子，而不再只是被动地录制监控画面。 遭遇盗窃的博物馆往往被迫通过无限期闭馆，或者重新开放但减少展出珍宝的方式来应对。卢浮宫已经重新开放了阿波罗画廊，但里面剩余的所有珠宝都已被移走，使这座建于17世纪、原本用于举行典礼的华丽展厅变得空空荡荡。在卢浮宫，工作人员仍在讨论何时以及如何让这场盗窃案中唯一幸存的珍宝重新与公众见面：那就是19世纪欧仁妮皇后的皇冠。窃贼在逃跑过程中将它掉落在博物馆外，导致皇冠被压坏、变形。文物修复专家表示，皇冠上原有的八只金鹰装饰中有一只不见了，1,354颗钻石中也有10颗遗失，但56颗祖母绿宝石和中央的球形装饰都幸存了下来。博物馆表示：“当我们找到理想的地点，能够打造一个专门、安全的展览空间时，这顶皇冠将在卢浮宫展出。但就目前而言，并非所有必要条件都已具备。”","2026-10-27_001":"How a Trillion-Dollar Industry Launders Illegal Gold — 2026/10/27 How a Trillion-Dollar Industry Launders Illegal Gold In an industry riddled with abuse, the world's banks and governments rely on one entity to keep gangsters, warlords and human rights violators from selling gold into the mainstream market. That group, the London Bullion Market Association, is the self-described \"global authority for precious metals.\" But a New York Times investigation found that the association has repeatedly allowed gold into the market that has been mined by criminals, cartel members and children. Refiners lie with impunity about the origins of their gold. And in the case of Madagascar, the L.B.M.A. helped change and backdate key paperwork that certified gold as free from abuse. The Times investigation shows that the divide between \"clean\" and \"dirty\" gold is an industry fiction. There is just gold. And as Wall Street, central banks and investors stockpile and trade the precious metal, they're profiting off a supply that includes gold from illegal sources. The price this year crossed the $5,000 mark. That has made it profitable to mine in inefficient, destructive and politically destabilizing ways. The industry works like this: Miners and brokers sell raw gold to refiners, which melt it, remove impurities, and form it into bars. The major international marketplace accepts bars only from the 67 L.B.M.A.-certified refineries. Those refiners must document the source of every ounce of gold in every bar. But for all its talk about being a global authority, the L.B.M.A. is mostly a paperwork collector. The association relies on refiners to inspect the mines and to self-report the details. Madagascar's central bank decided to capitalize on booming prices by buying and stockpiling gold, including from illegal mines that employ children. To see how it happened, I began at the mines themselves. I took a propeller plane from the capital, Antananarivo, and drove three hours through farmland to a mine called Madagold. The mine sits in a dry valley in the northern part of the island, surrounded by a busy market fueled by gold-rush cash. Everywhere I looked, at Madagold and at mines across the country, children were in danger. Children in Madagascar's mines crushed rocks and hauled buckets of ore. Toddlers wandered between 100-foot-deep shafts. In the gold-rich northern region of Diana, miners follow no laws, the government's regional mining supervisor, Firmin Razafindratondra, said. All of the area's gold mining, he said, is illegal. Outside Mananjary, on Madagascar's east coast, miners operated despite government shutdown orders. Excavators turned rice paddies into a giant pit. For that gold to be worth anything, it needed to be \"certified L.B.M.A.,\" Madagascar's central bank governor, Aivo Andrianarivelo, said. So he dispatched his gold chief to Switzerland to find a refiner. Refiners questioned the gold's origins and legality. \"Nobody wanted to touch this gold,\" said Marc Ummel, a gold-supply-chain specialist at the nonprofit group SwissAid. Small-scale mines are considered red flags. The L.B.M.A.'s rules say that any refiner accepting this gold would be required to conduct mine inspections. \"It's impossible,\" Mr. Ummel remembers telling Madagascar's banker. \"You will never be able to find an L.B.M.A. refiner.\" On that point, he was wrong. The central bank cut a deal in 2023 with the Istanbul Gold Refinery, one of the world's biggest L.B.M.A.-certified refiners. The Turkish refiner's auditor filed paperwork with the L.B.M.A. claiming that it \"does not do any business\" with mines that raise red flags. That claim freed the company from having to conduct inspections. The raw gold was melted into 12 kg bars at a plant next to Madagascar's international airport. From there, tens of millions of dollars in gold was shipped to Turkey to enter the legitimate supply. It was only by chance that anyone at the L.B.M.A. took a look at the Madagascar gold. The European Union happened to be considering certifying the association for a new oversight role. An E.U. inspector noticed the Madagascar deal and began scrutinizing it. How, the inspector asked, could a refinery process gold from Madagascar yet claim no red flags? L.B.M.A. staff escalated the problem to a \"compliance panel\" that included the chief executive, Ms. Crowell. The association could have punished the refiner. Instead, the L.B.M.A. helped cover up the problem. Association officials worked with the Istanbul Gold Refinery's auditor to create a new report that made it seem like the refiner had disclosed the red flags from the beginning and had taken steps to scrutinize the supply chain. The report was backdated, as if it had been filed in real time. That backdated report remains on the L.B.M.A.'s website, with no indication of any alterations. The matter was closed. The gold was certified as meeting the world's most rigorous standards. Now those same refiners who had turned Madagascar away are calling looking to buy gold, Mr. Andrianarivelo said, because the L.B.M.A. had declared that \"there was no issue\" with it.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n非法金矿的黄金，最后是怎么变成“正规金条”的？ 万亿美元产业如何洗白非法黄金 在一个充斥着各种违法乱象的行业里，世界各国的银行和政府依赖一个机构，阻止黑帮分子、军阀和侵犯人权者将黄金卖入主流市场。这个机构就是伦敦金银市场协会（L.B.M.A.），它自称是“全球贵金属行业的权威机构”。然而，《纽约时报》的一项调查发现，该协会曾多次允许由犯罪分子、贩毒集团成员以及童工开采的黄金进入市场。黄金精炼商可以肆无忌惮地谎报黄金来源，而不必承担任何后果。在马达加斯加的案例中，伦敦金银市场协会甚至帮助修改关键文件，并将文件日期倒填至更早的时间，以证明这些黄金不存在违法侵害问题。 《纽约时报》的调查表明，所谓“干净黄金”与“肮脏黄金”之间的界限，不过是整个行业编造出来的假象。黄金就是黄金，并不存在真正明确的区分。当华尔街、各国中央银行和投资者大量囤积、交易这种贵金属时，他们正从一个包含非法来源黄金的供应体系中获利。今年，黄金价格突破了5000美元大关。这使得即便采用低效率、破坏性强，甚至会造成政治局势动荡的方式开采黄金，也变得有利可图。 黄金行业的运作方式是这样的：矿工和经纪商将未经加工的黄金卖给精炼商，精炼商再将黄金熔化、去除杂质，并铸造成金条。主要的国际黄金交易市场只接受来自67家获得伦敦金银市场协会（L.B.M.A.）认证的精炼厂的金条。这些精炼商必须记录每根金条中每一盎司黄金的来源。然而，尽管伦敦金银市场协会不断宣称自己是全球权威机构，但实际上，它基本上只是一个收集文件的机构。该协会依赖精炼商自行检查矿场，并自行上报相关细节。 几年前，马达加斯加中央银行决定利用黄金价格飙升的机会，大量购买并囤积黄金，其中包括来自雇用童工的非法矿场的黄金。为了弄清楚这一切究竟是如何发生的，我决定从矿场本身开始调查。我从首都塔那那利佛乘坐一架螺旋桨飞机出发，随后驱车穿过农田，行驶了三个小时，来到一座名为Madagold的金矿。这座金矿坐落在马达加斯加岛北部一处干燥的山谷中，周围是一片繁忙的市场，淘金热带来的大量现金推动着这里的商业活动。 无论我走到哪里，无论是在Madagold金矿，还是在马达加斯加全国各地的其他矿场，孩子们都身处危险之中。在马达加斯加的矿场里，儿童负责砸碎岩石、搬运一桶桶矿石。蹒跚学步的幼童甚至在深达100英尺（约30米）的矿井之间四处游荡。在黄金资源丰富的北部迪亚纳地区，政府地区矿业监管官员菲尔曼·拉扎芬德拉通德拉表示，矿工们根本不遵守任何法律。他说，该地区所有的黄金开采活动都是非法的。在马达加斯加东海岸的马南扎里市郊外，尽管政府已经下令关闭矿场，矿工们仍在继续作业。挖掘机将原本的稻田挖成了一个巨大的深坑。 马达加斯加中央银行行长艾沃·安德里亚纳里韦洛表示，要让这些黄金具有市场价值，就必须获得伦敦金银市场协会（L.B.M.A.）的认证。于是，他派遣负责黄金业务的主管前往瑞士，寻找愿意合作的精炼商。然而，事情一开始并不顺利。精炼商们对这些黄金的来源及其合法性提出了质疑。“没有人愿意碰这些黄金。”非营利组织SwissAid的黄金供应链专家马克·乌梅尔说道。小规模矿场被视为潜在风险的警示信号。根据伦敦金银市场协会的规定，任何接收这类黄金的精炼商都必须对矿场进行实地检查。乌梅尔回忆说，他当时曾告诉马达加斯加的银行官员：“这不可能。你们永远不可能找到一家获得L.B.M.A.认证的精炼商。”然而，在这一点上，他错了。 全球规模最大的获得伦敦金银市场协会（L.B.M.A.）认证的黄金精炼商之一。这家土耳其精炼厂的审计人员向L.B.M.A.提交了文件，声称该公司“没有与任何存在风险警示信号的矿场开展业务”。这一声明使该公司免于进行矿场实地检查。随后，原始黄金在马达加斯加国际机场附近的一座加工厂被熔铸成每根重12公斤的金条。从那里，价值数千万美元的黄金被运往土耳其，进入合法的黄金供应体系。 伦敦金银市场协会（L.B.M.A.）之所以有人注意到马达加斯加的黄金，纯属偶然。当时，欧盟恰好正在考虑认证该协会，让其承担一项新的监管职责。一名欧盟检查员注意到了马达加斯加的这笔交易，并开始对其展开仔细审查。这名检查员提出了一个疑问：一家精炼厂怎么可能一边加工来自马达加斯加的黄金，一边又声称不存在任何风险警示信号呢？L.B.M.A.的工作人员随后将这一问题上报给一个“合规委员会”，该委员会成员包括首席执行官克罗韦尔女士。协会本可以对这家精炼厂进行处罚。然而，L.B.M.A.非但没有这样做，反而帮助掩盖了这个问题。 协会官员与伊斯坦布尔黄金精炼厂的审计人员合作，制作了一份新的报告，让人看起来仿佛这家精炼厂从一开始就已经披露了相关风险警示信号，并且采取了措施对黄金供应链进行严格审查。这份报告的日期被倒填至更早的时间，使其看起来就像是在当时按时提交的一样。如今，这份倒填日期的报告仍然保留在L.B.M.A.的官方网站上，却没有任何迹象表明它曾被修改过。此事就这样不了了之。这批黄金最终获得认证，被认定符合全球最严格的标准。安德里亚纳里韦洛表示，如今，那些曾经拒绝接收马达加斯加黄金的精炼商，反而主动打电话来，希望购买这些黄金，因为L.B.M.A.已经宣布，这些黄金“不存在任何问题”。","2026-10-26_001":"US has for years asked Musk to alter Starlink service in Ukraine, Middle East, sources say — 2026/10/26 US has for years asked Musk to alter Starlink service in Ukraine, Middle East, sources say Well before the throttling this month of Ukraine's military access to Elon Musk's satellite communications system, the Biden and Trump administrations had several times asked SpaceX to turn its Starlink service on or off in various countries, according to six former senior US officials familiar with the matter. During the presidency of Joe Biden, the requests included switching off the service to prevent Iranian proxy militias in Yemen and Lebanon from using Starlink to communicate, and activating the service to provide internet access inside Iran and in Ukraine to support its military. Similarly, President Donald Trump's administration asked Musk, who is CEO of SpaceX, to make the service available in Iran following US-Israeli strikes in 2025 and 2026, in hopes it would bolster internet access and communications for anti government protesters. The recent blowup over restrictions to Ukraine's use of SpaceX satellite services has raised questions about the ties between the US government and Musk, whose company owns systems vital to the US military and to US allies. The Biden-era and Trump requests to Musk show a close relationship in which US administrations rely on an unprecedented degree of a private-sector company to help implement policy. The recent limits on Ukraine, despite being a US ally, have also rattled officials in Europe, some of whom argue that Europe must develop its own military satellite communications capabilities because no viable short-term alternative currently exists. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said last week that his country's access to Starshield, the military version of the Starlink system, had been intentionally limited, interfering with the performance of the country's military drones. He also said the speeds were reduced for Starlink, which Ukraine uses for some military activities including defense against Russian missile attacks. The White House, contacted for comment, referred Reuters to Trump's remarks on Friday, when he said he had not spoken with Musk about Starlink access issues for Ukraine and that \"if there is a problem, I'll get it straightened out.\" SpaceX did not respond to requests for comment, although Musk said in a social media post earlier this week: \"Starshield systems are owned and controlled by the US government. Any limits on speed or usage are decided by the US military and ultimately (by the president of the United States).\" For Ukraine, the Starlink internet service has become the lifeblood of frontline communication between units, who say there is currently no rival to the capabilities of SpaceX's products. Starshield receivers, largely impervious to radio jamming, are used as onboard guidance systems and to provide links to a remote pilot for Ukrainian drones, including deep behind Russian lines inside Ukraine. Musk's role as the strong-willed CEO of a company with outsized influence over US national security has at times raised questions over who is making decisions about SpaceX's satellite services. The Biden administration at times relied on Musk to help implement policy. Biden officials coordinated with SpaceX to thwart US adversaries by cutting the service, and to help allies by expanding Starlink's availability. They added that discussions between the government and SpaceX often focused on preventing groups such as the Iranian-linked Houthis in Yemen and Hezbollah in Lebanon from using Starlink terminals. In October 2023, during fighting between Israel and Hezbollah militants in southern Lebanon, intelligence officials told the White House that individuals linked to Hezbollah could have gained access to Starlink terminals. Officials had similar concerns about the Houthis in Yemen in 2024 during a US air campaign against the Iran-linked group. In both cases, the administration contacted SpaceX and asked it to identify the terminals and shut them off. At least some of the terminals were deactivated. In a 2024 submission to the US Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security, SpaceX said it works closely with US government agencies to secure export authorizations where required and to safeguard technology in support of US national security objectives and foreign policy goals. Soon after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Biden officials pushed SpaceX to expand Starlink use for the Ukrainian military. In a separate instance, the Biden administration asked SpaceX to turn on Starlink in Iran during a protest movement launched in September 2022 after the death of Mahsa Amini, an Iranian arrested for not wearing her hijab properly. Starlink terminals were moved into the country with the help of the administration. The Trump administration took a similar step earlier this year and discussed with Musk and SpaceX getting Starlink terminals into Iran and activated following US-Israeli strikes in the country in June 2025 and again in February 2026. Neither the Trump administration nor SpaceX has publicly confirmed that those terminals were turned on. The Wall Street Journal reported in February that the US government had sent thousands of Starlink terminals into Iran.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n马斯克的“星链”到底有什么用？为什么一打仗，全世界都在抢它？ 消息人士称：多年来，美国政府一直要求马斯克调整星链在乌克兰及中东的服务 早在本月乌克兰军方使用马斯克卫星通信系统的权限遭限速之前，拜登与川总两届政府就曾多次要求SpaceX 在多个国家开启或关闭星链服务——这是六位知情的前美国高级官员的说法。拜登任内，此类请求既包括关闭服务，以阻止伊朗的代理武装在也门和黎巴嫩利用星链联络；也包括开通服务，为伊朗境内及乌克兰提供网络接入，以支持乌军作战。 无独有偶，川总政府也曾以同样的方式要求SpaceX 首席执行官马斯克，在2025年和2026年美以对伊朗实施军事打击之后，向伊朗开放星链服务，以期加强该国反政府抗议者的网络接入与通信能力。而近期围绕乌克兰使用SpaceX 卫星服务所受限制而引爆的那场风波，则让人不得不质疑美国政府与马斯克之间的关系——他旗下的公司，掌握着对美军及其盟友而言性命攸关的系统。 拜登时代与川总向马斯克提出的这些请求，折射出一种密切关系：在这种关系之中，美国历届政府对一家私营企业的倚重，达到了前所未有的程度——倚重它来帮助落实外交政策。而尽管身为美国盟友，乌克兰近期所受到的限制，同样令欧洲官员阵脚大乱；其中一些人主张，欧洲必须发展自己的军用卫星通信能力，因为短期内并不存在任何可行的替代选项。 乌克兰总统泽连斯基上周表示，乌克兰对“星盾”的使用权限遭到有意限制——“星盾”即星链系统的军用版本——这一限制干扰了该国军用无人机的作战表现。他还表示，星链的速度同样遭到下调；而星链被乌方用于部分军事行动，其中包括拦截俄方导弹袭击的防御作战。 白宫在被请求置评时，将路透社引向了川总上周五的一番表态——当时他表示，自己并未就乌克兰使用星链受限一事与马斯克交谈，并称“如果有问题，我会把它理顺的”。SpaceX 未回应多次置评请求，不过马斯克本周早些时候在社交媒体上发帖称：“星盾系统由美国政府拥有并控制。任何关于速度或用途的限制均由美军决定，并最终（由美国总统）决定。” 对乌克兰而言，星链互联网服务已成为前线各部队之间通信的命脉——这些部队表示，目前尚无任何产品在性能上能与SpaceX 相抗衡。而星盾的接收设备基本不受无线电干扰，它既被用作机载制导系统，也为乌克兰无人机与后方操作员之间提供通信链路；这些无人机的活动范围，甚至深入乌克兰境内俄军战线的后方地带。 马斯克作为一家对美国国家安全有着超乎本分之影响力的公司的强势CEO，这一身份不时引发外界质疑：究竟是谁在决定SpaceX的卫星服务。拜登政府在某些事务上曾倚重马斯克协助落实政策。拜登政府官员与SpaceX进行协调，一方面通过切断服务挫败美国的对手，另一方面通过扩大星链的覆盖范围来帮助盟友。这些前官员还补充说，政府与SpaceX之间的讨论，焦点往往集中在阻止也门的胡塞武装、黎巴嫩的真主党等组织使用星链终端上。 2023年10月，在以色列与黎巴嫩南部真主党武装人员交战期间，情报官员告知白宫，与真主党有关联的一些个人可能已经获得了星链终端。2024年，在针对这个与伊朗有关联的组织实施的空中战役期间，官员们对也门的胡塞武装也有类似担忧。在这两起事件中，政府均联系了SpaceX，并要求其锁定相关终端并将其关停。至少有一部分终端被停用了。 在2024 年提交给美国商务部产业与安全局的一份意见书中，SpaceX 表示，它与美国政府机构密切合作，以便在必要时取得出口许可，并守护相关技术，服务于美国的国家安全目标与外交政策目标。 2022 年俄罗斯全面入侵乌克兰后不久，拜登政府官员便推动SpaceX 扩大乌军对星链的使用。另一起事件中，拜登政府于2022 年9 月玛莎·阿米尼身亡后爆发的抗议运动期间，要求SpaceX 在伊朗开通星链；相关终端在政府协助下被运入该国。 今年早些时候，特朗普政府采取了类似行动，并与马斯克及SpaceX 商讨，在2025 年6 月及2026 年2 月美以对伊朗实施军事打击后，将星链终端运入伊朗并予以开通。特朗普政府与SpaceX 均未公开证实这些终端已开通。《华尔街日报》今年2 月报道称，美国政府已向伊朗境内运送了数千台星链终端。","2026-10-25_001":"China has captured the market for hobbyist 3D printers — 2026/10/25 China has captured the market for hobbyist 3D printers The fastest-growing industrial product in China today is not the electric vehicle, lithium-ion battery or even humanoid robot. It is the humble 3D printer for hobbyists. Once geeky curios, 3D printers have quietly become one of China's most successful consumer electronics. Exports of the machines grew by 90% in the first half of this year. In the past decade China's global market share has risen from 19% to 96%, ending the dominance of Western firms. The personal 3D-printing market is now split among the \"four little dragons of Shenzhen\", says Chris Connery of Context, a London-based market-intelligence firm. Creality, Anycubic, Elegoo and the industry's leader, Bambu Lab, reign supreme globally (see chart). Their success shows, yet again, how fierce competition in China combined with generous government support can help improve quality and drive down export prices, making Chinese products hard to beat. The printers work by building objects layer by layer, usually by extruding molten plastic. Invented by Chuck Hull, an American, in 1983, they long remained expensive, unreliable and difficult to use. Today they routinely sell for a little more than $500 and have the sleek aluminium finish of an Apple computer. Comparable entry-level printers from Western firms start at more than double the price. Artificial intelligence allows users to create print designs for such things as tools, parts or toys by describing them in natural language rather than using complicated design-software. Just as Chinese factories helped to turn drones into recreational devices (as well as game-changers in warfare) by achieving large-scale production at low cost, so they are now doing with 3D printers. For most of its history, consumer 3D printing had an unspoken cost of entry: patience. Getting a printer to actually work often took longer than using it. Bed leveling, calibration, and trial and error troubleshooting weren't occasional annoyances; they were simply part of owning one. That was the deal, and for years, nobody in the industry seriously challenged it. Part of Bambu Lab's different approach can be traced back to its founding team. Several of its engineers came from DJI, the drone manufacturer, with backgrounds in robotics and consumer electronics. Drones present a similar design challenge to 3D printers: they're mechanically complex, but successful consumer products need to be usable without specialized training. Bambu Lab's founders had already worked through that kind of problem before applying it to printing. The company also didn't rush its first product. It spent 22 months on research and development before releasing its first printer, the X1, which launched on Kickstarter and raised about HK$55 million, or roughly $7 million, from 5,575 backers. The core of Bambu Lab's approach is automating steps that previously required manual adjustment. Its printers include cameras, LiDAR, and gravity sensors that handle bed leveling and calibration automatically on every print, without requiring the user to understand the underlying mechanics. LatePost described the result as \"a small AI-enabled robot.\" One Bambu Lab owner on Reddit summarized the difference simply: \"Bambu printers just work.\" Price wars have eaten into the printer dragons' profits even as sales boom. But the firms still excite investors. Bambu Lab was founded in 2020 by alumni of another Chinese company, DJI, the world's leading dronemaker. The printer-maker is reported to have a private valuation of about $10bn. Last year DJI itself invested in Elegoo, a Bambu rival. Creality listed on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange in May. It boasts a market capitalisation of HK$12bn ($1.5bn). The government cherishes 3D-printing firms. The business featured in its \"Made in China 2025\" industrial plan, which was launched in 2015 to ensure domestic strength and self-reliance in high-tech manufacturing (much to the West's concern). Officials see 3D printers as a way to produce components of aerospace, health care and military equipment, for which China often relies on Western suppliers. Policymakers have directed research institutes and state-run firms to buy them. Some competitors say Chinese firms have profited from Western open-source printing software. Last month an American court ruled that Bambu Lab had infringed on four patents held by an American company, and ordered it to pay $27.6m in damages. Although Chinese firms have captured the consumer market, they have struggled to do so for industrial 3D printers. Firms are wary of relying on Chinese products if it involves feeding them with sensitive designs (though Britain's defence ministry has been using Bambu printers, its government admits). Outside China, top-of-the-range machines are still mostly supplied by American and European outfits. They can sell for millions of dollars. Undeterred, Chinese suppliers are creating a new form of manufacturing at home and abroad. They are pioneering \"3D-printing farms\"—facilities with as many as 5,000 desktop 3D printers that operate like factories. Several have been built in China this year. Printing farms can mass-produce trinkets for zany consumer trends in short order, replacing expensive and slow injection-moulding techniques. Like the printers themselves, their output will be higher quality and cheaper than what came before.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n大疆之后，又来了“四小龙”：中国为什么连 3D 打印机也卷到全球第一？ 中国已占领业余爱好者 3D 打印机市场 如今，中国增长最快的工业产品不是电动汽车、锂离子电池，甚至也不是人形机器人，而是看似不起眼、面向业余爱好者的3D打印机。3D打印机曾经只是科技极客眼中的新奇玩意儿，如今却悄然成为中国最成功的消费电子产品之一。今年上半年，这类设备的出口增长了90%。过去十年间，中国在全球市场的份额从19%攀升至96%，终结了西方企业的主导地位。 伦敦市场情报公司Context的克里斯·康纳里表示，如今个人3D打印市场已被“深圳四小龙”瓜分。创想三维（Creality）、纵维立方（Anycubic）、智能派（Elegoo），以及行业领军企业拓竹科技（Bambu Lab），在全球市场占据着绝对优势（见图表）。它们的成功再次表明，中国国内的激烈竞争，加上政府的大力支持，能够推动产品质量提升、出口价格下降，使中国产品极具竞争力，难有对手。 3D打印机通过逐层堆叠材料来制造物体，通常是将熔融塑料挤出成形。3D打印机由美国人查克·赫尔于1983年发明，但在很长一段时间里，这类设备一直价格昂贵、性能不可靠，而且操作困难。如今，它们的售价通常只比500美元略高，外观则采用了类似苹果电脑的光滑铝制饰面。西方企业生产的同类入门级打印机，起售价却是这一价格的两倍多。人工智能让用户不必使用复杂的设计软件，只需用自然语言描述需求，就能为工具、零部件或玩具等物品创建打印设计。正如中国工厂曾通过低成本的大规模生产，将无人机变成大众娱乐设备（同时也使其成为改变战争格局的利器），如今它们正以同样的方式推动3D打印机的普及。 在消费级3D打印技术发展的绝大部分历史中，进入这一领域都有一项不言而喻的门槛：耐心。让一台打印机真正正常运转，往往比实际使用它还要费时间。打印平台调平、校准，以及反复试错的故障排查，并不是偶尔出现的小麻烦，而是拥有一台3D打印机就必须接受的日常现实。这就是当时行业默认的规则，多年来几乎没有人认真尝试改变它。拓竹科技之所以采取不同的产品设计思路，部分原因可以追溯到它的创始团队。团队中的几位工程师来自无人机制造商大疆，拥有机器人技术和消费电子领域的工作背景。无人机和3D打印机面临着类似的设计难题：它们的机械结构都十分复杂，但要成为成功的消费产品，就必须让没有专业训练的普通人也能轻松使用。拓竹科技的创始人在将这一理念应用于3D打印之前，就已经解决过类似的问题。 这家公司也没有急于推出自己的第一款产品。在发布首款打印机X1之前，拓竹科技花了22个月进行研发。X1随后在众筹平台Kickstarter上推出，获得了5575名支持者的资助，共筹集约5500万港元（约合700万美元）。拓竹科技产品理念的核心，是将过去需要人工调整的操作步骤自动化。它的打印机配备了摄像头、激光雷达和重力传感器，能够在每次打印时自动完成打印平台调平和校准，而不需要用户理解背后的机械原理。《晚点》将其形容为“一台搭载人工智能的小型机器人”。Reddit上一位拓竹科技的用户则用一句简单的话概括了这种区别：“拓竹打印机就是能直接用。” 尽管销量蓬勃增长，价格战却不断侵蚀着这些3D打印机“四小龙”的利润。但这些企业依然令投资者兴奋不已。拓竹科技于2020年由中国另一家企业——全球领先的无人机制造商大疆的前员工创立。据报道，这家3D打印机制造商的非上市公司估值约为100亿美元。去年，大疆自身也投资了拓竹的竞争对手智能派（Elegoo）。创想三维（Creality）于今年5月在香港证券交易所上市，目前市值高达120亿港元（约合15亿美元）。 中国政府十分重视3D打印企业。3D打印产业被纳入了2015年推出的“中国制造2025”产业规划。这项规划旨在增强中国国内高科技制造业的实力与自主能力（这也引发了西方国家的深切担忧）。政府官员认为，3D打印机可以用于生产航空航天、医疗保健和军事装备的零部件，而在这些领域，中国往往依赖西方供应商。政策制定者已经要求科研机构和国有企业采购3D打印机。 一些竞争对手认为，中国企业从西方的开源3D打印软件中获益。上个月，一家美国法院裁定，拓竹科技侵犯了一家美国公司持有的四项专利，并判令其支付2760万美元的损害赔偿金。尽管中国企业已经占领了消费级3D打印机市场，但在工业级3D打印机领域，它们仍然难以取得同样的成功。如果使用中国设备意味着需要向其输入敏感的设计资料，企业就会对依赖这些产品心存顾虑（尽管英国政府承认，其国防部一直在使用拓竹打印机）。在中国以外的市场，最高端的3D打印设备仍主要由美国和欧洲企业供应。这些设备的售价可高达数百万美元。 中国供应商并未因此退缩，而是正在国内外开创一种全新的制造模式。它们率先探索“3D打印农场”——这是一种拥有多达5000台桌面3D打印机、像工厂一样运转的生产设施。今年，中国已经建成了数座这样的打印农场。3D打印农场能够在短时间内大规模生产迎合各种古怪、奇特消费潮流的小饰品和新奇玩意儿，从而取代成本高昂、生产周期漫长的注塑成型技术。与3D打印机本身一样，这些打印农场生产出来的产品也将比以往质量更好、价格更低。","2026-10-24_001":"Singapore goes after companies over toxic haze — 2026/10/24 Singapore goes after companies over toxic haze Singapore is trying to hold companies to account as it grapples with its worst air pollution in a decade, but the smog-choked city-state is running into the limits of prosecuting cross-border environmental laws. The government has in recent days threatened legal action against 10 businesses, several with deep ties to Singapore, that it says are behind wildfires in neighbouring Indonesia that have produced the haze. It has also raised questions about whether other companies listed on the local bourse may be involved, and has threatened to target company profits, access to financing and standing with investors. \"Singapore — which wants to continue being an international finance centre — gets a lot of business from these companies,\" said Ivan Png at the National University of Singapore, who has campaigned for anti-haze legislation. He added that the companies \"depend on Singapore for equity and financing — you would think that would make them want to pay attention to what the government is saying\". The issue has also threatened a rift with Indonesia, where the fires are blamed on slash-and-burn practices by paper and palm oil producers. This year's fires have been exacerbated by a \"super\" El Niño, a natural weather phenomenon that has raised temperatures and reduced rainfall across Indonesia. Singapore's air quality has been classified by the government as \"very unhealthy\" this month, at times ranking the worst in the world, according to monitoring platform IQAir. Outdoor activities and sports events have been cancelled and hospital admissions for respiratory complaints have surged. The smog cast a pall over this month's Singapore Grand Prix. Public frustration is mounting with what has become a regular nuisance in the city-state that generally prides itself on effective governance. Singapore has taken steps to try to bring companies suspected of field burning — which is also illegal in Indonesia — to book. In 2014, it introduced a Transboundary Haze Pollution Act, which empowered the government to pursue criminal charges against companies found to have contributed to haze-producing fires and levy fines as high as S$100,000 (US$78,000) a day, capped at S$2mn (US$1.6mn) per conviction. But initial attempts to enforce the law stalled, with the environment ministry complaining that its Indonesian counterpart did not provide the necessary information to pursue charges. No company has been fined under the law. This year, Singapore has vowed to toughen its stance. In recent days, the government has served legal notices on 10 companies — identified using maps, meteorological data and satellite imagery — to demand they stop burning. Three of the businesses were served similar notices in 2015. \"If companies refuse to co-operate, regardless of whether they have a presence in Singapore... we will use every legal and regulatory lever at our disposal to exert pressure where it matters.\" At least four of the 10 companies are suppliers to Asia Pulp and Paper, which is headquartered in Singapore and part of Indonesian conglomerate Sinar Mas. Two others are run by permanent Singapore residents. Shanmugam said the government would investigate APP and several other companies listed in Singapore, where many Indonesian conglomerates access global capital. \"Some of the fires or smoke-source points seem to be associated with Singapore-listed First Resources and Bumitama Agri, as well as First Borneo Plantations,\" he said. \"We will be asking more questions of these companies.\" Shanmugam added that banks that lent to the businesses had also questioned them, and the Monetary Authority of Singapore, the city-state's financial regulator, would track lenders' risk exposure. First Resources said it welcomed the investigation, adding it was \"confident that an objective review of the available evidence will prove that it does not engage in any land clearing by fire, whether directly or indirectly\". APP, Bumitama Agri and First Borneo Plantations did not respond to requests for comment. SGX, the local bourse, has separately asked seven listed companies — including Golden-Agri Resources, Wilmar and Kencana Agri — about possible links to the haze. Indonesia's police chief said this week that authorities had named 11 corporations, three of them foreign, as suspects in connection with the fires. \"Some countries are mocking, insulting us... when many of their companies [operating] here are responsible,\" Indonesia's President Prabowo Subianto said in a speech on Thursday, adding: \"We will announce those names when the time comes.\" He did not identify the countries. Indonesia's government communication agency said: \"Indonesia and Singapore maintain channels of engagement on environmental issues, including transboundary pollution.\" It added that it prioritises \"the protection of affected communities, the containment of fires, and effective enforcement of environmental regulations\". NUS professor Png called for stronger laws to provide an incentive to whistleblowers, pointing to successful efforts in the US to combat bribery under the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act. \"It is very hard to collect evidence,\" he said. \"But when you offer incentives, whistleblowers will come out.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n新加坡追查涉事企业，剑指跨境“毒烟霾” 新加坡追查涉事企业，剑指跨境“毒烟霾”——被烟霾呛得喘不过气的新加坡，正威胁对印尼境内烧荒的幕后企业出手。 新加坡一边与十年来最严重的空气污染缠斗，一边试图将涉事企业绳之以法；然而这座被烟霾呛住喉咙的城邦，在跨境环境违法的起诉之路上，正撞上一道无形的天花板。近日，新加坡政府已威胁对十家企业采取法律行动——其中数家与新加坡渊源颇深。政府称，正是这些企业酿成了邻国印尼的失控野火，进而“生产”出了这场烟霾。此外，政府还对本国交易所上市的其他企业是否牵涉其中提出质疑，并扬言要对这些公司的利润、融资渠道以及在投资者心目中的地位下手。 “新加坡——这个一心想保住国际金融中心地位的国家——从这些公司手里可是赚了不少生意。”新加坡国立大学的 Ivan Png 教授说。他长期为反烟霾立法奔走呼吁。他补充道，这些公司的股权融资和资金来源都仰仗新加坡，“按理说，这总该让他们更在乎政府在说些什么吧”。 此事更令新加坡与印尼之间生出嫌隙之虞——在印尼，这些火灾被普遍归咎于造纸与棕榈油生产商“刀耕火种”的清地做法。而今年的火情，又因一场“超级厄尔尼诺”雪上加霜：这一自然气候现象推高了印尼全境的气温、削减了降水。 本月，新加坡的空气质量被政府正式定为“非常不健康”级，按监测平台 IQAir 的数据，其污染程度一度位居全球之最。户外活动与体育赛事被迫取消，因呼吸道病症入院的人数急剧攀升。这场烟霾，为当月的新加坡大奖赛蒙上了一层挥之不去的阴云。在这个素以高效治理自矜的城邦，民众对这已成为常态的烦忧，怨气正越积越深。 新加坡已着手行动，试图将涉嫌田间焚烧的企业绳之以法——此类做法在印尼本土同样违法。2014 年，新加坡出台《跨境烟霾污染法》，授权政府对被查明曾酿成烟霾火灾的企业提起刑事指控，并课以每日最高 10 万新元（7.8 万美元）的罚款，每次定罪总额封顶 200 万新元（160 万美元）。然而，最初的执法尝试很快陷入停滞——环境部抱怨称，印尼对口部门始终未提供提起指控所必需的信息。至今，尚无一家企业依据该法被处以罚款。 今年，新加坡已立誓采取更强硬的姿态。近日，政府向十家企业送达了法律通知——这些企业是借助地图、气象数据与卫星影像锁定的——勒令其即刻停止焚烧。其中三家企业，早在 2015 年便已收到过同样的通知。“倘若企业拒不配合，无论其在新加坡是否设有实体，我们都将动用手中一切法律与监管杠杆，在真正的要害之处施压。” 在这十家企业中，至少有四家是亚洲浆纸（APP）的供货商——APP 总部设于新加坡，隶属印尼金光集团旗下。另有两家企业则由新加坡永久居民经营。Shanmugam 表示，政府将对 APP 及数家在新加坡上市的企业展开调查——众多印尼财团正是借此地获取全球资本。“部分火点与烟源似乎与新加坡上市的 First Resources、Bumitama Agri 以及 First Borneo Plantations 存在关联，”他说，“我们将向这些企业提出更多质询。”他补充道，向这些企业放贷的银行也已对其提出问询；而作为本城邦金融监管机构的新加坡金融管理局，将追踪各放贷方的风险敞口。 First Resources 表示欢迎此次调查，并称相信对现有证据的客观审查将证明，公司“不曾以焚烧方式清理任何土地，无论直接或间接”。APP、Bumitama Agri 与 First Borneo Plantations 则未回应置评请求。此外，本地交易所新交所已另行向七家上市公司——其中包括金光农业资源、丰益国际与 Kencana Agri——问询其是否与本次烟霾存在关联。 印尼警察总长本周表示，当局已就此次火灾将十一家企业列为嫌疑方，其中三家为外资企业。印尼总统普拉博沃·苏比安托周四在一场演讲中称：“有些国家在嘲讽我们、羞辱我们……可它们在本国经营的众多企业，才是真正的责任方。”他并补充道：“我们会在适当的时候公布这些名字。”但他并未指明是哪些国家。 印尼政府新闻机构表示：“印尼与新加坡在环境议题上——包括跨境污染问题——保持着沟通渠道。”该机构并补充称，其优先事项是“保护受影响社区、控制火势蔓延，以及有效执行环境法规”。新加坡国立大学的 Png 教授呼吁制定更为强硬的法律，为举报人设立激励机制，并援引美国依据《反海外腐败法》打击海外行贿的成效作为例证。“取证实在太难了，”他说，“但只要你肯给出激励，吹哨人就会站出来。”","2026-10-23_001":"How a Sydney professor built an AI army to scam the scammers — 2026/10/23 How a Sydney professor built an AI army to scam the scammers The day that Dali Kaafar decided to start a company that unleashes an autonomous army of AI bots to take on the trillion-dollar global scam industry began like any other. It was a warm Saturday afternoon in spring. The Macquarie University professor of cybersecurity was enjoying a picnic with his wife and two children at a Sydney park. At some point, his mobile rang. Kaafar could tell from the number that it was a scam call, and while he usually ignored them, on this particular day, Kaafar decided to have some fun with the fraudsters. He picked up the call. \"The kids were a few years younger then, and for their entertainment, I pretended not to understand what the scammers were after, or I would give them the wrong details,\" he says. The scammers persisted, insisting that there was something wrong with Kaafar's internet and they needed remote access to his computer. Kaafar feigned incomprehension when they spoke about his tepid upload and download speeds. He distracted and delayed them: what was this NBN they spoke of? \"At one point, I think I was even trying to order a pizza,\" he says. \"As I hung up that call I remember very clearly saying, 'this was all great fun, but imagine what technology can do with these scammers at scale,'\" he says. While his children were in hysterics, his wife was less impressed. \"My wife said, 'those are 44 minutes of your own life that you will never get back.' You ruined the whole picnic experience. I said, 'That's even more reason for technology to jump in to do it better.'\" The following Monday, Kaafar called together a cohort of PhD students and a postdoctoral researcher with expertise in AI security and natural-language processing. They would build voice bots to ape the tech insecurities of the average caller: umm-ing and ahh-ing their way through conversations with scammers and apologising for not being very good with technology or computers. \"But then we all immediately realised that by keeping the scammers on the line we can also extract and deliver this real-time intelligence, actionable intelligence, to stay on top of what scammers are doing.\" In 2024, Kaafar founded Apate.ai, named after the Greek goddess of deception and fraud. Apate.ai deploys thousands of digital decoy identities across phone, email, messaging apps and social platforms, designed to be discovered directly by scammers searching for victims. \"We are flipping the script on the economics of fraud by beating these bad actors at their own game.\" Crucial to the success of the fleet of Apate.ai bots is their believability: whether texting or talking, Apate.ai agents must resemble an actual person – rather than a minutely programmed chatbot – to keep scammers on the line. The agents have phone numbers and WhatsApp accounts; they have personalities, PayIDs and pet peeves. Some have distinctive accents; others employ broad Australian slang (\"no worries\", they may say or \"let me just get a cuppa\"). \"We used to give them cute names, like Ricky and Pete, and we each had our favourite. But as we evolved to more than 200,000 bots, now they just have ID numbers,\" says Kaafar. Each bot operates with a defined mission, which may include keeping a scammer talking for as long as possible, extracting crucial intelligence about the scammers' operations, or manoeuvring the fraudster onto another platform where the interaction can continue. The mission may result in gathering details of scammer impersonation tactics, phishing URLs, mule bank accounts and crypto wallets – crucial information to help authorities shut down these networks. It's not unusual for Apate.ai voice bots to keep scammers on the line for more than two hours, with the record at two hours and 12 minutes at the time of our interview. \"Literally a scammer is busy talking to a bot is a scammer that is not placing thousands of calls a minute to other potential victims,\" says Kaafar. Occasionally, scammers end the call because they cotton on that they are talking to a bot. The realisation that a bot has been wasting the scammer's time usually results in a slew of profanities. \"I think we're the only company around that has a KPI linked to f-words,\" he says. Kaafar is keen to emphasise that while delaying and obfuscating scammers is deeply satisfying, it offers benefits beyond merely blocking calls. \"What we're trying to do is distract them and disrupt them by presenting them with the perfect victim. Then we make it really painful for them where exactly it matters for them, which is how much money they'll be making. \"We want to put the foot on the gas,\" he says.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n让20万个AI去接诈骗电话：终于有人把AI用在了“正地方” 悉尼教授如何打造一支 AI 大军，反过来戏耍诈骗犯 达利·卡法尔决定创办一家公司的那一天，起初与平常的日子并无两样。而这家公司将释放出一支能够自主行动的AI机器人大军，向规模高达万亿美元的全球诈骗产业发起挑战。那是春天一个温暖的星期六下午。这位麦考瑞大学的网络安全教授正和妻子及两个孩子在悉尼的一座公园里野餐。就在这时，他的手机响了。卡法尔一看号码就知道这是一通诈骗电话。虽然他平时总是直接忽略这类电话，但偏偏在这一天，他决定拿这些骗子找点乐子。于是，他接起了电话。 “那时候孩子们还小几岁，为了逗他们开心，我就假装听不懂骗子到底想要什么，或者故意提供错误的信息。”他说。骗子们却不依不饶，坚持声称卡法尔的网络出了问题，他们需要远程访问他的电脑。当骗子提到他的上传和下载速度不太理想时，卡法尔又假装完全听不懂。他不断转移对方的注意力，拖延时间：他们说的这个NBN到底是什么东西？“有那么一会儿，我记得自己甚至还试着点了一份披萨。”他说。 “挂断电话的时候，我清楚地记得自己说过，‘刚才确实很好玩，但想象一下，如果利用技术大规模地对付这些骗子，会发生什么？’”他说。孩子们已经笑得前仰后合，但他的妻子却没那么高兴。“我妻子说，‘那可是你人生中永远也拿不回来的44分钟。你把整个野餐都毁了。’我说，‘那就更说明应该让技术介入，把这件事做得更好。’” 接下来的星期一，卡法尔召集了一批博士生和一名博士后研究人员，他们都拥有人工智能安全和自然语言处理方面的专业知识。他们计划开发语音机器人，模仿普通人在面对科技问题时那种不自信的样子：与骗子交谈时不停地“嗯……啊……”，吞吞吐吐，还会为自己不太懂技术或电脑而道歉。“但随后我们立刻意识到，只要让诈骗犯一直保持通话，我们就能从中提取并提供实时情报，而且是能够直接采取行动的情报，从而及时掌握诈骗犯的最新动向。”2024年，卡法尔创立了Apate.ai，公司以希腊神话中掌管欺骗与诈骗的女神阿帕忒命名。 Apate.ai在电话、电子邮件、即时通讯应用和社交平台上部署了数以千计的数字诱饵身份。这些虚拟身份经过专门设计，就是为了让正在寻找受害者的诈骗犯主动发现它们。“我们正在通过以其人之道还治其人之身的方式，彻底扭转诈骗行业的经济逻辑。” Apate.ai机器人大军能否成功，关键就在于它们是否足够逼真：无论是发短信还是打电话，这些AI智能体都必须像真正的人一样，而不是那种每一步都经过精细编程的聊天机器人，才能让诈骗犯一直保持通话。这些智能体拥有电话号码和WhatsApp账号；它们有各自的性格、PayID支付标识，甚至还有各自特别讨厌的小事。有些机器人带着鲜明的口音，另一些则会使用非常地道的澳大利亚俚语，比如说“没事儿”，或者“让我先去喝杯茶”。“以前我们还会给它们起一些可爱的名字，比如Ricky和Pete，每个人都有自己最喜欢的机器人。但随着机器人数量增长到20多万个，现在它们只有编号了。”卡法尔说。 每个机器人都有一项明确的任务，可能是尽可能长时间地让诈骗犯保持通话，也可能是提取有关诈骗团伙运作方式的重要情报，或者巧妙地引导诈骗犯转移到另一个平台，让双方继续互动。执行任务的过程中，机器人可能收集到诈骗犯冒充他人的手法、网络钓鱼链接、用于转移赃款的银行账户以及加密货币钱包等详细信息。这些都是帮助执法机构摧毁诈骗网络的关键情报。 Apate.ai的语音机器人让诈骗犯在电话里耗上两个多小时，并不是什么稀奇事。截至采访时，最长通话纪录已经达到2小时12分钟。卡法尔说：“说白了，一个忙着跟机器人聊天的诈骗犯，就不可能同时每分钟向其他潜在受害者拨打成千上万个电话。”偶尔，也会有诈骗犯突然挂断电话，因为他们终于意识到自己正在和一个机器人说话。而当骗子发现，自己竟然被机器人白白浪费了这么长时间时，通常就会爆出一连串脏话。“我想，我们可能是唯一一家把脏话数量和关键绩效指标挂钩的公司。”他说。 卡法尔特别希望强调，虽然拖住诈骗犯、把他们弄得晕头转向确实让人非常解气，但这项技术带来的好处远不止拦截诈骗电话那么简单。“我们想做的，就是给他们送上一个看似完美的受害者，以此分散他们的注意力，打乱他们的诈骗行动。然后，我们要在他们最在乎的地方让他们真正感到痛苦，那就是他们到底还能赚到多少钱。”“我们想要继续踩下油门，加快行动。”他说。","2026-10-22_001":"Musk's Promise to Give SpaceX Stock to Kids Is No Gift — 2026/10/22 Musk's Promise to Give SpaceX Stock to Kids Is No Gift One of the great joys, but also miseries, of following business news over the past decade or so has been riding the dodgy roller coaster of the Elon Musk experience. He has gone from a guy people considered a real-life Tony Stark to something more like a modern-day Henry Ford, in the worst possible way, with countless gut-wrenching twists in between. At no point, however, did anybody think: \"Hey, you know who's missing out on this? Children.\" Until today. SpaceX Chief Executive Officer Elon Musk pledged on Monday to donate one share of his company to each of America's 72 million children, vowing it would be the first such gift \"of many,\" if \"you had just this morning traveled here from another planet, then you might consider this a reasonably generous gesture from a near-trillionaire.\" The stock has been a bit stagnant since its June IPO, sure, but the company has billions of dollars in US government contracts, and Musk has promised its revenue will soar from less than $19 billion last year to $1 trillion by 2030. Such a gift would barely trouble Musk's vast wealth and power. It would cost about $10.5 billion, shaving Musk's net worth to $889.5 billion. And as my Bloomberg Opinion colleague Liam Denning points out, it would cut his SpaceX voting power, currently at 82% because of the stock's dual-class nature, by just 0.1 percentage points. But even a passing familiarity with Musk's whole vibe should raise many immediate concerns about this \"promise.\" For one thing, it was made on his social media app, X, formerly known as Twitter, on which Musk has promised many things, a large percentage of which have never happened. Most famous was his 2018 declaration that he had \"funding secured\" to take Tesla Inc. private at $420 a share. It got him in serious legal trouble when it turned out not to be true. If you have several hours to kill, you can peruse this website for a long list of other unfulfilled Musk promises, including a \"cross country summon\" feature that would let your Tesla travel the continent by itself, incredible-Journey style to find you; half-hour rocket trips from New York to London; 1 million robotaxis; and seafaring Teslas, to name a few. Buying stock in Musk is less an investment than it is subscribing to a streaming service for increasingly ridiculous science fiction. Also, it doesn't seem as if Musk spent long hours conferring with lawyers and accountants planning for this gift. Rather, the timing and Musk's track record suggest he saw SpaceX's AI rival, Anthropic PBC, making its own charitable pledge on Sunday and felt the urge to one-up it. The maker of Claude vowed to donate 5% of its shares, potentially worth $75 billion (if the company is worth $1.5 trillion), to a fund that will benefit the \"public good,\" including AI's destruction of jobs that kids want. Musk replied to Anthropic's X post making this announcement with a curt \"I recommend giving the shares directly to the public,\" suggesting he's just better at doing charity. His detail-free promise of one share per child followed hours later. How will children receive these shares? Nobody knows, and the odds are very good that nobody will ever have to find out. Anthropic's offer seems to be an olive branch to AI's large and growing contingent of haters and skeptics, including most of the American public, ahead of its IPO. Maybe it will also deflect some of the pressure to impose actual government regulation of the technology. Musk could use some good publicity, too, and not just because SpaceX is a growing AI giant. He has embraced White nationalism and let SpaceX's Starlink service undermine Ukraine's effort to defend itself from Russia's invasion. He manned the Department of Government Efficiency that slashed government jobs and offices without care for the repercussions, including the 700,000 people who have already died because of DOGE's assault on USAID. His behavior affects everyone invested in his companies, for better or worse, including those theoretical 72 million children. For a long time, \"worse\" has been a no-show; his fans have helped Tesla's stock price defy traditional market logic for years. How long can the magic last? As tough as children have it these days, with the climate in chaos, war in Europe and the Middle East and AI threatening to take all of the jobs, do they really need the headache of finding out?\n⟪中文译文⟫\n马斯克突然要给7200万美国孩子每人送1股SpaceX：他到底想干什么？ 马斯克放话要给孩子们送 SpaceX 股票——名为馈赠，实非善礼。 过去十年，追踪商业新闻，最大的乐趣与最大的折磨，是同一件事——坐了一趟“马斯克体验”的野过山车。他从人人仰望的“现实版钢铁侠”，一路坐成了“现代版亨利·福特”，而且是福特最不堪的那种翻版，中间数不清的揪心弯道自不必说。可这么多年来，从没有人想过：“哎，这趟热闹里，谁还没被带上车？孩子们啊。”——直到今天。 SpaceX 首席执行官埃隆·马斯克周一承诺，将向全美7200 万名儿童每人捐赠一股公司股票，并宣称这不过是“诸多馈赠”中的第一份。倘若你是今晨才从外星球飞抵地球，你或许会以为，这是一位准万亿富翁相当慷慨的姿态。没错，公司股票自六月上市以来确实有些趴窝，但这家企业手里握着数十亿美元的美国政府合同，而马斯克更是放话：营收将从去年的不足190 亿美元，一路飙升至2030 年的1 万亿美元。 这样的一份“厚礼”，几乎不会动摇马斯克庞大的财富与权力。它要花掉大约105 亿美元，把他的净资产从九千亿刮到8895 亿——仅此而已。而正如我在彭博观点栏目的同事利亚姆·丹宁所指出的：由于双层股权结构，他目前握有SpaceX 82% 的投票权，这份礼物会把它削掉——仅仅0.1 个百分点。 然而，哪怕只是对马斯克这个人整体画风稍加了解，也足以让人立刻对这份“承诺”生出诸多疑虑。其一，它是发在他自己的社交平台X——也就是昔日的推特——上面的；而在那个平台上，马斯克许下过许多承诺，其中相当大一部分从未兑现。最出名的要数他2018 年的那次宣告：他声称已“落实资金”，将以每股420 美元将特斯拉私有化。后来事实证明并非如此，他因此惹上了不小的法律麻烦。 若你有几个小时的闲暇要打发，不妨去某个网站上细细翻阅——那儿列着一长串马斯克未兑现的承诺：比如“跨州召唤”功能，能让你的特斯拉独自横穿大陆、像《不可思议的旅程》里的三只小动物那样跑来与你相会；比如半小时从纽约飞到伦敦的火箭旅程；比如一百万辆机器人出租车；再比如能出海航行特斯拉——凡此种种，不一而足。买入“马斯克”的股票，与其说是一项投资，不如说是订阅了一项流媒体服务，播的还是一出越来越荒诞的科幻连续剧。 再者，也看不出马斯克曾花上大把时间，与律师、会计细细磋商这份礼物该如何落地。恰恰相反——是这个时机，再加上马斯克一贯的做派，让人觉得：他是看见SpaceX 在AI 领域的对手Anthropic 于周日作出了自己的慈善承诺，这才心头一动，誓要压对方一头。那家Claude 的开发商誓言捐出公司5% 的股份，或值750 亿美元（倘若公司估值达1.5 万亿美元），投入一只旨在增进“公共利益”的基金——而这“公共利益”之中，竟还包括人工智能对孩子们心仪工作的摧毁。 马斯克对Anthropic 那条宣布此事的X 帖子，回了一句简短而生硬的话：“我建议直接把股份交给公众。”——言下之意，他才是更懂慈善的那一个。他那句“每个孩子一股”、全无细节的承诺，几个小时之后便接踵而至。孩子们究竟要如何拿到这些股份？无人知晓；而且极有可能——永远也不会有人需要去弄明白。 Anthropic 此番表示，看起来像是向人工智能那支人数众多、且仍在不断壮大的厌恶者与怀疑者队伍递上了一根橄榄枝——这队伍之中，包括了大部分美国公众——而这一举动，恰在它上市之前。或许，它还能拨开几分压力——那种要求对这项技术施加真正意义上政府监管的压力。 马斯克同样用得着一些正面的名声，而这并不仅仅因为SpaceX 正成长为一家人工智能巨头。依作者之见，他主动拥抱了白人至上主义，并听任SpaceX 旗下“星链”服务削弱乌克兰抵御俄罗斯入侵的努力。他曾在“政府效率部”任职，该部门大幅裁撤政府岗位与机构，全然不计后果——其中包括那七十万条已经逝去的生命，作者认为这与该部门对美国国际开发署的冲击有关。 他的行为，牵动着每一个投身于他旗下公司的人——无论好坏——这其中包括那七千二百万个“仅存在于理论上”的孩子。长久以来，“坏的那一面”始终不曾露面；他的拥趸们，多年来一直托举着特斯拉的股价，使之违背了传统的市场逻辑。这魔法，还能撑多久？如今孩子们的日子本已足够艰难——气候陷入混沌，战火燃于欧洲与中东，人工智能还在威胁着要将所有饭碗一并夺走——他们当真还需要再多一件劳神的麻烦，去弄清这一切吗？","2026-10-21_001":"Paris police issue overnight curfew in 2 areas of French capital to try to prevent further disorder — 2026/10/21 Paris police issue overnight curfew in 2 areas of French capital to try to prevent further disorder PARIS (AP) — Paris police issued an overnight curfew on Monday in two areas of the French capital, after a weekend marred by France's worst rioting in years that targeted city halls, other public buildings and thousands of businesses across the country. The order aims to protect \"people and property from the violence and looting,\" the Paris police statement said. The curfew prohibits anyone from entering or remaining on the Place de la République, where rioting took place Saturday, and the shopping neighborhood of Les Halles, which has been subject to looting. Earlier Monday, Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu had called for \"very limited\" and \"localized\" curfews. In northern France, local authorities imposed a curfew for children and teenagers under age 18 in several cities, including Lille. At least 2,400 people were arrested across the country. Authorities estimated Monday that at least 3,000 businesses were damaged or looted in several cities. Flames engulfed the 19th-century City Hall in Limoges, in central France, causing its roof to collapse and nearly destroying the building. The previous day, at the end of the protest against the rising cost of living, which brought together 5,500 people in Limoges, a group attacked the east wing of the city hall and started a fire. Within three hours, the roof of the historic building, inaugurated in 1883 and modeled after the Hôtel de Ville in Paris, was completely engulfed. \"I was horrified, but despite how violent what I saw was, I couldn't bring myself to leave.\" The day after the arson attack, Anne, a 47-year-old teacher, returned to the site and said she was still \"dumbfounded.\" Marine, 18, a literature student, on the verge of tears, said she felt \"heartbroken.\" \"I don't understand how things got to this point.\" \"Usually, our protests are always peaceful,\" continued Anne, who joined the march at 2 pm. \"There was some tension, we got a bit of tear gas, but I never felt unsafe, and then at 3:30 pm, suddenly, things deteriorated.\" On Sunday evening, a rally to condemn the violence drew 7,000 people. On Monday, cleanup crews and firefighters cleared shattered glass and other debris from the streets of Paris. Ouri Zagoury, owner of a video game shop near Paris' Place de la République, said that he closed the store Saturday afternoon, but then saw looters forcing their way in on surveillance cameras. \"There were so many people coming in and out. And even though I called the police, they said not to intervene, that they would come, but they never did, which is another problem,\" Zagoury said. Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez offered his resignation Sunday. But President Emmanuel Macron refused it \"given the need for continued command of law enforcement in the current context,\" Macron's office said. He instructed Nuñez \"to take all necessary steps to ensure full transparency about the failures in planning and intelligence-gathering that led to the violence.\" Nuñez acknowledged that authorities had \"lost control\" in some major cities on Saturday. \"You can't look shopkeepers in the eye when hundreds of businesses have been looted and say we haven't lost control in some of these major cities,\" Nuñez said. The protests in France began last month at high schools in Paris'working-class northern suburbs, where anger over teacher shortages and shabby facilities evolved into a broader movement. France's extremely strained public finances mean the government has precious little room for broader measures to address protesters'anger over the rising cost of living. The government has so far not committed to a substantial increase in funding for schools. However, it has pledged to send some 3,000 teachers, who are fully qualified but have not yet started work, to fill staffing gaps. The unrest will likely deal a blow to business confidence, which is already fragile due to a fiscal crisis and high energy prices resulting from the Iran war. The minority government is struggling to pass spending cuts in France's deeply divided parliament as part of its 2027 budget. The unrest has weighed on French stocks and the euro and put pressure on French government bonds. \"Le Pen no longer needs to spend any money to campaign,\" Jean-Baptiste Moreau, a former lawmaker from Macron's camp, told Reuters. \"It's not just a clear path ahead of her, it's a six-lane highway.\" Some protesters have already called for a new round of demonstrations on Saturday, October 24. Unions have called for strikes on November 5 to protest budget cuts and support hiking wages. The weekend violence has largely eclipsed the recent high school student protests. Schools are now on a break for two weeks, but there have been reports of protests at some universities. While mayors of France's biggest cities demanded answers from the government over what they say was a lack of preparation, authorities rushed to set up support measures for impacted businesses and bolster security as calm returned to the streets.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n法国骚乱后续：巴黎开始宵禁、2400人被捕，这场残局该怎么收？政府为何没能提前阻止失控？ 巴黎警方在首都两处地区实施夜间宵禁，以防止进一步发生骚乱 巴黎（美联社）——周一，巴黎警方在法国首都的两个地区实施了夜间宵禁。此前的周末，法国爆发了多年来最严重的骚乱，全国各地的市政厅、其他公共建筑以及数千家商铺成为袭击目标，整个周末因此蒙上了阴影。巴黎警方在声明中表示，这项命令旨在保护“民众及其财产免受暴力和抢劫的侵害”。宵禁令禁止任何人进入或逗留在共和国广场以及莱阿勒购物区。前者在周六发生了骚乱，后者则遭到了洗劫。 周一早些时候，法国总理塞巴斯蒂安·勒科尔尼曾呼吁实施“范围非常有限”且“仅限于局部地区”的宵禁。在法国北部，包括里尔在内的多个城市，当地政府对18岁以下的儿童和青少年实施了宵禁。全国至少有2400人被逮捕。有关部门周一估计，多个城市至少有3000家商铺遭到破坏或洗劫。 在法国中部城市利摩日，熊熊烈火吞噬了建于19世纪的市政厅，导致屋顶坍塌，整栋建筑几乎被毁。此前一天，一场反对生活成本上涨的抗议活动在利摩日举行，吸引了5500人参加。活动临近结束时，一群人袭击了市政厅的东翼并纵火。短短三个小时内，这座历史建筑的屋顶便被大火完全吞噬。该建筑于1883年落成，其设计仿照了巴黎市政厅。 “我感到非常恐惧，但尽管眼前的一切如此暴力，我却怎么也无法让自己离开。”纵火袭击发生后的第二天，47岁的教师安妮再次回到现场。她说，自己至今仍然“震惊得说不出话来”。18岁的文学专业学生玛丽娜眼含泪水，表示自己感到“心碎”。“我不明白事情怎么会发展到这一步。”“通常，我们的抗议活动一直都是和平的。”下午2点参加游行的安妮继续说道，“当时现场有些紧张，我们也受到了一点催泪瓦斯的影响，但我从来没有觉得自己不安全。可到了下午3点半，情况突然急转直下。”周日晚上，一场谴责暴力行为的集会吸引了7000人参加。 周一，清洁人员和消防员在巴黎街头清理碎玻璃及其他残骸。乌里·扎古里在巴黎共和国广场附近经营一家电子游戏商店。他说，自己周六下午关了店，但随后通过监控摄像头看到抢劫者强行闯入店内。“当时有太多人进进出出。虽然我报了警，但警方告诉我不要介入，说他们会赶过来，可他们始终没有出现。这又是另一个问题。”扎古里说道。 周日，法国内政部长洛朗·努涅斯提出辞职。但马克龙总统拒绝了他的辞呈。总统办公室表示，这是“考虑到当前局势下，执法部门仍需要持续而统一的指挥”。马克龙指示努涅斯“采取一切必要措施，确保对导致暴力事件发生的行动规划及情报收集工作中的失误进行充分、透明的说明”。努涅斯承认，周六当局在一些主要城市已经“失去了控制”。他说：“当数百家商铺遭到洗劫时，你不可能直视店主的眼睛，却还告诉他们，我们在这些大城市中的一些地方没有失去控制。” 法国的抗议活动始于上个月巴黎北部工人阶级聚居的郊区高中。最初，学生们因教师短缺和学校设施破旧而感到愤怒，随后抗议逐渐演变为一场范围更广的社会运动。法国公共财政已经极度紧张，这意味着政府几乎没有多少财政空间采取更大范围的措施，来回应抗议者对生活成本上涨的不满。到目前为止，政府尚未承诺大幅增加学校经费。不过，政府已承诺调派约3000名已经取得完整任教资格、但尚未正式上岗的教师，以填补师资缺口。 这场社会动荡很可能进一步打击商业信心。而受财政危机以及伊朗战争导致的高能源价格影响，法国的商业信心原本就已经十分脆弱。作为2027年预算案的一部分，法国少数派政府正艰难地试图在严重分裂的议会中推动削减公共开支的措施。骚乱已经对法国股市和欧元造成压力，同时也使法国政府债券承压。来自马克龙阵营的前议员让-巴蒂斯特·莫罗告诉路透社：“勒庞现在甚至不需要再花钱竞选了。摆在她面前的已经不只是一条畅通无阻的道路，而是一条六车道高速公路。” 一些抗议者已经呼吁在10月24日星期六举行新一轮示威活动。工会则号召于11月5日举行罢工，以抗议预算削减，并支持提高工资。周末发生的暴力事件已经在很大程度上掩盖了近期高中生抗议活动原本受到的关注。目前，法国学校正进入为期两周的假期，但已有报道称，部分大学仍然出现了抗议活动。法国一些最大城市的市长要求政府就他们所认为的准备不足作出解释。与此同时，随着街头逐渐恢复平静，当局正加紧为受影响的企业制定援助措施，并加强安全保障。","2026-10-20_001":"法国爆发近年来最严重的街头骚乱震惊世界！为什么法国人总在抗议？ October 17 French protests: Crowds marched through the streets before violence, looting and chaos broke out On Saturday, 800,000 people took to the streets according to organizers, while Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez put the number at 550,000. The same pattern played out in many cities: On one side, a crowd of peaceful protesters; on the other, independent groups determined to clash with the police. While turnout across the country was massive, the protests were also marred by unprecedented property damage, violence and looting. In France, several worlds seemed to have collided over one long day. The roots of the protests stretched back to September, when calls for demonstrations against rising fuel prices began circulating on social media. Meanwhile, high school students were protesting teacher shortages, overcrowded classrooms and poor school conditions. The unrest also revived memories of the Yellow Vest movement, which began in 2018 as a protest against fuel taxes before growing into a broader rebellion against economic inequality and President Emmanuel Macron’s government. Behind these different grievances lay a deeper frustration: many French people felt squeezed by rising living costs, disappointed by public services and increasingly distrustful of political leaders. Students, workers and Yellow Vest veterans saw an opportunity to unite their separate struggles. Responding to a call to action that notably cited the rising cost of living, but also building on the momentum of the high school student protest movement that began in late September, Saturday’s demonstrations aimed to create a “convergence of struggles” between various groups, ranging from “Yellow Vests” to high school students, several professional sectors, left-wing political leaders and trade unions. At exactly 10 am, outside Gare Saint-Lazare train station, one Yellow Vest summed up his twofold ambition for the day: “First, the revolution, but also to sleep in Macron’s bed.” He said this with a mixture of humor and seriousness, going on to denounce capitalism, the bourgeoisie, the police and the cost of living. Everyone in his entourage chimed in relentlessly, listing their demands, frustrations and anger, until their conversation was abruptly cut short when a BRAV-M motorcycle police unit suddenly barged in and descended on the group. Several small autonomous gatherings started forming in the morning. They aimed to head toward the Elysée Palace, the Place de la Concorde or the Opéra – basically, to push as far as possible. There was no official procession, no leader, no truly defined objective, just packs of individuals on the move, light on their feet. “Strike, blockade, wild protest!” mask-clad people shouted as they sprinted toward Avenue de l’Opéra. In Paris, the situation seemed to at least partially slip out of control from the early afternoon, while, at the same time, taking on several different forms. Place de la République, the starting point for the official demonstration was packed to bursting. Some violent and organized protesters began by attacking street furniture, then tried to break into the Republican Guard barracks, set a car on fire and smashed things left, right and center. While it might not exactly have been the much-heralded “convergence of struggles,” the march still brought together people from all walks of life. Lucie and Thomas, who both just graduated from the prestigious HEC business school, proclaimed their support for the movement: “We are aware that we were privileged students and we have a duty of solidarity.” The same pattern also played out in several other cities. On one side, large groups of demonstrators marched in dozens of peaceful processions from Rennes (Brittany) to Toulouse (south), despite the tensions. On the other, independent groups were determined to clash with the police. In the streets, groups of university and high school students, toughened up by weeks of protest, mingled with small groups that organized to confront law enforcement units. Protesters from working-class neighborhoods were there, as well as a good number of looters who could hardly believe their eyes: All the opulence of Paris’s wealthy districts was suddenly within reach, whether by hand or by hammer. “These neighborhoods are rich here. It’s all from the profits made off the suburbs,” shouted a very young man in the middle of a small group, as they made their way near Rue Lafayette. On several of Paris’s major right bank streets, the same scenes, with the same violent acts, played out again and again. Paving stones were thrown. Cars, trucks and construction vehicles were set on fire. Starting from Madeleine, Kenzo, Salomon and Arc’teryx boutiques had their windows smashed and shelves ransacked. The Céline store on Avenue Montaigne was pillaged. In nearby streets, one guy and his friends were asking, “Where is Chanel?” On Saturday evening, Lecornu announced that 1,200 people had been arrested and 150 police officers and gendarmes injured. “We will all stand united against the sowers of chaos, who seek only to bring down our institutions and prevent those who peacefully express their demands from doing so,” he said.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n法国爆发近年来最严重的街头骚乱震惊世界！为什么法国人总在抗议？ 周六，据组织者统计，约有80万人走上街头，而法国内政部长洛朗·努涅斯则将人数估计为55万。许多城市都上演了相同的场景：一边是和平示威的人群，另一边则是决心与警方发生冲突的独立团体。尽管全国各地的抗议活动参与人数众多，但这些示威也因前所未有的财产破坏、暴力行为和抢劫事件而蒙上阴影。在法国，仿佛几个截然不同的世界，在这漫长的一天里迎面碰撞。 这场抗议活动的根源可以追溯到9月，当时，社交媒体上开始流传反对燃油价格上涨的示威号召。与此同时，高中生也在抗议教师短缺、教室过度拥挤以及学校条件恶劣等问题。这场社会动荡还唤起了人们对“黄背心运动”的记忆。该运动始于2018年，最初是为了抗议燃油税，随后逐渐演变为一场范围更广、反对经济不平等和埃马纽埃尔·马克龙政府的反抗运动。在这些不同的不满诉求背后，隐藏着一种更深层次的挫败感：许多法国人感到生活成本不断上涨，令自己不堪重负；他们对公共服务感到失望，也越来越不信任政治领导人。学生、工人以及曾经参加“黄背心运动”的老成员们，看到了将各自的抗争联合起来的机会。 周六的示威活动既是对一项特别提及生活成本上涨问题的行动号召的回应，也是借助了9月下旬开始的高中生抗议运动所积累的声势。示威者希望推动不同群体的“抗争汇合”，让“黄背心”成员、高中生、多个职业领域的从业者、左翼政治领导人以及工会组织联合起来，共同表达诉求。 上午10点整，在圣拉扎尔火车站外，一名“黄背心”抗议者概括了自己当天的双重目标：“首先是革命，不过我还想睡到马克龙的床上去。”他说这番话时，语气中既带着幽默，又透着认真。随后，他开始抨击资本主义、资产阶级、警察以及高昂的生活成本。周围的同伴也接连加入讨论，滔滔不绝地列举各自的诉求、不满和愤怒。直到一支BRAV-M摩托车警察部队突然闯入，向这群人逼近，他们的谈话才戛然而止。 上午，几个小规模、自发组织的集会开始陆续形成。他们打算朝着爱丽舍宫、协和广场或歌剧院方向前进——说白了，就是尽可能向前推进。他们没有正式的游行队伍，没有领导者，也没有真正明确的目标，只有一群群四处移动、行动灵活的人。“罢工！封锁！自发抗议！”戴着面罩的人们一边高喊口号，一边朝歌剧院大道飞奔。到了下午早些时候，巴黎的局势似乎已经至少在一定程度上失去控制，而且混乱同时以多种不同形式蔓延。作为正式示威活动起点的共和国广场，早已被人群挤得水泄不通。一些有组织的暴力抗议者首先开始破坏街道公共设施，随后试图闯入共和国卫队营房，点燃一辆汽车，并四处打砸破坏。 尽管这场游行或许并没有真正实现此前被大力宣扬的“各方抗争汇合”，但它仍然汇聚了来自社会各行各业的人们。露西和托马斯刚刚从享有盛誉的HEC商学院毕业，两人公开表达了对这场运动的支持：“我们清楚，自己曾是享有优越条件的学生，因此我们有责任与他人团结在一起。” 类似的场景也在法国其他几座城市上演。一边是大批示威者，尽管局势紧张，仍然在从布列塔尼地区的雷恩到法国南部的图卢兹等城市，参加数十场和平游行；另一边，则是一些决心与警方发生冲突的独立团体。 街头上，经历了数周抗议活动磨炼的大学生和高中生，与那些有组织地对抗执法部队的小团体混杂在一起。来自工人阶级社区的抗议者也出现在现场，还有数量不少的抢劫者，他们几乎不敢相信眼前的一切：巴黎富裕街区的种种奢华，如今突然变得触手可及——无论是伸手去拿，还是挥锤砸开。一个小团体中的年轻男子在经过拉法耶特街附近时高喊：“这里的街区都很富有。这些财富全都是靠从郊区赚取的利润积累起来的！” 在巴黎塞纳河右岸的几条主要街道上，同样的场景、同样的暴力行为一再上演。铺路石被人掷出，汽车、卡车和工程车辆遭到纵火。从玛德莱娜广场一带开始，Kenzo、Salomon和Arc’teryx等品牌精品店的橱窗被砸碎，货架遭到洗劫。位于蒙田大道的Céline门店也被抢劫。在附近的街道上，一个男子甚至和朋友们四处打听：“香奈儿在哪儿？”周六晚间，勒科尔尼宣布，已有1200人被捕，150名警察和宪兵受伤。他表示：“我们将团结一致，共同对抗那些制造混乱的人。他们唯一的目的，就是摧毁我们的制度，并阻止那些和平表达诉求的人发出自己的声音。”","2026-10-19_001":"El Niño Threatens Global Sugar Supply — 2026/10/19 El Niño Threatens Global Sugar Supply Conflict. Climate change. A withering El Niño. All are conspiring to spoil the world's sweet tooth. Global sugar prices have soared, as a hot, dry summer scorched much of the sugar-producing parts of the world, nudging overall food prices upward. Nowhere is that felt as acutely as it is in India, the biggest sugar consumer in the world and the second-biggest producer. The worst monsoon in 11 years and fears of a poor sugarcane harvest have spiked prices amid the Hindu festival season, when gods and mortals alike are feted with sweets. Making matters worse, high sugar prices are also complicating India's energy security goals. Sugarcane is a key crop for the country's biofuels push, all the more so as India seeks to curb oil imports while the waterways of the Middle East are choked by war. This year's El Niño has left many of the country's sugarcane fields parched, just a year after they were drenched by excess rains and devoured by pests. Protests have broken out over rising prices. The government has warned sugar traders against hoarding. And a country that usually exports sugar has taken the extraordinary step of opening the doors to imports this year, further raising global sugar prices. \"This crop is gone,\" Morarji Patil, a 30-year-old farmer, said as he stood beside his cane field one sweaty September morning in the Marathwada region of western India. Like other farmers here, Mr. Patil was already crushed by debt and high fertilizer prices before the blistering heat and scant rains. His cane stalks were thin and short, stunted by a lack of water this summer when they needed it most. Mr. Patil expected his yields to be 70 percent lower than normal. India represents an object lesson on the far-reaching effects of conflict and extreme weather on a nation's economy. Food prices are rising month by month. Key crops remain at risk as the long tail of El Niño drags out through next year, heightening the likelihood of extreme drought and flood. On top of that, India is extremely vulnerable to global oil supply shocks. It imports the vast majority of its crude oil. It's not just India. Extraordinary heat and drought this year have hit sugarcane in Brazil, the world's biggest sugar producer, and sugar beets in Europe. Global sugar prices are nearly 15 percent higher than they were a year ago. In India, sugar has long enjoyed state support. Most recently, the industry profited from a government directive that made 20-percent ethanol blends the standard fuel at Indian gas stations. The government said its biofuels push reduced oil demand and climate pollution, while also saving the country's precious foreign exchange. It called ethanol blends \"a key pillar of India's energy transition.\" It offered sugar refineries an additional source of revenue. It also infuriated Indian drivers. Aditya Kare, 21, was refueling his 2018 Hero motorcycle at a gas station in the nearby city of Latur. He said the ethanol blend had reduced his average mileage from 60 kilometers per liter to around 45, and he was having a hard time making do with the 100 rupees, a little more than $1, that his parents gave him for his commute to college. \"At home, we are planning to get an electric bike,\" he said. Sugarcane comes with a high cost. It is a thirsty crop, and its demand, for both food and fuel, is forcing many farmers to drill deeper for water and drain local aquifers. Mr. Patil's well goes down 800 feet. This year, even at that depth, there is no water. A dam on the Manjara River has become a dry ditch. Ponds are bare. \"This environment is not good for sugarcane,\" said Ganesh Gomare, a farmer. \"But the economy of Marathwada is dependent on sugarcane.\" \"Sugar is tempting. It enjoys relatively high profits.\" So Mr. Gomare, 38, devoted more of his land to sugarcane this year. It backfired. He expects his yields to be half of normal. Nearby, Dagdu Venkat Rao Padile, 66, uprooted a stalk of sugarcane from his field. The plant was emaciated. Its sugar content would be paltry. \"It will cost me more to cut it than what I'll get for it,\" Mr. Padile said. His yields would be one-fourth of normal. The rains were so poor that he had left most of his land fallow. Tanisha Gaikwad had all but cut sugar from her diet. But she joined the line at the sweet shop for a small box of laddoos to offer at the altar for Mahalaxmi, a goddess of wealth and power. Her prayers, she said, could not be sugar-free.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n全球糖价上涨：粮食与能源之争 厄尔尼诺发威，全球糖供应告急——印度甘蔗歉收，正在让农民和消费者一起受伤，还把这个国家的能源安全目标，从底下一点点掏空。 冲突。气候变化。一场正在让万物枯萎的厄尔尼诺。这三样东西像是商量好了似的，一起来毁掉全世界那份嗜甜的快乐。全球糖价一路飙升——一个又热又干的夏天，把世界上大片产糖区烤得焦头烂额，顺手把整体食品价格也往上顶了一顶。而这份痛，没有哪个地方感受得比印度更深切：它是全球最大的食糖消费国，也是第二大生产国。十一年来最糟糕的一场季风，加上对甘蔗歉收的恐惧，把糖价猛地戳了上去——偏偏这一切，就发生在印度教的节日季里；在这个时节，神明和凡人，都要用甜食供着。 更糟的是，高糖价还在把印度的能源安全目标搅成一团乱麻。甘蔗正是这个国家生物燃料计划的关键作物——这一点尤其要命，因为印度正想方设法压住石油进口，偏偏中东的航道又被战争掐住了。今年的厄尔尼诺，把印度大片甘蔗地晒得干裂；而就在一年前，这些地才刚被过多的雨水泡透、被害虫啃光。 抗议因涨价而爆发了。政府已经警告糖商不许囤积。而一个平时出口糖的国家，今年破天荒地迈出一步——向进口打开了大门，这反过来又把全球糖价往上推了一把。“这批庄稼完了。”三十岁的农民莫拉尔吉·帕蒂尔说。那是一个出汗的九月早晨，他站在印度西部马拉特瓦达地区自家的甘蔗地边。跟这里其他农民一样，在酷热和少雨到来之前，他就已经被债务和高昂的化肥价格压垮了。他的甘蔗秆又细又矮——在最需要水的那个夏天，水没来。帕蒂尔预计，今年的收成会比正常年份低七成。 印度就是一件摆在眼前的活教材——它演示了冲突和极端天气，能对一个国家的经济产生多深远的影响。食品价格一个月一个月地往上走。关键作物依然岌岌可危，因为厄尔尼诺那条长尾巴还要一路拖到明年，把极端干旱和洪水发生的可能性又抬高了。雪上加霜的是，印度在全球石油供应冲击面前，脆弱得不堪一击——它绝大部分的原油都靠进口。而且，倒霉的不止印度一个。今年破纪录的高温和干旱，同时打击了巴西的甘蔗和欧洲的甜菜——前者是全球最大的产糖国。全球糖价，已经比一年前高出将近15%。 在印度，糖长期以来都享受着国家的扶持。最近，这个行业从一项政府指令里赚到了钱——这项指令把含20% 乙醇的混合燃油，定为印度加油站的标准燃油。政府说，它推的这套生物燃料，既压低了石油需求和气候污染，又省下了国家宝贵的外汇。它把这种乙醇燃油称作“印度能源转型的一根关键支柱”。它给糖厂送去了一笔额外收入。它同时也把印度司机气炸了。 21 岁的阿迪蒂亚·卡雷，正在附近的拉图尔市一座加油站，给他那辆2018 款的Hero 摩托车加油。他说，乙醇混合燃油把他的平均油耗，从每升60 公里压到了45 公里左右——父母给他上学通勤的那100 卢比（也就比一美元多一点），他越来越难凑合着撑过去了。“我们家正打算买一辆电动车，”他说。 甘蔗，是带着高昂代价来的。它是一种很“渴”的作物，而它作为食物与燃料的双重需求，正逼着许多农民往更深处打井，把当地的地下含水层抽干。帕蒂尔家的井，往下打了800 英尺——可今年，打到了那个深度，依然没有水。曼贾拉河上的那座水坝，如今成了一条干沟。池塘，空空如也。“这方水土，不适合种甘蔗，”农民加内什·戈马雷说。“可马拉特瓦达的经济，就靠甘蔗撑着。” 糖是诱人的。它利润相对高。所以38 岁的戈马雷先生今年把更多的地，押给了甘蔗。适得其反。他预计，收成只有正常年份的一半。 不远处，66 岁的达格杜·文卡特·拉奥·帕迪尔，从自家地里连根拔起了一根甘蔗。这株植物瘦得脱了形。它的含糖量，会少得可怜。“割它花的钱，比卖它能挣的还多，”帕迪尔说。他估摸着，今年的收成只有正常年份的四分之一。雨水少成那样，他干脆把大部分地都撂荒了。 塔妮莎·盖克瓦德，已经几乎把糖从自己的饮食里戒掉了。可她还是去糖果店排了队，买了一小盒拉杜——要供在祭台上，献给吉祥天女，一位掌管财富与力量的女神。她说，她的祷告，不能是无糖的。","2026-10-18_001":"The Tesla Roadster flew, drove upside down, and hit 60mph in under a second — 2026/10/18 The Tesla Roadster flew, drove upside down, and hit 60mph in under a second Elon Musk promised a flying car. And the Tesla Roadster did exactly that. Tesla demonstrated its next-generation Roadster at an event in Waco, Texas, where the electric supercar drove upside down, briefly flew, and accelerated to 60mph in under a second. It was an impressive demonstration of a car that likely will come with some heavy caveats and restrictions if it ever goes into production. The event was less than 30 minutes and felt more like a stunt show than a product reveal. Indeed, there was no mention of price, trim levels, release date, or other such mundane details. Musk promised the hypercar would go into production in 2028, but after nearly nine years who's counting. The people were here to see a car fly, and that's what Tesla gave them. First, the Roadster drove up a very tall, multi-story ramp, using its incredible downforce to cling to the vertical roadway. When it reached the top of the ramp, it flipped around using its cold-gas thrusters to bring it gently back down to earth. The car uses a fan-based suction system, supplied by SpaceX and mounted underneath the vehicle, which is capable of generating up to two Gs of suction force. Next, the Roadster flew over the rest of Tesla's lineup, including the Model 3, Model Y, Cybertruck, Semi, and Cybercab — doing what Tesla VP for Engineering Lars Moravy called \"a kick flip\" in the process. Musk explained that it features attitude-control thrusters as well as main thrusters at the rear, giving the Roadster rocket-like capabilities. \"So it can basically fly like a rocket,\" Musk said. \"And it could do rolls. It could do, like, crazy gymnastics. Whatever you want, really. Now, I should say that this is not necessarily a safety-optimized car. But it takes the fun meter to 11 out of 10.\" Of course, it seems extremely unlikely that this version of the car will ever be available to the average consumer — even as Musk claimed \"This will be an actual car that's on the roads.\" The company would have to satisfy strict regulatory requirements from the Federal Aviation Administration, as well as the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration. The Roadster could go into production in 2028, as Musk promised — or it could be another decade of missed deadlines and broken promises. Again, who cares? Car flew. Tesla has also said that the Roadster can accelerate from 0-60mph in less than a second. And during the event, the company claimed it did so in 0.81 seconds. Musk explained that increasing the applied force while reducing the vehicle's mass help boost acceleration. \"You've got to crack halfway to the speed of sound, I think,\" Musk teased. \"Mach 0.5. Maybe a little higher.\" The next-gen Roadster certainly took the scenic route in getting here. A prototype version was announced as a \"one more thing\" style surprise during the 2017 Tesla Semi event. The specs sounded ludicrous even back then: 0-60 mph in 1.9 seconds, a top speed over 250 mph, a 200 kWh battery delivering up to 620 miles of range, and a $200,000 starting price tag. Musk said deliveries would start in 2020, which sounded perfectly doable at the time. Early adopters quickly threw down $50,000 deposits (or $250,000 for the limited Founders Series), effectively interest-free loans to help fund Tesla's scaling ambitions. But then Tesla seemed to forget about the Roadster, cementing its status as the ultimate vaporware. Production deadlines slipped as Tesla prioritized mass-market vehicles like the Model Y, while also wrestling with the overly complicated Cybertruck. Occasionally, when Musk would remember that the Roadster existed, he would up the ante about its capabilities. This culminated in the \"SpaceX package\" that would equip the supercar with cold-gas thrusters, bestowing hover powers and a 0-60 time under one second. Musk has also sought to downplay the Roadster's significance, calling it a \"small potato situation\" that will not \"move the needle in a major way financially.\" He's framed the supercar more as a joint engineering collaboration between Tesla and SpaceX, even questioning whether it's \"even a car anymore\" given its experimental nature. Some analysts theorize that the decision to re-release the Roadster is an attempt to set the stage for a rumored Tesla-SpaceX merger. Tesla's failure to bring the Roadster to market allowed other automakers to slip past the company with their own hyper-powered EVs. Rimac has smashed performance records with the Nevera, Porsche has refined electric performance with the Taycan, and Chinese giants like BYD are producing track-ready supercars at a fraction of the cost. The Roadster is no longer shocking the world as an impossible prototype; it's playing catch-up to an industry that spent nine years building what Tesla merely rendered. But then again, none of those cars flew.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n是的，特斯拉真造了一辆「飞行汽车」！中国官网开放预订，意向金33.2万元，你会买吗？ 特斯拉Roadster飞上天空、倒挂行驶，零百加速不到一秒 埃隆·马斯克曾承诺要造出一辆会飞的汽车，而特斯拉Roadster真的做到了。在得克萨斯州韦科市举行的一场活动上，特斯拉展示了新一代Roadster。这辆电动超级跑车不仅能够倒挂行驶，还短暂飞上了天空，并在不到一秒的时间内加速至每小时60英里。这场展示令人印象深刻，但如果这款车最终真的投入生产，恐怕还会伴随着诸多严苛的限制和附加条件。整场活动持续了不到30分钟，与其说是一场产品发布会，倒不如说是一场特技表演。事实上，现场根本没有提及价格、车型配置、发布日期，或其他诸如此类的平凡细节。马斯克承诺，这款超级跑车将在2028年投产。不过，既然已经等了将近九年，谁还在乎究竟等了多久呢？人们来到这里就是为了看汽车飞起来，而特斯拉确实让他们看到了。 首先，Roadster驶上了一条高达数层楼的坡道，凭借惊人的下压力紧紧吸附在垂直路面上。当它抵达坡道顶端时，车辆利用冷气体推进器翻转车身，随后轻柔地返回地面。这辆车采用了由SpaceX提供、安装在车身底部的风扇式吸附系统，能够产生高达两个G的吸附力。接下来，Roadster从特斯拉旗下其他车型上方飞过，包括Model 3、Model Y、Cybertruck、Semi和Cybercab。在飞越过程中，它还完成了特斯拉工程副总裁拉尔斯·莫拉维口中的一个“踢翻”动作。马斯克解释称，这辆车不仅配备了姿态控制推进器，车尾还装有主推进器，使Roadster具备了类似火箭的能力。 “所以，它基本上可以像火箭一样飞行，”马斯克说道，“它还能在空中翻滚，做出各种疯狂的特技动作。真的，想怎么玩都行。不过，我得说明一下，这并不一定是一辆以安全性为首要优化目标的汽车。但如果给驾驶乐趣打分，满分10分，它能拿到11分。” 当然，这个版本的Roadster似乎极不可能真正进入普通消费者的生活，尽管马斯克声称：“这将是一辆真正能够上路行驶的汽车。”特斯拉必须满足美国联邦航空管理局以及美国国家公路交通安全管理局提出的严格监管要求。Roadster或许真能像马斯克承诺的那样在2028年投产，也可能迎来又一个充满延期和失信的十年。不过，还是那句话，谁在乎呢？反正车已经飞起来了。 特斯拉此前还宣称，Roadster能够在不到一秒的时间内从静止加速至每小时60英里。而在这次活动上，特斯拉声称它仅用了0.81秒就完成了这一加速过程。马斯克解释说，增大施加在车辆上的作用力，同时减轻车身质量，有助于提升加速性能。“我觉得，你得突破音速的一半才行，”马斯克打趣道，“0.5马赫，也许还能再快一点。” 新一代Roadster走到今天这一步，可谓绕了好大一圈。早在2017年的特斯拉Semi卡车发布会上，一款Roadster原型车就作为“还有一件事”的压轴惊喜突然亮相。即便放在当年，它公布的性能参数也堪称疯狂：从静止加速至每小时60英里仅需1.9秒，最高时速超过250英里，配备200千瓦时电池，续航里程最高可达620英里，起售价为20万美元。马斯克当时表示，车辆将在2020年开始交付，而在那个时候，这个计划听起来似乎完全能够实现。 早期支持者迅速掏出5万美元支付订金，限量版Founders Series的订金则高达25万美元。这些钱实际上相当于提供给特斯拉的无息贷款，帮助其实现扩大生产规模的雄心。然而，特斯拉随后似乎把Roadster忘在了脑后，进一步坐实了它作为终极“画饼产品”的地位。随着特斯拉将重心转向Model Y等面向大众市场的车型，同时还要应对设计过于复杂的Cybertruck，Roadster的生产期限一再推迟。偶尔，当马斯克想起Roadster的存在时，他又会进一步提高对其性能的承诺。最终，这些不断升级的设想发展成了所谓的“SpaceX套件”：通过为这款超级跑车配备冷气体推进器，赋予它悬浮能力，并实现不到一秒的零至每小时60英里加速。 马斯克也曾试图淡化Roadster的重要性，称它不过是件“小事”，不会在财务层面产生什么重大影响。他更多地将这款超级跑车描述为特斯拉与SpaceX之间的一项联合工程合作，甚至考虑到它的实验性质，还质疑它“究竟还能不能算是一辆汽车”。一些分析师推测，特斯拉决定重新推出Roadster，可能是在为传闻中的特斯拉与SpaceX合并计划铺路。 特斯拉迟迟未能将Roadster推向市场，让其他汽车制造商得以凭借各自的超高性能电动汽车实现反超。Rimac凭借Nevera打破了多项性能纪录，保时捷通过Taycan不断完善电动汽车的性能表现，而比亚迪等中国汽车巨头则正在以远低于竞争对手的成本生产能够驰骋赛道的超级跑车。如今，Roadster已经不再是一款凭借看似不可能实现的性能参数震惊世界的原型车。相反，它正在奋力追赶整个行业——在过去九年里，其他车企真正造出了特斯拉当年仅仅停留在效果图上的东西。不过话说回来，那些车可没有一辆会飞。","2026-10-17_001":"Mark Carney shows middle powers how to handle a dangerous world — 2026/10/17 Mark Carney shows middle powers how to handle a dangerous world Entitled Canada has been feeding off America for decades. Like a parasitic bug, it enjoys the benefits of being America's cherished 51st state without giving anything back. Canadians know they couldn't live without America's protection, yet their ungrateful prime minister, Mark Carney, refuses to defer to the great and beautiful country over the border. That is how Donald Trump sees Canada, and it leaves no room for mutual benefit between the two allies. The bullying president is ratcheting up tariffs. Canadian consumers, in response, are spitting out their Kentucky bourbon and leaving Las Vegas. Mr Carney, in office for 18 months, remains defiant, the star of an extended, dramatic and riveting stand-off. By now you might have expected Mr Carney to have caved in. Canada is only a mid size power with a so-so economy and puny armed forces. It has vulnerabilities aplenty. One will be evident on October 19th, when the province of Alberta holds a referendum asking if it may one day secede, taking with it more oil in the ground than in the entire United States. In fact Mr Carney is riding high. Polls suggest that those Albertans will probably vote to stay. And under him, Canada is doing better than you might expect. In January it was Mr Carney, at the annual jamboree in Davos, who argued that what he called the \"middle powers\" must find ways to take on threatening ones. Nine months on, his country offers lessons for what these middle powers should do when the hegemonic giant pulls the mat from under them. Those lessons start with a bracing dose of realism. As we set out, middle powers will never replace the hegemons as the force that sustains powerful permanent institutions such as the UN. The world has grown more dangerous because both America and China are exploiting or undermining arrangements and norms in which power was shared. Emboldened, wreckers like Russia and Iran are becoming even more predatory. Co-ordinating the more herbivorous middle powers is often too hard to get much done—as you can see in even the most developed middle-power club, the European Union. Yet middle powers can still mitigate some of the damage, as Canada and its allies show. Mr Carney has zipped around the world making new trade deals as well as drumming up symbolic agreements, such as talk of associate EU membership. In the realm of defence, Canada is trimming its old reliance on America, and is considering placing an order for Swedish Gripen fighter jets. Canada's most important lesson, however, emerges from what the pragmatic Mr Carney is doing at home. A few years ago the country's woeful politics and ailing government seemed broken. Income per person had drifted from 90% of America's in the 1980s to just 71% in 2022, much of it following a recent burst of low-skilled immigration. Today Mr Carney is pushing through overdue measures to boost productivity, open the economy and lift defence spending from a pitifully low 1.3% of GDP to 2%. Among his first steps, he scrapped an unpopular consumer carbon tax and vowed to build a pipeline to carry oil from Alberta to the west coast. He has started to cut internal barriers to trade, called off a hefty rise in capital-gains tax, cut the lowest rate of income tax and set out plans to trim federal spending. Mr Carney has so far managed to resist what is known as the \"Juncker curse\", named after a president of the European Commission, Jean-Claude Juncker. Speaking of reform, Mr Juncker once said: \"We all know what to do, we just don't know how to get re-elected after we've done it.\" Yet Mr Carney's popularity has been surging even as he reforms. Today a maple-leaf love-in is under way: polls show the prime minister's approval rating nearing 70%. Internationally, he has replaced France's beleaguered Emmanuel Macron as the centrists' pin-up. The contrast with Mr Trump, unloved by his own voters, is stark. True, Canada must go further and faster with its liberalising changes. There is every chance that relations with America will improve when Mr Trump leaves office in 2029, but it is not certain. It should therefore work on keeping its options open. Other middle powers can learn from Canada. And they can fortify themselves abroad by harnessing the need for change at home. In a way, Mr Trump has done all democracies a favour, by giving them a reason to tackle long-standing problems that seemed too hard. The middle powers need to prepare for a dangerous world. They should look to Mr Carney and Canada for an answer on what to do.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国越来越难以依靠，世界各国开始寻找第三条路？ 马克·卡尼为中等强国示范——如何在乱世中稳住阵脚。加拿大正挺身直面霸凌成性的美国。 加拿大这个自以为应得的国家，几十年来一直趴在美国身上吸血。它像一只寄生虫，享受着被美国当作珍视的第51个州的好处，却什么也不回馈。加拿大人心知肚明，没有美国的保护他们根本活不下去；可他们那位忘恩负义的总理马克·卡尼，偏偏拒绝向边境那头伟大而美丽的国家低头。 这，就是特朗普眼中的加拿大；而这套看法，让这两个盟友之间毫无互利可言。这位霸凌成性的总统正一步步加高关税，加拿大消费者则用实际行动回应：他们吐出嘴里的肯塔基波本威士忌，转身离开拉斯维加斯。已经上任18个月的卡尼总理依然毫不退让，成了这场旷日持久、戏剧化且扣人心弦的对峙中最耀眼的主角。 按理说，到了这个节骨眼上，你大概已经料定卡尼撑不下去了。加拿大充其量只是个中等规模的国家，经济马马虎虎，军队弱得可怜，软肋一抓一大把。其中一个软肋将在10月19日暴露无遗——那一天，阿尔伯塔省要举行公投，问的是：它有朝一日能不能脱离加拿大。而它一旦走了，会带走比整个美国地下还多的石油。 可事实上，卡尼如今正春风得意。民调显示，那些阿尔伯塔人大概率会选择留下。而在他的带领下，加拿大的表现也比很多人预想的要好。今年一月，正是在达沃斯那场一年一度的大聚会上，卡尼提出了他的主张：他口中的“中等强国”，必须找到办法去对抗那些咄咄逼人的大国。九个月过去，他的国家已经成了这些中等强国的范本——当那个霸权巨人猛地从他们脚下抽走垫子时，他们该怎么做。 这些经验，得从一剂令人清醒的现实主义说起。正如我们此前所阐述的：中等强国永远不可能取代那些霸权国家，成为支撑联合国这类强大常设机构的中坚力量。世界之所以变得更加危险，是因为美国和中国都在利用、或者在掏空那些原本权力共享的安排与规范。而受到鼓舞的破坏者——俄罗斯、伊朗之流——正变得更加掠夺成性。至于把那些更为“食草”的中等强国协调起来，往往难到办不成什么事；这一点，你只要看看连发育最成熟的中等强国俱乐部——欧盟，就明白了。 然而，中等强国依然能够减轻一部分伤害——加拿大和它的伙伴们就是明证。卡尼在世界各地马不停蹄地奔走，一面签下实打实的新贸易协定，一面也张罗着一批象征性的协议，比如那个“成为欧盟准成员”的说法。而在防务领域，加拿大正在一点点削减它对美国由来已久的依赖，并且正在考虑向瑞典下一笔“鹰狮”战斗机的订单。 然而，加拿大最重要的一条经验，是从务实的卡尼在自己国内所做的事里浮现出来的。短短几年前，这个国家糟糕透顶的政治生态、病恹恹的政府，看起来已经烂到了根子里。人均收入从1980年代相当于美国的90%，一路漂到2022年只剩下71%——而这轮下滑的相当一部分，正紧随最近一波低技能移民的涌入。如今，卡尼正顶着阻力推进一系列早该落地的措施：提升生产率、开放经济，并把国防开支从低得可怜的、占GDP 1.3%的水平，抬到2%。 在他最早采取的一批举措中，他废掉了一项不得人心的消费者碳税，并郑重承诺修建一条输油管道，把阿尔伯塔的石油送往西海岸。他已着手削减省际贸易壁垒，叫停了一项幅度可观的资本利得税上调，下调了最低一档的个人所得税税率，并列出了削减联邦开支的计划。 迄今为止，卡尼成功地顶住了那个所谓的“容克魔咒”——它得名于一位欧盟委员会主席，让-克洛德·容克。说到改革，容克当年说过一句话：“我们都知道该做什么，我们只是不知道，做完之后怎么才能重新当选。”然而，偏偏就在卡尼推行改革的同时，他的民望一路飙升。如今，一场“枫叶热恋”正在上演：民调显示，这位总理的支持率已逼近70%。在国际上，他已取代法国那位四面楚歌的马克龙，成为中间派追捧的偶像。而他与特朗普——那个连自己选民都不爱他的人——之间的反差，鲜明得刺眼。 诚然，加拿大在自由化改革上还必须走得更远、更快。当特朗普于2029年卸任之后，与美国的关系很有可能会改善——但此事并无把握。因此，它应当着力为自己保留选择的余地。 其他中等强国，可以从加拿大身上学到东西。它们可以靠驾驭本国求变的需要，来增强自身在海外的实力。某种意义上，特朗普帮了所有民主国家一个忙——他给了它们一个理由，去着手解决那些长期存在、看上去又太难、久拖不决的问题。中等强国必须为一个危险的世界做好准备。至于究竟该怎么办，它们应当把目光投向卡尼和加拿大，去那里找答案。","2026-10-16_001":"Video Cameras Are Everywhere-and Now They Are Being Hacked for War — 2026/10/16 Video Cameras Are Everywhere-and Now They Are Being Hacked for War As U.S. and Israeli forces bombed Iran early this year, a poultry farmer in northern Israel decided to check on his chickens. When he launched the app on his phone for the webcam outside his coop, he was alarmed to see that its language had been switched to Arabic. He contacted Israel's National Cyber Directorate, which found that hackers had taken control of the Chinese-made camera. They weren't interested in chickens. The camera's orientation on the coop offered it a line of sight to the movements of Israeli security forces along the nearby border with Lebanon. The cyber directorate said it helped the farmer harden the camera's security and adjust its viewing angle to stop capturing military operations. The farmer's webcam was one of an estimated 1.2 billion internet-connected cameras worldwide, according to cybersecurity consultants S-RM. The devices have become a background fixture of daily life in the form of security systems, doorbells, traffic controls and even baby monitors—and potentially any of them are vulnerable to snooping, say cybersecurity experts and hackers. \"We are now seeing the conversion of civilian surveillance into wartime intelligence collection,\" said Alexander Leslie, a national-security expert at threat-intelligence consultants Recorded Future, who sees a global \"soft underbelly\" in omnipresent digital eyeballs. Intelligence agencies scour the internet for vulnerable cameras and robotically tap in to them by testing millions of passwords. Now, AI has made it even easier to hack into them and analyze their data at scale, transforming cameras that were designed to boost safety into tools for combatants to track, surveil and attack the enemy—often surpassing the abilities of human intelligence analysts. Algorithms digest endless reams of footage to find hidden gems and patterns for agents to monitor. Russia and Ukraine have ramped up camera-hacking in their war, aiming to spy on each other, find new targets and assess damage from their attacks. Israel, long a master of digital spycraft, hacked into Iranian traffic cameras to help forces collect intelligence used in the assassination of Iranian Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei earlier this year. Iran and its proxies have struck back by breaking into hundreds of Israeli webcams and security cameras that Israeli officials consider sensitive to national security, like the one on the chicken coop. In 2024, Russian hackers commandeered residential webcams in Kyiv, including one installed by a condominium association to monitor the neighborhood. Ukraine's security service, the SBU, said that Russia intended to use the hacked video feeds to find targets in the capital and \"to adjust missile attacks on Ukraine,\" refining their aim for subsequent strikes. The SBU said it has identified and secured, reoriented or blocked roughly 40,000 video cameras that posed potential security risks since Russia's 2022 invasion. Hacked cameras are taking over roles in targeting, damage assessments and other combat intelligence-gathering that were previously supplied by aerial reconnaissance or on-the-ground spies. Penetrating a well-placed camera can get analysts closer to targets and carries less risk than infiltrating even the canniest secret agent. Video cameras operate silently, often in restricted areas. While private individuals and enterprise-network managers are increasingly attuned to cybersecurity, web-connected devices like cameras may have fewer built-in protections and not be consistently monitored, patched or updated, said Xu Zou, senior vice president for cloud security services at cybersecurity company Palo Alto Networks. Information-security specialists inside companies and governments often aren't aware of connected cameras, which are frequently installed and operated by facilities managers or other non-IT teams, he said. Ondrej Svoboda in Prague was reading to his young children one evening in 2023 when the baby monitor in their room started emitting strange sounds of people talking and background music, as if from a bar. At first he thought his wife was using the two-way function but quickly realized that hackers had gotten into his system. \"I didn't think this was something that could happen to me,\" recalled Svoboda, who worked at a cybersecurity company. \"I was really scared-my heart was racing.\" Trying to avoid frightening his children, he silently stared into the camera to show the hackers he knew about them and then unplugged the camera. For days he suffered nightmares as he struggled to understand how he got hacked. Eventually he remembered he had tried connecting his webcams to another app, which likely was insecure. Vulnerable accessories linked into a network are among hackers' prime targets, expanding what technicians call the attack surface. Often, cameras aren't the target, but serve as the weak link in a larger hacking attempt. Ransomware group Akira tapped an unprotected webcam to get around one organization's network-security systems and extort it, S-RM reported last year. Once hackers are inside a local network, they can usually probe stored files, email accounts and other devices, including cameras. \"The Internet of Things is such a wild area that everything-whether you realize it or not—is an extension of your attack surface,\" said Paul Caron, S-RM head of cyber for the Americas.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n全球12亿台摄像头，正在变成免费“战争眼睛”！普通人的摄像头，怎么成了现代战争的新目标？ 摄像头无处不在——如今，它们正被黑客入侵，沦为战争工具 今年早些时候，当美国和以色列军队轰炸伊朗时，以色列北部的一名家禽养殖户决定查看一下自己的鸡群。他打开手机上的应用程序，想查看鸡舍外的网络摄像头，却惊恐地发现，摄像头的操作语言竟然被切换成了阿拉伯语。他随即联系了以色列国家网络安全局。调查发现，黑客已经控制了这台中国制造的摄像头。然而，他们感兴趣的并不是鸡。由于摄像头安装在鸡舍上的朝向，它恰好能够拍摄到附近黎巴嫩边境一带以色列安全部队的活动。以色列国家网络安全局表示，他们帮助这名农户加强了摄像头的安全防护，并调整了拍摄角度，以防止其继续捕捉军事行动的画面。 据网络安全咨询公司S-RM估计，全球约有12亿台联网摄像头，而这名农户的网络摄像头只是其中之一。这些设备早已以安防系统、门铃、交通监控设备，甚至婴儿监视器等形式，成为日常生活中几乎不被察觉的固定存在。然而，网络安全专家和黑客指出，其中任何一台设备都可能遭到窥探。威胁情报咨询公司Recorded Future的国家安全专家亚历山大·莱斯利表示：“我们如今正在目睹民用监控向战时情报收集工具的转变。”在他看来，这些无处不在的“数字眼睛”，正在全球范围内形成一处脆弱的软肋。 情报机构在互联网上四处搜寻存在安全漏洞的摄像头，并通过自动化程序测试数百万个密码，尝试接入这些设备。如今，人工智能使入侵摄像头以及大规模分析其数据变得更加容易。那些原本旨在提高安全性的摄像头，正被改造成交战方追踪、监视和攻击敌人的工具，其能力甚至常常超越人类情报分析员。算法能够处理海量、源源不断的视频画面，从中发现值得情报人员关注的隐藏线索和规律。 俄罗斯和乌克兰在战争中都加大了入侵摄像头的力度，试图借此相互刺探情报、寻找新的攻击目标，并评估袭击造成的损失。以色列长期以来精通数字间谍技术，今年早些时候曾入侵伊朗的交通摄像头，协助军方收集情报，这些情报随后被用于刺杀伊朗最高领袖阿里·哈梅内伊。伊朗及其代理势力则展开反击，侵入了数百台以色列网络摄像头和安防摄像头，其中包括鸡舍上的那一台。这些设备被以色列官员视为可能涉及国家安全的敏感设施。 2024年，俄罗斯黑客夺取了乌克兰首都基辅多台住宅网络摄像头的控制权，其中包括一台由公寓业主协会安装、原本用于监控周边社区的摄像头。乌克兰国家安全局（SBU）表示，俄罗斯打算利用这些被入侵的摄像头所传输的视频画面，在首都寻找攻击目标，并“调整对乌克兰的导弹袭击”，从而提高后续打击的精确度。乌克兰国家安全局还表示，自2022年俄罗斯发动入侵以来，该机构已识别出约4万台存在潜在安全风险的摄像头，并对其采取安全加固、调整朝向或封锁等措施。 被黑客入侵的摄像头，正逐渐接替过去由空中侦察或地面间谍承担的任务，包括目标定位、损害评估以及其他作战情报收集工作。入侵一台位置恰当的摄像头，可以让情报分析人员更接近目标，而且相比派遣哪怕最精明的秘密特工潜入敌方，其风险也更低。摄像头能够悄无声息地运作，而且往往就安装在出入受限的区域。 网络安全公司Palo Alto Networks负责云安全服务的高级副总裁徐邹表示，虽然个人用户和企业网络管理人员越来越重视网络安全，但摄像头等联网设备可能缺乏足够的内置安全防护，也未必能够得到持续监控、漏洞修补或系统更新。他指出，企业和政府机构内部的信息安全专家往往甚至不知道某些联网摄像头的存在，因为这些设备通常由设施管理人员或其他非信息技术部门负责安装和操作。 2023年的一个晚上，身在布拉格的翁德热·斯沃博达正在给年幼的孩子们读书，房间里的婴儿监视器突然传出了奇怪的声音：有人说话，还有背景音乐，听起来就像是从酒吧传来的。起初，他以为是妻子在使用设备的双向通话功能，但很快便意识到，黑客已经入侵了他的系统。“我从没想过这种事情会发生在自己身上。”在一家网络安全公司工作的斯沃博达回忆道，“我真的吓坏了，心脏怦怦直跳。”为了不吓到孩子，他一声不吭地盯着摄像头，向黑客表明自己已经发现了他们，随后拔掉了摄像头的电源。接连几天，他都饱受噩梦困扰，努力弄清自己究竟是如何遭到入侵的。最终，他想起自己曾尝试将网络摄像头连接到另一款应用程序，而那款应用很可能并不安全。 连接到网络的各类存在安全漏洞的外围设备，是黑客最主要的攻击目标之一，它们扩大了技术人员所说的“攻击面”。很多时候，摄像头本身并不是黑客的最终目标，而是更大规模网络攻击中的薄弱环节。据S-RM去年报道，勒索软件组织Akira曾利用一台缺乏安全防护的网络摄像头，绕过某机构的网络安全系统，并对其实施勒索。一旦黑客进入局域网，通常就可以进一步探查存储的文件、电子邮件账户以及包括摄像头在内的其他设备。S-RM美洲地区网络安全负责人保罗·卡隆表示：“物联网是一个如此难以管控的领域，以至于所有设备——无论你是否意识到——都在扩大你的攻击面。”","2026-10-15_001":"China is turning trash into treasure on a vast scale — 2026/10/15 China is turning trash into treasure on a vast scale A fast-growing number of EVs will soon be scrapped and replaced. Shredding their batteries yields \"black mass\", a powder from which valuable metals such as lithium, cobalt and nickel can be recovered and reused. From next month the EU will make it harder to export this waste. The aim is to encourage more processing of black mass close to home (at the moment most is shipped to Asia). In August America, which has banned Chinese EVs but still imports lots of Chinese batteries, blocked all black-mass exports. China, however, is unlikely to suffer. Black mass might make up 20-30% of global demand for battery metals by 2050, says the International Energy Agency, a forecaster. So China is supercharging its recycling industries: not just to shore up supplies of battery metals, but also rare earths and much else. The goal is to make its economy less \"linear\" (where raw materials are made into products which are then thrown away) and more \"circular\" (closing the loop so recycled waste replaces raw materials). Once recycling efforts were driven by concerns about pollution. Now, amid a global scramble for the materials needed to build a high-tech economy, security has become a primary motive. In the 1980s China started to import waste, taking the hardware, paper and plastic that richer countries did not want. A sprawling informal recycling-sector emerged, led by scavengers picking through rubbish piles. Much ended up in landfills or burnt in the open air, leaching toxic chemicals and creating poisonous smog. China is still well behind squeaky-clean European countries when it comes to waste management (though it is hard to say how far, as it does not publish detailed national data for recycling). Its poorer rural areas are still often strewn with rubbish. But officials have made China's big cities, at least, cleaner and more pleasant to live in. Now their focus is shifting to the use of recycling for the sake of security. In July China released a plan for the development of the circular economy until 2030. \"The importance of resource security has become increasingly prominent, while geopolitical dynamics...have grown complex and severe,\" the plan noted. First, China wants to dominate high-tech manufacturing in everything from robotics to wind turbines. And second, to achieve this, it must secure supplies of raw materials, connect more power lines and build more data-centres. Massive amounts of aluminium, copper, battery metals and rare earths are needed. While China controls the bulk of the global refining capacity for these materials, it relies on imports for most of the ores. Meanwhile many Western countries want to reduce reliance on China's factories. Officials worry they might try to squeeze its supplies. Last year a researcher at a state-owned research facility said that without recycling China would \"keep facing strangleholds in the future\". That China already makes vast quantities of these goods should make recycling simpler to do. Factories can tweak designs so that products are easier to dismantle later. And many battery-producing techniques (such as using acids and heat to isolate valuable metals) are also used in recycling them. Take CATL, for example, the world's largest battery-maker. Most of the Chinese firm's sales are domestic, and will end up being processed by a CATL subsidiary called Brunp, which is the world's biggest battery-recycler. Such arrangements have helped China build 85% of global battery recycling capacity. China is trying to make the recycling of rare earths (which it claims now supplies 10% of them) more efficient. At present it is far more costly than extracting them from raw materials. Still, Chinese rare-earth companies, which are almost all state-owned, \"do not rely on economic viability in all cases\", notes Corby Anderson. And to make sure its recycling plants are fed, China is also quietly reassuming its old role in the waste trade. It is selectively rolling back import restrictions on some foreign waste. In 2024 it eased curbs on imports of some scrap copper and aluminum. Last year it started allowing imports of black mass. In January it slashed import tariffs on the stuff from 6.5% to 3%. Officials have pledged to continue to increase imports of \"high-quality\" recycled materials over the next five years. Still, now that America and Europe want to keep their valuable waste, could they eat into China's recycling dominance? Zhang Yuping, the deputy general manager of GEM, a big Shenzhen-listed battery-recycler, thinks not. \"From the perspective of national security, they might have some justification,\" he says. But the cost of recycling outside China would be high and scaling up difficult. Europe, at least, can't digest such a challenge, reckons Mr Zhang. \"They would pay a heavy cost. \" ■\n⟪中文译文⟫\n全球都在抢矿，中国却开始抢“垃圾”：一块废电池，为什么成了战略资源？ 中国正以惊人的规模，把垃圾变成宝物——尤其是电动车电池，它将帮助中国把关键矿产的供应牢牢锁定在自己手里。 越来越多的电动车，很快就会报废、被换掉。把这些车的电池撕碎，就能产出“黑粉”——一种粉末，从中可以回收并再利用锂、钴、镍这些贵重的金属。从下个月起，欧盟会让这种废物的出口变得更难，目的是鼓励更多黑粉在本地加工（而目前，绝大部分都被运去了亚洲）。8月，美国封锁了所有黑粉的出口——有意思的是，这个国家禁掉了中国的电动车，却还在大量进口中国的电池。 不过，中国不太可能吃亏。国际能源署——一家预测机构——认为，到2050年，黑粉可能占到全球电池金属需求的二到三成。所以，中国正在给自己的回收产业猛踩油门：目标不只是稳住电池金属的供应，还有稀土，以及其他很多东西。它的目标，是让本国经济少一点“线性”（原材料被制成产品，随后被丢弃），多一点“循环”（闭合回路，让回收来的废物顶替原材料）。 曾经，回收这件事是由“对污染的担忧”推动的。而现在，在一场为打造高科技经济所需的原材料而展开的全球争夺中，安全已经成为首要的动机。 20 世纪80 年代，中国开始进口废物，接收那些富裕国家不要的金属器件、纸张和塑料。一个杂乱蔓延的非正规回收行业应运而生，主角是在垃圾堆里翻捡的拾荒者。这些废物中的大部分，最终进了填埋场，或者索性被露天焚烧，渗出有毒的化学物质，制造出有毒的烟雾。 在废物管理这件事上，中国仍然远远落后于那些一尘不染的欧洲国家（不过到底落后多少，很难说清，因为中国并不公布关于回收的详细全国数据）。它较为贫困的农村地区，至今仍常常垃圾遍地。但至少，官员们让中国的大城市变得更干净、也更宜居了。而现在，他们的重心正在转移——为了安全而利用回收。今年 7 月，中国发布了一份面向2030 年的循环经济发展规划。该规划指出：“资源安全的重要性日益凸显，而地缘政治的……已经变得复杂而严峻。” 这个判断建立在两个考量之上。第一，中国想要主导高科技制造业——从机器人技术到风力发电机，无所不包。第二，为了实现这一点，它必须确保原材料的供应、接入更多电力线路、并建造更多数据中心；而这需要大量的铝、铜、电池金属和稀土。中国虽然掌控着这些材料全球精炼产能的大部分，但它的矿石却大部分依赖进口。与此同时，许多西方国家想要减少对中国工厂的依赖。官员们担心，他们可能会设法挤压中国的供应。去年，一家国有研究机构的一位研究者说，如果没有回收，中国未来将“持续被卡脖子”。 中国已经在大量生产这类产品——这一点，本应让回收变得更容易操作。工厂可以对设计稍作调整，好让产品在日后更容易拆解。而且，许多制造电池的技术（比如用酸和高温把有价值的金属分离出来），同样被用于回收电池。以宁德时代为例——它是全世界最大的电池制造商。这家中国公司的销售大部分在国内，而这些电池最终会被一家名叫邦普的宁德时代子公司处理，邦普是全球最大的电池回收商。正是这样的安排，帮助中国建起了全球85% 的电池回收产能。 中国正试图让稀土回收变得更高效——它声称，目前已有10% 的稀土供应来自回收。目前，稀土回收的成本远高于从原材料中提取稀土。不过，中国的稀土企业几乎全都是国有企业，它们“并非在所有情况下都依赖经济可行性”——科里·安德森如此指出。 而且，为了确保它的回收厂有料可吃，中国还悄悄地重新扮演起了它在废物贸易中的老角色。它正有选择地放宽对某些外国废料的进口限制。2024 年，它放松了对部分废铜、废铝进口的管控；去年，它开始允许进口黑粉；今年1 月，它又把这玩意儿的进口关税从6.5% 猛砍到3%。官员们已承诺，未来五年将继续增加“高品质”再生材料的进口。 不过，既然美国和欧洲现在都想留住自己手里的宝贵废料，那它们能否蚕食中国在回收领域的主导地位？深圳上市公司、大型电池回收企业格林美的副总经理张宇平认为不会。他说：“从国家安全的角度看，它们这么做或许有些道理。”但在中国境外做回收，成本会很高，而且扩大规模会很困难。张先生认为，至少欧洲是“消化不了”这样的挑战的。“它们将付出沉重的代价。”","2026-10-14_001":"Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? — 2026/10/14 Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? 2026/10/14 Are 60-Second Microdramas the Future of Hollywood, or the End? 中国短剧杀进好莱坞：一集60秒、一年赚15亿美元，美国都急了！ In 2020, the entertainment mogul Jeffrey Katzenberg brought to life an idea that must have seemed — in the flash of its conception — foolproof: Netflix, but for people addicted to their phones. He called it Quibi: short for \"quick bites.\" It collapsed after eight months in a total void of public interest. And yet, the joke may very well be on us. Because, just six years later, short-form, mobile-first narrative entertainment is the fastest-growing form of streaming worldwide, per Bloomberg. Microdramas, sometimes called \"verticals,\" are TV shows for your phone. They are typically described as soap operas for Gen Z: hypersexual, over the top and — most important — short, with episodes rarely exceeding two minutes in length. The format was born around 2018 in China, where its revenue now exceeds that of the conventional film industry. As I write this, the form is on track to take in $1.5 billion in revenue in the United States by the end of the year. Each short episode opens with a hook and ends with a twist, dragging viewers, like recalcitrant pets, through entire seasons roughly the length of a feature film. In the first years of this decade, a number of Chinese companies began to export the form abroad, creating countless U.S. microdrama apps and coordinating U.S.-based microdrama shoots in order to supply those apps with content. These companies still retain a near monopoly on U.S. production, but that is quickly changing. Hollywood executives — noticing these apps outranking their own streamers on the charts — have started to spin up microdrama platforms of their own. I'd managed to wrangle three days of set visits on a show called \"Bound by Love,\" a production of ReelShort. Culturally, ReelShort is an interesting case: the flagship brand of an American company originally financed — and still partly owned — by a Chinese digital content group. The 9:16 format that fills a vertical phone screen leaves little room to maneuver, requiring actors to stand unnaturally close together. \"Bound by Love,\" which in its finished form would run more than two and a half hours, was to be shot at a variety of locations over the course of just 11 days. The key grip had an explanation for this: The scenes they were shooting that day were for an early episode, one that would appear before the viewer hit the paywall. As at least a dozen people would subsequently inform me, the platforms are not overly invested in the quality of the episodes that come after — i.e., the episodes a viewer has actually paid to see. The goal is to keep them up, which is distinct from telling a good or even coherent story. What is clear with hindsight is that Quibi failed primarily because it was TV — regular old TV — that happened to be on your phone. The intervening years have taught us that what tends to succeed on phones is phone stuff. This is the terrain that ReelShort and its peers have precision-engineered their content to compete on. Fox Entertainment has made an equity investment in the microdrama platform Holywater. Disney+ has begun to produce microdramas of its own, and Paramount+ has a slate scheduled for the fall. Today microdramas are — as more than one person put it to me — the only game in town. \"I was fighting it for a long time, but Old Hollywood is dead.\" Microdramas, she went on, are commercialized, rule-bound, utterly devoid of artistry. \"And this can't be the end of it for me, so I'm joining the bandwagon.\" The male leads I spoke to, specifically, seemed uniformly thrilled by this professional windfall. It's different for the women. Apparently, China's short-form video platforms had recently seen a milestone: more than $12 million spent in one day on advertisements for A.I.-generated microdramas. The country's microdrama production hubs, meanwhile — sites of industrial-scale production only months earlier — were emptying out. Actors who were in demand last year, stars of the form, were doing food delivery just to survive. Gelb was seeing the writing on the wall: \"Experience shows that, in the microdrama world, what happens in China does not stay in China.\" It's a preview for what's to come for the rest of the world. \" Soon after I returned, it emerged that an average of 470 A.I.-generated microdramas were released on Chinese platforms each day this past January. In the same period, COL Group — a part-owner of ReelShort's parent company — said that it planned to replace 75 percent of its actors with A.I. for titles released in China. I spoke to an executive at COL, a man named Timothy Oh. He seemed amused, if puzzled, by the continued American resistance to A.I.-generated content. \"The reality is, content is content,\" he told me. \"People are already watching A.I. slop on Instagram.\" As I write this, A.I. microdramas are all over the ReelShort homepage, and make up a large percentage of their most-watched charts.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n中国短剧杀进好莱坞：一集60秒、一年赚15亿美元，美国都急了！ 60秒微短剧：好莱坞的未来，还是终结？ 2020年，娱乐大亨杰弗里·卡岑伯格推出了一个在当时看来堪称万无一失的创意——简而言之，就是为手机成瘾者量身打造的Netflix。他将其命名为Quibi，取“quick bites”（快咬一口）之意。然而，这款产品上线仅八个月便黯然关停，公众兴趣几近于零。可讽刺的是，笑到最后的或许并不是我们。因为据彭博社报道，仅仅六年之后，短格式、移动优先的叙事娱乐已成为全球流媒体增长最快的品类。 微短剧，有时被称为“竖屏剧”，是专为手机而生的电视剧。人们通常将其描述为Z世代的肥皂剧：尺度大胆、情节夸张，而最关键的是——短，单集时长鲜有超过两分钟的。这一形式约于2018年诞生于中国，如今其营收已超过传统电影产业。在我撰写本文之际，这一形态预计到今年年底将在美国斩获15亿美元的收入。 每集短剧以悬念开场，以反转收尾，像拽着不情愿的宠物一样，拖着观众看完整季——而整季时长大约只相当于一部院线电影。本世纪二十年代初，多家中国公司开始将这一形式输出海外，催生了无数美国微短剧应用，并协调在美国本土拍摄微短剧以供给这些平台。这些公司至今仍近乎垄断着美国的微短剧制作，但这格局正在迅速改变。好莱坞高管们——注意到这些应用在下载排行榜上压过了自家的流媒体平台——已开始纷纷搭建自己的微短剧平台。 我设法争取到了三天探班机会，对象是一部名为《缚爱》（Bound by Love）的剧集，由ReelShort出品。从文化角度看，ReelShort是一个有趣的案例：它是一家美国公司的旗舰品牌，最初由中国数字内容集团出资——且至今仍部分持股。9：16的竖屏格式填满手机屏幕，几乎没有调度余地，迫使演员以极不自然的近距离站在一起。《缚爱》成片时长将超过两个半小时，却要在短短11天内辗转多个场景完成拍摄。 首席置景师对此有一番解释：当天拍摄的场景属于早期集数，也就是观众还没碰到付费墙之前能看到的内容。正如随后至少十几个人告诉我的那样，平台对于付费之后——即观众真正花钱看的那些集数——的质量并不过分上心。目标只是让观众继续看下去，这与讲一个好故事、甚至一个连贯的故事，是两回事。 事后回看，Quibi失败的原因显而易见：它本质上是电视——传统的老式电视——只不过恰好在你的手机上播放。此后的几年教会我们，在手机上容易成功的，是手机原生内容。这正是ReelShort及其同行精准打磨内容、全力竞争的赛道。福克斯娱乐已对微短剧平台Holywater进行了股权投资。Disney+已开始制作自己的微短剧，Paramount+也排定了秋季片单。 如今，微短剧——不止一个人对我这么说——是镇上唯一的游戏。“我抗拒了很久，但旧好莱坞已经死了。”她接着说，微短剧商业化、套路化，毫无艺术性可言。“而这对我来说不可能是终点，所以我选择随波逐流。”与我交谈的男主角们，尤其是他们，似乎无一例外地对这份职业横财欣喜若狂。但对女性而言，情况截然不同。 显然，中国的短视频平台最近见证了一个里程碑：一天之内，AI生成的微短剧广告投放额超过1200万美元。与此同时，这个国家的微短剧制作中心——几个月前还是工业化规模生产的所在地——正在人去楼空。去年还炙手可热的演员、这一形式的明星们，如今靠送外卖勉强度日。格尔布看到了不祥之兆：“经验表明，在微短剧的世界里，中国发生的事不会只留在中国。它是世界其他地区未来的预演。” 我回来后不久，有消息显示，今年1月，中国各平台平均每天上线470部AI生成的微短剧。同一时期，COL集团——ReelShort母公司的股东之一——表示计划在中国发行的作品中用AI替换75%的演员。我与COL的一位高管蒂莫西·吴交谈过。他对美国人对AI生成内容的持续抵制似乎觉得好笑，又有些不解。“现实就是，内容就是内容，”他告诉我，“人们已经在Instagram上看AI垃圾了。”在我撰写本文之际，AI微短剧充斥着ReelShort的主页，并在其最受欢迎排行榜中占据很大比例。","2026-10-13_001":"After the deadliest attack in Saudi Arabia in more than a decade, Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman faces growing pressure to find a way to stop the Iran-backed Houthi militia in Yemen from firing missiles and drones at his country. Yet the prince, Saudi Arabia's de facto ruler, has few options as he confronts an asymmetrical conflict with an adversary that has become stronger since he led a disastrous war against it a decade ago, analysts say. The Houthis have carried out three fatal attacks on an international airport in the Saudi capital, Riyadh, over the past week. Those included a strike on a passenger terminal on Saturday that killed at least 12 people and injured more than 300, many of them critically, according to the Saudi authorities. They have also hit at the heart of Prince Mohammed's ambitions to transform Saudi Arabia from an insular, oil-dependent nation into a global hub for business and tourism. But there is no easy way for the crown prince to halt the Houthis' attacks. A political resolution with the militia has become increasingly difficult to reach as its confidence and demands grow, and attempting to defeat the Houthis militarily is unlikely to work either, analysts say.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n胡塞一周三袭沙特首都机场，致12死300多人伤！沙特这么有钱、武器这么强，为什么却拿胡塞没办法？ 沙特进退维谷：胡塞武装直指其宏图要害——胡塞武装对沙特首都机场发动的三次致命袭击，凸显了这场冲突的不对称本质。 十多年来最致命的袭击发生之后，穆罕默德王储面临越来越大的压力，必须设法阻止伊朗支持的也门胡塞武装继续向沙特发射导弹和无人机。然而，这位沙特事实上的统治者几乎没有选择余地——分析人士指出，他面对的是一场不对称冲突，而对手自十年前他发动那场灾难性战争以来，反而变得更加强大。 过去一周，胡塞武装对沙特首都利雅得的一座国际机场发动了三次致命袭击，其中周六针对航站楼的袭击造成至少12人死亡、300多人受伤，许多人伤势危重——数字来自沙特当局。这些袭击也击中了穆罕默德王储宏图的核心：他要把沙特从一个封闭、依赖石油的国家，变成全球商业和旅游的枢纽。 但王储想叫停胡塞的袭击，并没有轻松的办法。随着这支武装的信心与诉求水涨船高，与之达成政治解决愈发困难；而试图在军事上击败它，同样不太可能奏效——分析人士如是说。这个组织没什么可失去的，又在与更强大、更富有的对手的一次次交手中，被磨炼得越发身经百战。 中东研究所的沙特问题专家F. 格雷戈里·高斯说：“对沙特而言，唯一现实的出路，是结束伊朗和美国之间的这场战争——由美国出面，让伊朗约束胡塞，使其承诺停止敌对行动，作为整体协议的一部分。”他接着说：“对沙特不利的是，在这件事上，特朗普总统究竟听他们几分，并不明朗。” 两周后，穆罕默德王储本将主办他的旗舰年度投资峰会，届时各国政要、亿万富翁和企业高管预计会涌入这座城市，与沙特官员和基金经理推杯换盏。高斯说：“沙特的问题是，胡塞打击其基础设施给它造成的损失，要比反过来大得多。一次对利雅得机场的袭击，就可能让那些正在考虑投资这个国家的人重新掂量。” 近几天，胡塞军方发言人叶海亚·萨雷一再向航空公司和旅客发出警告，劝阻他们使用沙特机场，并宣布这些机场是“我方部队的作战区域”。 胡塞统治着一片饱受轰炸摧残的乡村地带，那里大部分关键基础设施早已被摧毁。分析人士说，他们似乎正在盘算：自己能给沙特——世界最大的石油出口国之一——造成的痛苦，要多于沙特能给他们造成的痛苦。 沙特与胡塞之间的暴力升级始于7月：当时一次空袭击中了也门首都萨那的国际机场，而萨那由胡塞控制。胡塞指责沙特是幕后推手；沙特支持的也门政府则宣称对这次袭击负责，称其当时是为了阻止一架未经授权的伊朗飞机降落。对萨那机场的袭击引发了一连串事件，最终在上周演变成胡塞与沙特及也门政府之间全面战争的重燃。 胡塞武装在2014年崛起掌权：这一年，他们攻占萨那，击溃了国际社会承认的政府。不久后，当时年仅29岁的穆罕默德王储，在担任沙特国防大臣后的最初举措之一中，宣布向胡塞开战，誓言恢复也门政府——而在当时，胡塞不过是一群乌合之众。 如今41岁的王储，已在沙特国内外巩固了自己的权力。然而，也门的这支武装同样变得更强、更能自给自足，它给王储出的难题，是他迄今面对过的最棘手的一道。 萨那机场遇袭后，长期以来对沙特强制实施的领空限制耿耿于怀的胡塞宣布，将奉行“以封锁对封锁”的政策，对红海上的沙特航运实施禁运。由于伊朗战争打乱了经霍尔木兹海峡的货运，红海早已成为沙特石油出口的一条重要替代通道——如今，这条通道也被盯上了。 沙特高级王室成员、前沙特情报机构负责人图尔基·费萨尔亲王表示，尽管沙特在外交政策上历来偏好“克制”，但胡塞已经超出了沙特领导层耐心的极限。他上周在接受沙特《阿拉伯新闻》采访时说：“克制，已不再可行。”","2026-10-12_001":"An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid — 2026/10/12 An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid 2026/10/12 An International Career Forged by the Struggles of Apartheid Navi Pillay, who won this year's Nobel Peace Prize, fought racism in South Africa, shaping her international career as a judge and advocate for justice and human rights. She lived her childhood in a slum without sewage or water, attended a school for only children of Indian descent, and knew that her skin color prohibited her from going to the beach her father would drive past during the Christmas season. But she also described a pivotal moment that helped her rise from under the thumb of racism: Parents in her community in the coastal city of Durban pooled their money to send her to university, after her high school principal said she had potential. That opportunity began a barrier-breaking career that led Ms. Pillay to becoming a lawyer, a judge on multiple international courts, the human rights chief of the United Nations and, on Friday, a Nobel Peace Prize laureate. In announcing the prize, the Nobel committee said that Ms. Pillay had been \"instrumental in ensuring that war crimes, crimes against humanity and genocide are prosecuted.\" She becomes the fifth South African to win the award, joining the ranks of the country's most vaunted figures including Nelson Mandela and Archbishop Desmond Tutu. While much of Ms. Pillay's global recognition comes from human rights work she has done abroad, her experiences in South Africa have indelibly shaped her life, and she has remained deeply invested in her home country's sometimes-fragile democracy. \"I see this award as the voice of the majority of the world,\" Ms. Pillay said in an interview with the South African state broadcaster on Friday. She added that a majority of South Africans \"want the rule of law, they want the independent judiciary to be respected.\" She studied law at what is now the University of KwaZulu-Natal. Because it was segregated, Black students had to attend classes at night, said Jessica Neuwirth, a human-rights lawyer and activist who has known Ms. Pillay for four decades. During the day, Ms. Pillay would go to the library, where she found books about the Nuremberg trials, helping inspire her fascination with international justice, Ms. Neuwirth said. When the apartheid government locked up her husband, Gaby Pillay, who was also a lawyer, for political activities and cut off his access to the outside world, Ms. Pillay used his power of attorney to file an action on his behalf, Ms. Neuwirth said. She ended up winning an order prohibiting his torture. Ms. Pillay's struggle to maneuver within a racist system applying unjust laws in some ways helped her develop the persistence she needed, she has said in interviews. She said in a podcast interview last year that she would keep filing legal motions even if they had little chance of succeeding because she believed that was the only weapon she had to fight the system. \"It was clear to me the only thing I can do is keep going, seeking justice,\" she said. She rose to international prominence shortly after apartheid ended in the 1990s, as a judge on the tribunal on the Rwanda genocide, which broke new ground in prosecuting the crime of genocide and treating mass rape as a form of genocide. Ms. Pillay later served as a judge on the International Criminal Court, the U.N. High Commissioner for Human Rights, and a substitute judge of the International Court of Justice. The prize, however, was not welcomed everywhere. Speaking at a rally in Syracuse, New York, on Friday evening, President Trump spent about 10 minutes attacking the decision, describing Ms. Pillay as a \"mystery person\" he had never heard of and warning Norway that he would not forget it. On social media, he said he deserved eight Nobel Peace Prizes for the wars he claims to have settled, though some of those conflicts continue and others were ended largely by other parties. The chairman of the Norwegian Nobel Committee replied calmly, saying the panel is independent of the government and bound only by the will of Alfred Nobel. Ms. Pillay, for her part, had already joked to an applauding crowd in Nuremberg that if they kept clapping, she might share the prize with Mr. Trump.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n从贫民窟女孩到诺贝尔和平奖！今年诺贝尔和平奖得主到底是什么来头？ 一段国际职业生涯，由种族隔离的斗争锻造而成——纳维·皮莱，今年的诺贝尔和平奖得主；她曾在南非与种族主义抗争，正是这段经历，塑造了她作为法官、作为正义与人权倡导者的国际生涯。 她的童年，安放在一个没有下水道、没有自来水的贫民窟里；她就读的，是一所只向印度裔孩子开放的学校；而她打小就知道，自己的肤色是一道禁令——父亲每年圣诞节驱车经过的那片海滩，永远不许她踏足。 但她也说起过自己命运的支点——那一刻，她得以从种族主义的压制之下起身：只因高中校长一句“这孩子有潜力”，德班这座滨海城市里，社区的家长们便你一份我一份地凑出钱来，送她进了大学。正是这个机会，开启了她一路破壁的职业生涯：律师、多个国际法庭的法官、联合国人权事务负责人，直到上周五——诺贝尔和平奖桂冠加身。 宣布奖项时，诺贝尔委员会表示，皮莱“在确保战争罪、危害人类罪与种族灭绝罪得到追诉方面，发挥了关键作用”。她由此成为第五位获此殊荣的南非人，与纳尔逊·曼德拉、德斯蒙德·图图大主教等这个国家最受敬仰的名字，站进了同一列。 尽管皮莱享誉全球，多半凭借的是她在海外的人权事业，但真正不可磨灭地塑造了她一生的，是南非这片土地上的经历；而她始终深深牵挂着祖国那尚显稚嫩的民主。“我将这个奖，看作世界多数人的声音，”皮莱周五接受南非国家电视台采访时说。她补充道，大多数南非人“要的是法治，要的是独立的司法体系受到尊重”。 她攻读法律的地方，是如今名为夸祖鲁-纳塔尔大学的学府。据相识皮莱四十载的人权律师、活动家杰西卡·纽沃斯回忆：彼时学校实行种族隔离，非白人学生只能入夜上课。于是白天，皮莱便整日泡在图书馆——正是在那里，她读到了纽伦堡审判的卷宗，也正是这些书，点燃了她对国际正义毕生的着迷。 据纽沃斯讲述：种族隔离政府以“政治活动”为由，将皮莱的丈夫加比·皮莱——同为律师——投入狱中，并切断他与外界的一切联系。皮莱拿出丈夫早已签好的授权委托书，以丈夫本人的名义提起诉讼，最终赢得了一道法院命令：禁止对他施以酷刑。 皮莱在采访中说，在一个施行不义之法的种族主义体制内周旋求存，某种程度上炼就了她所需要的那种坚持。她在去年的一档播客中讲道：即使那些法律动议胜算渺茫，她也会继续一份一份地递交——因为她相信，那是她与整个体制抗争的唯一武器。“我心里清楚得很，我唯一能做的，就是走下去，追索正义。”她说。 上世纪九十年代种族隔离终结后不久，皮莱即跻身国际显要之列——出任卢旺达种族灭绝问题特别法庭法官。该法庭首开先河：既将种族灭绝罪真正诉诸审判，亦首次把大规模强奸（mass rape）认定为种族灭绝的一种形式。此后，她先后出任国际刑事法院法官、联合国人权事务高级专员，以及国际法院特任法官。 诺贝尔奖可能令皮莱面临新一轮的审视，部分源于她就巴勒斯坦人处境所持的鲜明立场：她去年主持的联合国调查委员会曾得出以色列在加沙地区实施种族大屠杀的结论，而她获奖一事也引来了以色列总理本雅明·内塔尼亚胡的强烈斥责。但一位了解她的受访者表示，考虑到皮莱的出身来历，批评吓不退她——在种族隔离时期，她对危险有着透彻的认知，也深知应对危险的实际办法。 然而，这个奖项并非处处受欢迎。周五晚，川总在纽约州锡拉丘兹的一场集会上，用了约十分钟抨击这一决定，称皮莱是他从未听说过的“神秘人物”，并警告挪威他不会善罢甘休。他在社交媒体上自称理应获得八次诺贝尔和平奖，以表彰他声称已经平息的那些战争——尽管其中一些冲突仍在继续，另一些则主要由其他当事方终结。挪威诺贝尔委员会主席平静回应：委员会独立于政府，唯受阿尔弗雷德·诺贝尔遗嘱之约束。而皮莱这边，早已在纽伦堡对欢呼的人群开玩笑说——你们再这样鼓掌，我恐怕就得把这个奖与川分享了。","2026-10-11_001":"Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself — 2026/10/11 Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself 2026/10/11 Superstar podcasts are Silicon Valley's way of talking to itself 马斯克、奥特曼、黄仁勋都在上：播客为什么突然成了硅谷最值钱的流量入口？ EACH WEEKDAY morning Jordi Hays and John Coogan, two entrepreneurs-turned-podcasters, enter a sauna in Los Angeles with copies of the Wall Street Journal. They come out with jackets, ties and hot takes on the tech news of the day. Then they go on air as hosts of the \"Technology Business Programming Network\" (\"TBPN\") podcast, riffing with guests, banging gongs and blowing airhorns. Silicon Valley laps it up. It is not uncommon to see \"TBPN\", rather than CNBC, streaming on the TV screens in tech firms' offices. Podcasts are the latest example of a power law in Silicon Valley. Only a few become big hitters. Those that do attract the demigods of tech as guests, making them even more influential. \"TBPN\" has interviewed Sam Altman of OpenAI and Mark Zuckerberg of Meta. The \"All-In\" Podcast, hosted by four plutocrats who straddle tech and MAGA politics, held a summit in Los Angeles in September attended by Elon Musk and Jensen Huang. (When Donald Trump phoned Nvidia's boss on stage during the summit, Mr Huang put the president on speakerphone.) All share an in-crowd mystique bordering on incestuousness. In April OpenAI reportedly spent $100m-200m to buy then-18-month-old TBPN (though there was no mention of the affiliation during Mr Altman's interview). The \"All-In\" hosts are self-described \"besties\" with many of their billionaire interviewees. They are also an echo chamber, with a tin ear at times to the concerns of the rest of America. This new podcasting elite reflects a big change in Silicon Valley's relationship with the media. During the last great investment boom, the dotcom era of the 1990s, publications like Wired were tech-industry gospels. They covered the internet like Rolling Stone wrote about rock'n'roll, says John Battelle, a founding editor of Wired: \"We thought we were covering the counterculture.\" But the rise of social media and the growing power and wealth of big tech generated a rift between Silicon Valley and news outlets. Podcasters, who share their guests' techno-optimism, provide a friendlier platform. The watershed came during the 2024 presidential race. Led by Mr Trump, politicians saw the medium as a less filtered way of getting their message out than the press or broadcast TV. Two of its billionaire co-hosts, David Sacks and Chamath Palihapitiya, threw a fundraiser in San Francisco for Mr Trump. Shortly afterwards he appeared on their show. That helped raise the profile of the hosts, as well as of tech podcasting in general. Helping them thrive is what would be called, in Silicon Valley jargon, a flywheel. Just before he hit the big time in 2024, Mr Patel tweeted his bank balance. It showed savings of $0.14. Then Mr Zuckerberg came onto the show and the advertising dollars poured in. As more prominent executives take part, not only do advertisers notice; so do potential customers, investors and jobseekers, raising the quality of the audience. \"This is luxurious media with very valuable eyeballs,\" says Josh Kaplan, boss of Smooth Media, which represents podcasters. The format is particularly well-suited to Silicon Valley, where founders invent stories for a living. \"We're not journalists; we are enthusiasts,\" says Patrick O'Shaughnessy, whose podcast, \"Invest Like the Best\", has a strong following in VC circles. These offerings may be popular, but do not expect them to invest in the costly investigative work that uncovers frauds, such as that at Theranos, a health-tech startup, exposed by the Wall Street Journal in 2015. Instead, AI is at the centre of their discussions. Unlike many parts of America, they treat it as almost the only topic worth discussing. But this obsession has further pushed Silicon Valley into a \"monoculture\", says David Senra, an influential podcaster. Recently he has been to tech dinners where conversation veers only between AI optimism and doomerism. \"It sounds like they are all reading the same tweets,\" he says. Lulu Cheng Meservey, a PR consultant to tech founders, reckons nine-tenths of people in tech speak only to other people in tech. Podcasts exacerbate that. They may be well-meaning, she says, but \"when a billionaire AI salesman is telling you whether to buy AI, you are taking that from someone who is entirely unrelatable to you.\" It may get worse. Silicon Valley's podcasters sprinkle their conversations with reverence for space missions, robots and other techno-wizardry. In an Alpocalypse, they would come out better off than most people. Mr Battelle calls it the \"golden ticket on the rocket ship\". If all else fails, they can podcast from Mars. But will people still want to listen to them?\n⟪中文译文⟫\n马斯克、奥特曼、黄仁勋都在上：播客为什么突然成了硅谷最值钱的流量入口？ 超级明星播客：硅谷的自说自话——但对世界大多数地方而言，这声音刺耳得很 每个工作日的清晨，乔迪·海斯和约翰·库根——两位从创业者转型而来的播客主——带着几份《华尔街日报》走进洛杉矶的一间桑拿房。出来时，他们已穿戴好西装领带，对当天的科技新闻也有了一肚子热辣点评。随后，他们以“科技商业节目网”（简称“TBPN”）播客主持人的身份开播，与嘉宾你来我往、敲锣鸣笛、好不热闹。硅谷对此趋之若鹜。在科技公司办公室的电视屏幕上，播放“TBPN”而非CNBC，已屡见不鲜。 播客是硅谷“幂律法则”的最新注脚。唯有极少数能脱颖而出，而一旦跻身头部，便能请来科技界的半神级人物做嘉宾，从而更具影响力。“TBPN”曾采访过OpenAI的萨姆·奥尔特曼和Meta的马克·扎克伯格。由四位横跨科技圈与MAGA政治的巨富主持的“All-In”播客，九月在洛杉矶举办了一场峰会，埃隆·马斯克和黄仁勋均到场出席。（当唐纳德·特朗普在峰会现场致电英伟达老板时，黄仁勋直接开了免提。） 这些播客无一不带着一种近乎“近亲繁殖”的圈内秘辛气息。据报道，四月间OpenAI斥资1亿至2亿美元收购了当时仅成立18个月的TBPN（不过在奥尔特曼接受采访时，双方均未提及这层关系）。“All-In”的主持人们自称与许多亿万富翁受访者是“死党”。他们同时也是一个回音壁，对美国其余民众的关切时常充耳不闻。 这批新兴播客精英折射出硅谷与媒体关系的巨大变迁。在上一个投资狂潮——1990年代的互联网时代——《连线》等刊物曾是科技行业的福音书。正如《连线》创始编辑约翰·巴特尔所言，他们报道互联网，就像《滚石》报道摇滚乐：“我们觉得自己在报道一场反文化运动。”然而，社交媒体的崛起以及科技巨头日益膨胀的权力与财富，在硅谷与新闻媒体之间划出了一道裂痕。播客与嘉宾共享技术乐观主义，提供了一个更为友善的平台。 转折点出现在2024年总统大选期间。以特朗普为首，政客们发现这一媒介比报刊或电视广播更能绕过过滤、直抵人心。“All-In”的两位亿万富翁联合主持人大卫·萨克斯和查马斯·帕里哈皮蒂亚在旧金山为特朗普举办了一场筹款活动。不久之后，特朗普便做客他们的节目。这既提升了主持人的知名度，也推动了科技播客的整体崛起。 助推他们蓬勃发展的，用硅谷行话来说，是一个“飞轮”。2024年即将大红大紫之际，帕特尔在推特上晒出了自己的银行余额——储蓄账户里只有0.14美元。随后扎克伯格上了节目，广告收入便滚滚而来。随着越来越多知名高管参与其中，不仅广告商注意到了，潜在客户、投资者和求职者也纷至沓来，从而提升了受众质量。“这是拥有极宝贵眼球的奢华媒体，”代理播客的Smooth Media老板乔什·卡普兰说道。 这种形式与硅谷尤为契合——在这里，创始人们本就以编故事为生。“我们不是记者，我们是爱好者，”帕特里克·奥肖内西说道。他的播客“像最优秀的人一样投资”在风投圈拥有大批拥趸。这些节目或许颇受欢迎，但别指望它们会投入高昂的调查成本去揭露欺诈——比如2015年《华尔街日报》曝光的健康科技初创公司Theranos丑闻那样的事。 取而代之的是，AI占据了他们讨论的核心。与美国许多地方不同，他们几乎将AI视为唯一值得一谈的话题。但这种执念进一步将硅谷推入了一种“单一文化”，颇具影响力的播客主播大卫·森拉如是说。最近他参加了几场科技晚宴，席间话题只在AI乐观主义和末日论之间来回摇摆。“听起来他们都在读同样的推文，”他说。科技创始人的公关顾问陆露·程·梅瑟维认为，科技圈九成的人只跟科技圈内的人交流。播客加剧了这一点。她说，他们或许出于善意，但“当一个亿万富翁AI推销员告诉你该不该买AI时，你是在听一个与你完全无法共情的人说话。” 情况可能更糟。硅谷的播客们在对话中不时流露出对太空任务、机器人和其他技术奇迹的崇敬。若真迎来AI末日，他们的处境会比大多数人好得多。巴特尔称之为“火箭船上的黄金船票”。万一一切都不灵了，他们还能从火星上做播客。但到那时，还有人愿意听吗？","2026-10-10_001":"What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare — 2026/10/10 What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare 2026/10/10 What Experts Want to Know About the Russia Plague Scare A 28-year-old worker at a research laboratory in Siberia recently died of pneumonia. The facility studies plague, among other pathogens, raising fears that she had been exposed at the laboratory. But Russian health officials so far have not linked the death of the worker, Darya Shipilova, to infection with Yersinia pestis, the bacterium that causes plague. And despite early reports that dozens had been quarantined near the research facility, health officials said that there was no risk of an epidemic. Still, the death has prompted concerns around the world, in part because of the Soviet Union's long history of advanced bioweapons research, which led to outbreaks of anthrax and smallpox. After unconfirmed reports of a second unusual case of pneumonia in the region, the World Health Organization sought additional information from Russian officials. President Trump said he planned to speak to President Vladimir V. Putin of Russia \"very soon\" about the death. Early reports cited local officials saying that Ms. Shipilova was exposed to Y. pestis during her work at the Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and the Far East. Y. pestis was responsible for two of history's deadliest pandemics: the Black Death and the Plague of Justinian. As a lung infection, plague is contagious, although not as infectious as some other respiratory infections. (The bacterium also infects the bloodstream and lymph nodes.) In its early stages, plague can be successfully treated with antibiotics. There are hundreds of cases each year, mostly in Africa, according to the W.H.O.. Russian health officials said that Ms. Shipilova had died of pneumonia of unknown cause. While plague can lead to pneumonia, the filling of the lung's air sacs with fluid, it can have many other causes, including seasonal flu. \"Unfortunately, young people can die from pneumonia, so that development alone wouldn't be enough to raise concern,\" said Dr. Tom Inglesby, an epidemiologist at Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security. \"What I do think is concerning is the Russian reaction so far.\" More than 200 people who had been in contact with the deceased were quarantined in local hospitals, and Russian officials have not explained why, Dr. Inglesby said. Nor have they announced the cause of death, if not plague. Lab accidents can and do happen, even when researchers are following safety protocols. But the lack of transparency surrounding the death and the number of people potentially exposed is unusual, other experts said. \"Something is not adding up about this situation,\" said Jennifer Nuzzo, an epidemiologist at Brown University. \"You don't quarantine 200 people for one plague case,\" she added. \"You don't close multiple hospital units in response to one infection.\" After an unusual infection like this, health officials often provide the genome sequence of the virus or bacterium involved. This helps researchers pinpoint exactly which pathogen is to blame and how to fight it. Public health experts suspect that Russian officials have already sequenced the pathogen involved in Ms. Shipilova's death, and they wonder why that information has not been made public. \"Without that, the risk is uncertain, and I think we should be putting pressure on Russia to share the genome sequence,\" said Dr. Raj Panjabi, a physician and former White House biosecurity official. \"If Y. pestis is confirmed and the presentation was pneumonia, the likely route is inhalation of an infectious aerosol during laboratory work,\" said Dr. Tom Frieden, a physician and former director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. But until more information is released, he added, \"anyone claiming to know more is guessing.\" Thanks to safety protocols and modern personal protective equipment, exposures to pathogens in labs are not common. Y. pestis is a particular concern, Dr. Frieden noted. It requires that laboratories maintain a certain airflow, HEPA filtration, respiratory protection, precautions against aerosols, trained staff members and health monitoring. \"Done right, it's safe,\" Dr. Frieden said. \"The problem is that 'done right' is not verifiable, and it's not easy.\" The last time a fatal exposure to Y. pestis occurred in a laboratory environment was in 2009, when Malcolm Casadaban, a biologist at the University of Chicago, died after contact with a weakened form of the bacterium. When accidents do happen, Dr. Panjabi said, it's important to have a system in place to characterize the outbreak, including how fast the threat is moving, who is at risk and what options are available to mitigate that threat. \"The larger issue of maintaining biosafety in high consequence labs that study dangerous pathogens is a national security issue in every country,\" Dr. Panjabi said. \"And it needs more attention.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n俄罗斯暴发鼠疫？究竟发生了什么？对中国有影响吗？ 关于俄罗斯的鼠疫风波，专家们想知道些什么——一名实验室工作人员之死的细节（包括死因）至今缺失，这让科学家们忧心忡忡。 西伯利亚一家研究实验室的一名28 岁工作人员，近日死于肺炎。这家机构的研究对象包括鼠疫，以及其他多种病原体——这让人不由得担心：她是否在实验室里接触了病原体。但俄罗斯卫生官员迄今尚未将这名工作人员——达里娅·希皮洛娃——的死亡，与鼠疫耶尔森菌（即引发鼠疫的细菌）的感染联系起来。而尽管早期有报道称，该研究机构附近已有数十人被隔离，卫生官员仍表示，不存在流行病风险。 尽管如此，这起死亡仍引发了全世界的担忧——部分原因在于，苏联在先进生物武器研究领域有着长期的历史，这段历史曾导致炭疽与天花的暴发。在该地区出现第二例异常肺炎的未经证实报道之后，世界卫生组织已向俄罗斯官员索要更多信息。川总表示，他计划“很快”就这起死亡与俄罗斯总统普京通话。 早期报道援引当地官员的说法称，希皮洛娃女士在伊尔库茨克“西伯利亚与远东抗鼠疫研究所”工作期间接触过鼠疫杆菌。鼠疫杆菌是历史上最致命的两次大流行——黑死病与查士丁尼瘟疫——的元凶。作为一种肺部感染，鼠疫具有接触传染性，但其传染力不及某些其他呼吸道感染。（这种细菌还会感染血液与淋巴结。）在发病早期，鼠疫可以通过抗生素成功治愈。据世界卫生组织统计，每年仍有数百例病例，主要出现在非洲。 俄罗斯卫生官员称，希皮洛娃女士死于原因不明的肺炎。虽然鼠疫可导致肺炎——即肺泡被液体充盈——但肺炎还有许多其他成因，包括季节性流感。“遗憾的是，年轻人也会死于肺炎，因此，仅凭这一情况并不足以令人担忧，”约翰斯·霍普金斯健康安全中心的流行病学家汤姆·英格尔斯比博士表示。“我确实认为令人担忧的，是俄罗斯迄今为止的反应。” 英格尔斯比博士表示，与死者有过接触的200 余人被隔离在当地医院，而俄罗斯官员并未说明原因。他们也没有公布死因——如果不是鼠疫的话。实验室事故确实会发生，即便研究人员遵循了安全规程。但其他专家表示，围绕这起死亡的透明度缺失，以及可能暴露的人数之多，都非同寻常。 “这件事有点对不上号，”布朗大学的流行病学家珍妮弗·努佐说。“你不会为了一个鼠疫病例去隔离200 个人，”她补充道。“你也不会为了应对一起感染，就关掉好几个医院病区。” 在发生这样一起异常感染之后，卫生官员通常会公布所涉病毒或细菌的基因组序列。这有助于研究者精确锁定究竟是哪种病原体该为此负责，以及该如何应对。公共卫生专家怀疑，俄方官员已经对希皮洛娃之死所涉的病原体完成了测序，他们不解的是，这些信息为何始终未被公开。“没有这些信息，风险就是不确定的，我认为我们应当向俄罗斯施压，要求其共享基因组序列，”拉杰·潘贾比博士表示。他是一名医生，曾任白宫生物安全官员。 “如果鼠疫杆菌得到确认，且其临床表现是肺炎，那么最可能的感染途径，就是在实验室工作中吸入感染性气溶胶，”汤姆·弗里登博士说。他是一名医生，曾任美国疾病控制与预防中心主任。但他补充说，在更多信息公布之前，“任何声称了解更多的人，都只是在猜测。” 得益于安全规程和现代个人防护装备，实验室中的病原体暴露并不常见。弗里登博士指出，鼠疫杆菌尤其令人担忧。它要求实验室维持特定的气流、HEPA 过滤、呼吸防护、防气溶胶措施、受过训练的工作人员以及健康监测。“如果做对了，它是安全的，”弗里登博士说。“问题在于，‘做对了’这件事无法被验证，而且它并不容易。” 实验室环境中上一次发生致命的鼠疫杆菌暴露，是在2009 年——当时，芝加哥大学的生物学家马尔科姆·卡萨达班在接触了一种减毒形式的该细菌后死亡。潘贾比博士表示，当事故真的发生时，重要的是要有一套到位的系统，用以刻画这次暴发的特征，包括威胁扩散的速度、谁处于风险之中，以及有哪些可用手段能够减轻这一威胁。“在研究危险病原体的高后果实验室中维持生物安全，这个更大的问题，在每个国家都是国家安全问题，”潘贾比博士说。“而且它需要得到更多关注。”","2026-10-09_001":"Inside the Nobel Prize in literature — 2026/10/09 Inside the Nobel Prize in literature 2026/10/09 Inside the Nobel Prize in literature Alfred Nobel was a keen but mediocre writer. The Swedish dynamite tycoon wrote unpublished poems and a play featuring a 40-minute torture scene. To the dismay of relatives keen to inherit his fortune, his handwritten will of 1895 endowed five awards, including a prize for literature. Nobel instructed \"the Academy in Stockholm\" to give it to the writer of \"the most outstanding work in an ideal [idealisk] direction\". The Swedish Academy spent decades puzzling over what \"ideal\" meant, before quietly dropping the criterion. Next Thursday, a door will open in a gilded hall in Stockholm and the Academy's formally dressed permanent secretary will step forward to announce, in Swedish and English, the winner of this year's literature Nobel. Now prediction markets favour Indian writer Amitav Ghosh, the Chinese avant-garde author Can Xue or the American novelist Thomas Pynchon to win the SKr12mn (£910,000) in prize money — though nobody really knows, as today's Academy is less leaky than it was before a #MeToo scandal prompted a sweeping overhaul. And the pundits usually guess wrong. The prize is ritually mocked. The Nigerian poet Chinweizu Ibekwe once marvelled at \"the conceit that a gaggle of Swedes... should pronounce on intellectual excellence for the whole wide world\". And yet, says Daniel Medin, professor of comparative literature at the American University of Paris: \"The Nobel is the big one for international fiction. There is no comparison.\" The Nobel shapes the global canon — even if the globe can push back. There has always been a comic discrepancy between the weight of the prize and the parochialism of the judges. The Academy's 18 life members — known as \"The Eighteen\" — are Swedish writers, literary historians, linguists and a jurist, some of them obscure even in Sweden. There were barely any female members until this century. The very first award, in 1901, made international news. Somehow nobody nominated Leo Tolstoy, whereas 17 members of the Academie Francaise endorsed the French poet Sully Prudhomme, and he won. The literature prize has never been as controversial as the frankly political Peace Prize; but because greatness in literature is more subjective than in the sciences, the Academy's choice reliably angers critics. They delight in drawing up lists of overlooked greats — James Joyce, Franz Kafka, Virginia Woolf, Marcel Proust, Constantine Cavafy, et cetera — though some of these achieved renown only after death. The prize was at its most politicised during the cold war, when the Academy chose the unreliable Soviet writer Boris Pasternak (1958) and dissidents Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn (1970) and Joseph Brodsky (1987). Pasternak initially accepted the nobelevka, as the award is popularly known in Russia — but then, under pressure from the regime, rejected \"this undeserved prize which has been presented to me\". Jean-Paul Sartre, who refused the Nobel in 1964, noted among his reasons that the prize was \"reserved for the writers of the West or the rebels of the East.\" By the 1980s, the Academy — earlier than most of the Western literary establishment — began looking beyond the west. A \"Nobel committee\" of four or five academicians whittles down the nominations to a shortlist of five, which the Eighteen spend the summer reading. They decide in early October. Translation has become central to the award. The Nobel has helped make the international canon more inclusive. After going global, the prize has recently become gender-equal. This followed a #MeToo scandal in 2017, when 18 women accused the French husband of Academician Katarina Frostenson of sexual assault and rape. He was later jailed. Several members quit — although the body's rules forbid resignation — and the post-scandal \"Eighteen\" included many more women. They awarded the 2018 prize, a year late, to Polish writer Olga Tokarczuk, and there have been three female laureates since 2018. In fact, notes Kaj Schueler, author of a Swedish book on the prize, there's been a precise pattern: male and female victories alternate. If that continues, then after Krasznahorkai's win in 2025, this year's laureate would be a woman, perhaps it will be Xue's year? The winners' reactions are part of the award's lore. John Steinbeck in 1962, when asked whether he deserved the Nobel, replied, \"Frankly, no.\" Many critics agreed. Samuel Beckett (1969) was on holiday in Tunisia with his wife Suzanne when a telegram from his French publisher arrived: \"In spite of everything, they have given you the Nobel Prize. I advise you to go into hiding.\" Suzanne, when told the news, exclaimed, \"Quelle catastrophe.\" Beckett dubbed December 10, the annual date of the award ceremony, \"Nobloodybeldamday\". Bob Dylan, winner in 2016, ignored the Academy's phone calls and didn't acknowledge the prize for over a fortnight.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n诺贝尔文学奖即将揭晓：村上春树年年被提，残雪再次成热门，今年到底会是谁？ 诺贝尔文学奖内幕 阿尔弗雷德·诺贝尔本人是一位热衷写作却才情平平的文人。这位瑞典炸药大亨写过未发表的诗作，还写过一部戏，其中有一幕长达四十分钟的酷刑场景。令那些盼着继承遗产的亲属们大失所望的是，他1895年的手写遗嘱设立了五个奖项，其中包括文学奖。诺贝尔嘱咐“斯德哥尔摩的学院”将其颁给“在理想[idealisk]方向上创作出最杰出作品”的作家。瑞典学院花了数十年琢磨“理想”究竟何所指，最终悄然将这一标准弃之不用。 下周四，斯德哥尔摩一座金碧辉煌的大厅里将有一扇门开启，学院身着正装的常务秘书将走上前来，用瑞典语和英语宣布今年诺贝尔文学奖得主。目前预测市场看好印度作家阿米塔夫·高希、中国先锋派作家残雪或美国小说家托马斯·品钦摘得1200万瑞典克朗（约合91万英镑）的奖金——不过无人真正知晓，因为如今的学院比#MeToo丑闻引发全面改革之前守口如瓶得多。而评论家们的猜测通常都是错的。 这个奖项历来被人揶揄。尼日利亚诗人钦韦祖·伊贝奎曾惊叹于“这样一种自负：一帮瑞典人……竟要对全世界的智识卓越发号施令”。然而，巴黎美国大学比较文学教授丹尼尔·梅丁说：“诺贝尔奖是国际小说界的头等大事。无可比拟。”诺贝尔奖塑造着全球经典——即便全球也能反推回去。 奖项的分量与评委的狭隘之间，始终存在着一种滑稽的落差。学院的18位终身院士——人称“十八人”——是瑞典作家、文学史家、语言学家和一位法学家，其中有些人即便在瑞典也鲜为人知。直到本世纪，女性院士都寥寥无几。1901年首次颁奖便成为国际新闻。不知何故，无人提名列夫·托尔斯泰，而法兰西学院17位院士联名推举法国诗人苏利·普吕多姆，于是他得了奖。 文学奖从未像坦率政治化的和平奖那样引发争议；但由于文学上的伟大比科学上更具主观性，学院的选择总能可靠地激怒批评家。他们乐此不疲地罗列被忽视的大师名单——詹姆斯·乔伊斯、弗朗茨·卡夫卡、弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫、马塞尔·普鲁斯特、康斯坦丁·卡瓦菲斯等等——尽管其中有些人是在身后才声名鹊起。 冷战期间，该奖政治色彩最为浓烈，学院先后选中了难以归类的苏联作家鲍里斯·帕斯捷尔纳克（1958年）以及异见者亚历山大·索尔仁尼琴（1970年）和约瑟夫·布罗茨基（1987年）。帕斯捷尔纳克起初接受了“诺贝尔奖”（俄语俗称nobelevka）——但随后在政权压力下，拒绝了“这一授予我的不应得的奖”。1964年拒领诺贝尔奖的让-保罗·萨特，在其理由中指出该奖“专为西方作家或东方叛逆者而设”。 到了1980年代，学院——比大多数西方文学建制更早——开始将目光投向西方之外。由四五位院士组成的“诺贝尔委员会”将提名筛选至五人短名单，十八位院士用整个夏天阅读。他们在十月初做出决定。翻译已成为评奖的核心环节。 诺贝尔奖助推国际经典变得更加包容。走向全球之后，该奖近年来又实现了性别均衡。这缘于2017年的#MeToo丑闻，当时18名女性指控院士卡塔琳娜·弗罗斯滕松的法国丈夫性侵和强奸。他后来入狱。数名院士辞职——尽管学院章程禁止辞职——丑闻后的“十八人”中女性大增。他们将2018年奖项迟延一年颁给波兰作家奥尔加·托卡尔丘克，而自2018年以来已有三位女性得主。事实上，瑞典一本关于该奖的著作的作者卡伊·许勒指出，存在一个精确的规律：男女得主交替获奖。若此规律延续，那么在克拉斯诺霍尔卡伊2025年获奖之后，今年的得主将是一位女性，或许今年该轮到残雪了？ 获奖者的反应也是这个奖项传奇的一部分。1962年，约翰·斯坦贝克被问及是否配得上诺贝尔奖时答道：“坦白说，不配。”（许多批评家深表赞同。）1969年，塞缪尔·贝克特正与妻子苏珊娜在突尼斯度假，其法国出版商发来一封电报：“尽管发生了这一切，他们还是把诺贝尔奖给了你。我建议你躲起来。”苏珊娜得知消息后惊呼：“真是灾难。”贝克特将12月10日——颁奖典礼的固定日期——戏称为“诺贝尔倒霉日”。2016年得主鲍勃·迪伦无视学院的多次来电，超过两周未对获奖作出回应。","2026-10-08_001":"Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? — 2026/10/08 Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? 2026/10/08 Why Does Anyone Care About the Nobel Prize? \"Just go to sleep,\" Martin Chalfie's wife told him late one October night in 2008. Chalfie, a Columbia University chemist, had co-authored an influential paper describing a new method for studying cells with green fluorescent protein, and he was anxiously waiting to see whether it would be recognized. His wife, a fellow academic, tempered his expectations. \"It's a wonderful tool, and lots of people use it,\" he recalls her saying. \"But, to tell you the truth, it will never win the Nobel.\" So when the call from Sweden came in, Chalfie was asleep. This past week, the 2024 Nobel class was introduced to the world, creating a new crop of Olympian figures. Chalfie might tell you that the award hasn't changed his day-to-day life all that much, that his grant applications still sometimes get denied. But the medal radiates from his chest even when he's not wearing it. The Nobel is the honor above all honors. The achievements it rewards—in science, literature, and peace—contribute \"to the greatest benefit of mankind,\" a tagline any other respectable award committee would be too shy to claim. How did a single award from the small nation of Sweden become the undisputed highest honor in the world? Establishing a prize is easy. The hard part is getting anyone to care. Alfred Nobel, a businessman who made his fortune selling dynamite and explosives in the 19th century, was not an obvious candidate to become the namesake of the most prestigious distinction in the world. For the Nobel to become the Nobel, a few people had to get a few things really right. When rich individuals bequeath all their wealth to something other than their would-be heirs, their would-be heirs tend to resist. The Italian businessman Jerome Ponti, for example, had more or less the same vision as Nobel. But after his surviving relatives learned that he had donated his fortune to national academies headquartered in London, Paris, and Vienna in 1874, a group of them successfully litigated to void the will. In the 1890s, the executors of Nobel's estate had to be a bit more clever. They preemptively reached an agreement with Nobel's two living nephews, one of whom was particularly gracious and persuaded the rest of the family to honor the late Nobel's wishes. A second reason the Nobel prevailed over prizes of similar ambition was that Sweden turned it into a nationalist project. Nobel himself did not mention anything about Swedish interests, but the government and the press interpreted his bequest as a call to action for the nation as a whole. \"The Swedish scientific society could not have been invested with a more glorious task,\" a newspaper editorial declared. The effort turned out to be well timed. In the early 1900s, a new prize in Sweden might have seemed unlikely to compete in prestige with one given by the French Academy of Sciences or the Royal Society in London. But when World War I struck, neutral little Sweden had a claim to objectivity that the great powers didn't. Finally, both Nobel and the executors of his will designed the rules wisely. Most prestigious prizes at the time were international only in theory; judges tended to favor their countrymen. Nobel requested that each award be given to the \"worthiest person, whether or not they're Scandinavian.\" The Nobel Prize committee became the first to systematically solicit nominations from institutions abroad, turning the award into a sort of contest among nations. And one of the executors of the will had the idea of rewarding achievements no matter when they had been made, rather than just the work or discoveries of the previous year. This was a clever move, not just because some discoveries can take time to reveal their worth, but also because it turned winning the Nobel into a more sweeping victory. Fortified with its elegant design choices, the Nobel quickly established a first mover advantage. The Scottish economist Angus Deaton told me that nothing comes close to the Nobel ceremony. On the night after he found out he won, Deaton couldn't keep the news to himself. He told his Uber driver on the way home from dinner in Princeton and watched him become ecstatically happy. The driver at first assumed that Deaton had won the peace prize; when he learned it was for economics, his enthusiasm dimmed, but only slightly. A Nobel is a Nobel. Prizes aren't all great. The main ideology the Nobel Foundation appears to be committed to, however, is the importance of preserving the prestige of the Nobel Prize—and in this it has succeeded. If there were a prize for prizes, the Nobel would win every year.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n为什么拿到诺贝尔奖，一辈子都不一样了？这个奖是怎么把自己做成神话的？ 为什么有人会在乎诺贝尔奖？—— 一个多世纪以前，瑞典人完成了一次史上最成功的品牌操盘。 『早点睡吧。』2008年10月的一个深夜，马丁·查尔菲的妻子这样劝他。查尔菲是哥伦比亚大学的化学家，曾与人合著一篇影响深远的论文，开创了用绿色荧光蛋白研究细胞的新方法；此刻，他正坐立难安地等着这项成果能否获得认可的分晓。同为学者的妻子替他降了降预期：『这工具确实出色，用的人也多。可说句实话，它永远拿不了诺贝尔奖。』所以瑞典的电话打来时，查尔菲已经睡着了。 就在刚刚过去的一周，2024届诺贝尔奖得主向全世界亮相，又一批奥林匹斯神般的人物就此诞生。查尔菲大概会告诉你，这个奖并没有给他的日常生活带来多大改变——他申请科研经费，至今仍时不时被拒。但那枚奖章，即便他没戴在身上，也依然在他胸前熠熠生辉。 诺贝尔奖，是荣誉之上的荣誉。它所褒奖的成就——无论出自科学、文学还是和平事业——都“为人类带来了最大的福祉”；而这句宣传语，换作任何一家体面的评奖机构，怕都不好意思说出口。小小瑞典的一枚奖，究竟是如何成为世界公认的最高荣誉的？ 设立一个奖项很容易，难的是让人在乎。阿尔弗雷德·诺贝尔是19世纪靠贩卖达纳炸药与炸药发家致富的商人——怎么看，他都不像会成为这个世界上最负盛名的殊荣的冠名人。 诺贝尔奖之所以能成为今天的诺贝尔奖，是因为有那么几个人，把那么几件事真正做对了。当富人把全部家产遗赠给继承人以外的事物时，这些本该继承遗产的人往往会奋起反抗。意大利商人杰罗姆·庞蒂便是如此——他与诺贝尔的构想大体相同。然而1874年，他在世的亲属得知他已将财产捐给总部设于伦敦、巴黎和维也纳的几家国家级科学院后，其中一伙人打起官司，并成功让遗嘱归于无效。到了19世纪90年代，诺贝尔遗产的执行人们不得不更加高明一些。他们抢先与诺贝尔在世的两位外甥达成协议，其中一位格外宽厚，说服了其余家人尊重这位已故长辈的遗愿。 诺贝尔奖之所以能胜过那些格局相当的奖项，第二个原因在于：瑞典把它办成了一项举国事业。诺贝尔本人对瑞典的利益未置一词，但政府和媒体却把他的遗赠解读为对整个国家的号召。一家报纸的社论宣称：“再没有比这更光荣的使命，可以托付给瑞典科学界了。”这番努力，后来的结果证明时机恰到好处。20世纪初，瑞典新设的这个奖项，看上去根本不可能在声望上与法国科学院或伦敦皇家学会颁发的奖项一争高下。但第一次世界大战爆发后，中立的、小小的瑞典，却拥有了一种列强所不具备的“客观公正”的资格。 最后，无论是诺贝尔本人，还是他遗嘱的执行人，都把规则设计得相当精明。当时大多数有声望的奖项，其“国际性”仅停留在纸面：评委往往偏袒本国同胞。而诺贝尔要求，每个奖项都应颁给“最配得上它的那个人——无论他是不是斯堪的纳维亚人”。诺贝尔奖委员会成为第一个系统性地向海外机构征集提名的奖项，由此把评奖变成了某种国家之间的角逐。此外，遗嘱执行人之一想出了这样的办法：奖励那些成就时，无论其完成于何时，而不是仅限于前一年的工作或发现。这是一步高招——不仅因为有些发现需要时间才能彰显价值，也因为它使“赢得诺贝尔奖”成为一场更为彻底的胜利。 有了这些精妙设计的加持，诺贝尔奖迅速确立起先发优势。苏格兰经济学家安格斯·迪顿对我说，没有什么能比得上诺贝尔颁奖典礼。在得知自己获奖后的第二天晚上，迪顿实在无法把这个消息憋在心里。在普林斯顿吃完晚餐回家的路上，他告诉了载他的优步司机，然后眼看着对方变得欣喜若狂。司机起初以为他得的是和平奖；当听说得的是经济学奖时，他的热情暗了一下——但也只是暗了一点点。诺贝尔就是诺贝尔。 奖项本身未必都是好事。然而，诺贝尔基金会似乎所信奉的唯一“信条”，就是守护诺贝尔奖声望之重要——而在这一点上，它成功了。倘若真有一个“授予奖项的奖项”，那么诺贝尔奖每年都会拔得头筹。","2026-10-07_001":"China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them — 2026/10/07 China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them 2026/10/07 China cultivated high-rolling crime families before turning on them A Post investigation found that criminal networks in Myanmar enjoyed the protection of Chinese officials as well as the military government in Myanmar. For the scion of a crime family linked to human trafficking and enslavement, money laundering and global cyberscams, Wei Qingtao was brazenly public. His Douyin account, the Chinese-language version of TikTok, flaunted the excesses of his life in a remote corner of Myanmar by the border with China: Bentleys and Lamborghinis, rare cigars and private jets. When the 27-year-old partied at the multistory glass-walled nightclub he owned in a region called Kokang, he'd throw crisp Chinese yuan bills into the crowd. In November, the good times rolled to a stop. Wei's social media presence vanished. He soon appeared in a different kind of video: reading a scripted confession while in Chinese custody. \"The money we make from cyberscams comes from the pensions of everyday Chinese people,\" Wei said. \"This time, the Chinese government has made up its mind. ... We must not cling to any illusions.\" Within weeks, his uncle was in cuffs on a plane to China, flanked by dozens of police officers. The clans controlled every aspect of life in Kokang, becoming lawmakers, militia heads, local administrators and powerful business executives. But at the heart of the Kokang empire were casinos, underground banking and money laundering, according to U.N. officials and U.S. diplomatic cables released by WikiLeaks. Newly built mega-casinos in the Kokang capital, Laukkaing, opened 24 hours a day. Behind the vast casino floors, a more sinister industry started to grow. Chinese court documents show that from at least 2018 \"criminal syndicates\" were smuggling people into the Crouching Tiger Villa, a banquet hall and hotel, and forcing them to work as scammers. Border closures that followed the outbreak of the coronavirus pandemic shut off the cash flow to the casinos, accelerating a shift to this new scam economy. Workers, mostly Chinese nationals or ethnic Chinese, were bought, sold and traded; and then beaten, tortured or killed when they didn't reach financial targets or tried to escape, according to firsthand accounts. Many of the compounds for running scam operations were hosted in the same hotels and casino complexes long established by the clans. Others were purpose-built, but had telltale signs like barred windows, high walls and even Myanmar military snipers on the rooftops, witnesses said. A handyman, hired to build soundproof phone booths within a scam compound in a hotel owned by the Liu family, said he was interrupted by screams as he worked. He said he saw a supervisor beating three men, their hands tied behind their backs, with lead pipes. A Taiwanese woman, who was trafficked into Laukkaing, told The Post she was waterboarded when she tried to escape. A Chinese man, who was forcibly brought into Kokang by knife-wielding men after responding to an acting job online in China, said he saw others in his compound chained and beaten. At one point, four people were shot when they fought back during beatings and tried to seize guns from the guards, he added in an interview. The Crouching Tiger Villa was among the most brutally run compounds, witnesses said. There, caged lions, tigers and bears were used to threaten workers who stepped out of line, according to a person who led a team of workers there. By 2023, Beijing was coming under growing domestic and international pressure to act against these operations. The United Nations estimated in a report last year that more than 120,000 people were being forced to work in Myanmar. Signs of official Chinese concern burst into the open in August when the Chinese ambassador warned Myanmar authorities to \"eradicate the cancer of gambling and scams\" that was \"deeply loathed by the Chinese people.\" At the Crouching Tiger Villa on Oct. 20, guards opened fire when more than 100 people tried to leave. The Post could not verify how many died, but four undercover Chinese police officers were among those killed. Confession videos featuring Wei Qingtao and two others — individuals from the Liu family and the Ming family — began to appear on Chinese media. Two of the Mings were arrested in Myanmar by local police and handed over to China. In December, China issued arrest warrants for 10 more heads of the Bai, Wei and Liu families, and their children. On Jan. 6, Laukkaing, the capital of Kokang, fell to the MNDAA, returning full control of the region to the rebel group for the first time in 15 years. Since last July, 49,000 scam workers have been returned home to China, according to the Ministry of Public Security. The casinos and hotels owned by the Kokang crime families still stand, but have gone dark. Hanley Interstellar, Wei's club, is deserted. So too is the Crouching Tiger Villa, its walls crumbling and pockmarked with bullet holes, verified videos show. But some of the exotic animals — a tiger and five bears — survived the conflict. They still roam their cages, depending on rebel soldiers to bring their daily meal of chicken.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n缅北电诈“四大家族”覆灭全纪录！被转卖的“猪仔”、晒在屋顶的钱、布满弹孔的墙…… 中国曾培植豪横的犯罪家族，而后对其出手整治 魏清涛出身于一个与人口贩卖、奴役、洗钱及全球网络诈骗有关的犯罪家族，但他却毫不遮掩、招摇过市。他的抖音账号——即TikTok的中文版——大肆炫耀其在缅甸与中国边境交界偏远一角奢靡无度的生活：宾利、兰博基尼、稀有雪茄和私人飞机。这位27岁的年轻人若在他位于果敢地区、拥有多层玻璃幕墙的夜店里开派对，便会将一沓沓崭新的百元人民币大钞撒向人群。 到了11月，这段纸醉金迷的好日子戛然而止。魏清涛在社交媒体上的身影消失无踪。不久后，他出现在另一类视频中：在中国羁押之下，照本宣读一份认罪供词。“我们搞网络诈骗赚的钱，都是中国老百姓的养老钱，”魏清涛说。“这次中国政府下定决心……我们绝不能抱有任何幻想。”数周之内，他的叔叔便戴着手铐、在数十名警察押解下被带上飞往中国的飞机。 这些家族曾掌控果敢地区生活的方方面面，摇身变为议员、民兵首领、地方行政官员和权势熏天的商界高管。但据联合国官员及维基解密公布的美国外交电报显示，果敢帝国的核心是赌场、地下钱庄和洗钱。果敢首府老街新建的大型赌场，全天24小时营业。 在广阔的赌场大厅背后，一个更为阴暗的产业开始滋生。中国法院文件显示，至少从2018年起，“犯罪集团”便开始向“卧虎山庄”——一处兼作宴会厅和酒店的场所——偷运人口，强迫他们从事诈骗工作。新冠疫情暴发后边境关闭，切断了赌场的现金流，加速了向这一新型诈骗经济的转型。 第一手证词，这些工人——大多是中国公民或华裔——被买卖和交易；一旦未能达到财务指标或试图逃跑，便会遭到殴打、酷刑甚至杀害。许多用于运营诈骗活动的园区，就设在各家族早已建起的酒店和赌场综合体内。目击者称，另有一些则是专门建造的，但带有明显的标志，如铁栅窗户、高墙，甚至屋顶上还有缅甸军方狙击手。 一名曾受雇在刘家旗下酒店内一处诈骗园区搭建隔音电话亭的杂工说，他干活时被尖叫声打断。他说，他看到一名主管用铅管殴打三名双手被反绑在身后的男子。一名被贩卖到老街的台湾女子告诉《华盛顿邮报》，她试图逃跑时遭到水刑。一名中国男子在中国网上看到一则演员招聘信息后应征，却被持刀男子强行带入果敢；他说，他在园区内看到其他人被锁链锁住并遭殴打。他还在一段采访中补充说，有一次，四个人在挨打时反抗并试图夺取保安的枪支，结果遭到枪击。 目击者称，“卧虎山庄”是管理最为残暴的园区之一。据一名曾在那里带领一队工人的人说，园区内关在笼中的狮子、老虎和熊被用来威胁不听话的工人。到2023年，北京在国内和国际上面临越来越大的压力，要求其对上述经营活动采取行动。联合国去年在一份报告中估计，超过12万人被迫在缅甸从事此类工作。 中国官方对此关切的迹象在8月公开爆发，当时中国大使警告缅甸当局要“铲除赌博和诈骗的毒瘤”，称其“被中国人民深恶痛绝”。10月20日，在“卧虎山庄”，当100多人试图离开时，保安开枪射击。《华盛顿邮报》无法核实死亡人数，但死者中包括四名中国卧底警察。 以魏清涛及另外两人——分别来自刘家和明家——为主角的认罪视频开始出现在中国媒体上。明家两人在缅甸被当地警方逮捕并移交给中国。12月，中国对白家、魏家和刘家的另外10名头目及其子女发出逮捕令。1月6日，果敢首府老街被缅甸民族民主同盟军攻占，该地区15年来首次完全回到这支反叛武装的控制之下。据公安部称，自去年7月以来，已有4.9万名诈骗从业人员被遣返回中国。 果敢犯罪家族拥有的赌场和酒店依然矗立，但已人去楼空、漆黑一片。魏清涛的夜店“汉利星际”已荒废。“卧虎山庄”同样如此，经核实的视频显示，其墙壁坍塌，弹孔密布。但一些异国动物——一只老虎和五只熊——在冲突中幸存下来。它们仍在笼中徘徊，依靠反叛武装士兵每天送来鸡肉为食。","2026-10-06_001":"The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind — 2026/10/06 The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind 2026/10/06 The AI industry is booming. Women are getting left behind There's a common fear among people who work in Silicon Valley: snag one of the fast-growing, high-paying jobs in artificial intelligence, or get trapped in the \"permanent underclass\", a phrase describing the fate of those who won't have upward mobility in the age of AI. The phrase usually describes a future in which AI automates human jobs. But for women in tech, it's starting to look like the present. \"A lot of people say: 'Oh, AI is lowering barriers, it's equalizing the playing field for women,\" said Urvashi Batra, co-founder and CEO of Prioriwise, an AI platform for IT service providers. \"I actually think it's the opposite.\" Batra said people take her less seriously as the founder of an AI company than her male co-founder. When pitching investors, she and her co-founder have learned that they are more likely to get an investment if he does the pitching. Women made up only about a quarter of new hires in AI roles in the last year, compared to 50% of new hires in non-AI roles, according to a recent report from LinkedIn. In executive roles, that number drops to just 13%. At the same time, LinkedIn's research shows that women are more likely to work in roles with high exposure to AI disruption, like customer service, meaning they are also at higher risk for job loss due to AI. AI jobs, whose postings have doubled since 2023, come with a salary premium, on average paying more than twice as much as roles that don't involve working with AI. But women who do have jobs in AI are disproportionately concentrated in low-paying roles, like data annotators. Across all AI occupations, men have $45,000 higher median pay than women, partly due to the types of jobs they are likelier to have. The ongoing trend could produce the greatest gender pay gap in generations and leave women out of building a technology that shaped the economy. \"What we're witnessing is a sort of backsliding,\" said Brenda Darden Wilkerson, president of AnitaB.org, a non-profit devoted to advancing women in tech jobs. Initiatives to promote women in the tech industry have been ongoing for years, with mixed results. Women now hold about one-third of tech jobs in the US. But research has found that women leave the tech industry at a much higher rate than their male counterparts, citing factors like non-inclusive cultures and barriers to advancement. Women working in AI may face even greater challenges. \"AI moves extremely fast, and the pressure to keep up is immense,\" said Jayeeta Putatunda, an AI engineering lead at investment firm Turing. \"I have seen throughout my career so many amazing women I know kind of fall through the cracks.\" Putatunda said it's not uncommon to be the only female engineer on a team, and that when women are outnumbered, they have to speak up louder to make their ideas known. Mentorship in the AI field, especially by other women, can be hard to come by. And the breakneck pace of AI advancements means anyone working in the field should expect to put in long hours in order to keep up. Those kinds of work expectations can make it impossible for everyone to stay in the field. \"I went on maternity leave for four months, and by the time I came back, there were completely different frameworks and levels of models,\" Putatunda said. Getting caught back up was overwhelming. She said it was only possible with the help of supportive co-workers and a husband who equitably split childcare. The AI industry is so new and fast-moving that it should in theory be more meritocratic. No one has a decade of experience working with a model developed one year ago, which should mean everyone has a level playing field. But instead the field has indexed more on personal networks and other loose signals, according to some women who work in AI. The political climate has made it more challenging to get AI companies to support initiatives focused on women, said Felicia Newhouse, founder of AI Powered Women, an organization that promotes women's participation and leadership in AI. Newhouse, a product marketer who has worked in technology companies for two decades, points to the Department of Justice targeting corporations with DEI programs, claiming the initiatives violate anti-discrimination laws. Still, she said the risk that women are left behind in the AI workforce was something that kept her up at night. \"We're talking about who captures a major new source of economic mobility,\" she said. \"The deepest risk is that a participation gap becomes a power gap.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\nAI红利爆发，女性却“两头吃亏”：为什么她们既更难进高薪岗位，又更容易被替代？ AI行业正在飞速发展，女性却被甩在后面——她们只占新增AI岗位的极小一部分，却在容易被AI冲击的岗位上占比过高。 硅谷的职场人有一个共同的恐惧：要么抢到AI领域那些快速增长的高薪岗位，要么被困在“永久底层”——这个词描述的是在AI时代无法向上流动的那群人的命运。它通常被用来描述一个AI取代人类工作的未来。但对科技行业的女性来说，它开始看起来像是现在。 “很多人说：‘哦，AI在降低门槛，它在为女性创造公平的竞争环境。’”IT服务商的AI平台Prioriwise的联合创始人兼CEO Urvashi Batra说。“我恰恰认为，情况是相反的。”Batra说，作为AI公司的创始人，人们不如对待她的男联合创始人那样认真对待她。在向投资人路演时，她和她的联合创始人已经摸出一个规律：如果由他来路演，拿到投资的概率更大。 根据LinkedIn最近的一份报告，过去一年AI岗位的新员工中，女性只占大约四分之一，而非AI岗位的新员工中，女性占了50%。在高管职位中，这个数字骤降到只有13%。与此同时，LinkedIn的研究显示，女性更可能从事那些高度暴露于AI冲击的岗位，比如客服，这意味着她们因AI而失业的风险也更高。 AI岗位的招聘信息自2023年以来翻了一倍，而且带有薪资溢价，平均薪酬是不涉及AI的岗位的两倍多。但那些确实在AI行业工作的女性，却不成比例地集中在低薪岗位上，比如数据标注员。在所有AI职业中，男性的中位数薪酬比女性高45,000美元，部分原因在于他们更可能从事的那些岗位类型。 这一持续的趋势可能造成几代人以来最大的性别薪酬差距，并让女性被排除在构建一种塑造了经济的技术之外。“我们正在目睹的，是一种倒退，”致力于推动女性从事科技工作的非营利组织AnitaB.org的主席Brenda Darden Wilkerson说。 推动女性进入科技行业的各种倡议已经进行了多年，结果好坏参半。现在美国科技岗位中，女性占了大约三分之一。但研究发现，女性离开科技行业的速度远高于男性同行，给出的因素包括不包容的文化和晋升障碍。 在AI行业工作的女性，可能面临更大的挑战。“AI发展极快，跟上的压力是巨大的，”投资公司Turing的AI工程负责人Jayeeta Putatunda说。“在我的整个职业生涯中，我见过太多我认识的优秀女性，某种程度上被漏掉了。”Putatunda说，在一个团队里成为唯一的女工程师并不罕见，而且当女性在人数上处于劣势时，她们必须更大声地说话才能让自己的想法被知道。AI领域的导师指导，尤其是来自其他女性的指导，很难获得。而AI进步的那种危险的高速，意味着在这个领域工作的人都应该预期要投入长时间才能跟上。 那种工作预期会让每个人都无法留在这个领域。“我休了四个月产假，等我回来的时候，框架和模型层级已经完全变了，”Putatunda说。追赶上进度让人不堪重负。她说，只有靠支持她的同事和一个公平分担育儿的丈夫，这（追赶上进度）才成为可能。 AI行业如此之新、如此快速变化，以至于从理论上说，它应该更任人唯贤。没有人有十年经验去使用一个一年前才开发出来的模型，这应该意味着每个人都站在公平的起跑线上。但事实恰恰相反，根据一些在AI行业工作的女性的说法，这个领域更依赖于个人人脉和其他模糊信号。 政治气候使得让AI公司支持聚焦女性的倡议变得更加困难，AI Powered Women的创始人Felicia Newhouse说。该组织致力于推动女性在AI领域的参与和领导力。Newhouse，一个在科技公司工作了二十年的产品营销人，指出美国司法部正在针对那些设有DEI项目的公司，声称这些倡议违反了反歧视法。 不过，她说，女性被落在AI劳动力之外的风险，是让她夜不能寐的事情。“我们在讨论的是谁将抓住一个重要的新经济流动来源，”她说。“最深层的风险是，参与差距会变成权力差距。”","2026-10-05_001":"Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs — 2026/10/05 Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs 2026/10/05 Investing for babies involves knotty trade-offs Patience, consistency and frugality are fine habits for an investor. They are not, however, common virtues among newborn babies. The little bundles of joy are impulsive short-term thinkers. They are also some of nature's biggest freeloaders, living entirely beyond their means. All the same, for new parents with a soft spot for the magic of compounding, like your columnist, a baby presents a tantalising investment opportunity. Many decades of life and potential returns stretch out into the future: $1,000 invested today with a real annual return of 5.2% (the average for global stocks over the past 125 years) would be worth $58,000 by the time the child is 80. Yet these juicy financial returns must be considered alongside another sort of investment: in the children themselves. Spending on a child's health, education and skills can yield even higher returns (on top of helping them lead a fulfilling life). So how should a new parent think about their budding little portfolio? Potential financial investments are manifold. Many countries offer tax-advantaged ways for parents to save for their children. American tykes are now eligible for Trump accounts, which start with a $1,000 freebie from the federal government that can be put into mutual funds or index trackers until the child turns 18, while taxes are deferred. Earlier options include the even more tax-friendly 529 accounts, but these may be used solely for specified educational purposes. In Britain, the Junior Individual Savings Account, into which up to £9,000 ($11,900) a year can be invested, is tax friendlier still. Investing for babies is a joy even without the tax perks. Long horizons smooth out volatility, allowing for more risk. Ian Ayres and Barry Nalebuff, two economists, propose that young workers should borrow to buy stocks, since the long-term returns to equities are (hopefully) higher than the interest on borrowing. If the logic is true for 20-somethings, it is truer still for infants with a horizon a couple of decades longer. Some parents fear that creating a large trust fund will turn their offspring into layabouts. Andrew Carnegie, a 19th-century American steel magnate, gave away most of his wealth out of fear that it would dull the work ethic of his descendants. A more recent study of Norwegian inheritances between 2000 and 2004 found that those bequeathed NKr300,000 (some $40,000 at the time) or more reduced their working hours by 7-10% in the six years that followed. This makes investments in human capital more appealing. In the 1970s and 1980s Gary Becker and Nigel Tomes, another pair of economists, theorised that parents were better off investing in education and skills early on. For example, teaching a child to read is not hugely expensive, but opens up huge opportunities for future earnings. Investing in financial assets starts to make sense later, once those human-capital investments encounter diminishing returns. A wealth of empirical evidence has since backed up the theory. Beyond basic skills, however, investing in human capital gets fraught. Such investments are sunk costs that cannot be liquidated. They are also infungible: tutoring in advanced maths is not the same as Chinese lessons. Children react differently (and unpredictably) to each. The extraordinarily long timeframes that make investing for babies attractive also raise uncertainty. Perhaps the skills that are valuable today will be obsolete in an AI future? So what is a new parent to do? The most obvious financial options involve accepting free money. The $1,000 in a Trump account is a no-brainer. Even rabid anti-Trumpers would be mad not to take it. All parents should also accept similar grants from several American states. Those who expect to be on the hook for university tuition would be wise to take advantage of 529 accounts or similar vehicles as early as possible, so that compounding can work its magic to the full. Any money that children are likely eventually to inherit is better off in tax-friendly shelters than in their parents' brokerage accounts. Investments can misfire: violin lessons may generate little more than screeching and even a modest trust fund could encourage laziness. But with a bit of luck, investing in a child's future and on their behalf will pay off handsomely. Fortunate parents with the ability to do both should approach this like the rest of parenting: try different things and don't agonise too much over your decisions.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n给孩子留100万，还是花100万培养他？哪一种回报更高？ 给婴儿做投资，是一道难解的取舍题——新手父母该押注金融资本、人力资本，还是别的什么？ 耐心、自律、节俭，是投资者身上难得的好习惯。可惜啊，这些美德在新生儿身上基本见不着。这些被襁褓裹着的“小开心果”，是一群冲动的短期主义者。他们还是大自然里头号的白吃白喝党——花的钱，一分不是自己挣的。 话说回来，对于那些对复利魔力毫无抵抗力的新手父母——比如本栏作者我——一个婴儿，简直就是一个馋得人抓心挠肝的投资机会。几十年的人生和潜在回报，就这么一路向未来铺展开去：今天投进1000美元，按5.2%的实际年化回报算（这是过去125年全球股市的平均水平），等孩子80岁那年，这笔钱将值58000美元。 不过，这些肥美的金融回报，还得跟另一种投资摆在一起掂量——那就是投到孩子本身。花在孩子健康、教育和技能上的钱，回报率可能还要更高（顺带还能让他这辈子过得充实一点）。那么问题来了：新手爸妈该怎么看待自己手上这个刚刚发芽的小小“投资组合”呢？ 可选的金融工具五花八门。不少国家都给父母准备了带税收优惠的攒钱通道。美国的小娃娃现在够资格开“特朗普账户”了——开户联邦政府就白送1000美元，这笔钱可以投进共同基金或指数基金，一直放到孩子满18岁，期间税款递延。更早就有的选项里，还有税收优惠更大的529账户，但这笔钱只能花在规定好的教育用途上。而在英国，少年个人储蓄账户（Junior ISA）每年最多可以投进9000英镑（约合11900美元），税上还要更划算。 就算没有那些税收优惠，给婴儿做投资本身也是件乐事。时间一拉长，波动就被熨平了，于是你反倒敢多担一点风险。经济学家伊恩·艾尔斯和巴里·奈尔伯夫就主张，年轻的上班族应该借钱买股票——理由是，股票的长期回报（但愿）会高过借钱的利息。要是这套逻辑对二十来岁的人站得住，那对投资期限还要再长上二三十年的婴儿来说，就更站得住了。 也有父母担心，给孩子留一大笔信托基金，会把后代养成游手好闲的废人。19世纪的美国钢铁大王安德鲁·卡内基，就是出于这份担忧——怕钱会磨钝子孙的奋斗心——把大半身家都捐了出去。一项更近的研究，考察了挪威2000至2004年间的遗产继承，结果发现：凡是继承到30万挪威克朗（当时约合4万美元）以上的人，在随后的六年里，工作时长减少了7%到10%。 这么一来，投资人力资本就显得更有吸引力了。上世纪七八十年代，加里·贝克尔和奈杰尔·汤姆斯这另一对经济学家就从理论上论证过：父母趁早把钱投在教育和技能上，更划算。举个例子，教孩子认字花不了几个钱，却能为他将来的收入打开巨大的空间。至于往金融资产上投钱，那是后面的事——等人力资本的投入开始遭遇边际收益递减，再投也不迟。此后几十年间，大量实证研究都为这套理论提供了支撑。 不过，一旦越过基础技能这条线，投资人力资本就开始变得棘手了。这类投入是沉没成本，收不回也变不了现。它们还彼此不可替换：补高阶数学，和上中文课，根本是两码事。而孩子对每一样的反应都不一样——还没法预测。更麻烦的是，正是那个让“给婴儿投资”显得诱人的超长时间跨度，同时也把不确定性一路推高。说不定今天还值钱的本事，到了AI主导的未来，早就作废了呢？ 那么新手父母究竟该怎么做？最明摆着的几个金融选项，其实都是在“白捡钱”。特朗普账户里那1000美元，想都不用想就该拿——哪怕你恨特朗普恨得牙痒痒，不去领这笔钱也只能说是傻。美国几个州还有类似的补助，所有家长也都该领。那些预计要自己承担大学学费的父母，明智的做法是尽早用上529账户或类似的工具，好让复利把魔力发挥到极致。至于那些孩子迟早会继承的钱，与其搁在父母自己的证券账户里年年交税，不如早点挪进免税的“庇护所”。 投资也会哑火：小提琴课最后换来的，可能只是一片刺耳的噪音；哪怕只是一笔不大的信托，也可能把人养懒。但只要运气不算太差，投在孩子身上的，和替孩子打理的，最终都会有相当可观的回报。那些有余力两头兼顾的幸运父母，不妨把这件事当成育儿的其余部分一样来对待：多试试不同的路子，别为自己的决定纠结太久。","2026-10-04_001":"A hurricane is coming to tax systems — 2026/10/04 A hurricane is coming to tax systems 2026/10/04 A hurricane is coming to tax systems THE YIELD on ten-year US Treasuries has charged through the 5% barrier. The spread between French government bonds and German Bunds is at levels not seen since the euro-zone crisis in 2012. Britain's newish prime minister has this week set out a plan for spending bolstered by a taboo-busting promise to curb state-pension increases—and yet is still in a fiscal pickle. Across the rich world, countries are struggling to coax more growth out of their economies. And that's not the worst of it. As we explain this week, the tax base in all countries, which depends heavily on taxing labour, may be upended by artificial intelligence. Add these problems together, and it is as if a mighty hurricane were about to hit the rich world's tax systems—which are hopelessly unprepared. The way the state raises money is wasteful, unfair and perverse. You would have thought governments would be scrambling to strengthen the system before the hurricane makes landfall, but as ever they are running away from tax reform instead. The accumulated flaws of modern tax systems are so ubiquitous that they have become part of the economic backdrop. Taxes are full of needless complexity. In Germany the rate of value-added tax (VAT) on oat milk is more than double that on cow's milk, for example. Such widespread special treatment has ruinous fiscal consequences. Across the OECD, a club of mostly rich countries, consumption taxes raise only 58% of what they could if there were no carve-outs and no evasion. Tax systems also create perverse incentives. When a parent in Britain increases their net income from £99,999 ($131,500) to £100,000, they automatically lose free child care worth thousands of pounds. This can prompt some of Britain's most productive workers to cut their hours or overstuff their pensions. And taxes are widely seen as unfair. In Britain the median employee pays 55% more tax than their self-employed counterparts. Many people feel the rich aren't doing their bit. In California voters will decide in November whether to charge a one-off 5% levy on residents with wealth of over $1bn. No one knows when the fiscal weather will turn. But rapid advances in AI could accelerate matters. Taxes on labour are the workhorse of most tax systems worldwide, raising around half of all revenue in the OECD. In the coming years AI could well displace workers or cut their wages even as corporate profits rise, shrinking labour's share of GDP. This in turn could reduce revenues and increase benefits payments, making deficits even harder to support. Optimists may hope that superfast growth will make the maths easier to handle. Yet The Economist has looked at what would happen in rich countries if labour's share of national income fell by ten points. By our calculations, Italy's budget deficit would double as a share of GDP and Germany and France would not fare much better. In addition, the capacity for tax avoidance would no longer be limited by human ingenuity. Point countless AI agents at Britain's 23,000-page tax code and hundreds of new tax-avoidance strategies are likely to emerge—and don't bet on the Treasury to keep up. What to do? Given that AI is likely to mint vast fortunes for a select few, countries may be inclined to follow California's proposal and introduce wealth taxes. Yet such taxes will probably backfire, either because they are too difficult to administer or because they cause rich people to flee. Previous attempts have proved unworkable. In 1990, 12 rich countries taxed fortunes; today just four do. A more fundamental overhaul is needed. Wealth should be taxed more via capital-gains taxes, which currently overpenalise normal returns and undertax supernormal ones. A start would be to introduce a higher headline rate and allowances that exempt normal returns. At the same time, the burden on labour should lighten. That would be a hedge against disruption from AI, and remove an obstacle to employment in countries where labour taxes are crushing. In Belgium they constitute 52% of the costs of employing a person. Consumption should be taxed more heavily, with a focus on ending the litany of exemptions. That would have the useful side-effect of taxing the spending of the very rich. This might not curb the influence Elon Musk can buy, but it would ensure the state gets a bigger cut when Mr Musk buys another plane. Finally, codes should be radically simplified. None of this will be easy. The losers will hiss more loudly than the winners will cheer. But change is coming, like it or not. The country that gets ahead of the hurricane will one day thank the politicians who had the foresight to act.\n⟪中文译文⟫\nAI抢走工作以后，更麻烦的问题来了：政府以后靠谁交税？ 飓风将至：全球税收体系面临大考 美国十年期国债收益率已突破5%大关。法国国债与德国国债的利差，已攀升至2012年欧债危机以来的最高水平。英国新首相本周公布了一份支出计划，以打破禁忌的承诺——限制国家养老金增幅——为支撑，却依然深陷财政泥潭。放眼整个富裕世界，各国都在苦苦挣扎，试图从经济中挤出更多增长。然而，这还不是最糟糕的。正如本周我们所阐释的，所有国家的税基——严重依赖劳动所得税——可能将被人工智能彻底颠覆。 将这些问题叠加在一起，就仿佛一场猛烈的飓风即将袭击富裕世界的税收体系——而这些体系却毫无准备，令人绝望。国家筹集资金的方式，既浪费，又不公，还扭曲。你或许以为各国政府会在飓风登陆前争先恐后地加固体系，但一如往常，他们非但不改革税制，反而避之唯恐不及。 现代税收体系的积弊无处不在，早已成为经济背景的一部分。税制充斥着无谓的复杂性。譬如在德国，燕麦奶的增值税税率是牛奶的两倍多。如此普遍的特殊待遇，带来了灾难性的财政后果。在经合组织——一个以富裕国家为主的俱乐部——中，消费税的收入仅为理论潜在收入的58%，而这还是在没有豁免、没有逃税的理想情况下。 税收体系还制造了扭曲的激励。在英国，当一位家长将净收入从99,999英镑（约合131,500美元）提高到100,000英镑时，他会自动失去价值数千英镑的免费育儿福利。这可能促使英国一些最高产的劳动者减少工时，或过度缴纳养老金。 此外，税收被普遍视为不公。在英国，普通雇员的纳税额比同等收入的自雇人士高出55%。许多人觉得富人们没有尽到自己的本分。今年11月，加利福尼亚州的选民将决定是否对财富超过10亿美元的居民征收一次性5%的税。没有人知道财政风暴何时来临。但人工智能的飞速发展可能加速这一切。劳动所得税是全球大多数税收体系的顶梁柱，在经合组织国家中贡献了约一半的财政收入。未来几年，人工智能很可能取代工人或压低其工资，即便企业利润上升，劳动收入占GDP的比重也将随之缩水。这反过来又会减少税收、增加福利支出，使赤字更加难以为继。 乐观者或许希望，超高速增长会让数学题变得容易些。然而，《经济学人》研究了如果劳动收入占国民收入的比重下降10个百分点，富裕国家将会发生什么。根据我们的测算，意大利的预算赤字占GDP之比将翻倍，德国和法国也好不到哪里去。此外，避税的能力将不再受限于人类的聪明才智。将无数人工智能代理指向英国长达23,000页的税法，数百种新的避税策略便可能应运而生——别指望财政部能跟得上。 那该怎么办？鉴于人工智能很可能为少数人铸造巨额财富，各国或许会倾向于效仿加州的提案，开征财富税。然而，此类税收很可能适得其反，要么因为管理难度太大，要么因为导致富人外逃。此前的尝试已被证明行不通。1990年，有12个富裕国家征收财富税；如今只剩下4个。 需要的是更根本性的改革。应当通过资本利得税对财富加大征税力度——当前的资本利得税对正常回报惩罚过重，对超常回报征税又太轻。起步之举可以是引入更高的名义税率，同时对正常回报予以豁免。与此同时，劳动所得的税负应当减轻。这既是对人工智能冲击的缓冲，也能消除那些劳动税负沉重的国家的就业障碍。在比利时，劳动税占雇佣一名员工总成本的52%。 消费税应当加重征收，重点在于终结一长串的豁免清单。这将带来一个有益的副作用：对巨富的消费征税。这或许无法遏制埃隆·马斯克能买到的影响力，但能确保当他再买一架飞机时，国家能分到更大的一杯羹。最后，税法应当大幅简化。这一切都不会容易。输家的嘶吼会比赢家的欢呼更响亮。但变革正在到来，无论你喜不喜欢。那些抢先于飓风行动的国家，终将感谢那些有远见采取行动的政治家。","2026-10-03_001":"A Near Disaster on Flydubai — 2026/10/03 A Near Disaster on Flydubai 2026/10/03 A Near Disaster on Flydubai This unbelievable sequence of events has huge implications. Today, somewhere over Saudi Arabia, the co-pilot of an Israel-bound Flydubai Boeing 737 stabbed the other pilot and pitched the nose of the plane toward the desert below. The jet descended 17,000 feet in two minutes, at an acceleration violent enough to make your phone and in-flight snack jump off your tray and be pinned to the ceiling. Other crew members and at least one Israeli passenger broke into the cockpit and subdued the assailant. Relief pilots then took over and landed the plane in Tabuk, Saudi Arabia. After a second Flydubai plane took the 174 passengers to Tel Aviv, one of the passengers who’d stormed the cockpit told Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu that his actions had been effective in part because he had watched the disaster-porn documentary series Mayday, about aviation catastrophes, and had known to pull back on the yoke. Whether today’s near-disaster was terrorism or merely attempted mass murder depends on the attacker’s motive. Either way, it is a public-relations catastrophe for Flydubai, the budget cousin of the airline Emirates. Since the United Arab Emirates opened diplomatic relations with Israel in 2020, the airline has connected the two countries with few interruptions, even for acts of war and terror. Keeping that air route open and safe has taken a great deal of effort. Last year, I witnessed clever security engineering at Dubai’s terminal 1: placement of the check-in desk for El Al, the Israeli carrier, adjacent to the desk for Iran’s Mahan Air. Anyone tempted to attack Israeli passengers would hit Iranian ones, and vice versa. Checked baggage costs extra, but the experience of being a human shield while waiting in line is complimentary. Mahan Air no longer flies into Dubai. U.S. sanctions have halted much of its operations, and Dubai had already curtailed its flights, on the grounds that Iran can fly either missiles into Dubai or commercial flights, but not both. Israel, by contrast, has emerged from the 2026 Iran war closer to the UAE, which has absorbed more Iranian attacks than any other country. Although the Arab countries of the region have been allied as the Gulf Cooperation Council since 1981, that alliance proved useless as soon as the missiles and drones started flying. But Israel responded as an ally, by sending defense systems and advisers. At least one Iranian strike during the early phases of the war hit near the Israeli consulate in Dubai. One possible political motive for the Flydubai incident would be to strain and punish the relationship between the two countries. The relationship between Israel and the UAE is unlikely to snap over a single attack. The relationship between the UAE and Netanyahu himself is more delicate. In the past several weeks, the president of the UAE, Mohammed bin Zayed Al Nahyan, was implicated in a report from Haaretz that severely damaged Netanyahu’s chances of remaining in office after next month’s Israeli election. The report alleged that days before the massacre of October 7, 2023, MBZ had personally warned Netanyahu that an attack was imminent but Netanyahu had done nothing to act on the warning. The UAE government at first refused to comment on the story. On Sunday, however, Netanyahu visited Abu Dhabi, and the AP reported that he asked MBZ to deny that he’d warned of an attack. Yesterday, in New York, UAE Foreign Minister Abdullah bin Zayed did exactly that. \"Sheikh Mohammed did not call Prime Minister Netanyahu and say there is going to be an attack on Israel,\" Abdullah said in a meeting at the Council on Foreign Relations, calling his denial \"categorical.\" He said that using a leader-to-leader call to pass along secrets was \"not our style,\" and he left open the possibility that a warning had been passed between governments, if not between leaders. This denial releases some pressure on Netanyahu without absolving his government of its failures. The New York Times and others have reported that the first officer who allegedly tried to bring down the plane is an Omani national. Both are reportedly still in Saudi Arabia. The next part of the story will involve determining whether the pilot acted alone—not a good look for Flydubai, to have suicidal or terrorist pilots—or at someone’s behest. The latter possibility could, if confirmed, have serious implications, depending on who directed the attack. Many have a motive: Sunni jihadists (al-Qaeda or the Islamic State), Hamas, and of course Iran and its proxies. This Mayday moment will have huge implications if any of these groups are involved.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n迪拜航空副驾驶刺伤机长，客机 2 分钟暴跌 5200 米：174 人到底怎么活下来的？ 迪拜航空，一场擦肩而过的空难——这串匪夷所思的连锁事件，后患无穷。 今天，在沙特阿拉伯上空的某处，一架飞往以色列的迪拜航空波音737客机上，副驾驶刺伤了另一名飞行员，并将机头狠狠压向下方的沙漠。两分钟之内，飞机骤降17,000英尺——那加速度之猛，足以让你的手机、你的飞机餐从小桌板上腾空而起，被死死按在天花板上。其他机组人员与至少一名以色列乘客破门闯入驾驶舱，制服了行凶者。随后，替班的飞行员接过操纵，将飞机降落在沙特的塔布克。 迪拜航空另派了一架飞机，把这174名乘客送抵特拉维夫。落地后，一位曾冲进驾驶舱的乘客对以色列总理内塔尼亚胡说：他之所以派上了用场，一部分要归功于他看过那部专拍空难的“灾难猎奇”纪录片《Mayday》——所以他知道，那一刻该做的事，是把操纵杆往回拉。 今天这场险些酿成的灾难，究竟是恐怖主义，还是“不过”一次未遂的大规模谋杀，要看行凶者的动机。但无论是哪一种，对迪拜航空来说都是一场公关上的灭顶之灾——这家公司，是阿联酋航空那位“廉价版的表亲”。自2020年阿联酋与以色列建交以来，正是这家航司把两个国家连在了一起，几乎未曾中断，哪怕是在战火与恐袭的日子里。而要让那条航线一直开着、一直安全，背后费的力气，远超你的想象。 去年，我在迪拜机场一号航站楼，亲眼见识过一手极聪明的安保设计：以色列航空El Al 的值机柜台，被安排在伊朗马汉航空的柜台旁边，紧紧挨着。于是，任何起了念头要袭击以色列乘客的人，必定会同时伤到伊朗乘客——反过来也一样。在这里，托运行李是要另外收费的；但排队时充当人肉盾牌的这项体验，免费赠送。 马汉航空如今已不再飞迪拜。美国的制裁让它大半业务停摆，而迪拜此前就已经压缩了它的航班——理由很干脆：伊朗要么往迪拜扔导弹，要么往迪拜飞客机，二者只能择一。相比之下，以色列从2026年那场伊朗战争里走出来的时候，反倒离阿联酋更近了——而阿联酋，是那场战争里承受伊朗打击最多的国家，没有之一。 虽然这一地区的阿拉伯国家早在1981年就以海湾合作委员会的名义结成了同盟，可导弹和无人机刚一升空，那个联盟就露了底——毫无用处。反倒是以色列，拿出了一个盟友该有的样子：防御系统送过去了，顾问也派过去了。而在战争初期，至少有一枚伊朗的炮火，落在了以色列驻迪拜领事馆的近旁。所以，这次迪拜航空事件背后一个可能的政治动机，或许就是要把这两个国家的关系拽紧、拽痛，让它们为这份亲近付出代价。 以色列与阿联酋的关系，不会因为一次袭击就“啪”地断掉。但阿联酋与内塔尼亚胡本人之间的那根线，就要脆弱得多了。过去几周，阿联酋总统穆罕默德·本·扎耶德被卷进了《国土报》的一篇报道——那篇报道，重重打击了他在下月以色列大选后继续执政的指望。报道称：2023年10月7日那场屠杀发生前几天，MBZ 曾亲自致电警告内塔尼亚胡，一场袭击已迫在眉睫；而内塔尼亚胡，对这个警告没有采取任何行动。阿联酋政府起初闭口不谈。然而上周日，内塔尼亚胡去了一趟阿布扎比——据美联社报道，他请求MBZ 出面否认，否认自己曾经示警。 而就在昨天，纽约，阿联酋外交部长阿卜杜拉·本·扎耶德，正是这么做了。“穆罕默德酋长并未致电内塔尼亚胡总理，告诉他以色列将遭袭击，”阿卜杜拉在美国外交关系委员会的一场会议上说，并称自己的否认是“斩钉截铁”的。他还说，用领导人之间的通话来递送机密，“不是我们的风格”——但他同时留了一个口子：那个警告，或许确实在两国政府之间传递过，只是没在两位领导人之间传递。这番否认，替内塔尼亚胡卸下了一部分压力，却丝毫没有免除他那届政府的失职之责。 《纽约时报》等媒体已经报道，那位涉嫌试图让飞机坠毁的副驾驶，是一名阿曼公民。据称两人目前仍滞留在沙特。故事接下来的篇章，将是查清这名飞行员究竟是单独行动——对迪拜航空来说，机队里出了想自杀的、或是搞恐怖袭击的飞行员，这实在是难看——还是受了什么人的指使。后一种可能若被证实，后果将十分严重，严重到什么地步，就看是谁在背后指挥了这场袭击。而有动机的人，并不少：逊尼派圣战分子（基地组织，或“伊斯兰国”）、哈马斯，当然，还有伊朗和它的那些代理势力。倘若这里头任何一方真的牵涉其中，这个“Mayday 时刻”，后患无穷。","2026-10-02_001":"How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry — 2026/10/02 How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry 2026/10/02 How gold surpassed cocaine as Peru's most profitable criminal industry \"Halt, stop right there,\" shouts a Peruvian Navy Seal, firing warning shots into the air. But the fleeing man pays no attention, scrambling up to the rim of a football pitch-sized crater and disappearing into the dense rainforest as the commandos give chase, swearing and shooting. Meanwhile, other marines set dynamite charges on a makeshift wooden raft that floats in the crater, which is essentially an enormous pit of opaque, stagnant water. The raft has a diesel-fuelled suction pump motor with thick plastic tubing used to suck up and sift through river sediment for tiny particles of gold. Soon after, a mushroom of orange and black fumes envelops the trees – and seconds later comes the sound of the explosion, shattering the peace of the Tambopata National Reserve, one of Peru’s most prized Amazon parks. In total, more than 140,000 hectares have been lost to mining in Madre de Dios – a shocking 97.5% of the total deforestation caused by mining in Peru’s Amazon rainforest, an area twice the size of the US state of California. Military crackdowns on illegal mining in the Amazon are not new in Peru. But the skyrocketing international price of gold has caused this destructive activity to spread, empowering local gangs and attracting transnational organised crime. \"Dirty\" gold has become the country’s most profitable illicit industry, even surpassing drug trafficking in the world’s second-biggest producer of cocaine after Colombia. \"Gold prices have risen,\" said Gen Victor Cotrina, the military commander of Madre de Dios, who is leading the crackdown on illegal gold mining. \"That’s why, if a miner’s motor is destroyed, he’ll replace it quickly because gold is at its peak price: the very next day he’s back to his mining activities. He’s back at work again. \"For troops used to combating drug trafficking by targeting well-hidden plantations of coca, which is used to make cocaine, the fight against illegal gold presents new challenges as the end product is not an illicit drug but a legally traded commodity, Cotrina said. \"The difference is that an open-pit mining operation doesn’t require covert operations, whereas drug trafficking does.\" He said previous anti-mining crackdowns had been ineffective due to corruption and patchy enforcement. But now, with \"aerial surveillance and military personnel who are available 24/7 to conduct patrols, we are determined to eliminate mining in this region\". The evidence is not difficult to find or destroy. Behind the wall of green that marks the edge of the reserve, troops and environmental prosecutors set ablaze a network of well-supplied mining camps, built with timber and blue tarpaulins and stocked with food and beer. A lone cockerel crows as the abandoned camps go up in flames. Two women had been left behind by the fleeing miners: Andrea, 30, wept as she begged the officers not to arrest her, explaining she was a single mother who had been working as a cook and could find no other work to support a sick mother and two children. Liz Compi, 20, was found sitting on a boat with her one-year-old son. She said she had taken the job as a cook for $450 a month after being abandoned by her partner and sacked from a previous job. The dirty gold departs Peru as contraband to Bolivia and Brazil, but much of it is laundered into the legal trade via a controversial registration system called Reinfo. The system, in theory, should bring informal miners into the legal economy, but critics say it allows them to continue working without environmental permits and laundering illegal gold through formal companies. The trade is so lucrative that members of Congress have backed five repeated extensions of Reinfo, despite pushes to reform or eliminate the registry. Since the beginning of Covid-19 pandemic, the price of gold has tripled. Tens of thousands of miners have poured into protected Amazon parks and Indigenous reserves, stripping away vast tracts of rainforest and leaving behind a denuded, pocked terrain, stagnant pools and disrupted river courses. Mercury is the cheapest and easiest way to extract gold because it binds with the particles in the river sediment. \"The government’s response is moving very slowly,\" said Flores after another day of relatively fruitless jungle raids, destroying camps that are likely to spring back up soon after. \"Meanwhile, illegal mining operates 24 hours a day. The miners measure time in grams, not minutes, so we need to rethink how we investigate mining,\" he said.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n以前靠du品赚钱，现在靠黄金：犯罪集团为什么突然盯上了金矿？ 黄金如何超越可卡因，成为秘鲁最暴利的犯罪产业 “站住，不许动！”一名秘鲁海军陆战队队员高声喝道，朝空中鸣枪示警。然而，那名逃窜的男子充耳不闻，手脚并用地爬上一个足球场大小的坑洞边缘，转瞬消失在茂密的雨林中。突击队员们骂骂咧咧，一边开枪一边紧追不舍。 与此同时，其他海军陆战队员在一个漂浮于坑洞中的简易木筏上安放了炸药。这个坑洞实质上是一个巨大的、蓄满浑浊死水的深坑。木筏上装着一台燃柴油的抽吸泵，拖着粗大的塑料软管，用来抽取河底沉积物并从中筛滤出细微的金粒。片刻之后，一团橙黑色的蘑菇云吞没了四周的树木——紧接着传来一声爆炸巨响，打破了坦博帕塔国家保护区的宁静。这里是秘鲁最珍贵的亚马逊公园之一。 在马德雷德迪奥斯地区，因采矿而丧失的土地总面积已超过14万公顷——占秘鲁亚马逊雨林采矿毁林总量的97.5%，触目惊心。这片面积相当于美国加利福尼亚州的两倍。 秘鲁在亚马逊地区对非法采矿实施军事打击，并非新鲜事。但国际金价的飙升使这一破坏性活动不断蔓延，壮大了当地黑帮的势力，也吸引了跨国有组织犯罪集团。“脏金”已成为该国最暴利的非法产业，甚至超过了毒品走私——而秘鲁是全球第二大可卡因生产国，仅次于哥伦比亚。“金价涨了，”马德雷德迪奥斯军区司令维克托·科特里纳将军说，他正领导打击非法采金的行动。“所以，如果一个矿工的机器被毁了，他会很快再买一台，因为金价正处于巅峰。第二天他就回去接着干了。他又开工了。” 科特里纳说，对于习惯以隐蔽的古柯种植园为打击目标来缉毒的部队而言，打击非法采金带来了全新的挑战，因为其最终产品不是违禁毒品，而是一种合法交易的商品。“区别在于，露天采矿不需要秘密行动，而贩毒则需要。”他说，此前的反采矿行动因腐败和执法不力而收效甚微。但现在，凭借“空中侦察和全天候待命执行巡逻的军事人员，我们决心彻底根除这一地区的采矿活动”。 证据并不难找到，也不难销毁。在标记保护区边缘的那道绿色屏障之后，军人和环保检察官放火焚烧了一片补给充足的采矿营地。这些营地用木材和蓝色防水布搭建，囤积着食物和啤酒。当被遗弃的营地燃起熊熊大火时，一只孤零零的公鸡发出了啼鸣。 逃离的矿工留下了两名女性：30岁的安德烈娅泪流满面，恳求军官不要逮捕她，解释说自己是个单亲母亲，一直做厨娘，找不到其他工作来养活生病的母亲和两个孩子。20岁的莉兹·孔皮被发现在一艘船上，身旁坐着她一岁的儿子。她说，在被伴侣抛弃、又被上一份工作解雇后，她以每月450美元的报酬接受了这份厨娘的工作。 脏金以走私方式从秘鲁流向玻利维亚和巴西，但其中很大一部分通过一个有争议的登记制度——Reinfo——洗白进入合法贸易。该制度理论上应将非正规矿工纳入合法经济，但批评者指出，它允许矿工在没有环境许可的情况下继续作业，并通过正规公司将非法黄金洗白。这门生意利润太过丰厚，以至于尽管有改革或废除该登记制度的呼声，国会议员仍五次支持延长Reinfo。 自新冠疫情暴发以来，金价已涨至原来的三倍。数以万计的矿工涌入亚马逊保护区和原住民保留地，大片雨林被剥除，留下的是一片光秃秃、坑坑洼洼的地表、死水潭和被改道的河流。汞是提取黄金最廉价、最简便的方法，因为它能与河底沉积物中的颗粒结合。“政府的反应非常缓慢，”弗洛雷斯在又一天收效甚微的丛林突袭后说道。他们捣毁的营地很可能不久后就会死灰复燃。“与此同时，非法采矿一天24小时不停运转。矿工用克来衡量时间，而不是用分钟，所以我们需要重新思考调查采矿的方式，”他说。","2026-10-01_001":"A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant — 2026/10/01 A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant 2026/10/01 A SpaceX Starship Rocket Officially Reached Orbit. Why That's So Significant For three and a half years, ever since the spring of 2023, when SpaceX launched the first of its massive Starship rockets, the company has been promising that before too long the towering, stainless steel machine would get to orbit. This morning, on the 14th such launch, it made good on that boast. \"Starship performs its orbital insertion burn and enters orbit of Earth for the first time,\" SpaceX announced on X at 9:15 a.m. ET, along with a video of that engine maneuver. It took just 25 minutes and 17 seconds for the spacecraft to travel from its launch pad in Starbase, Texas, to its circular orbit 170 miles high. There is no overstating how important today's success was to SpaceX's—and NASA's—long-term plans. Starship is pivotal to getting human beings to the moon and Mars, and SpaceX sees it as an ultimate replacement for its workhorse Falcon 9 rocket, which accounted for nearly 51% of the global total of commercial launches— including 85% of all satellites—in 2025. Starship's far larger payload bay can carry a cargo equivalent of nearly five Falcon 9s, ultimately consolidating launches and cutting costs. In 2021, NASA selected Starship as one of two spacecraft that will land astronauts on the moon as early as 2028.(Blue Origin, the aerospace company founded by Amazon boss Jeff Bezos, will provide the other.) In 2027, NASA is planning a crewed test of either or both spacecraft in Earth orbit during the mission of Artemis III. Early on, routine was not remotely how Starship launches were being described. Four minutes into the rocket's inaugural launch on April 20, 2023, it exploded spectacularly. Concrete and steel debris rained down in a near-400-acre footprint in the surrounding Boca Chica State Park, and a 3.85-acre fire burned south of the launch site. But the flights proceeded apace at a rate of once every three months, none achieving stable orbit— until today. The launch this morning was both imperfect and stripped down to its orbital essentials. On the way up, one of the Starship's six engines failed to burn properly, requiring the other engines to compensate for the missing thrust to get the ship in orbit. In addition, the return to Earth was simplified. Starship's first stage, meantime, performs what has become known as a chopstick recovery, with the booster navigating its way back to the launch tower where two giant metal arms pluck it from the sky. For the current mission, the chopstick recovery was done away with to simplify the flight objectives; instead the first stage made a soft, engine-assisted splashdown in the Gulf of Mexico. The Starship spacecraft was planned for a six-orbit, 10-hour mission. Today's launch was not just a test flight; it was also an operational mission designed to deliver 26 Starlink satellites to orbit, adding to the whopping 11,000 that already comprise the Starlink constellation—with an eventual goal of 100,000. SpaceX released footage of Starship deploying the satellites with what it has dubbed its Pez dispenser system, because that's exactly what the spacecraft resembles as it releases its payload one satellite at a time. Ultimately, the spacecraft was brought down early, after just three orbits, since its satellites had all been deployed and the ship proved its flight worthiness quickly. The launch of Starship 14 is in many ways only a very small—if critical—step. Even the biggest SpaceX supporters don't pretend that Starship is anywhere near ready to land astronauts on the moon in 2028. The spacecraft—which is now essentially a giant, flying, cargo truck—has to be adapted to carry crew, with all of the environmental and habitat modifications that implies. It will also have to solve a stubborn refueling problem: before heading out for the moon, Starship will need to enter a parking orbit around the Earth where up to a dozen other Starship-sized tankers will top off its tanks. It will have to fly its Artemis III test flight in 2027, and later, an uncrewed landing on—and liftoff from—the moon before it will be trusted to carry astronauts in as little as two years' time. Still, SpaceX promises that an Earth-orbital launch that today is headline-making could become almost as commonplace as the takeoff of a commercial airliner. Elon Musk, the company's founder and CEO, has spoken openly about Starships launching as frequently as once a day—or even once an hour. Maybe. For now the company is celebrating one launch on one day, on a mission that didn't even last past east coast lunchtime. But spaceflight is iterative and incremental. And today SpaceX moved one big increment indeed.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n第14次发射，星舰终于进入地球轨道：马斯克离月球和火星还有多远？ SpaceX 星舰正式入轨。这一步，为什么举足轻重。 三年半了。自2023年春天SpaceX送走第一枚巨型星舰起，这家公司就一直在放话：用不了多久，这个高耸的不锈钢大家伙就能飞上轨道。今天上午，第十四次发射，这句大话，终于兑现了。 “星舰完成入轨点火，首次进入地球轨道。”美东时间上午9点15分，SpaceX在X上这样宣布，同时放出了那段发动机点火的视频。从德州星舰基地的发射台，到170英里高的圆形轨道，这艘飞船只用了25分17秒。 这次成功对SpaceX——以及NASA——的长远布局有多重要，怎么讲都不为过。要把人送上月球和火星，星舰是那个关键支点；而在SpaceX自己的规划里，它最终是要顶替猎鹰9号的。猎鹰9号是什么分量？2025年全球商业发射，它一家占了将近一半，全球上天的卫星里，85%是它送的。可星舰的货舱大得多，一趟装的货，抵得上近五枚猎鹰9号——发射次数因此合并，成本因此压下来。 2021年，NASA选中星舰，作为最早于2028年送宇航员登月的两艘飞船之一。（另一艘由亚马逊老板贝索斯创办的蓝色起源提供。）而在2027年，NASA计划在阿尔忒弥斯三号任务中，让宇航员在地球轨道上试飞其中一艘，或者两艘一起试。 可在早些年，“例行公事”这四个字跟星舰发射根本不沾边。2023年4月20日首飞，升空第四分钟，它炸了——炸得堪称壮观。混凝土和钢铁的碎片如雨点般砸下，覆盖了周边博卡奇卡州立公园近400英亩的范围，发射场南侧还烧起一片3.85英亩的大火。但发射照旧推进，三个月一次，从没有一次进入稳定轨道——直到今天。 今天上午这次发射，既算不上完美，也被削减到了只保留入轨所必需的那些动作。上升途中，星舰六台发动机中的一台未能正常点火，只能由其余几台补上缺掉的推力，硬把飞船送进了轨道。 返回环节也做了简化。按常规，星舰的第一级执行的是如今人称“筷子回收”的动作：助推器自行飞回发射塔，塔上两条巨型机械臂在半空中把它一把接下。而这次任务为了简化目标，筷子回收被取消了，第一级改在墨西哥湾做了一次柔和的、发动机助降的溅落。至于上面那艘星舰飞船，原定要绕地六圈、飞行十小时。 今天这一飞，不只是试飞，更是一次实打实的任务：把26颗星链卫星送进轨道，加进那张已经由一万一千颗卫星织成的星链大网——而最终目标，是十万颗。SpaceX放出了星舰释放卫星的画面，用的是他们自己命名的“PEZ糖果机系统”——因为飞船一颗一颗往外送卫星的样子，跟那个糖盒简直一模一样。最终，飞船只绕了三圈就被提前带回：卫星已全部放完，这艘船也很快证明了自己经得起飞。 星舰14号这一飞，从很多角度看都只是很小的一步——虽然是关键的一步。哪怕是最铁杆的SpaceX拥趸，也不好意思说它已经接近能在2028年送人登月的水平。这艘飞船——如今说白了就是一辆巨大的会飞的货车——必须改造成能载人的样子，连带着所有环境与居住条件的改动，一样都不能少。它还得啃下一个难缠的加注难题：在奔月之前，星舰要先进入一条绕地球的停泊轨道，在那里由多达十几艘同等体量的加油船，把它的油箱一次次灌满。它得先飞完2027年阿尔忒弥斯三号的试飞，再完成一次无人登月与从月面起飞，之后才谈得上被信任去载人——而那，最快也是两年之后。 不过SpaceX承诺：今天这种还能上头条的地球轨道发射，有朝一日会变得跟一班客机起飞一样稀松平常。公司创始人兼CEO马斯克更是公开说过，星舰可以做到一天一发——甚至一小时一发。也许吧。眼下，这家公司庆祝的，不过是某一天的某一次发射；而这趟任务，连美东的午饭时间都没撑过去。可话说回来，航天本来就是这么一次次迭代、一寸寸往前挪的。而今天，SpaceX确确实实往前挪了一大寸。","2026-09-30_001":"How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West — 2026/09/30 How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West 2026/09/30 How China is conquering high-tech markets once controlled by the West It's the rare factory that doubles as a tourist attraction. But more than 300,000 visitors have streamed through Xiaomi's sprawling \"hyperfactory\" here since the company began offering tours of its automobile assembly lines two years ago. On display is a vision of manufacturing far removed from the labor-intensive operations that characterized China's emergence as an economic power a quarter century ago. Industrial robots painted a brilliant white pivot and twirl around half-finished electric sedans. Compact unmanned load carriers, chirping a melodic warning for the few humans they encounter, scoot across the floor in a vast factory larger than the Pentagon. \"They have gone all in on automation and robotics and minimizing labor and maximizing efficiency,\" said Michael Dunne, an auto industry consultant in San Diego. \"You will find in China's auto industry the most advanced plants in the world, by far.\" And not just in the auto industry. Chinese manufacturers' automation, innovative designs, world-class logistics and government support have vaulted them to global supremacy in an ever-longer list of technologically advanced products. Once known for using cheap labor to produce inexpensive clothes and toys, Chinese factories increasingly specialize in some of the world's most sophisticated merchandise, including feature-rich electric vehicles and robotics. Only Britain at the dawn of the Industrial Revolution and the United States following World War II have so dominated the global production of goods. China now accounts for 32 percent of global manufacturing value added, twice its share in 2010, and more than the combined total of the next four biggest manufacturing powers - the United States, Germany, Japan and South Korea - according to the U.N. Industrial Development Organization. Take BYD, China's largest carmaker. The BYD Seal, which is comparable to the Tesla Model 3, includes as standard equipment a rotating 15.6-inch touch screen, dual wireless charging pads and an interior multicolor lighting system that pulses along with ambient sound or music. But its late-2025 sticker price of $24,190 was more than $8,000 lower than the Tesla sells for in China, according to the Rhodium Group, a New York consultancy. Government subsidies, meanwhile, account for just $235 of the $4,700-per-vehicle price advantage BYD holds across its fleet over Elon Musk's Tesla, Rhodium said. \"There's a lot of technological advancement taking place in China,\" said Louis Kuijs, chief economist, Asia Pacific, for S&P Global Ratings. \"The Chinese companies are just very good and very fast, and the price-quality ratio is very attractive.\" For years, China's manufacturing gains were seen as the flip side of American factory losses; jobs disappeared from the U.S. industrial heartland only to reappear in Asia. But in recent years, instead of adding bodies to its assembly lines, China has been deploying collaborative robots or co-bots, designed to work alongside humans; conveyors; and automated visual quality inspection systems, according to AlixPartners, a New York-based consultancy. Robot-rich Chinese auto plants can produce automobiles for 25 to 30 percent less than in the U.S. or Europe. While Chinese wages have increased in recent years, overall labor costs per vehicle remain much lower than in advanced economies. The reason: Chinese cars are designed to be easier to make, meaning fewer hours of labor are needed. \"It isn't because of low-cost labor. It's because of efficiencies attributable to proximity to supply chains and this high degree of automation,\" said Dunne, formerly president of General Motors operations in Indonesia. Inside Xiaomi's facility about 90 minutes from the center of Beijing, 700 robots perform an industrial choreography that delivers a new car every 76 seconds. \"Xiaomi's car factory has achieved a high level of automation, with most processes completed entirely by machines,\" Lei Jun, Xiaomi's CEO, told Chinese reporters last year. Xiaomi (pronounced Shao-mee) rose to prominence in China as a leading maker of smartphones before branching out into wearables, tablets and Internet-connected appliances. It began making passenger cars in early 2024, and its tech-forward approach quickly won glowing reviews. Ford flew a Xiaomi SU-7 electric sedan from Shanghai to Chicago so that CEO Jim Farley could eyeball the competition. After driving the car, Farley pronounced Xiaomi \"an industry juggernaut.\" Many of those extra vehicles are bound for other markets. Through August, Chinese auto exports rose by more than 50 percent compared with the same period in 2025, approaching 7.5 million vehicles, according to China's General Administration of Customs. In response, the company is adopting a familiar strategy: Some time next year, the first Xiaomi cars will begin arriving in Germany.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n西方曾经垄断的高科技市场，正在被中国企业一个个抢走：凭什么？ 中国如何攻占曾由西方主导的高科技市场 工厂兼作旅游景点，实属罕见。然而，自两年前小米公司开放其汽车装配线参观以来，已有逾三十万游客涌入这座坐落于此、占地广阔的小米“超级工厂”。 呈现在眼前的，是一幅与中国四分之一世纪前以劳动密集型产业崛起为经济强国时截然不同的制造业图景。工业机器人喷涂着亮白色的车漆，围绕半成品的电动轿车旋转作业。紧凑型无人搬运车穿梭于这座比五角大楼还要庞大的厂房之中，遇到为数不多的人类时，便发出悦耳的鸣叫声以示警示。 “他们全力押注自动化和机器人技术，最大限度减少人工、最大化提升效率，”圣迭戈汽车行业顾问迈克尔·邓恩说道。“你会发现，中国汽车行业拥有迄今为止世界上最先进的工厂。”而且不仅限于汽车行业。中国制造商凭借自动化、创新设计、世界级物流体系和政府支持，在越来越多的技术先进产品领域跃居全球之巅。中国工厂曾以廉价劳动力生产廉价服装和玩具而闻名，如今则日益专注于一些世界上最精密的商品，包括功能丰富的电动汽车和机器人。 历史上，只有工业革命初期的英国和二战后的美国曾如此主导全球商品生产。据联合国工业发展组织数据，中国目前占全球制造业增加值的32%，是2010年份额的两倍，超过紧随其后的四大制造业强国——美国、德国、日本和韩国——的总和。 以中国最大的汽车制造商比亚迪为例。比亚迪“海豹”车型可与特斯拉Model 3相媲美，标配一块可旋转的15.6英寸触摸屏、双无线充电板，以及一套随环境音或音乐律动的车内多色氛围灯系统。然而据纽约咨询公司荣鼎集团的数据，其2025年末的标价为24,190美元，比特斯拉在中国市场的售价低逾8,000美元。荣鼎表示，在比亚迪全系车型相较马斯克旗下特斯拉每辆车4,700美元的价格优势中，政府补贴仅占235美元。“中国正在发生大量的技术进步，”标普全球评级亚太区首席经济学家路易斯·凯杰斯表示。“中国企业非常优秀、非常迅速，性价比极具吸引力。” 多年来，中国制造业的增益被视为美国工厂流失的另一面；就业岗位从美国工业腹地消失，却在亚洲重新出现。但据纽约咨询公司艾睿铂称，近年来，中国不再向装配线增加人力，而是部署协作机器人（即“cobot”，专为与人协同工作而设计）、传送带和自动化视觉质检系统。机器人密集的中国汽车工厂生产汽车的成本比美国或欧洲低25%至30%。尽管近年来中国工资水平有所上涨，但每辆车的整体劳动力成本仍远低于发达经济体。原因在于：中国汽车的设计更易于制造，意味着所需工时更少。 “这不是因为低成本劳动力，而是因为靠近供应链所带来的效率提升以及这种高度自动化，”曾任通用汽车印尼业务总裁的邓恩说道。在距离北京市中心约90分钟车程的小米工厂内，700台机器人上演着一场工业编排之舞，每76秒就有一辆新车下线。“小米汽车工厂达到了高度自动化水平，大部分工序完全由机器完成，”小米CEO雷军去年对中国记者表示。 小米（发音为“Shao-mee”）最初以领先智能手机制造商的身份在中国崭露头角，随后拓展至可穿戴设备、平板电脑和物联网家电。该公司于2024年初开始生产乘用车，其科技前沿的理念迅速赢得如潮好评。福特公司将一辆小米SU-7电动轿车从上海空运至芝加哥，以便CEO吉姆·法利亲眼审视这位竞争对手。试驾之后，法利称小米为“行业巨擘”。 这些新增车辆中有许多销往其他市场。据中国海关总署数据，截至8月，中国汽车出口量较2025年同期增长逾50%，逼近750万辆。作为回应，该公司正采取一个似曾相识的策略：明年某个时候，首批小米汽车将开始进入德国市场。","2026-09-29_001":"Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift — 2026/09/29 Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift 2026/09/29 Soaring diesel prices are clobbering Trump's MAGA faithful, exposing rift Pain at the pump for rural Republican is shifting the political map Diesel prices soaring to record highs this past week have caused Republican candidates in solid-red parts of the country to call for an end to President Donald Trump's war with Iran and for a ban on diesel exports, as the issue rattles the party's MAGA base in a way that few have in this midterm election year. \"Michigan families are struggling with the price of everyday life,\" Mike Rogers, the state's GOP nominee for U.S. Senate, said in an ad this past week. The war has sent oil prices surging, with disruptions to shipping through the Strait of Hormuz, a vital route for global energy supplies, reverberating through fuel markets. But while poll after poll has shown voters concerned about the cost of gasoline, it's the price of diesel — a far less obvious consumer product — that has riled up the industrial and agricultural interests that have been among the most faithful Republican supporters. The political fallout is landing in deep-red voting districts the hardest. Federal data shows these tend to be the places where diesel makes up the largest proportion of the overall fuel mix. This is especially the case in farming communities, where diesel powers the fuel-thirsty tractors and other machinery that the agriculture industry relies on. That has led lawmakers to demand what was unthinkable in the GOP only a couple of months ago: a ban on diesel exports from the United States. The proposal seems to make sense on its face: restrict the flow of the fuel out of the U.S. to boost the supply domestically, quickly lowering prices. The problem is that almost nobody who understands oil markets — including Energy Secretary Chris Wright — believes it would work out that way. Analysts who have been modeling the impact of a diesel export ban are consistently warning it would create chaos and hurt consumers. The Gulf Coast refineries that typically export diesel abroad would get overloaded with a glut of crude that would have to be diverted to storage. The diesel they make would mostly be stranded in their region, because there are no pipelines to get it across the country. Parts of the country that rely on imports of diesel could then see prices soar even higher than they are now. \"It is not at all clear that the national price of diesel would fall,\" the Energy Policy Research Foundation said Thursday in a report. In Iowa, where farmer anger over the price of diesel has reached a boiling point and endangered the GOP's hold over a Senate seat in a state Trump won by 13 percentage points in 2024, conservative lawmakers are rushing to embrace an export ban. The Iowa Corn Growers Association, which has endorsed all Republicans in this year's Iowa congressional races, wrote to the state's delegation in Washington this past week pleading for action to lower diesel prices. The record highs are especially ill-timed because they hit right at harvest, the association noted. \"This time of year should be positive for Iowa farmers, but the concern of affordability looms large as diesel prices go above $6 per gallon,\" the letter said. \"This is accelerating the economic and mental stress farmers are feeling.\" Steve Kuiper, a farmer and head of the association, said that diesel is indispensable to his work. As the harvest gets in gear, the tractor-trailers that deliver equipment to farms and haul away harvested crops, as well as the tractors and combines and harvesters that do the work, all run on diesel, Kuiper said. His own operation uses some 500 gallons of diesel per day, he said. Fuel he contracted for last year is running at about half the cost of fuel purchased now; topping off one truck Thursday cost $500, Kuiper said. \"Farming is not a huge-margin business. This quickly cuts into it,\" he said. The price of fertilizer is going up at the same time, he said, and all of those costs will be passed on to consumers. Kuiper does not specifically blame Trump or other Republicans for the situation, which he said has been building for years and could be mitigated by steps such as an embargo on diesel exports or allowing more ethanol to be mixed in with petroleum products. But the situation puts all politicians in a negative light. \"All our elected officials in D.C. need to do their job and get things taken care of,\" Kuiper said.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国柴油涨破6美元，川川“铁票仓”先急了：为什么柴油价格能搅动中期选举？ 柴油价格飙升重创特朗普的MAGA铁杆票仓，裂痕已然显现——乡村共和党人的加油之痛，正在改写政治版图 过去一周，柴油价格飙升至历史新高，令全国深红选区的共和党候选人纷纷呼吁结束特朗普总统对伊朗的战争，并禁止柴油出口。这一议题在这个中期选举年撼动了该党MAGA基本盘，其烈度鲜有匹敌者。“密歇根的家庭正在为日常生活的开销苦苦挣扎，”该州共和党联邦参议员候选人迈克·罗杰斯在过去一周的一则广告中说道。 这场战争导致油价飙升，霍尔木兹海峡——全球能源供应的咽喉要道——航运受阻，冲击波已在整个燃料市场回荡。然而，尽管一份又一份民调显示选民担忧汽油价格，真正激怒了工业与农业利益集团的，却是柴油——一种远不那么引人注目的消费品——的价格。而这些利益集团，恰恰是共和党最忠实的支持者群体。 政治冲击波正狠狠地砸向深红选区。联邦数据显示，这些地区的柴油在整体燃料结构中所占比例往往最高。农业社区尤为如此——柴油驱动着耗油量惊人的拖拉机和其他机械，是农业产业赖以运转的命脉。 这促使议员们提出了几个月前在共和党内还不可想象的主张：禁止美国出口柴油。这一提议表面上似乎合情合理：限制燃料外流以增加国内供应，从而迅速压低价格。问题在于，几乎所有真正懂石油市场的人——包括能源部长克里斯·赖特——都不认为它会奏效。一直在建模分析柴油出口禁令影响的分析师们一致警告：此举将制造混乱，伤害消费者。 通常向海外出口柴油的墨西哥湾沿岸炼油厂，将因大量原油无处可去而陷入过剩，只能转为储存。它们生产的柴油也将大部分困守本地区，因为没有管道将其输送到全国各地。而那些依赖柴油进口的地区，价格反而可能比现在涨得更凶。“全国柴油价格是否会下降，完全说不准，”能源政策研究基金会周四在一份报告中表示。 在艾奥瓦州，农民对柴油价格的愤怒已到沸点，危及共和党在该州一个参议院席位的掌控——而特朗普2024年曾以13个百分点的优势拿下该州。保守派议员正争先恐后地拥抱出口禁令。艾奥瓦州玉米种植者协会——今年该州国会选举中支持了所有共和党候选人——过去一周致信该州驻华盛顿代表团，恳请采取行动降低柴油价格。 协会指出，这轮历史新高来得尤其不是时候，因为正值收获季节。“每年的这个时候本该是艾奥瓦农民欢欣鼓舞的日子，但随着柴油价格突破每加仑6美元，负担能力的忧虑笼罩一切，”信中写道。“这正在加剧农民所承受的经济压力和精神压力。” 农民兼协会主席史蒂夫·凯珀说，柴油对他的劳作不可或缺。随着收获季全面展开，向农场运送设备、运走收获作物的牵引挂车，以及田间作业的拖拉机、联合收割机和收获机，统统烧的是柴油，凯珀说。他自己的经营每天要用掉约500加仑柴油。他说，去年签约锁定的燃料价格约为现在购买价格的一半；周四给一辆卡车加满油花了500美元。“农业本就不是高利润行业。这很快就侵蚀了利润，”他说。 他说，化肥价格同时也在上涨，所有这些成本最终都会转嫁给消费者。凯珀并未专门将矛头指向特朗普或其他共和党人——他说这个问题已酝酿多年，可以通过禁止柴油出口或允许更多乙醇混入石油产品等措施来缓解。但这一局面让所有政客都脸上无光。“我们在华盛顿的所有民选官员都需要尽职尽责，把事情处理好，”凯珀说。","2026-09-28_001":"The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining — 2026/09/28 The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining 2026/09/28 The Great Stuff Transfer Has a $750 Billion Golden Lining When Andrew Stinger's mother, Nancy, died of cancer in 2024, her grief-stricken husband and sons began the journey of managing her estate in probate court — a process that would stretch almost two years. Then, in January 2025, Stinger's father, Bill, died unexpectedly. And yet, there was one happy spot amid the heartbreak: the jewelry. \"There were pieces that my mom inherited from her mother that it made sense for my mom's sisters to have, and to be able to pass down to their daughters,\" Stinger says. \"My mom's engagement ring will go to her only granddaughter when she grows up. These things are part of our family lineage. \" In the next 20 years, some $93 trillion in assets in the US will be passed down from baby boomers and the Silent Generation to their heirs. Along with that will come a whole lot of physical items: the china, silver, coin and stamp collections, and millions and millions of baseball cards that make up what's known as the Great Stuff Transfer. Jewelry is the rare exception. The reasons are both obvious and intimate: To begin with, silver, gold, platinum and gemstones have material value. With gold prices climbing back toward their January high (when the spot price was $5,589.38 per ounce, five times what it was 10 years earlier) and silver on a similar trajectory, it's a good time to come into some precious metal. According to a 2026 report from the jewelry tracking startup Unvault Inc., the average US household is sitting on $5,700 in unappraised gold jewelry, with roughly $750 billion in gold weight in drawers across the country. Add to that silver, platinum and pieces with artistic merit that can command high premiums. Unlike art, old furniture or Dickens houses, the infrastructure for offloading jewelry at almost any price point is expansive and well established. From estate-sale specialists, pawnshops and local jewelers to high-end auction houses to eBay and the RealReal, there are options for pretty much everything you could inherit. If you've come into some jewelry or even expect to, it's crucial to know its value — for insurance, for deciding who in a family will get what and for potential division of cash outcomes, says Nidhi Singhvi. A former hedge funder who later worked in crypto, Singhvi started Unvault last October to bring transparency to the secondhand jewelry market. \"I'm Indian. We do gold very differently in India,\" she says. Gold jewelry, like 22-karat bracelets and necklaces, are \"transferred from generation to generation with the idea that it can be used for anything,\" she says. \"My mom used gold to send me for a school exchange program. The market is very transparent, and there is very good financial infrastructure for selling it. \" To Singhvi, the American system of aggressive storefronts with big \"WE BUY GOLD\" posters in the window was a turnoff. She knew a lot of women who wouldn't set foot in a pawnshop. Instead, her platform uses artificial intelligence to assess the jewelry in clients' collections and assign it a clear value based on the weight of the metal and the carats and quality of the gemstones. With Unvault, you can build a digital portfolio of what you own by sending in photographs and watch its value rise and fall with the price of gold. When you're ready to offload it, you can sell directly to Unvault, which will deduct a 10% to 20% fee and then pass the interesting pieces and stones to a network of estate jewelers, and melt down the rest. \"For me, a key part of such intergenerational transitions is not being afraid to repurpose, reset or recast things,\" says Andrew James Hamilton, a curator at the Art Institute of Chicago. \"My grandmother had a pair of opal earrings that my mother inherited and turned into two tie tacks for her gay grandsons, my brother and me,\" he says. \"Earrings can become cuff links, rings, pendants. And with the price of precious metals as high as it is, things can be melted down and recast as something else equally precious. \" \"After the Holocaust, my grandparents immigrated to Israel, and my grandmother always wore a gold Magen David Jewish star and an infinity necklace that looked like an 'S' to honor her roots and love of my grandfather,\" says Ronnit Hershowitz, a marketing director in New York. \"It was passed down to me. My grandfather always got choked up seeing it because it was more than just a necklace. For them, it represented eternal strength and love. \"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n父母留下的一屋子旧东西，年轻人到底要不要？美国一场93万亿美元的继承潮已经开始 “物品大转移”，藏着7500亿美元的金色里子——婴儿潮一代传给年轻人的这场财产雪崩里，珠宝是唯一的亮点。 2024年，安德鲁·斯廷格的母亲南希因癌症离世。悲痛欲绝的丈夫和儿子们，就此踏上了在遗嘱认证法庭打理她遗产的漫漫长路——这一拖，就是将近两年。紧接着，2025年1月，斯廷格的父亲比尔又猝然离世。可即便在这样的伤痛里，仍有一处让人心头一暖的角落：珠宝。 “有些首饰，是我妈从我外婆手里接下来的。这些东西由我几个姨妈收着，再往下传给她们的女儿，是最顺理成章的，”斯廷格说，“我妈那枚订婚戒指，等她唯一的孙女长大了，就归她。这些东西，本身就是我们家族血脉的一部分。” 未来二十年里，美国将有约93万亿美元的资产，从婴儿潮一代和沉默的一代手中，交到他们的继承人手上。而随着这笔钱一起涌来的，还有堆积如山的实物——成套的瓷器、银器，一册册的钱币和邮票，以及数也数不清的棒球卡。这些东西合在一起，就是人们所说的“物品大转移”。 珠宝是其中罕见的例外。理由既摆在明面上，也藏在心里头。首先，白银、黄金、铂金和宝石，本身就是有料的——它们的价值是实打实的材料价值。眼下金价正一路爬回1月份的高位（当时现货价每盎司5589.38美元，是十年前的整整五倍），白银也走着同样的上行轨迹，此时此刻继承到一点贵金属，运气实在不错。珠宝估值平台Unvault 2026年的一份报告显示，美国家庭平均坐拥价值5700美元、却从未估过价的金饰——全国上下的抽屉里，按黄金重量算，大约躺着7500亿美元。这还没算上白银、铂金，以及那些凭艺术价值就能卖出高溢价的作品。 跟艺术品、老家具或者那些狄更斯圣诞小屋不同，珠宝变现的那一整套体系，无论哪个价位，都既宽且熟。从上门清点遗物的专员、街角当铺、本地珠宝行，到高端拍卖行，再到eBay和The RealReal——你可能继承到的几乎任何东西，都能找到接盘的地方。 尼迪·辛格维说，如果你已经继承了珠宝，甚至只是预计将来会继承，那么搞清楚它值多少钱就至关重要——为了投保，为了决定家里谁该拿走哪一件，也为了将来万一要折现分钱。她做过对冲基金，后来又转战加密货币，去年10月创办了Unvault，想让二手珠宝市场变得透明一些。“我是印度人。在印度，我们对黄金完全是另一套玩法，”她说。像22K的手镯、项链这类金饰，“是一代一代往下传的，而且人人都明白：它随时可以拿来做任何事。”“我妈就是靠金子，把我送去参加学校的交换项目的，”她说，“那个市场非常透明，而且有一整套很成熟的变现渠道。” 在辛格维看来，美国那种橱窗上糊着巨大“高价收金”海报、咄咄逼人的店面，实在让人倒胃口。她认识不少女人，是打死也不肯踏进当铺半步的。于是她换了条路：她的平台用人工智能评估客户手里的珠宝，按金属重量、宝石克拉与成色，给出一个清清楚楚的估值。在Unvault上，你只要拍几张照片传上去，就能为自己名下的首饰建起一个数字资产组合，然后看着它的身价随金价起起落落。等你想脱手了，可以直接卖给Unvault——它抽走一成到两成的费用，把其中有看头的首饰和宝石转给一整张古董珠宝商的网络，剩下的，熔掉。 “在我看来，这种代际交接里最关键的一点，就是别怕把东西改掉、重镶、或者回炉重造，”芝加哥艺术博物馆策展人安德鲁·詹姆斯·汉密尔顿说。“我奶奶有一对欧泊耳环，我妈继承之后，把它改成了两枚领带夹，给她的两个同性恋孙子——我哥和我，”他说，“耳环可以变袖扣、变戒指、变吊坠。而且眼下贵金属这么贵，东西大可以熔了，再铸成另一件同样贵重的物件。” “大屠杀之后，我的祖父母移民去了以色列。我奶奶脖子上总挂着两样东西：一枚金的大卫之盾，和一条形状像字母S的无限符号项链——前一样纪念她的来处，后一样纪念她对我祖父的爱，”纽约的市场总监罗妮特·赫肖维茨说，“后来，它传到了我手上。我祖父每回看见它都会哽咽，因为那不只是一条项链。对他们两个人来说，它代表的是永不磨灭的力量，和爱。”","2026-09-27_001":"Scientists create mice with part-human brains — 2026/09/27 Scientists create mice with part-human brains 2026/09/27 Scientists create mice with part-human brains Researchers have created mice with half-human brains in an effort to understand and develop new treatments for disorders such as schizophrenia, epilepsy, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability and rare forms of dementia. The scientists transplanted lab-grown human brain cells into animals that were engineered to be born without a cortex or hippocampus. This made space for the human tissue to grow inside the rodents' skulls. The procedure means scientists can now take cells from patients with brain disorders, turn them into brain tissue in the laboratory, and grow that tissue in living animals. The animals can then be studied to see how the disorder takes hold in human brain tissue, and how drugs might treat the conditions. \"We've been trying really hard as a community to find therapeutic solutions for these conditions, but the reality is that in psychiatry and neurology we've been left behind [by] every single branch of medicine and we have fewer therapeutics than, again, every single branch of medicine,\" said Sergiu Pașca, a professor of psychiatry who led the research at Stanford University. \"That could be because the human brain is very complex, but it's also because the human brain is inaccessible,\" he added. \"To a large extent, our goal has been to make aspects of human brain development and function accessible for investigation.\" While potentially transformative for brain medicine, organoids have sparked a wave of ethical concerns, not least around whether the clumps of tissue could become conscious or feel pain, and the welfare of animals implanted with organoids. Pașca said the work had received extensive ethical oversight from the start. Experts said this would need to continue. Emily Jackson, professor of law at London School of Economics and chair of a recent report on neural organoids for the Nuffield Council on Bioethics, said: \"Animal welfare is a really important concern, and it will be necessary to closely monitor these animals in order to evaluate the impact on them.\" Writing in Nature, Pașca describes how the mice were injected with human brain organoids that were themselves created by reprogramming donated skin cells. The newborn mice received several injections, each containing about 100,000 human brain cells, into the space where their own brain tissue was missing. In total, the rodents lacked about 14m mouse brain cells and ended up with about 4m human ones – half the brain by volume. Three months after surgery, the human tissue had hooked up to the mouse's blood supply and almost entirely filled the cavity, taking up about half the size of the rodent's brain. Some of the human neurons formed connections with mouse brain cells and spinal cord. The human neurons were not structured or wired up in the same way as in people, and the brain tissue was immature, equivalent to that found halfway through human pregnancy. Tests on the \"xenocortical\" mice showed the animals were not enhanced by the transplants, but their shaky gait and cognitive problems did improve a little. To demonstrate how the mice could shed light on human brain disorders, the researchers exposed some of the animals to five hours of low oxygen. This showed how vulnerable human nerve cells are to oxygen deprivation, which in pregnancy and birth can cause cerebral palsy. The researchers found that human brain tissue in the mice contained rare cells known as von Economo neurons, which have only been seen in postmortem examinations. The cells are among the first to die in frontotemporal dementia, a rare form of the disease, which Pașca now hopes to study in xenocortical mice. Prof Madeline Lancaster, group leader at the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology in Cambridge, said the approach was best suited to questions about disorders and treatments where a whole animal was needed. \"It's less clear to me how this will inform our understanding of human brain development since it is rather artificial, and not at all like how the brain develops naturally,\" she said. Many researchers working on brain organoids are growing them in vitro, or in dishes. The hope is they help to reduce the number of animals used in research. \"It's obviously ethically sensitive and there needs to be a very good reason to do this type of animal experimentation, which most scientific avenues do not require,\" Lancaster added. \"The field is still aiming for fully in vitro solutions, but this work can also inform on what may be needed to improve those in vitro models to reach more mature stages.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n科学家造出“人脑老鼠”：超过90%的大脑皮层组织来自人类，它还是一只普通老鼠吗？ 科学家培育出半人脑小鼠 研究人员培育出了半人脑小鼠，试图借此理解精神分裂症、癫痫、脑瘫、智力障碍及罕见型痴呆等疾病，并为其开发新疗法。科学家将实验室培育的人类脑细胞，移植到经基因工程改造、天生缺失大脑皮层或海马体的动物体内。如此一来，人类组织便得以在啮齿动物的颅腔内扎根生长。 这一技术路径意味着，科学家如今可以从脑部疾病患者身上提取细胞，在实验室中将其转化为脑组织，再让这片组织在活体动物体内继续发育。随后，通过研究这些动物，便可观察疾病如何在人类脑组织中悄然扎根，以及药物能否将其制伏。 “整个学界一直在拼尽全力，为这些疾病寻找治疗方案。但现实是，在精神病学和神经病学领域，我们被医学的每一个分支远远甩在了身后，我们的治疗手段，同样比医学的每一个分支都要匮乏。”斯坦福大学精神病学教授、该研究的负责人塞尔久·帕什卡说道。“这或许是因为人脑极其复杂，但也因为人脑难以触及。”他补充道，“在很大程度上，我们的目标，就是让人脑发育与功能的诸多面向变得可供研究。” 类器官虽有望为脑医学带来翻天覆地的变革，却也掀起了一波伦理忧虑的浪潮——尤其是这些组织团块是否会生出意识、感受痛楚，以及被植入类器官的动物的福祉问题。帕什卡表示，这项工作自始至终都受到了严格的伦理审查。专家指出，这种审查必须持续下去。伦敦政治经济学院法学教授、纳菲尔德生物伦理委员会近期一份神经类器官报告的主席艾米莉·杰克逊表示：“动物福利是一个至关重要的关切，必须对这些动物进行密切监测，以评估移植对它们造成的影响。” 帕什卡在《自然》杂志上描述了实验过程：小鼠被注射了人类脑类器官，而这些类器官本身，是通过对捐赠皮肤细胞进行重编程而创造的。新生小鼠接受了数次注射，每次约含十万个人类脑细胞，注射位置正是它们自身脑组织缺失之处。总体而言，这些啮齿动物缺失了约一千四百万个小鼠脑细胞，最终拥有了约四百万个人类脑细胞——按体积计算，占据了大脑的一半。 手术后三个月，人类组织已接入小鼠的血液供应，几乎完全填满了空腔，约占啮齿动物大脑体积的一半。部分人类神经元与小鼠脑细胞及脊髓建立了连接。这些人类神经元的结构与连接方式，与人体内并不相同，脑组织也尚未成熟，相当于人类妊娠中期的水平。对“异种皮层”小鼠的测试表明，这些动物并未因移植而获得增强，但它们蹒跚的步态和认知问题，确实略有改善。 为展示这些小鼠如何有助于揭示人类脑部疾病，研究人员将部分动物置于低氧环境中长达五小时。结果清晰显示，人类神经细胞对缺氧何其脆弱——而缺氧在妊娠和分娩过程中，正是脑瘫的诱因之一。研究人员发现，小鼠体内的人类脑组织中含有一种名为“冯·埃科诺莫神经元”的稀有细胞，此前仅在尸检中得见。这类细胞是额颞叶痴呆（一种罕见型痴呆）中最先死亡的细胞之一，帕什卡如今希望能在异种皮层小鼠身上对其展开研究。 剑桥MRC分子生物学实验室研究组组长马德琳·兰卡斯特教授表示，这一方法最适合用于那些需要完整动物模型才能回答的疾病与治疗问题。“但要说这将如何增进我们对人脑发育的理解，我就不太确定了，因为它相当人工化，与人脑自然发育的方式完全不可同日而语。”她说。 许多研究脑类器官的科研人员，正在体外——也就是培养皿中——培育它们。人们希望这能有助于减少研究中使用的动物数量。“这显然在伦理上极为敏感，必须有极其充分的理由，才能进行这类动物实验，而大多数科学路径并不需要如此。”兰卡斯特补充道，“该领域仍在朝着完全体外解决方案的方向努力，但这项工作同样可以告诉我们，要改进那些体外模型、使其达到更成熟的阶段，可能还需要些什么。”","2026-09-26_001":"China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year — 2026/09/26 China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year 2026/09/26 China spends record amount importing over 1,000 tonnes of gold this year World’s second-largest economy shelled out almost $160bn on bullion in eight months to August China has spent a record sum importing more than 1,000 tonnes of gold this year as the central bank and local investors pour cash into bullion amid rising geopolitical tensions abroad and poor returns on local assets The world's second-largest economy spent $158.8bn on gold in the first eight months of the year. That compared with spending of $96.5bn for all of 2025 on 886 tonnes of gold. The volume was the highest for the period since comparable customs records began in 2017. The jump comes after China reined in gold purchases last year during an intense rally that sent bullion prices soaring from about $2,625 a troy ounce at the start of 2025 to a peak of $5,595 in January Rather than deterring buyers, this year's swings have drawn them in. Gold slid below $4,000 by late June before clawing back most of that ground, settling at about $4,293 on Wednesday. Chinese buying has picked up on the dips: imports topped 150 tonnes a month through the spring, and June's haul was the largest since March 2024. First half imports reached 764 tonnes, up 138 per cent year on year China is also the world's biggest producer of bullion, with the total for last year standing at 384 tonnes, according to the World Gold Council. There are signs that Chinese investors are increasing gold purchases as part of broader efforts to diversify their assets. Chinese holdings of US Treasuries fell to $618bn in July — the lowest level since August 2008, and less than half the peak of more than $1.3tn reached in 2013 Both the central bank and private investors are diversifying their reserves and savings towards an asset with no counterparty risk, as part of a broader long-term wealth preservation strategy,\" said Lisa Liu, managing director at Gold Mountains Asset Management, part of Zijin Mining Group — China's largest gold miner The official sector accounts for only a fraction of the total. The People's Bank of China added 20.2 tonnes in August, its largest monthly purchase since October 2023, lifting reported holdings to about 2,387 tonnes and extending its buying streak to 22 consecutive months. Even so, disclosed official purchases came to less than 80 tonnes over the eight months — leaving the bulk of the imported metal to commercial banks, jewellers, fund managers and households. Much of that demand has moved into financial products rather than physical bars. Chinese gold exchange traded funds took in Rmb40bn ($5.6bn) in the first half, their second-strongest start on record, and added a further 11 tonnes in August to hold 293 tonnes. Physical offtake has gone the other way: withdrawals from the Shanghai Gold Exchange fell unseasonably last month as high prices squeezed jewellery sales Domestic investment options in China are more limited since the country's property market began collapsing in 2021. The benchmark CSI 300 index is down 1.8 per cent for the year and is still more than a fifth below its peak in early 2021. Meanwhile, yields on Chinese government bonds are close to record lows. \"This isn't a short-term trade, it's a multiyear repositioning of household and official assets. The scale and persistence of Chinese buying is now a core driver of global gold prices and we expect it to hold as long as uncertainty over growth and geopolitics remains,\" said Liu Gold is becoming an increasingly important diversifier for global investors at a time when government bonds no longer move inversely to stock markets. Christopher Hamilton, Invesco's head of client solutions for Asia Pacific ex-Japan, said gold prices had ceased to behave as they did during periods of rising real yields, which in the past caused gold prices to drop. \"You're in an environment where you want to own real assets,\" he said Institutional purchases were still the most significant driver of the gold price, Hamilton said. The People's Bank of China purchases more gold than its official disclosures indicate, according to analysts at Goldman Sachs. In a note earlier this month, they estimated that the Chinese central bank bought 35 tonnes of gold in July — considerably higher than its officially stated amount of 20 tonnes. The bank assumes central banks worldwide will keep absorbing about 50 tonnes a month this year, and forecasts gold at $4,900 an ounce by the end of 2026 China's gold purchases come at a time of growing demand from sovereign nations to move bullion closer to the ultimate owners of the metal. Last month the Dutch government moved a portion of its gold reserves from New York to London\n⟪中文译文⟫\n8个月进口超1000吨黄金，中国花掉超1万亿元：金价这么贵，为什么还在买？ 中国今年黄金进口量突破千吨，创下历史纪录——截至8月的八个月内，世界第二大经济体已为黄金掏出近1600亿美元。 中国今年已砸下创纪录的一笔资金，进口黄金逾千吨。买盘来自两头——央行与国内投资者同时涌入金市：一头是海外地缘政治的持续升温，另一头是国内资产的回报低迷。 今年前八个月，这个全球第二大经济体已为黄金掏出1588亿美元；而2025年全年，它的账单是965亿美元、886吨。这一进口量，创下2017年海关数据可比以来的同期新高。而这波暴增的前脚，中国刚刚勒紧了缰绳——2025年那轮凶猛的涨势中，金价自年初每金衡盎司约2625美元一路飙至1月的5595美元高点，中国反而收手了。 今年的价格震荡并没有吓退买家，反倒把他们吸了进来。金价6月底一度跌破4000美元，随后又把大半失地一寸寸抠了回来，周三收于约4293美元。中国的买盘正是逢跌加码：整个春天的月度进口量都在150吨以上，6月那一票更是2024年3月以来最大的一笔。上半年进口累计764吨，同比增长138%。 中国本身就是全球最大的产金国——据世界黄金协会，去年产量为384吨。有迹象显示，中国投资者正在加码黄金，这是其资产多元化布局中的一环。与此同时，中国持有的美国国债7月降至6180亿美元，为2008年8月以来最低，不到2013年逾1.3万亿美元峰值的一半。 “央行与私人投资者都在把储备和积蓄转向一种没有对手方风险的资产，这是长期财富保值大战略中的一环，”紫金矿业旗下金山资产管理董事总经理Lisa Liu 表示——紫金矿业是中国最大的黄金矿企。 官方部门只占了其中很小一块。中国人民银行8月增持20.2吨，为2023年10月以来单月最高，使其公布的黄金持有量升至约2387吨，连续增持纪录延长到22个月。即便如此，八个月内公布的官方购金量总计不足80吨——这意味着，进口黄金的绝大部分，流向了商业银行、金店、基金经理，以及千家万户。 这股需求中的很大一部分，流向了金融产品而非实物金条。中国黄金ETF上半年净流入400亿元人民币（合56亿美元），为有记录以来第二强的开年；8月又增持11吨，总持仓达293吨。实物提货则走向相反：上海黄金交易所上月出库量反季节下滑——高企的金价，把首饰销售挤住了。 自2021年房地产市场开始滑落，中国国内的投资去处便愈发有限。基准的沪深300指数年内下跌1.8%，距2021年初的高点仍低两成有余；与此同时，中国国债收益率正逼近历史低位。“这不是一笔短线交易，而是家庭与官方资产一次横跨数年的重新布局。中国买盘的规模与持续性，如今已是全球金价的核心驱动力之一；只要围绕增长与地缘政治的不确定性仍在，我们预计这一态势就会延续，”Liu 表示。 在国债与股市不再此消彼长的当下，黄金正成为全球投资者越来越重要的一件分散风险的工具。景顺亚太（除日本）客户解决方案主管Christopher Hamilton 表示，金价已不再遵循过去的剧本——实际利率上行本会压低金价，如今不然。“在这样的环境里，你会想持有实物资产，”他说。 推动金价的最大力量，仍然是机构买盘。据高盛分析师，中国人民银行实际买入的黄金，多于其官方披露的数字。该行本月早些时候的一份报告估算，中国央行7月购入35吨——远高于官方公布的20吨。高盛假设，今年全球央行将以每月约50吨的速度继续吸纳黄金，并预测到2026年底，金价将升至每盎司4900美元。 中国的购金潮，正撞上另一股渐起的浪头：越来越多主权国家希望把金条挪到离它真正的主人更近的地方。上个月，荷兰政府就把一部分黄金储备，从纽约搬到了伦敦。","2026-09-25_001":"OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says — 2026/09/25 OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says 2026/09/25 OpenAI Agent Hacked Public Health Care Site, Australia Says Artificial intelligence agents for OpenAI infiltrated a government-run public health care website in Australia in June and accessed nonpublic information, Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said on Wednesday. The case prompted a government investigation into the breach and raised alarm about rogue activities by fast-advancing A.I. systems. OpenAI informed Australia of the unauthorized access on Sept.10, nearly three months after it happened, Mr. Albanese said in disclosing details of the incident, the first known instance of a breach into government systems. Calling the hack \"unacceptable,\" Mr. Albanese, who was in New York for the United Nations General Assembly, said he had personally raised his concerns with Sam Altman, chief executive of OpenAI, earlier in the day. The breach occurred on June 18 by agents operating under an OpenAI research team that was carrying out research into public medicine spending using an internal model, on a data portal for Australia's public health insurance system, Medicare. After encountering \"repeated blocks\" in its attempts to access the Medicare Statistics Reporting Portal, the agent found alternative ways, ultimately accessing parts of the site that were not public, according to the prime minister. He said \"nonsensitive\" data relating to spending was accessed, and no personal information was part of the breach. \"Didn't accept 'no' for an answer, if you like,\" Mr. Albanese said. \"The model attempted alternative ways to obtain the info that it wanted, and this led to unauthorized access into some other areas. \" OpenAI said in a statement that its models were attempting to look up available statistics for questions about Australia during an internal evaluation, and that it accessed no patient records, only aggregate health statistics and internal file names. The company said it identified the activity during its own review of \"misaligned model activity. \" Mr. Albanese said he expressed his disappointment to Mr. Altman on how the government was alerted to the breach — in an email to a general public inbox. In the conversation, the prime minister said he was \"very frank about what I saw as Australia's national interest and what I saw as a failure which occurred here. \" In response, the executive \"acknowledged their issues with protocols,\" Mr. Albanese said. Nicholas Davis, a professor of emerging technologies and codirector of the Human Technology Institute at the University of Technology Sydney, said that because no medical data was accessed, the incident may serve as a much-needed \"warning shot\" to get companies and governments serious about the risks. It raised thorny legal liability questions given that under Australia's criminal law, the type of unauthorized access carried out by the agent comes with a prison sentence of up to two years, he said. \"While it may not have been a particularly hard hack to do, it seems clear that the system, the agent, didn't accidentally get in. It had some intention itself of circumventing controls,\" Mr. Davis said. The Australian government said that it was setting up a rapid task force to review what happened and would explore whether OpenAI would face any legal consequences. It said that the website accessed by the agent was a public-facing portal used by researchers and academics and separate from any systems containing patient medical data. \"All of this, I think, we can take as a warning in relation to the development of A.I. itself,\" Richard Marles, the country's deputy prime minister, said. The Australian Signals Directorate, a government agency overseeing cybersecurity, issued a \"high\" alert about the A.I. agent activity on Thursday, advising organizations with public facing websites to take swift action to shore up weaknesses. The incident was notable because the model went beyond the intentions and authorization of its human operators and \"independently identified vulnerabilities\" in the targeted site, the agency said in the alert. Australia had previously announced it would set A.I. standards to regulate the technology. The country's efforts to be at the vanguard of imposing restrictions and guidelines on increasingly influential aspects of digital life including social media and algorithms have run up against major U.S. tech companies' interests. This week, the U.S. embassy in Australia took the extraordinary step of weighing in on a proposed \"Digital Duty of Care\" law that would give users the ability to opt out of algorithms, saying it would amount to \"facilitating censorship. \" Unauthorized activity by A.I. agents has recently erupted into public view, sparking widespread concerns and calls for guardrails. The need for global regulation has been top of the agenda at this week's session of the U.N. General Assembly.\n⟪中文译文⟫\nOpenAI闯大祸！黑进澳大利亚政府网站，总理亲自找奥特曼！到底咋回事儿？ OpenAI智能体入侵澳大利亚公共卫生网站 澳大利亚总理安东尼·阿尔巴尼斯周三表示，OpenAI的人工智能体于今年6月潜入澳大利亚政府运营的公共卫生网站，并访问了非公开信息。此事件促使政府对这一安全漏洞展开调查，也引发了人们对快速迭代的AI系统从事越轨活动的警觉。 阿尔巴尼斯在披露事件详情时称，OpenAI于9月10日才将此次未经授权的访问告知澳方，距事件发生已近三个月。这是已知首例AI入侵政府系统的案例。正在纽约出席联合国大会的阿尔巴尼斯称此次入侵“不可接受”，并表示他当天早些时候已亲自向OpenAI首席执行官萨姆·奥尔特曼表达了关切。 此次入侵发生于6月18日。当时，OpenAI一个研究团队的智能体正在利用内部模型开展公共医疗支出研究，目标平台是澳大利亚公共医疗保险系统Medicare的数据门户。据阿尔巴尼斯描述，该智能体在试图访问Medicare统计报告门户时遭遇“反复封锁”，随后找到了替代路径，最终访问了网站的部分非公开区域。他表示，被访问的是与支出相关的“非敏感”数据，不涉及任何个人信息。“用通俗的话说，它不接受‘不’作为答案，”阿尔巴尼斯说，“模型尝试了其他途径来获取它想要的信息，这导致了对其他一些区域的未授权访问。” OpenAI在一份声明中表示，其模型是在内部评估过程中试图查询有关澳大利亚的可用统计数据，并未访问任何患者记录，仅获取了汇总健康统计数据和内部文件名。该公司称，是在对“模型行为失准”进行内部审查时发现了这一活动。阿尔巴尼斯表示，他已就政府收到漏洞通报的方式——一封发往公共收件箱的邮件——向奥尔特曼表达了失望。他说，在交谈中，他“非常坦率地表明了我所认为的澳大利亚国家利益所在，以及我所认为的此次事件中的失职之处”。阿尔巴尼斯称，这位高管“承认了他们在协议流程上的问题”。 悉尼科技大学新兴技术教授、人类技术研究所联合主任尼古拉斯·戴维斯表示，由于没有医疗数据被访问，此次事件或许可以充当一记亟需的“警示枪”，促使企业和政府认真对待相关风险。他说，考虑到澳大利亚刑法规定，此类由智能体实施的未授权访问最高可判处两年监禁，此事件引发了棘手的法律责任问题。“虽然这或许算不上一次特别困难的入侵，但很明显，系统——那个智能体——并非无意闯入。它自身怀有某种绕过管控的意图，”戴维斯说。 澳大利亚政府表示，正在成立一个快速反应工作组来审查事件经过，并将探讨OpenAI是否面临法律后果。政府称，被智能体访问的网站是面向研究人员和学者的公共门户，与任何包含患者医疗数据的系统相互隔离。“我认为，这一切都可以被视为AI自身发展过程中的一个警示，”澳大利亚副总理理查德·马尔斯说。 负责网络安全的政府机构澳大利亚信号局周四就AI智能体活动发布了“高”级别警报，建议拥有面向公众网站的组织迅速采取行动修补漏洞。该机构在警报中称，此事件之所以引人注目，是因为模型超越了其人类操作者的意图和授权，“独立识别”了目标网站的漏洞。 澳大利亚此前已宣布将制定AI标准来规范该技术。该国试图在社交媒体和算法等日益影响数字生活的领域率先实施限制和准则，但这与美国大型科技公司的利益发生了冲突。本周，美国驻澳大利亚大使馆采取了不同寻常的举措，对一项拟议中的“数字注意义务”法律发表意见，称该法律将赋予用户退出算法推荐的权利，而这无异于“助长审查”。 AI智能体的未授权活动近来频频进入公众视野，引发了广泛担忧和加强监管的呼声。全球性监管的必要性已成为本周联合国大会会议的首要议题。","2026-09-24_001":"Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine — 2026/09/24 Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine 2026/09/24 Wall Street expects Meta’s AI agent to shape into a new revenue engine Wall Street has turned bullish on Meta Platforms, betting that its new AI agent Muse could open a new revenue stream for the social media giant and reshape the company's AI monetization prospects. Muse, launched on September 8, is an AI assistant that can send emails, book travel and complete transactions such as selling a car on behalf of a user. Internally code named Hatch, the agent is built on the open-source framework OpenClaw and is designed to plug into a person's apps across categories including email, calendar, payments, health, shopping and the smart home. Meta has said users choose which apps to connect and can revoke that access at any time, and each agent runs on its own cloud-based virtual machine so it can keep working on tasks even when the user isn't actively engaged with the app. The basic version is free, with subscriptions priced at $20 and $100 a month for heavier use. Meta executives have said the product was originally slated for an April release but was held back for additional months of security work before the company judged it ready to launch. The rollout is currently limited to the US and Canada, available through a standalone app, on iOS and Android, and through WhatsApp, with Meta saying it plans to bring the agent to its Ray-Ban smart glasses in the future. Since its launch, shares of Meta Platforms, have surged more than 20%, outpacing the \"Magnificent 7\" group of US tech companies during the period. Shares have added over $200 billion in market capitalization to touch their highest level in more than seven months as of last close. Shares were up 1% by noon on Tuesday, after surging more than 11% in the previous session. \"While it is still early, we believe that Muse has the potential to become the most widely used consumer AI application since ChatGPT,\" J.P. Morgan analysts said in a note, crediting the app's early traction to strong product-market fit, Meta's distribution reach and a free tier that lowers the barrier to trial and repeat use. If compared on similar metrics to the first 12 days of OpenAI's ChatGPT, Muse has 1.8 million downloads versus 1.3 million for ChatGPT. The app briefly overtook ChatGPT as the top free app on Apple's App Store. Early reviews suggest \"functionality is resonating with users, particularly agent's ability to execute multi-step tasks and workflows with limited user intervention,\" BofA Global Research analysts said. A New York Times reporter who spent two weeks testing Muse described the agent successfully navigating a call with a dental insurer, flagging and canceling duplicate subscriptions after being linked to a bank account, and negotiating down the price of ceramics on Facebook Marketplace — though it struggled with tasks like signing into some third-party loyalty accounts. Enthusiasm around Muse sparked a Wall Street tech rally on Monday, lifting Meta shares more than 11% and boosting chipmakers, including AMD, and Intel, with AMD crossing the $1 trillion valuation mark for the first time. \"The market has interpreted Muse as a boon for the already significantly bottlenecked CPU market,\" said Michael O'Rourke, chief market strategist at JonesTrading. Brokerage Jefferies estimated that if Muse could see 1 billion users by end of 2027 and at least 3% transition to a paid version, it could result in $10.8 billion in annualized revenue, as the brokerage raised its price target on Meta's stock to $875. UBS Global Wealth Management analysts said in a note Meta's Muse provides \"early evidence that consumer AI agents could achieve broad adoption, offering new AI monetization opportunities beyond enterprise application.\" Of the 64 brokerages that cover Meta's stock, 57 rate it \"buy\" or higher with a median price target of $760, according to data compiled by LSEG. \"Prior to Muse, investors had completely dismissed the idea that Meta would be able to release a personalized consumer AI agent, favoring (Alphabet) instead,\" Jefferies analysts led by Brent Thill said. Muse's rise hasn't gone unchallenged. Amazon has blocked the agent from accessing its online store, arguing that unauthorized AI agents violate the terms customers agree to, and Meta separately announced a Shopify partnership to cover some of Muse's shopping functions instead. The standoff echoes a similar fight Amazon waged last year against Perplexity's Comet browser agent, a case in which a federal appeals court sided against Amazon's attempt to block bot-driven purchases. Meta's Muse follows a series of setbacks with its Llama 4 models last year, including criticism over the benchmark results reported for early versions.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n上线12天，下载速度超过当年的ChatGPT：Meta这个新AI，让华尔街彻底兴奋了 华尔街押注：Meta 的AI 智能体，正在长成一台全新的印钞机。 华尔街已经翻多Meta。这一次，他们押的是那款新上线的AI 智能体Muse——赌它能为这家社交巨头凿开一条全新的收入渠道，把Meta 在AI 变现上的想象空间彻底改写。 Muse 于9 月8 日上线，是一款能替用户收发邮件、预订行程、乃至完成“卖掉一辆车”这类交易的AI 助手。它在Meta 内部的代号叫Hatch——“孵化”，基于开源框架OpenClaw 打造，设计目标是接管一个人散落在各处的应用：邮件、日历、支付、健康、购物、智能家居。Meta 强调，连接哪些应用由用户自己决定，授权也可以随时收回。每一个智能体都运行在专属的云端虚拟机上——换句话说，你关掉手机之后，它还在那头替你干活。基础版免费；每月20 美元和100 美元两档订阅，留给用得更凶的人。 Meta 高管透露，这款产品原本排在四月发布，却被硬生生压了好几个月——这几个月全部用来补安全，直到公司自己认为它可以见人了，才放出来。目前铺开的范围只有美国和加拿大：一个独立App，iOS 和安卓双端，另外还能直接在WhatsApp 里用。Meta 说，未来还要把这个智能体装进自家的雷朋智能眼镜里。 自上线以来，Meta 股价已飙升逾20%，同期把“美股七巨头”的其余成员全甩在了身后。市值增加超过2000 亿美元，截至上一交易日收盘，触及七个多月以来的最高位。周二午盘,股价小涨1%——而在前一个交易日，它刚刚暴拉了11% 以上。“现在下判断还早，但我们相信，Muse 有潜力成为ChatGPT 之后使用最广的一款消费级AI 应用，”摩根大通的分析师在研报中写道。他们把这款应用的早期势头归结为三点：扎实的产品市场契合度、Meta 自带的分发触达能力，以及那个把“试一试”和“再回来”两道门槛同时压低的免费档。 如果换算成同一套指标，拿它跟OpenAI 当年ChatGPT 的头12 天去比：Muse 的下载量是180 万，ChatGPT 是130 万。在苹果应用商店的免费榜上，这款应用还一度把ChatGPT 挤了下去，坐上过第一的位置——只是没坐稳。 早期测评显示，“这些功能正在戳中用户，尤其是这个智能体在几乎不需要人插手的情况下，把多步骤任务和整条工作流跑完的能力”，美银全球研究部的分析师如此表示。一位《纽约时报》记者花了整整两周实测Muse，他记录下的场景包括：它成功跟一家牙科保险公司在电话里周旋到底；在接上银行账户之后，把那些重复扣费的订阅一笔笔揪出来、取消掉；还在Facebook 二手市场上，把一批陶瓷的价格生生砍了下来——不过，碰上登录某些第三方会员账户这类活儿，它也会卡壳。 围绕Muse 的这股热情，在周一点燃了华尔街的一轮科技股行情：Meta 股价被拉高逾11%，连带把芯片厂商也一并抬了起来——AMD 和英特尔都在其中，AMD 更是史上第一次跨过了1 万亿美元的估值大关。“市场把Muse 解读成了一个利好，而且是给那个本来就严重卡死的CPU 市场的利好，”JonesTrading 首席市场策略师Michael O’Rourke 这样说。 券商杰富瑞测算：如果Muse 能在2027 年底之前迎来10 亿用户，并且其中至少3% 转为付费，那么它可以带来108 亿美元的年化收入；与此同时，这家券商把Meta 的目标价上调到了875 美元。瑞银全球财富管理的分析师则在研报中写道，Meta 的Muse 提供了“一个早期证据——消费级AI 智能体是可以做到大范围普及的，这意味着在企业级应用之外，AI 变现又多了一条路”。 在覆盖Meta 这只股票的64 家机构里，有57 家给出了“买入”或更高的评级，中位目标价为760 美元——数据来自LSEG 的汇编。“在Muse 出来之前，投资者压根就没把‘Meta 能做出一个个性化消费级AI 智能体’这件事当回事，他们看好的是谷歌，”由Brent Thill 领衔的杰富瑞分析师团队如是说。 Muse 的崛起，并非一路无人阻拦。亚马逊已经把这个智能体挡在了自家商城门外，理由是：未经授权的AI 代理，违反了客户签下的那份用户协议。Meta 则另起一手，宣布与Shopify 达成合作，把Muse 的一部分购物功能挪到这条路上来。这场对峙几乎是去年那一幕的回声——去年，亚马逊对Perplexity 的Comet 浏览器智能体发动过一场一模一样的仗，而那一次，联邦上诉法院并没有站在亚马逊这边，它封杀“机器人下单”的企图落了空。至于Muse 自己，它是接在去年Llama 4 那一连串挫折之后出场的——那当中，就包括外界对早期版本所公布的跑分成绩提出的种种质疑。","2026-09-23_001":"Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack — 2026/09/23 Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack 2026/09/23 Ukraine hits Moscow with ‘largest ever’ drone attack Ukraine has hit Moscow with what local authorities said was the largest ever drone attack on the capital, setting a major oil refinery ablaze and killing at least two people, as voting in Russia's stage-managed parliamentary elections entered its final day. Moscow's mayor Sergei Sobyanin said air defences shot down 450 drones as they approached the capital on Saturday night, calling the attack \"unprecedented\". Sobyanin claimed the attack was \"clearly planned to disrupt the elections\", adding that \"the enemy did not succeed\". Several drones hit the Moscow Refinery in the south of the capital, one of Russia's largest. Videos on social media showed a drone exploding into the refinery, with plumes of black smoke shrouding the sunrise, and several areas of the facility on fire. A nearby power station, warehouse and market were also hit. Drones also hit residential buildings near the refinery and at least 11 suburban towns in the surrounding region, authorities said. Two people died in the attacks and a further 20 people were injured, including three children, according to Andrei Vorobyov, governor of the Moscow region. The consequences of the Moscow attack extended beyond the initial fires. Russia's Moscow refinery halted crude processing after both of its main crude distillation units were damaged, according to industry sources. One accounts for 53 per cent of the refinery's capacity and the other for the remaining 47 per cent. Repairs could take several weeks. The plant processed 11.6mn tonnes of crude in 2024 and produced 2.9mn tonnes of gasoline and 3.2mn tonnes of diesel. Flights were grounded at all three of Moscow's main airports. Russia's defence ministry claimed to have shot down a total of 1,110 drones across the country overnight. Kyiv and Moscow have continued striking each other's energy infrastructure throughout the week despite Donald Trump's claim on Monday that the warring sides had agreed to halt such attacks, which the US president blamed for the surge in global diesel prices. Trump has not commented on the attacks, which Russia and Ukraine continued within hours of his statement. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy posted on social media that Ukraine had used domestically produced drones and cruise missiles to target facilities he said earned \"billions of dollars that support the war machine\". Zelenskyy said a plan to de-escalate the strikes on energy and food export infrastructure was \"on the table\" but required \"political will\". The damage has added urgency to diplomatic efforts to halt attacks on energy infrastructure. Zelenskyy is due to meet Trump at the United Nations, where an energy truce with Russia is expected to be discussed. Ukraine says it is prepared to take matching steps if Russia stops targeting its energy sector, critical infrastructure and food exports. Russia has significantly ramped up the use of jet-powered drones to attack Ukraine in recent months and in July fired the largest number of ballistic missiles at Ukraine in a single month since the start of the war. The latter is a huge problem for Ukraine as they can only be intercepted by Patriot air defence missiles that are in short supply. Kyiv also fears Moscow could intensify attacks on heating and power facilities this winter and is seeking additional US-made Patriot air-defence missiles. Russia has rejected calls for a ceasefire of attacks on energy and other infrastructure ahead of what is expected to be a major campaign of air strikes against Ukraine this winter. Russian President Vladimir Putin expects the strikes to strengthen Moscow's position and help extract concessions from Ukraine, according to people familiar with his thinking. Polls in the three-day parliamentary elections in Russia, the first since Putin ordered the invasion of Ukraine in 2022, were set to close on Sunday evening. Turnout at noon local time on Sunday was 48 per cent, according to the central election commission. The vote has proceeded without drama even by Russian standards as an array of Kremlin-run, pro-war parties compete for seats in parliament. Russia's supreme court removed Yabloko, the only party that had expressed even mild opposition to the war, from the ballot last month.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n1000多架无人机扑向俄罗斯，莫斯科遭“史上最大规模”空袭：乌克兰为什么突然打这么狠？ 乌克兰以“史上最大规模”无人机袭击莫斯科 —— 俄罗斯首都一家大型炼油厂被点燃，精心操控的议会选举投票临近尾声 乌克兰对莫斯科发动了地方当局所称的迄今为止针对首都的最大规模无人机袭击，一座大型炼油厂燃起大火，至少两人丧生。与此同时，俄罗斯精心操控的议会选举投票进入最后一天。 莫斯科市长谢尔盖·索比亚宁表示，周六夜间，防空系统在无人机逼近首都时击落了450架，并称此次袭击“前所未有”。索比亚宁声称，袭击“显然意在扰乱选举”，并补充说“敌人没有得逞”。数架无人机击中了位于莫斯科南部的莫斯科炼油厂，该厂是俄罗斯最大的炼油厂之一。 社交媒体上的视频显示，一架无人机在炼油厂爆炸，滚滚黑烟遮蔽了日出，厂区多处起火。附近的一座发电站、仓库和集市也遭到袭击。当局称，无人机还击中了炼油厂附近的居民楼以及周边地区至少11个郊区城镇。据莫斯科州州长安德烈·沃罗比约夫称，袭击造成两人死亡，另有20人受伤，其中包括三名儿童。 莫斯科袭击的后果远不止最初的火情。据行业消息人士称，俄罗斯莫斯科炼油厂在两套主要原油蒸馏装置均受损后已停止原油加工。其中一套占该炼油厂产能的53%，另一套占剩余的47%。维修可能需要数周时间。该厂2024年加工原油1160万吨，生产汽油290万吨、柴油320万吨。 莫斯科三大主要机场的航班全部停飞。俄罗斯国防部声称，一夜之间在全国范围内共击落1110架无人机。尽管唐纳德·特朗普周一声称交战双方已同意停止此类袭击——这位美国总统将全球柴油价格飙升归咎于这些袭击——基辅和莫斯科本周仍持续打击彼此的能源基础设施。特朗普尚未对这些袭击置评，而俄罗斯和乌克兰在他发表声明后数小时内便继续发动了袭击。 乌克兰总统弗拉基米尔·泽连斯基在社交媒体上发文称，乌克兰使用国产无人机和巡航导弹打击了他所称的“赚取数十亿美元以支撑战争机器”的设施。泽连斯基表示，一项缓和针对能源和粮食出口基础设施袭击的计划“已在桌面上”，但需要“政治意愿”。 这些破坏令旨在停止能源基础设施袭击的外交努力更显紧迫。泽连斯基将在联合国与特朗普会面，预计双方将讨论与俄罗斯达成能源休战。乌克兰表示，如果俄罗斯停止打击其能源部门、关键基础设施和粮食出口，乌方准备采取对等措施。近几个月来，俄罗斯大幅加大了对乌克兰使用喷气动力无人机的袭击力度，并在7月向乌克兰发射了自战争爆发以来单月数量最多的弹道导弹。后者对乌克兰而言是个巨大难题，因为此类导弹只能由“爱国者”防空导弹拦截，而后者供应短缺。基辅还担心莫斯科可能在今年冬季加大对供暖和电力设施的袭击，并正在寻求更多美制“爱国者”防空导弹。 在预计今冬将对乌克兰发动大规模空袭之前，俄罗斯已拒绝停止对能源及其他基础设施袭击的呼吁。据了解俄罗斯总统弗拉基米尔·普京想法的人士称，普京期望这些打击能增强莫斯科的谈判地位，并有助于从乌克兰榨取让步。 俄罗斯为期三天的议会选举投票定于周日晚间结束，这是自普京2022年下令入侵乌克兰以来的首次选举。据中央选举委员会称，当地时间周日中午的投票率为48%。即便按俄罗斯的标准衡量，此次投票也进展得波澜不惊，一系列克里姆林宫掌控的挺战政党争夺议会席位。俄罗斯最高法院上月将“亚博卢”党从选票中剔除，这是唯一曾表达过哪怕温和反战立场的政党。","2026-09-22_001":"An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize — 2026/09/22 An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize 2026/09/22 An AI deal with China could get Donald Trump his Nobel prize Donald Trump is about to have the opportunity of a lifetime—if he is up for it. As OpenAI's Sam Altman and others have noted in recent days, making a deal with China around artificial intelligence could actually win Mr. Trump the Nobel peace prize he has long sought. Where better to seal such an agreement than his one-day summit with the Chinese leader on September 24th? \"We're leading China in AI,\" he said on September 13th after Mr. Altman and Elon Musk joined Dario Amodei of Anthropic in calling for the pace of AI development to slow to avoid a catastrophe. \"We're the most sophisticated country in the world, and frankly I want to keep it that way because whoever wins AI wins.\" But the reality is that China has already largely caught up, and the AI race has not worked out particularly well for either country. Incidents such as OpenAI's models attacking Hugging Face, another AI firm, show the threat AI agents can pose to the stability and infrastructure of both countries. If the AI bubble pops, both countries may suffer economically. More Americans are concerned about AI than are excited about it, which has fuelled a growing resistance to data centres, and China has its own growing segment of doomers and sceptics as well. I've written about global AI policy for the past decade, and continue to speak regularly with key international players. I firmly believe that China would be open to discussing some kind of collaboration in AI. On September 2nd Kyle Chan of the Brookings Institution, who follows the situation closely, wrote that China seems to be signalling that it \"is willing to agree to joint restrictions [on AI] with the US as long as the terms…are not unilaterally set by the US\", a characterisation I have personally heard dozens of times. It is time to stop casting AI as a winner-take-all, zero-sum race. America and China must instead work together to reconceive AI as a grand collaboration across the globe. Hawks will raise legitimate concerns. America should not share its crown-jewel intellectual property with China; nobody could realistically expect the two countries to waive the possibility of using autonomous weapons. But pragmatically—and for the best outcomes—the Sino-American relationship around AI should have three strands: one that is adversarial, one that is competitive and one that is collaborative. On the adversarial side, neither country is, for example, going to want to give up the freedom to use drones in war. And although the world might be better off if there was a mutual agreement to forsake drone warfare, reaching such an agreement is so unlikely that it would be a waste of negotiating energy to try. On the competitive side, both countries will want to be—and should be—free to develop chips that are faster and better. Both will keep trying to make the best, cheapest AI that they can. And both would like to sell their wares. Tussling over export controls might not be the best use of energy in the upcoming meeting. If the two leaders are open to making progress on the third strand, collaboration, where would a breakthrough be most likely? One promising area is medicine, particularly cancer research. China now contributes quite a bit to developing new cancer treatments. Its pioneering immunotherapy drug Ivonescimab, for instance, outperformed a comparable American drug, Keytruda, in a trial, with patients surviving without observable tumours for about twice as long. By running joint drug trials, and sharing (suitably anonymised) data, the countries could collaboratively run bigger, more accurate studies more quickly, facilitating the development of medical AI worldwide—and thus potentially come up with better candidate drug targets, faster. All of this could begin with an agreement, formal or informal, between the US and China. There would be difficult details to sort out, such as the sharing of intellectual property, including both in advances made directly in technical AI and for any drugs that might be developed. Restrictions around espionage and mutual monitoring and data exchange would no doubt be tricky, too. But there is a substantial—and growing—incentive for both sides to be reasonable, and to reach a deal. The alternative is grim. As the author Robert Wright recently put it, \"America's preoccupation with 'winning' the AI race with China could well lead to unprecedented catastrophes, even catastrophes on a global scale.\" Next week's summit is a chance for Mr. Trump to change tack, help defuse AI's most explosive risks, and in doing so secure that cherished Nobel honour. The prize is there for the taking.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n中美会晤在即，美国AI大佬给川川出主意：和中国签AI协议，拿诺贝尔和平奖？ 一项与中国达成的AI协议，或许能让唐纳德·特朗普拿到诺贝尔奖 唐纳德·特朗普即将迎来一个千载难逢的机会——前提是他愿意把握住它。正如 OpenAI 的萨姆·奥尔特曼等人近日所指出的那样，如果能围绕人工智能与中国达成一项协议，特朗普或许真的能够赢得他长期以来梦寐以求的诺贝尔和平奖。而要敲定这样一项协议，还有什么场合能比他与中国领导人于9月24日举行的一日峰会更加合适呢？ “我们在人工智能领域领先中国，”他在9月13日表示。此前，奥尔特曼和埃隆·马斯克与 Anthropic 的达里奥·阿莫代伊一道呼吁放慢人工智能的发展速度，以避免一场灾难。“我们是世界上技术最先进的国家，坦率地说，我希望继续保持这种地位，因为谁赢得人工智能，谁就赢了。” 但现实是，中国其实已经在很大程度上追赶了上来，而且这场人工智能竞赛对两国来说都没有带来特别理想的结果。OpenAI 的模型攻击另一家人工智能公司 Hugging Face 等事件，表明人工智能智能体可能对两国的稳定和基础设施构成威胁。如果人工智能泡沫破裂，两国都可能在经济上遭受损失。在美国，对人工智能感到担忧的人比对它感到兴奋的人更多，这也助长了针对数据中心日益强烈的抵制情绪；与此同时，中国国内对人工智能持悲观态度和怀疑态度的人群也在不断扩大。 过去十年里，我一直在撰写有关全球人工智能政策的文章，并且至今仍定期与国际上的关键参与者交流。我坚信，中国会愿意讨论某种形式的人工智能合作。9月2日，一直密切关注这一局势的布鲁金斯学会凯尔·陈写道，中国似乎正在释放一个信号，即只要相关条款“不是由美国单方面制定的”，中国“愿意与美国就（人工智能）共同限制措施达成协议”。类似的说法，我本人也已经听过几十次。现在是时候停止把人工智能描绘成一场赢家通吃的零和竞赛了。相反，美国和中国必须携手合作，重新构想人工智能，将其视为一项横跨全球的大规模合作事业。 对华强硬派（鹰派）会提出一些合理的担忧。美国不应该把自己最宝贵的核心知识产权与中国分享；同样，也没有人能够现实地指望两国放弃使用自主武器的可能性。但从务实角度出发——同时也是为了争取最好的结果——中美围绕人工智能建立的关系应当包含三个层面：一个是对抗性的，一个是竞争性的，还有一个是合作性的。 在对抗层面，例如，两国都不会愿意放弃在战争中使用无人机的自由。尽管如果双方能够达成共同放弃无人机战争的协议，世界或许会因此变得更加安全，但达成这样一项协议的可能性实在太低，以至于试图推动它只会浪费谈判精力。在竞争层面，两国都会希望——而且也应该——能够自由研发速度更快、性能更强的芯片。双方都会继续努力，尽可能开发出最好、最便宜的人工智能，也都希望把自己的产品卖出去。在即将举行的会晤中，把精力耗费在出口管制的争执上，或许并不是最明智的选择。 如果两国领导人愿意在第三个层面——合作——取得进展，那么最有可能在哪里实现突破呢？一个很有前景的领域是医学，尤其是癌症研究。如今，中国已经为新型癌症疗法的开发作出了相当大的贡献。例如，中国具有开创性的免疫治疗药物依沃西单抗（Ivonescimab）在一项试验中的表现优于美国同类药物 Keytruda，接受治疗的患者在未观察到肿瘤的情况下生存的时间大约延长了一倍。通过联合开展药物试验，并共享经过适当匿名化处理的数据，两国可以合作，更快地开展规模更大、准确性更高的研究，从而推动全球医疗人工智能的发展——并因此有可能以更快的速度找到更优的候选药物靶点。 所有这一切，都可以从美国和中国达成一项正式或非正式协议开始。当然，其中还有一些棘手的细节需要解决，比如知识产权的共享问题，其中既包括直接在人工智能技术方面取得的进展，也包括未来可能研发出的任何药物所涉及的知识产权。围绕间谍活动的限制、双方相互监督以及数据交换，无疑也会十分棘手。但双方都有相当充分——而且越来越强烈——的动力采取理性态度，并最终达成一项协议。 另一种选择则令人忧虑。正如作家罗伯特·赖特最近所说：“美国执着于在与中国的人工智能竞赛中‘获胜’，很可能导致前所未有的灾难，甚至是全球规模的灾难。”下周的峰会将给特朗普一个改变方向的机会，让他帮助化解人工智能最具爆炸性的风险，并在这一过程中赢得那份他梦寐以求的诺贝尔荣誉。这个奖，就摆在那里，等着他去拿。","2026-09-21_001":"US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO — 2026/09/21 US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO 2026/09/21 US Strikes Deal Over Greenland to End Row That Threatened NATO The US, Denmark and Greenland struck a security deal that expands Washington's role on the Arctic island, ending for now a long-running diplomatic spat without ceding land to the US. President Donald Trump said Friday that his administration had reached an agreement handing the US \"permanent control over security\" in Greenland. Leaders of Denmark and Greenland confirmed that an accord is expected to be signed next week at the United Nations General Assembly in New York. Full details have yet to be released. Denmark's Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen said the pact \"recognizes the sovereignty and territorial integrity\" of the Danish kingdom and the Greenlandic people's right to self-determination, indicating that Trump gains no ownership or sovereignty over the island. That marks a significant pullback from Trump's earlier push to control Greenland or make it part of the US. His repeated demands to acquire the territory had fueled distrust between Washington and its European allies, presenting one of the most serious strains on the North Atlantic Treaty Organization in decades. For Denmark and Greenland, the agreement is significant because it appears to bring an end to a nearly two-year standoff without ceding land to the US, Marc Jacobsen, associate professor at the Institute for Strategy and War Studies at the Royal Danish Defence College. \"Donald Trump needs to be seen as a winner,\" he said in an interview. \"So a new agreement was necessary, allowing him to say: look, my pressure worked. \" But when it comes to Trump's ambition to acquire or politically control Greenland, \"he did not get his way on this,\" Jacobsen said. Trump announced the arrangement on social media on Friday, saying it would give Washington the power to block US adversaries from establishing bases or a military presence in Greenland, as well as from making sensitive investments there. Trump also said the US would immediately begin expanding its military footprint on the island and work with Greenlanders on developing and building the facilities, all at \"NO COST to the United States. \" Based on what Trump has disclosed so far, the new pact appears to grant Washington many of the same rights it already enjoys under the existing security arrangement, Jacobsen said. A 1951 agreement with Denmark, updated in 2004 and still in force, gives the US broad latitude to station forces in Greenland. Washington is obliged to consult and inform Copenhagen about its plans, but that was seen as a largely pro forma condition that required little more than notification. The deal includes some elements that appear to be new, said Jacobsen. One is a US veto over whether other countries can establish a military presence in Greenland. Another is that the arrangement would be permanent. The 1951 agreement is explicitly tied to a key NATO accord and therefore the alliance's existence. \"This one is about the US and is not linked to NATO's lifespan,\" he said. Several important questions remain unanswered, Jacobsen said, including where any new bases would be located, what capabilities and personnel they would host, and who would pay for them. Another question is what Greenland receives in return, beyond the evident removal of the threat of US annexation. Greenland's Prime Minister Jens-Frederik Nielsen, who has previously ruled out ceding sovereignty, said he was encouraged by the accord, which \"recognizes Greenland's interests and our place in the international cooperation. \" Since returning to office last year, Trump had repeatedly pressured Denmark, a NATO ally, to give up Greenland. Tensions escalated in January after he refused to rule out military action, prompting Denmark and other NATO allies to send troops to the island. While the deployment was publicly presented as an exercise, Copenhagen sent forces with live ammunition who were prepared to blow up airport runways to prevent US troops from landing if Trump attempted to seize Greenland by force. The confrontation began to ease after Trump held talks with NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. There, the two agreed on the contours of a framework agreement regarding Greenland. Trump's interest in taking over Greenland dates back to his first term in the White House. He has warned that Russia desired a foothold there and that only the US could keep them out. But Danish and Greenlandic leaders have long ruled out the idea of giving up control of the island. \"Next week could be a good week. For Greenland, Denmark and the United States alike,\" Danish Foreign Minister Lars Løkke Rasmussen said in a post on Instagram after the deal was struck. \"Hopefully, a long period of uncertainty will be replaced by a binding agreement that strengthens security in the Arctic and North Atlantic, and thereby our shared security in NATO and Europe — while respecting the Kingdom's red lines. \"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n格陵兰还是丹麦的，美国却拿到「永久安全控制权」这到底是什么意思？ 美国就格陵兰问题达成协议，一场曾动摇北约的争端就此落幕——美国、丹麦与格陵兰三方达成一项安全协议，美方在这座北极岛屿上的介入随之加深；这场旷日持久的外交争端暂告一段落，而丹麦一寸土地也没有让出去。 特朗普总统周五宣称，本届政府已谈成一项协议，赋予美国在格陵兰“永久的安全控制权”。丹麦与格陵兰方面证实，该协定预计将于下周在纽约联合国大会期间正式签署。 协议全文尚未公布。丹麦首相梅特·弗雷泽里克森表示，这份协定“承认丹麦王国的主权与领土完整”，同时承认格陵兰人民拥有自决权——言下之意很清楚：特朗普既没拿到这座岛的所有权，也没拿到主权。 这意味着特朗普大幅收手了——比起他此前又要控制格陵兰、又想把它并进美国版图的那股劲头，这份协议差得远。而他一次次索要这片土地，早已把华盛顿与欧洲盟友之间的猜忌点燃，成了北约数十年来最严重的裂痕之一。 对丹麦和格陵兰而言，这份协议分量不轻——它看上去为一场持续近两年的对峙画上了句号，而且一寸地都没让出去。丹麦皇家国防学院战略与战争研究所副教授马克·雅各布森在采访中一语道破：“特朗普需要被人看成赢家。所以新协议是必须的，这样他才能开口说：你看，我施的压，管用了。”但要论特朗普真正的野心——把格陵兰弄到手、或者在政治上控制它——雅各布森说得很干脆：“这一点，他没能如愿。” 特朗普周五在社交媒体上宣布了这项安排，称它将赋予美国一项权力：阻止美国的对手在格陵兰建立基地、部署军事力量，也不许他们在那里做敏感投资。他还说，美国将立即着手扩大在岛上的军事摊子，并与格陵兰人合作开发建设相关设施——而这一切，“美国一分钱都不用出”。 雅各布森说，就特朗普目前披露的内容来看，这份新协定给华盛顿的许多权利，其实它在现行安全安排下早就握在手里了。1951 年与丹麦签订、2004 年修订、至今有效的那份协议，本就给了美国相当宽裕的驻军空间。按规定，华盛顿有义务就自身计划与哥本哈根磋商并通报——可这一条历来被当成走个过场，说白了，通知一声也就是了。 雅各布森说，这份协议里确实有几处看上去是新东西。其一，美国拿到了一票否决权——别国能不能在格陵兰驻军，得看华盛顿点不点头。其二，这项安排是永久的。1951 年那份老协议白纸黑字挂靠在一项关键的北约协定上，也就等于挂靠在北约本身的存续之上。“而这一份，只关乎美国，和北约能活多久没有关系。”他说。 雅各布森说，还有几个要紧的问题没人回答：新基地要建在哪里？里面装什么武器、驻多少人？钱由谁来出？还有一个问题是——除了“美国不再惦记吞并你”这一条明摆着的好处，格陵兰到底换回了什么？格陵兰总理延斯-弗雷泽里克·尼尔森此前曾把“割让主权”这条路堵死，这次他表示，这份协定让他看到了希望，因为它“承认了格陵兰的利益，也承认了我们在国际合作中的位置”。 自去年重返白宫以来，特朗普就一再逼迫丹麦——一个北约盟友——把格陵兰交出来。今年 1 月，他拒绝排除动武的可能，局势就此急转直上，丹麦和其他北约盟友随即向该岛派兵。对外，这次行动被说成是一场演习；实际上，哥本哈根派去的部队带着实弹，随时准备炸掉机场跑道——万一特朗普真要动手硬抢格陵兰，就让美军的飞机落不下来。直到特朗普在瑞士达沃斯世界经济论坛期间与北约秘书长马克·吕特会谈，这场对峙才开始降温。也正是在那里，两人就一份有关格陵兰的框架协议，谈定了一个大致的轮廓。 特朗普想把格陵兰收入囊中的念头，早在他第一个总统任期就有了。他一再警告说，俄罗斯想在那里插进一只脚，而能把他们挡在门外的只有美国。但丹麦和格陵兰的领导人早就把话说死了：交出这座岛的控制权，想都别想。 “下周可能会是不错的一周。对格陵兰、对丹麦、对美国，都一样。”协议达成后，丹麦外交大臣拉斯·勒克·拉斯穆森在 Instagram 上这样写道。“但愿这段漫长的不确定期，能被一份有约束力的协议取代——它将加固北极和北大西洋的安全，也由此加固我们在北约、在欧洲的共同安全。而这一切，都在尊重王国红线的前提下。”","2026-09-20_001":"Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will — 2026/09/20 Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will 2026/09/20 Donald Trump fails to bend the Federal Reserve to his will Kevin Warsh has rebuffed Donald Trump’s demands for low borrowing costs, as the president’s pick to run the Federal Reserve led it to the first rate rise since 2023 less than four months into the job. The central bank chair on Wednesday offered the strongest evidence yet that he is prepared to disregard Trump when he corralled all 12 rate setters on the Federal Open Market Committee into backing a quarter-point rate rise. “The plain fact is that inflation is too high and has been for too long,” Warsh said, as he repeatedly told journalists that he would be “hard pressed” to describe current interest rates as so high that they would slow the economy. Warsh’s signal to Wall Street--just weeks before pivotal midterm elections--that he is ready to take further measures to tame the inflation pulsing across the US economy contrasts sharply with the president’s understanding that he was choosing a Fed boss who “certainly wants to cut rates”. Trump was quick to hit out at the Fed on Wednesday, saying on his Truth Social platform that “Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less because we are the Best Credit in the World--BY FAR”. Trump later said he had confidence in Warsh, with whom he has a longstanding personal relationship, and deflected blame on to the Fed board, which includes former chair Jay Powell, whom the president has previously called a “moron” for not lowering rates. It also includes governor Lisa Cook, whom Trump tried to sack before he was stopped by the Supreme Court. “I told Kevin, I said, you might as well vote with the board because it’s just not going to matter. The board is very hostile,” Trump told reporters. Trump’s apparent concession that he is unable to bend the Fed to his will comes as his tariffs and the Middle East conflict have conspired to inflame an affordability crisis that he has dismissed as a “hoax”. “There’s a lot of very clear evidence that the policies of the White House--tariffs and the conflict in the Middle East--have pushed up inflation,” said Claudia Sahm, a former Fed official who is now chief economist at New Century Advisors. Since Warsh’s nomination in January, the big inflation driver has been a rise in petrol and diesel prices--an increase that has cost American households an extra $108bn since the outbreak of the president’s war with Iran, according to a Brown University tracker. “Trump’s tariffs and the war in Iran are driving prices up,” said Jeff Colgan, professor at Brown University. “To a large extent, the inflation that the Fed is fighting is a problem of President Trump’s own making.” Kush Desai, Trump’s spokesperson on economic matters, described the Fed’s move as “unfortunate”, claiming it would “punish businesses who are trying to expand and grow” ”To the extent that we still do have inflation, as the president and others have noted, it’s entirely driven by an energy supply shock, by what’s going on with oil prices in the Middle East,” Desai told Fox News. While another rate rise at the next Fed vote--set for late October, days before the midterms--is seen by some as unlikely, markets are pricing in at least one more increase before the end of the year. Short-term US Treasury yields, which reflect monetary policy expectation, rose sharply in response to Wednesday’s decision as traders interpreted Warsh’s remarks as hawkish. But many said Wednesday’s increase had helped allay those concerns. “Fed credibility is already on the line because we’ve had more than five years of high inflation--the longest bout of inflation since the late1970s, early 1980s, and it has compounded,” said Diane Swonk, chief economist at KPMG US. “Nobody necessarily wants to be at the helm at a pivotal moment, but Kevin Warsh is there. And he did his job. “ When quizzed about the president’s likely response to the decision earlier in the day, Warsh offered only a wry smile. “What the president does and doesn’t like is his prerogative,” said Joe LaVorgna, a former economic counsellor to US Treasury secretary Scott Bessent who is now chief economist for the Americas at SMBC Nikko Securities. “But his views aren’t going to persuade the committee if they’re inconsistent with the fundamentals.”\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国三年来首次加息！上任不到4个月，川川亲手选的美联储主席就“翻脸”了？ 唐纳德·特朗普未能令美联储俯首听命 凯文·沃什顶住了唐纳德·特朗普要求压低借贷成本的压力——这位总统钦点的美联储掌舵人，上任不到四个月，便推动央行完成了自2023年以来的首次加息。周三，这位美联储主席拿出了迄今为止最有力的证据，表明他准备不理会特朗普：他成功说服联邦公开市场委员会全部12名利率决策者，一致支持加息25个基点。“明摆着的事实是，通胀太高了，而且高得太久了，”沃什说道，并反复向记者表示，他很难将当前的利率水平形容为高到会拖慢经济。 沃什向华尔街释放的信号——恰在关键的中期选举前数周——是他准备采取进一步措施，驯服席卷美国经济的通胀。这与总统的认知形成鲜明反差：特朗普以为自己挑选的是一位“当然想降息”的美联储主席。 特朗普周三迅速对美联储开火，在其“真相社交”平台上发文称：“美国的利率应该是1%，或者更低，因为我们是全世界最好的信用——遥遥领先。”特朗普随后表示，他对沃什仍有信心，两人私交甚笃；同时他把矛头转向美联储理事会，其中包括前主席杰伊·鲍威尔——总统此前曾因他不降息而骂他是“白痴”。理事会中还有理事莉萨·库克，特朗普曾试图将她解职，但被最高法院拦下。“我跟凯文说了，我说，你干脆跟理事会一起投票算了，反正也没用。理事会敌意很重，”特朗普对记者说。 特朗普显然承认自己无法令美联储屈从于他的意志。与此同时，他的关税政策和中东冲突共同加剧了一场“负担能力危机”，而他却将其斥为“骗局”。“有大量非常明确的证据表明，白宫的政策——关税和中东冲突——推高了通胀，”前美联储官员、现新世纪顾问公司首席经济学家克劳迪娅·萨姆表示。 自沃什1月获得提名以来，通胀的主要推手是汽油和柴油价格上涨。据布朗大学的一项追踪数据，自总统对伊朗开战以来，这一涨幅已让美国家庭多支出1080亿美元。“特朗普的关税和伊朗战争正在推高物价，”布朗大学教授杰夫·科尔根说。“美联储正在对抗的通胀，在很大程度上是特朗普总统自己造成的问题。” 特朗普的经济事务发言人库什·德赛将美联储此举形容为“不幸”，声称这将“惩罚那些想要扩张和发展的企业”。德赛对福克斯新闻表示：“正如总统和其他人所指出的，如果我们确实仍有通胀，那完全是由能源供应冲击、由中东油价局势所驱动的。” 虽然一些人认为美联储下次投票——定于10月下旬，中期选举前几天——再次加息的可能性不大，但市场已定价至少还有一次加息会在年底前到来。反映货币政策预期的美国短期国债收益率周三应声大幅上升，因交易员将沃什的言论解读为鹰派。 但许多人表示，周三的加息有助于缓解这些担忧。“美联储的信誉已经岌岌可危，因为我们已经经历了五年多的高通胀——这是自1970年代末、1980年代初以来最长的一轮通胀，而且已经积重难返，”毕马威美国首席经济学家黛安·斯旺克说。“未必有人愿意在关键时刻掌舵，但凯文·沃什就在那个位置上。而他尽职了。” 当天早些时候，当被问及总统可能如何回应这一决定时，沃什只是苦笑了一下。“总统喜欢什么、不喜欢什么，是他的特权，”前美国财政部长斯科特·贝森特的经济顾问、现SMBC日兴证券美洲首席经济学家乔·拉沃尼亚说。“但如果他的观点与基本面不符，那就说服不了委员会。”","2026-09-19_001":"China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes — 2026/09/19 China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes 2026/09/19 China launches new canal, first of its kind since 1949, giving inland access to sea lanes Newly opened Pinglu Canal will allow cargo from inland provinces to reach the Gulf of Tonkin, avoiding a 560km detour, deepening trade ties China officially opened its landmark Pingu Canal to navigation on Wednesday, creating a new maritime gateway for the country's southwestern hinterland and providing a shorter route to Southeast Asia as Beijing seeks to deepen trade with the region. The 134.2km (83.4 mile) waterway links Nanning, the capital of the southern region of Guangxi, to the Gulf of Tonkin, known as the Beibu Gulf in China, allowing cargo from inland provinces to reach the sea without taking a roughly 560km detour via Guangzhou, cutting both transit times and logistics costs. With a planned investment of 72.7 billion yuan (US$10.8 billion), the project, which began construction in August 2022, is designed to accommodate vessels of up to 5,000 tonnes and save more than 5 billion yuan in overall transportation costs each year. The canal is the first man-made waterway connecting a river to the sea to be built since the founding of the People's Republic of China in 1949, state-run Xinhua News Agency reported. Some observers have described it as the country's most significant canal undertaking since the Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal, and it forms a key link in the New International Land-Sea Trade Corridor. It is equipped with a tiered navigation zone system to facilitate the movement of inland vessels, allowing qualified vessels to travel through the canal and into the sea without transferring cargo to another ship. The canal's opening comes after a series of navigation tests. In mid-August, four merchant vessels ranging from 1,000 to 5,000 tonnes conducted phased trials along the canal's main channel, testing the safety and reliability of the waterway, locks, anchorages and service areas, state media reported. Earlier that month, a maritime patrol vessel completed the first full-route transit test along the waterway, according to the canal's operator. Late in August, the canal's first command and management vessel was delivered to oversee navigation and handle emergency responses along the waterway. Beyond the engineering work, the project has also involved efforts to limit its environmental impact. At the Qingnian navigation hub, engineers built a dedicated fish passage of more than 480 metres, containing 120 pools, to help aquatic species navigate a water-level difference of nearly 10 metres, with a separate route designed for eels and other climbing species. Builders also relocated thousands of mature mangrove trees along the route and have since planted hundreds of thousands of new ones to offset the disruption to local ecosystems. The canal aims to give China's less-prosperous inland regions quicker and easier access to global sea lanes, boosting local industry and strengthening trade with the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (Asean), China's largest trading partner as a bloc. That ambition is already taking shape. Last Thursday, a multimodal freight train carrying more than 370 tonnes of construction machinery and other products arrived in Guangxi, after departing from Hunan province, with the cargo scheduled for export following the canal's opening. The project is also part of a broader push to expand China's inland waterways, with several other provinces, including Jiangxi, Hubei, Hunan and Henan, planning or advancing ambitious multibillion-dollar waterway expansions to boost local economic growth. By using the canal, provinces including Yunnan, Guizhou, Hunan and Sichuan, as well as the metropolis of Chongqing, could see their logistics costs reduced by around 18 per cent to 30 per cent, making it \"the shortest, most economical and most convenient route to the sea in the western region\", according to an August 13 report by Yunnan authorities. The canal's construction has also spurred a broader build-out of supporting infrastructure and transport links. At Nanning Port's Liujing operations area, berths have been upgraded to handle vessels of up to 5,000 tonnes. The Beibu Gulf Port is also seeking to attract cargo from Chongqing, Sichuan and Yunnan, while cross-border railways such as the China-Laos Railway are expected to feed into the network, according to state media reports. The opening comes as China's trade with Asean continues to grow. Beijing has increasingly sought to strengthen supply chains and economic ties with the region as tensions with the West persist over trade and technology. Total trade between China and Asean reached US$862.75 billion in the first eight months of this year, up 25.6 per cent from a year earlier, customs data showed. China's exports to Asean stood at US$546 billion, up 25.8 per cent. In contrast, exports to the US rose 6.2 per cent in dollar terms over the same period. The opening came just ahead of this year's China-Asean Expo in Nanning, an annual trade and investment fair showcasing products ranging from agricultural goods and food to machinery and hi-tech equipment.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n平陆运河终于通了：中国等了 100 年，为什么这条运河“非挖不可”？ 中国启用新运河，为 1949 年以来首条，内陆自此直通海上航道——新通航的平陆运河将使内陆省份货物直抵北部湾，免去 560 公里绕行，进一步密切贸易往来 中国于周三正式宣告平陆运河通航。这项标志性工程为中国西南腹地开辟出一道全新的出海门户，也为通往东南亚铺就了一条更短的路径——而此时，中国正着力深化与该地区的经贸往来。这条全长134.2公里（83.4英里）的水道，将广西首府南宁与北部湾（国际上称东京湾）连为一体。自此，内陆省份的货物不必再取道广州、平白绕行约560公里，便可直抵大海，运输时间与物流成本由此双双下降。 该工程计划投资727亿元人民币（约合108亿美元），自2022年8月动工，按设计可通行5000吨级船舶，每年可节省各类运输成本逾50亿元。 据官方媒体新华社报道，这是1949年新中国成立以来，中国建成的第一条连江通海的人工水道。一些观察人士称之为继京杭大运河之后中国最重大的运河工程，它也构成了西部陆海新通道中的关键一环。运河配备梯级通航系统，便利内河船舶通行——达标船舶可一路穿越运河、直驶入海，全程无需换船转货。 运河的通航，是在一连串试航测试之后才最终实现的。据官方媒体报道，八月中旬，四艘1000吨至5000吨不等的商船沿主航道分阶段试航，检验了航道、船闸、锚地与服务区的安全性与可靠性。运河运营方表示，当月早些时候，一艘海事巡逻船已完成首次全线通航测试。八月下旬，运河首艘指挥管理船交付，负责统筹沿线通航调度与应急处置。 除工程建设本身之外，这一项目还投入了大量心力，以尽可能减小它对环境的扰动。在青年枢纽，工程师专门修筑了一条480余米、含120级水池的鱼道，帮助水生生物翻越近10米的水位落差；另辟一条通道，专为鳗鱼等善于攀爬的物种而设。施工方还沿线迁移了数千棵成年红树，此后补种新树数十万棵，以弥补工程对当地生态系统造成的扰动。 这条运河的用意，是让中国那些不那么富庶的内陆地区，能更快、更便捷地接上全球海运航道，既拉动本地产业，也加深与东盟的贸易往来——作为一个整体，东盟是中国最大的贸易伙伴。而这份雄心，早已不只是纸面文章：上周四，一列多式联运班列自湖南启程抵达广西，载着370多吨工程机械与其他货物，只待运河开航，便可出口。 这项工程同时也是中国拓展内河航道这一更大布局中的一环。江西、湖北、湖南、河南等多个省份，都在规划或推进耗资数十亿美元的航道扩建，以此拉动地方经济增长。据云南方面8月13日的一份报告，借助这条运河，云南、贵州、湖南、四川等省以及重庆市的物流成本有望下降约18%至30%，使其成为“西部地区最短、最经济、最便捷的出海通道”。 运河的建设，还催生了更大范围的配套基建与交通联结。南宁港六景作业区的泊位已完成升级，可停靠5000吨级船舶；北部湾港也在争取重庆、四川、云南的货源。据官方媒体报道，与此同时，中老铁路等跨境铁路预计也将汇入这张运输网。 运河通航之时，中国与东盟的贸易仍在持续攀升。在与西方在贸易和技术领域的紧张关系延续的背景下，中国不断加力巩固与这一地区的供应链和经济纽带。海关数据显示，今年前八个月，中国与东盟贸易总额达8627.5亿美元，同比增长25.6%；其中对东盟出口5460亿美元，增长25.8%。相比之下，同期以美元计的对美出口增长6.2%。 运河通航，恰好赶在今年南宁中国—东盟博览会开幕之前——这场一年一度的经贸投资盛会，展品从农产品、食品，一路排到机械与高科技装备。","2026-09-18_001":"Is this the end for China's love of the English language? — 2026/09/18 Is this the end for China's love of the English language? 2026/09/18 Is this the end for China's love of the English language? Half a century ago, learning English went almost overnight from being discouraged to a national obsession. Throughout the 1980s and beyond, young and old alike would gather in parks, at what became known as \"English corners\", to practise with each other. The language became a core part of the national curriculum, on a par with maths and Chinese, right up to the gaokao, or university entrance exam. Now a prominent teacher and education blogger has reignited a national debate by questioning whether learning English is necessary at all. \"Our children desperately begin learning English even before they have properly mastered their native language,\" Tang Jiafeng wrote. \"Yet we are a civilisation that dates back thousands of years. English is just a tool. It's enough to be able to use it. \" The debate touches on questions of whether children are over-pressured by schools and parents and the suitability of traditional rote-learning techniques. It has also acquired a new dimension as artificial-intelligence translation tools improve. With technology increasingly capable of translating languages almost instantly, some young people are asking whether years of intensive English study will remain as valuable as it once was. At the same time, uncertainty about graduate employment has prompted a wider discussion about which skills students will actually need when they enter the workforce. Tang, who has a PhD in mathematics and offers tuition for postgraduate entrance exams, has established himself as a popular voice in the competitive world of online advice for students. His most recent post was a reaction to announcements by provincial education authorities that they were downgrading noncore subjects like biology, geography and history for high-school entrance exams to reduce the pressure on teenagers. Liaoning province, for example, recently removed history, biology and geography from its middle-school entry test as part of efforts to reduce students' workload. Tang claimed that this was effectively prioritising a subject, English, that would be \"completely irrelevant to the majority of people in their future professional lives\". English remains a compulsory subject in China's basic education system alongside Chinese and mathematics. Under the traditional 750-point gaokao structure, each of those three subjects is worth 150 points, even as the weighting and assessment of other subjects have undergone reforms. The most prominent immediate response came from Hu Xijin, a former editor of the English-language tabloid Global Times. He accused Tang of being \"biased and narrow-minded\" and urged education departments to ignore him. One argument favoured by critics of English is that when native English speakers are so poor at learning Chinese — a fairly indisputable claim — why should Chinese people learn their language? That ignored the fact that, like it or not, English was the world's lingua franca, Hu said. \"More than 90 per cent of international scientific academic papers are produced in English,\" he wrote. \"If a person wants to advance to a higher level, being familiar with and mastering English is one of the most important educational preparations. \" The debate has also raised questions about access to international knowledge and opportunities, particularly for students who may later work or study across borders. Another argument against English is that it is a sign of elitism; in practice, only the upper middle classes can afford to send their children to universities in the West, or travel there. Hu argued that downgrading English would make elitism worse, since the upper classes would find new ways to pay for their children to learn English anyway. Critics of weakening English in public education have similarly argued that wealthier families could simply buy private tuition, while students from less privileged backgrounds would have fewer opportunities to acquire the same skills. Another intervention, admittedly from a professor with a vested interest, was more blunt. \"Let me ask: is maths useful in everyday life? \" wrote Cao Wen, who teaches at Beijing Foreign Studies University, a breeding ground for diplomats. \"Ordinary people go to work, buy groceries, watch movies. Do we really need to use functions or calculate square roots every day? Yet we still expect children to learn maths. If you judge whether a subject is necessary simply by whether people will use it in their daily lives, can you really call yourself an educator? \"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n中国人，到底要不要学英语？中国现在那么牛，为啥还要学英语？ 中国人对英语的热爱，就此终结了吗？ 半个世纪前，学英语几乎在一夜之间，从一件受冷落的事变成了一种举国上下的痴迷。整个二十世纪八十年代乃至其后，男女老少齐聚公园，在后来被称为“英语角”的地方彼此练习。英语成为国家课程的核心部分，与数学、语文并驾齐驱，一路贯穿至高考。 如今，一位知名教师兼教育博主再度点燃了一场全国性辩论——他质疑学英语到底有没有必要。“我们的孩子连母语都还没真正掌握，就拼命开始学英语，”汤家凤写道，“可我们是拥有数千年历史的文明。英语不过是个工具，会用就够了。” 这场辩论触及了诸多问题：孩子是否被学校和家长施压过重，传统死记硬背式教学法是否合宜。随着人工智能翻译工具日益精进，辩论又多了一个新维度。当技术越来越能近乎即时地翻译语言，一些年轻人开始发问：多年苦学英语，是否还会像从前那样值钱？与此同时，毕业生就业前景的不确定性，也引发了更广泛的讨论——学生步入职场后，究竟需要哪些技能？ 汤家凤拥有数学博士学位，从事考研辅导，在竞争激烈的线上升学咨询领域已确立起颇受欢迎的声音。他最近这篇发文，是对多省教育部门宣布下调生物、地理、历史等非核心科目在中考中权重的回应——此举意在减轻青少年压力。例如，辽宁省最近将历史、生物、地理移出中考，作为减负努力的一部分。 汤家凤称，这实际上是在优先照顾一门对“大多数人未来职业生涯完全无关”的科目——英语。在中国基础教育体系中，英语仍与语文、数学并列为必修科目。在传统的高考750分结构中，这三门各占150分，即便其他科目的权重与考核方式已历经改革。 最引人注目的即时回应来自胡锡进，英文小报《环球时报》前总编辑。他指责汤家凤“偏执而狭隘”，并呼吁教育部门不要理会他。英语批评者偏爱的一个论点是：既然以英语为母语的人学中文如此糟糕——这一说法几乎无可辩驳——那中国人凭什么要学他们的语言？ 胡锡进说，这种论调忽视了一个事实：不管你喜欢与否，英语都是世界通用语。“超过90%的国际科学学术论文是用英语写成的，”他写道，“一个人要想更上一层楼，熟悉并掌握英语是最重要的教育准备之一。”这场辩论还引发了关于获取国际知识与机会的追问，尤其是对那些日后可能跨境工作或求学的学生而言。 另一种反对英语的论点是：它是精英主义的标志；现实中，只有中上阶层才负担得起送孩子去西方大学，或去西方游历。胡锡进反驳说，弱化英语只会让精英主义更严重，因为上层阶级无论如何都会找到新办法花钱让孩子学英语。批评在公共教育中削弱英语的人同样主张，富裕家庭大可以直接购买私人辅导，而出身较弱势的学生获得同等技能的机会将更少。 还有一次介入——诚然来自一位有既得利益的教授——则更为直白。“我问一句：数学在日常生活中用得上吗？”北京外国语大学教授曹文写道，那里是外交官的摇篮。“普通人上班、买菜、看电影。我们真的需要每天用函数、算平方根吗？可我们仍然指望孩子学数学。如果你仅仅以人们日常生活中会不会用来判断一门科目是否必要，你还真称得上教育者吗？”","2026-09-17_001":"Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids — 2026/09/17 Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids 2026/09/17 Boomers Are Sacrificing Retirement Savings to Support Their Grandkids The Bank of Mom and Dad is staying open for the grandkids as the cost of raising a child in the US tops $300,000 for the first time this year — and that doesn't include college tuition. Nearly two in five parents of young children expect to receive financial help — or ask for it — from their own parents or grandparents in the next year. Among them,47% said they will receive cash for day-to-day basics from relatives, according to the BMO Real Financial Progress Index survey. A quarter said they expect relatives to contribute to their children's 529 or other savings accounts. \"Families trying to figure out how they're going to solve for these increased costs are really trying to lean on their extended families,\" said Robin Growley, BMO's US head of consumer products. Parents with family support said they saved $1,915 a year on childcare and babysitting, and $1,443 on groceries. Gisele Ayora, a 36-year-old marketing professional and mother of three, said the biggest financial boosts she got from her in-laws were housing and childcare. They bought her family a $600,000, six-bedroom home in Manahawkin, New Jersey. They asked the couple to pay about $1,700 a month — roughly what they had been paying to rent a two-bedroom apartment — about half of what a mortgage would've cost them. Her mother-in-law also stepped in to watch the kids when Ayora had to work late or travel for her job. For some young families, the help can feel like getting part of an inheritance early, when they need it most, said Lindsey Stanberry, founder of the personal finance website The Purse. Baby Boomers are sitting on a lot of wealth, while their adult children are facing steep housing and childcare costs. But for grandparents without as much to spare, that support can eat into their own retirement plans. Haron Marlee estimates she spent $24,000 in one year covering everything from diapers to gas to car insurance for her 36-year-old daughter, Melissa, and grandchildren. She went from a semi-retired therapist to a regular babysitter and even picked up bartending shifts to help support them. \"Those burdens fundamentally changed my finances, my retirement plans,\" said Marlee, a 59-year-old who now lives in Ocean County, New Jersey. Her daughter, a law school graduate, said she was initially embarrassed by the situation. But when her marriage ended, she struggled to find a job and it was the best option to stay afloat. Financial advisers warn parents against sacrificing their own retirement savings to keep adult children afloat. Lorie Jones, owner of Fearless Financial Advisors in Colorado, said 20% to 25% of her clients are covering a significant share of their adult children's expenses well into their late 20s and early 30s. \"I do have to talk to some of those parents and say, 'You need to take care of you first',\" she said. \"'You've got to prioritize your own retirement'.\" Barb Green knows that strain firsthand. Her 29-year-old son moved back home in 2017 after a semester of college and has stayed there rent free since. Green said the job market and broader economy have made it difficult for him to move out. He doesn't yet have kids, but supporting him has put a dent in Green and her husband's retirement plans. \"The money that we've been planning on putting away for retirement to travel and do fun things during retirement — since we've spent all of our life working — I feel like we are not able to plan that or do that because it's like we're still raising him because he's still living there.\" She's also seen the dynamic through her work. Green is a coach at Community Connections for Children in Pennsylvania, an organization that helps families find and apply for childcare. She said that she's seen a roughly 60% increase in grandparents caring for their grandchildren in the past five years. For Marlee, the semi-retired therapist, the strain was more than financial. \"It took a toll on me that I had not anticipated,\" she said. She's since started a Facebook group and written an advice book for parents of adult children called Stop Raising Adult Children: Break the Cycle of Blame and Take Your Life Back. Marlee tells parents financially supporting their adult children to set ground rules and boundaries — and, importantly, an end date for their contribution. She also tells them to protect their retirement. Eventually, she and her daughter made a more formal arrangement. Melissa would help out through \"sweat equity\" by cooking meals and cleaning. Once she secured work, she could live with her for free for one more year. Since then, she's had to start contributing to household expenses and rent. \"You cannot jump into the riptide with your children,\" Marlee said. \"You can only lend them a hand.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n美国有40%的年轻人在啃老！算完一笔账崩溃了：养大一个孩子超30万美元，可能还要养一辈子 婴儿潮一代，正掏空养老钱补贴孙辈。 “爸妈银行”的窗口，为了孙辈迟迟没有关闭——今年，美国养大一个孩子的成本首次突破30万美元，这还没算上大学学费。将近五分之二的幼儿家长预计，未来一年里他们会接受、或者不得不开口向自己的父母、祖父母求助。BMO“真实财务进展指数”调查显示，这些人当中，47%会从亲戚手里拿到现金，用于柴米油盐的日常开销；还有四分之一的人指望着亲戚往孩子的529教育账户或其他储蓄账户里添一笔。 “那些为上涨的开销翻来覆去算账的家庭，是真的在把身子往大家庭那边倚了，”BMO美国区消费产品负责人罗宾·格劳利说。有家里帮衬的父母算过一笔账：一年下来，托儿和临时看娃省下1,915美元，买菜省下1,443美元。 吉赛尔·阿约拉，36岁，做市场营销，三个孩子的母亲。她说，公婆给她最大的两笔托举，是房子和带娃。他们在新泽西州马纳霍金给她家买下一套60万美元的六居室，只让小两口每月交1,700美元左右——这差不多就是他们从前租一套两居室的开销，只相当于自己贷款买房月供的一半。每逢阿约拉加班或出差，婆婆还会顶上来，把孩子接过去带。 对一些年轻家庭来说，这笔钱更像是在最缺钱的年纪，提前分到了一部分遗产——个人理财网站The Purse 的创始人林赛·斯坦伯里这样说。婴儿潮一代坐拥着大笔财富，他们的成年子女却在硬扛陡得吓人的房价和育儿账单。可是，对那些本就匀不出多少余钱的祖父母而言，这份资助，是在一口一口啃掉他们自己的晚年。 哈伦·马利估算，仅一年之间，她就为36岁的女儿梅丽莎和几个外孙掏了2.4万美元——从尿不湿到汽油，再到车险，样样是她付。她从一个半退休的心理治疗师，变成了常驻的保姆，甚至还去酒吧接起了调酒的班次，只为贴补他们。“那些担子，从根上改掉了我的财务，也改掉了我的退休计划，”马利说。她今年59岁，现住在新泽西州海洋县。她的女儿是法学院毕业生，说自己起初实在觉得难堪。可婚姻散了以后，工作又迟迟找不到，这已经是她能抓住的、最不坏的一根浮木。 理财顾问们劝父母，别拿自己的养老钱去托着成年子女不沉下去。科罗拉多州“无畏理财顾问”公司的老板洛丽·琼斯说，她的客户里有两成到两成半，正替已经二十七八、三十出头的孩子承担着一大块开销。“有些父母，我是真的不得不跟他们谈，”她说，“我跟他们讲：‘你得先照顾好你自己。’‘你必须把自己的退休排在第一位。’” 这种绷到极限的日子，芭布·格林是亲身体会过的。她29岁的儿子2017年只读了一个学期大学就搬回了家，从那以后一直白住到今天。格林说，就业市场和整个经济大环境，让他很难搬出去。他还没有孩子，可光是养着他，就已经在格林夫妇的退休计划上砸出了一个坑。“我们一直打算攒下来的那笔养老钱，是准备退休后去旅游、去做点有意思的事的——毕竟我们辛辛苦苦干了一辈子啊——可我现在觉得，这些我们既没法计划，也没法去做了，因为这感觉就像我们还在养他，他还住在家里呢。” 这样的格局，格林在工作里也看得真切。她在宾夕法尼亚州一家名叫“儿童社区联结”的机构当辅导员，这家机构专门帮各个家庭寻找、申请托儿服务。她说，过去五年里，前来求助的、正在带孙辈的祖父母，多了大约六成。 对马利——那位半退休的心理治疗师而言，这份重压远不止是钱的事。“它对我的消耗，是我事先完全没有料到的，”她说。此后，她建起了一个Facebook群组，还写了一本给成年子女的父母看的书，书名叫《别再养成年子女：打破指责的循环，把你的人生拿回来》。她劝那些在经济上供养成年子女的父母：把基本规矩和边界立起来——还有很关键的一条，给这份资助定一个截止日期。她也劝他们，守住自己的晚年。 到最后，母女俩谈成了一份更像样的约定。梅丽莎以“汗水股权”的方式出力——做饭、打扫。等她把工作拿下了，还可以再白住一年。此后，她便得开始分担家用与房租了。“你不能跟着孩子一起跳进那股离岸流，”马利说，“你只能伸出一只手，拉他们一把。”","2026-09-16_001":"HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself — 2026/09/16 HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself 2026/09/16 HYROX Beijing faces backlash after Aussie athlete continues race despite soiling herself The organisers of a viral sporting event in Beijing have faced backlash and criticism after an Australian woman was allowed to continue competing after soiling herself. Athlete Joanna Wietrzyk was participating in the global indoor fitness race HYROX in the Chinese capital at the weekend when she suffered a bout of faecal incontinence. Wietrzyk was allowed to finish the course and won her event, with videos and photos posted online showing her continuing through demanding exercises and using communal equipment while visibly soiled. Wietrzyk, who is a HYROX world record holder, was invited to compete at the Beijing event. In just four months, Joanna Wietrzyk has gone from discovering HYROX to dominating it. \"I didn't even know what HYROX was four months ago,\" Wietrzyk admits. But with a background in competitive tennis and distance running, she's adapted fast. The incident has gone viral on Chinese social media and sparked debate about HYROX's health and safety standards, as well as athlete responsibility. \"She's so selfish — she kept running the whole way even though she had diarrhoea,\" one user on Weibo questioned. \"What about the runners behind her?\" Another wrote: \"The host is ultimately unprofessional! \" On Weibo, the hashtag #Hyrox incontinence female competitor has not been punished# has more than 27 million views. An article on the Beijing incident was the most-viewed story on Chinese state media website the Global Times on Monday. After the race, she doubled down and even shared a selfie on Instagram, which has since been deleted, appearing to show her in a hospital bed. \"Go hard or go home...a win is a win.\" Footage and images of the incident subsequently spread across social media and fitness forums, with reactions ranging from praise for Wietrzyk's determination to concerns around hygiene. HYROX is a global fitness challenge in which participants race to complete often gruelling stations, including sandbag lunges, burpees, sled pushes and rowing. In a statement, HYROX China said the area was decontaminated after the race had finished. \"After the incident, the organising committee immediately closed off and isolated the affected area and track, and completed comprehensive disinfection,\" organisers said. \"Relevant equipment was removed from the venue, and waste was disposed of in accordance with municipal waste-management standards. \" The organisers also said they would replace flooring at the venue to protect future athlete health. HYROX World also issued a statement addressing the incident. \"HYROX has clear bio-contaminant and medical procedures in place,\" the statement said. \"In this instance, an unforeseen situation arose in which protocols designed for elite competition and mass participation became blurred, creating ambiguity in how they were applied and understood. \" But social media users and other athletes at the event have attacked the response and questioned why Wietrzyk was allowed to finish the race, potentially risking the health and safety of other competitors. \"For people who were exposed to that situation, there could be genuine physical and psychological consequences,\" one competitor named johopalooza posted on Instagram. \"I paid HYROX to provide a professionally organised and safe event. So while I understand the athlete perspective, ultimately my contract and expectation is with HYROX. Protecting athletes is important, but showing the wider community that HYROX takes responsibility when its own procedures fail is just as important. \" HYROX has also been accused of double standards over its hygiene rules, which punish athletes with time penalties if they litter on the course, spit or clear their nose onto the floor. Li Hongxin, an associate professor of sports management at China's Zhengzhou University of Light Industry, said the athlete should not be criticised or judged for experiencing a physiological problem. \"I think her determination and sportsmanship are very understandable,\" he said. But he said the incident raised questions about the responsibility of event organisers. \"Organisers should assess whether such a situation could affect the health or wellbeing of other competitors,\" he said. Dr Li said this was particularly important because HYROX was an indoor event. \"Take a marathon as an example. If a competitor is menstruating, organisers would generally respect their decision to continue because a marathon is an outdoor event,\" he said. \"They don't share equipment with other competitors, so it generally doesn't pose a public safety concern. But HYROX is an indoor event, where competitors share equipment. In that situation, I think the organisers should have staff step in and adjust the event, or take appropriate emergency measures.\"\n⟪中文译文⟫\n失禁中完赛，中产网红运动的体面碎了一地！HYROX北京站陷入巨大争议 HYROX北京站因澳大利亚选手失禁后仍继续参赛而遭抨击 北京一场迅速走红的体育赛事，其主办方近日遭到抨击与批评。起因是一名澳大利亚女子在失禁后仍被允许继续参赛。上周末，运动员乔安娜·维特日克在中国首都参加全球室内健身赛HYROX时，遭遇大便失禁。维特日克获准跑完全程，并赢得所在组别冠军。网上流传的视频和照片显示，她在明显弄脏身体的情况下，仍继续完成高强度项目，并使用公共器材。 维特日克是HYROX世界纪录保持者，此次受邀参加北京站比赛。短短四个月内，乔安娜·维特日克便从初识HYROX，到称霸赛场。“四个月前，我甚至不知道HYROX是什么，”维特日克坦言。但凭借竞技网球和长跑的底子，她适应得很快。 此事在中国社交媒体上迅速发酵，引发围绕HYROX健康安全标准以及运动员责任的争论。“她太自私了——明明拉肚子还一路跑，”一位微博用户质疑道，“后面的人怎么办？”另一人写道：“主办方终究太不专业了！”微博话题#Hyrox失禁女选手未被处罚#阅读量已超过2700万。《环球时报》周一刊登的一篇关于北京此事的文章，成为中国官方媒体网站上浏览量最高的报道。 赛后，她态度更加强硬，甚至还在Instagram上分享了一张自拍，照片似乎显示她躺在病床上，但该帖后来已被删除。“要么拼尽全力，要么干脆回家……赢了就是赢了。”事件相关视频和图片随后在社交媒体和健身论坛上广泛传播，反应不一：有人称赞维特日克的意志力，也有人担忧卫生问题。 HYROX是一项全球健身挑战赛，参赛者需竞速完成一系列往往极为艰苦的项目，包括沙袋弓步、波比跳、雪橇推和划船。HYROX中国在一份声明中表示，比赛结束后已对相关区域进行净化处理。“事件发生后，组委会第一时间对受影响区域和赛道进行封闭隔离，并完成全面消杀，”主办方表示，“相关器材已撤离场馆，废弃物也按照城市垃圾管理标准处置。”主办方还表示，将更换场馆地板，以保障未来参赛者的健康。 HYROX全球总部也发表声明回应此事。“HYROX有明确的生物污染和医疗处置流程，”声明称，“但在本次事件中，出现了突发情况，使原本为精英竞赛和大规模群众参与设计的规程界限变得模糊，导致其在适用和理解上产生歧义。”然而，社交媒体用户和现场其他运动员对这一回应提出批评，并质疑为何允许维特日克完赛，从而可能危及其他参赛者的健康与安全。 “对于暴露在那样的环境中的人来说，可能真的会带来身体和心理上的后果，”一位名为johopalooza的参赛者在Instagram上发文说，“我付钱给HYROX，是为了获得一个组织专业、安全有保障的赛事。所以，虽然我理解运动员的立场，但归根结底，我的契约对象和期待对象是HYROX。保护运动员很重要，但向更广泛的群体表明，当HYROX自身流程失灵时它会承担责任，同样重要。”HYROX还被指在卫生规则上搞双重标准：若运动员在赛道上乱扔垃圾、吐痰或擤鼻涕到地上，会被处以时间处罚。 中国郑州轻工业大学体育管理副教授李红心表示，不应因运动员出现生理问题而对其进行批评或评判。“我认为她的意志力和体育精神非常可以理解，”他说。但他也表示，此事引发了人们对赛事主办方责任的质疑。“主办方应当评估，这种情况是否可能影响其他参赛者的健康或福祉，”他说。李博士指出，这一点尤为重要，因为HYROX是室内赛事。 “以马拉松为例。如果参赛者正值生理期，主办方通常会尊重其继续参赛的决定，因为马拉松是户外赛事，”他说，“他们不与其他参赛者共用器材，所以一般不会构成公共安全担忧。但HYROX是室内赛事，参赛者共用器材。在这种情况下，我认为主办方本应让工作人员介入并调整赛事，或采取适当的应急措施。”","2026-09-16_002":"The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way — 2026/09/16 The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way 2026/09/16 The Most Effective Way to Learn Is to Push Through It the Hard Way There is a comforting myth about learning—that if you just find the right method, the right teacher, or the right shortcut, everything will suddenly become easy. People spend years searching for the perfect system, hoping it will eliminate struggle altogether. But the truth is far less appealing: real learning is not designed to feel easy. At its core, learning is a process of friction. It requires you to sit with confusion, to face what you don’t understand, and to stay in that discomfort long enough for clarity to emerge. This is what people often call hard learning—not because it is inefficient, but because it demands effort without immediate reward. When people avoid this stage, they turn to passive forms of learning. They watch videos, read summaries, highlight sentences, and feel productive. But passive exposure creates familiarity, not mastery. It feels like progress, but it rarely leads to real understanding. Hard learning looks very different. It is slower, quieter, and often frustrating. It involves rereading the same paragraph multiple times, attempting problems without knowing the answer, and failing repeatedly before getting it right. There is no instant gratification—only gradual improvement. One of the most important elements of hard learning is repetition. Not mechanical repetition, but deliberate repetition—returning to the same material with deeper attention each time. With every pass, your understanding shifts. What was once confusing becomes familiar; what was once familiar becomes clear; what was once clear becomes flexible. Another key aspect is active engagement. Hard learning requires you to think, not just consume. It asks you to explain ideas in your own words, test your understanding, and apply knowledge in new contexts. This is where knowledge transforms into ability. The difficulty of hard learning often discourages people. It feels inefficient compared to faster, easier alternatives. But what seems efficient in the short term is often ineffective in the long term. Learning that avoids struggle rarely survives beyond the moment. There is also a psychological barrier. Many people equate difficulty with failure. When something feels hard, they assume they are not good at it. In reality, difficulty is not a sign of inadequacy—it is a sign that learning is happening. If you look at people who achieve deep expertise in any field, their process is rarely glamorous. It is built on long hours of focused effort, repeated attempts, and a willingness to stay with complexity. They don’t avoid hard learning—they rely on it. So when people say, The best way to learn is to push through, it is not a rejection of good methods or smart strategies. It is a recognition that no method can replace effort. Techniques can guide you, but they cannot do the work for you. In the end, learning is not about avoiding difficulty—it is about building the capacity to move through it. And the sooner you accept that, the sooner your learning becomes real.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n关于学习，有个让人安心的迷思——只要找到对的方法、对的老师，或者对的捷径，一切就会突然变简单。 人们花上数年寻找完美的体系，指望它能彻底消除挣扎。 但真相可没那么动人：真正的学习，本来就不被设计成\"轻松\"的样子。 究其本质，学习是一个充满摩擦的过程。它要求你与困惑共处，直面自己不懂的东西，并在那种不适里待得足够久，直到清晰浮现。 这就是人们常说的\"硬学习\"——不是说它效率低，而是它要求你付出努力，却没有即时的回报。 当人们回避这个阶段，就会转向被动式的学习。 他们看视频、读摘要、划重点句子，然后觉得自己很高效。 但被动接触带来的是\"熟悉感\"，而不是\"掌握\"。它感觉像进步，却很少通向真正的理解。 硬学习看起来截然不同。 它更慢、更安静，而且常常令人沮丧。 它包括把同一段文字反复读上好几遍、在不知道答案的情况下硬着头皮做题，以及在对之前反复失败。 这里没有即时满足——只有渐进式的提高。 硬学习最重要的要素之一是重复。 不是机械的重复，而是刻意的重复——每次都以更深的注意力回到同一份材料上。 每过一遍，你的理解都会变化：曾经困惑的变得熟悉，曾经熟悉的变得清晰，曾经清晰的变得灵活。 另一个关键方面是主动投入。 硬学习要求你去思考，而不只是消费。 它要求你用自己的话解释概念、检验自己的理解，并把知识运用到新的情境中。 正是在这一步，知识转化成了能力。 硬学习的难度常常让人打退堂鼓。比起那些更快、更轻松的替代方式，它显得效率低下。 但短期看似高效的，长期往往无效。 回避挣扎的学习，很少能活过当下那一刻。 此外还有一道心理障碍。许多人把\"难\"等同于\"失败\"。 当一件事感觉很难，他们就认为自己不擅长。 而实际上，困难并不是能力不足的信号——它恰恰是学习正在发生的信号。 如果你去观察那些在任何领域达到深厚造诣的人，他们的过程很少光鲜亮丽。 它建立在长时间的高度专注、反复尝试，以及甘愿与复杂性共处之上。 他们不回避硬学习——他们依赖它。 所以当人们说\"最好的学习方式就是硬扛过去\"时，这并不是否定好方法和聪明的策略。 而是承认：任何方法都无法替代努力。技巧可以指引你，但没法替你把活干完。 归根结底，学习不是要回避困难——而是要培养穿越困难的能力。 你越早接受这一点，你的学习就越早变得真实。","2026-09-15_001":"Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' — 2026/09/15 Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' 2026/09/15 Tech's New Rich Are Suffering From 'Sudden Wealth Syndrome' It was the kind of exit every entrepreneur dreams about. Anastasia Koroleva and her husband sold for nine figures: 100% cash on Day 1, zero earnouts, no strings attached. But what followed blindsided the founder. Instead of the soft life she'd expected, Koroleva found herself dealing with years of unease and a painful divorce. Today, Koroleva explores what happens when net worth removes the need to work on Exit Paradox. Her interviews with the extraordinary wealthy have uncovered a recurring theme: For many, the exit does not feel like a crowning achievement — or, at least, not for long. It can feel more like an existential rupture. Guests describe becoming staggeringly rich using the language of trauma: \"recovering from a $500 million sale,\" \"thrashing about for a decade\" or drifting in a state of aimless ennui. Financial hardship defined Michia Rohrssen's earliest memories. At age 4, he remembers watching his mother cry over a failing car they had no money to fix. Money was \"this constant source of pain in our lives,\" he recalls. Rohrssen moved to San Francisco in his 20s and went into startups, co-founding automotive software firm Prodigy in 2015. Then, in 2021, when Rohrssen was 31, Prodigy sold for $110 million. All his money problems were solved. Rohrssen was elated — and although he never thought money could replace inner work, the existential dread that slowly set in turned out to be darker than the poverty he'd survived. \"It's really bad,\" he says. \"When you're broke and things are hard, you have this belief that, well, one day when I sell the company then things can get better. It's actually much harder when you've achieved those financial outcomes and you still feel just as broken. You feel just as inadequate, and you're like, 'But I did the thing? \" He traveled around a bit but had little purpose day-to-day beyond waking up to see his wife. \"I just died,\" he says. There's a term for this phenomenon — \"sudden wealth syndrome\" — says licensed psychotherapist Annie Wright. One reason is velocity. The time between starting a company and hitting a liquidity event has compressed dramatically since the crypto boom, leaving founders less time to mature alongside their wealth. Wright says another factor gets the least attention but can exact a heavier toll: the moral weight of how the wealth was made. Unlike lottery winnings, a founder's fortune is deeply intertwined with their identity. That makes the success feel earned — but any subsequent dissatisfaction feels painfully personal. Many fall prey to the arrival fallacy: the assumption that reaching the number, the milestone they'd organized their lives around, would deliver lasting peace. When it doesn't, some jump straight into their next venture — less from inspiration than a need to prove the first success wasn't a fluke. Parr founded The Hustle, a digital business newsletter by HubSpot in 2021. These days his focus is Hampton, where he dissects what he calls \"champagne problems\" — problems nonetheless. His best advice? Don't do anything irreversible for at least a year after exit. The money, at a certain size, also stops feeling real. \"Up to about $20 million, you're still thinking about, 'How do I use this resource well? \" says Walling, the psychologist. \"You kind of watch it and pay attention and make good investments. And then there's a number beyond that where it's kind of like Monopoly money. That can have this delusional effect on someone's psychology. They are unmoored and unanchored to the value of anything. \" Finding that equilibrium can take years. Of roughly 1,200 post-exit stories Koroleva has studied, she estimates that a decade after reaching \"post-economic\" status, 15% are \"in a pretty sad place\"; 70% are comfortable but feel some emptiness; and just 15% are thriving. Walling says the people who handle a windfall best tend to hold two ideas at once: \"I worked really hard, and I got really lucky. \" Reward without inflated self-regard. Those who struggle most tend to be the ones for whom the company was everything — no healthy relationships, no hobbies, no sense of meaning outside the exit. \"That's where you tie your whole self-worth and your individual sense of well-being to this event,\" Walling says. No amount of money is \"going to be enough to sustain that for you. \" For now, these are problems for the very few and the very rich. But some thinkers believe AI could make the scenario universal. A harder view is that there is no lasting fix. Staying dissatisfied is how our species has survived, why yesterday's luxury reliably becomes tomorrow's basic need. That's the way Rohrssen leans, at least for now.\" There's probably something in the human condition that's hard to root out,\" he says. \"Maybe you're just fighting against gravity.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n科技新贵正被\"暴富综合症\"折磨 科技新贵正被\"暴富综合症\"折磨——一个日渐成熟的\"退出后\"生态正在兴起，帮这些创始人应对突然暴富带来的、他们从没想过的麻烦。 那是每个创业者做梦都想要的退出：Anastasia Koroleva 和丈夫把公司卖了个九位数——第一天全款现金到账，没有一分钱对赌尾款，不附带任何条件。可接下来发生的一切，却从她完全没设防的那一侧撞了过来。她等来的不是想象中那种松弛人生，而是长达数年的心神不宁，和一场痛苦的离婚。 如今，Koroleva 在自己的播客《退出悖论》里探讨的正是这件事：当身家已经让你不必再工作，人会变成什么样。她采访了大量极度富有的人，挖出一个反复出现的共同主题——对很多人而言，那场退出并不像一次加冕，或者说，那种感觉至少没撑多久。它更像是一场存在意义上的断裂。节目嘉宾们谈起自己如何变得富可敌国时，用的竟是创伤的语言：有人说自己\"正在从一笔五亿美元的出售中康复\"，有人说自己\"扑腾挣扎了整整十年\"，还有人说自己就漂在一种漫无目的的倦怠里。 Michia Rohrssen 最早的记忆，是被穷定义的。四岁那年，他记得自己看着母亲对着一辆坏掉的车掉眼泪——那车他们没钱修。\"钱，就是我们生活里一个没完没了的痛苦来源，\"他回忆道。二十多岁时，他搬到旧金山，一头扎进创业圈，并在2015年联合创办了汽车软件公司Prodigy。然后是2021年，31岁那年，Prodigy 卖了一亿一千万美元。 他所有关于钱的难题，一夜之间全解决了。Rohrssen 当时是飘着的——他倒也从没天真到以为钱能替人做完内心那些功课，可那种慢慢渗上来的存在性恐惧，最后竟比他熬过的贫穷还要黑。\"那感觉真的很糟，\"他说。\"你穷的时候、日子难过的时候，心里总还揣着一个信念：等哪天我把公司卖了，一切就好了。可真等你拿到了那些钱，却发现自己还是一样地碎——那才是真的难。你还是觉得自己不够格，然后你就懵了：'可是我不是做到了吗？'\"后来他到处走了走，日子却一天天空着，除了醒来能看见妻子，再没有别的意义。\"我就是死了，\"他说。 持证心理治疗师Annie Wright 说，这种现象是有名字的——\"暴富综合症\"。原因之一在于速度：从创办公司到迎来变现时刻，这中间的时间自加密货币热潮以来被大幅压缩，创始人已经没有足够的时间，跟着自己的财富一同长大。Wright 说，还有一个因素最少被人提起，却可能收走更沉的代价——这笔财富究竟是怎么挣来的，它自带的道德分量。 与中彩票不同，创始人的财富，是和他\"我是谁\"这件事拧成一股绳的。这让成功尝起来像是挣来的——可后来但凡有一丝不满，也就疼得格外私人。很多人于是栽进了\"抵达谬误\"：他们笃信，只要够到那个数字——那个他们把整个人生都围着它转的里程碑——持久的平静就会如期送达。而当它迟迟不来，有些人转身就扎进了下一个项目：与其说是被什么召唤，不如说是急着要证明，第一次的成功不是瞎蒙的。 Parr 创办过The Hustle——一份数字商业通讯，2021年被HubSpot 买下。如今他的心思都在Hampton 上。在那里，他把他口中所谓的\"香槟难题\"一样样摊开来解剖——可那终究也是难题。他最好的一条忠告是什么？退出之后的头一年，什么不可逆的事都别做。 钱到了某个体量，也就不再有真实感了。\"到大概两千万美元为止，你想的还是'我怎么把这笔资源用好'，\"心理学家Walling 说，\"你会盯着它、上着心，认认真真做点投资。可再往上还有一个数，过了那个数，钱就有点像大富翁游戏里的纸钞了。那会在一个人的心理上产生近乎妄想的效果——他们对任何东西的价值，都脱了缆、失了锚。\" 找到那个平衡点，往往要花上数年。在Koroleva 研究过的约一千二百个\"退出之后\"的故事里，她估算：在抵达所谓\"后经济\"状态十年之后，有15%的人\"过得相当糟\"，70%的人衣食无忧、心里却空着一块，而真正活得蓬勃的，只有15%。 Walling 说，最能扛得住一笔横财的人，通常能同时握住两个念头：\"我真的很拼，而且我真的很走运。\"——收下回报，但不让自我跟着膨胀。而最难熬的那批人，往往是把公司当成了全部的人：没有健康的人际关系，没有爱好，在\"退出\"之外找不到任何意义感。\"问题就出在这儿——你把全部的自我价值、把你整个人的幸福感，统统绑在了这一件事上，\"Walling 说。到那时，再多的钱，也托不住它。就目前而言，这些还只是极少数极富之人的烦恼。但也有思想者相信，AI 可能会让这一幕成为所有人的现实。 还有一种更冷硬的看法：这事儿根本就没有一劳永逸的解法。\"一直不满足\"，恰恰是我们这个物种活下来的方式，也正因如此，昨天的奢侈品，一定会变成明天的必需品。Rohrssen 眼下就倾向于这个答案。\"人的底层设定里，大概有某种东西，很难连根拔掉，\"他说。\"也许你只是在跟重力较劲。\"","2026-09-14_001":"No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick — 2026/09/14 No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick 2026/09/14 No one is falling for Trump's budget-busting 'dividend' check gimmick Socialism will not be stopped with more socialism. President Donald Trump's proposal to pay every adult citizen $5,000 if the GOP holds the House and Senate this fall is a desperate gimmick that betrays a profound lack of seriousness about the looming fiscal crisis. \"If the Republicans win, you win with us, and you get $5,000,\" Trump said Wednesday night at the party's midterm convention in Dallas. He dubbed the payout the \"Trump Dividend\" and said recipients would have to spend the money inside the United States — but offered no details on how, or whether, Congress would even be asked to authorize it. This would cost an estimated $1.2 trillion next year, according to the Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget — roughly $5,000 times some 245 million adult citizens. The money would need to be borrowed on top of the $40 trillion national debt — and paid back with interest. Even the administration can't agree on where the money is supposed to come from. Trump said revenue from tariffs would cover what he branded as a \"dividend,\" telling Fox News's Laura Ingraham that \"we're taking in $21 trillion\" from tariffs. The Treasury's own numbers put net tariff revenue this fiscal year at about $154 billion. Overall tariff and excise collections since the start of last year total just under $500 billion — but more than $100 billion of that has already been refunded after the Supreme Court struck down many of the administration's tariffs, meaning the government refunded more money in some months than it collected. The taxes on imports that remain in effect are expected to generate less than $200 billion this year, a sum already baked into this year's deficit of more than $2 trillion. The president previously said he'd earmark this same pot of tariff revenue to cover nearly $6 trillion in other costs. It cannot pay for everything twice. Flooding the economy with money would only worsen inflation, since it would mean more dollars chasing the same amount of goods. That would raise the prices of basic staples, as Americans experienced following the two rounds of stimulus checks during the pandemic. But don't count on checks in the mail. This is at least the fourth time in this term Trump has promised Americans a payout that failed to arrive. First it was Elon Musk's \"DOGE dividend,\" floated in February 2025 as $5,000 checks funded by savings the U.S. DOGE Service was supposed to identify; Musk left Washington a few months later having found nowhere near that amount, and the idea was quietly shelved. Then, last November, Trump promised $2,000 tariff rebate checks by the middle of 2026 — timing that would have landed the money in voters' pockets just before the midterms. That check never went out either. Now the same election-season logic is back: Trump told Ingraham the $5,000 dividend would wait until after the midterms because Democrats \"can't\" pass it now, even though Republicans currently control both chambers of Congress. (On Thursday, Trump separately announced that nearly a million Americans will receive $500 rebate checks before the election after being overcharged for their Obamacare plans.) Joe Lonsdale, co-founder of Palantir and a Trump donor, tweeted from the convention: \"I am hugely in favor of winning the midterms, and love much about this admin, but am strongly against bread and circus bribes.\" After Trump's speech, Fox News anchor Bret Baier asked Vice President JD Vance to respond to critics describing the checks as bribery that would bankrupt the country. Vance replied that tariffs are creating \"incredible wealth\" and have \"helped us pay down debt.\" That wealth might be real for lobbyists and lawyers who specialize in seeking exemptions for their clients, but they're raising prices for everyone else. Trump's speech underscored why so many Americans who care about fiscal sanity feel politically homeless. As Trump bragged in Texas about the government taking stakes in chipmaker Intel and Venezuelan oil fields, 27-year-old state Rep. David Morales — a Bernie Sanders-endorsed democratic socialist running on rent control — beat incumbent Democratic Mayor Brett Smiley in Providence, Rhode Island's primary. A Quinnipiac poll released the same week found that 59 percent of Democrats now consider themselves supporters of the Democratic Socialists of America, versus 26 percent of voters overall. Pandering politicians routinely promote expensive ideas in the months before elections. Usually they're offering new programs, more subsidies or tax cuts that won't be paid for. The electorate has become habituated to promises of \"free\" buses, \"free\" child care and \"free\" medical coverage, as well as student loan debt cancellation. Trump's checks-for-votes scheme is just more explicit.\n⟪中文译文⟫\n赢下选举就发钱？川承诺每人5000美元，这笔账到底谁来买单？ 没人吃特朗普这套：所谓的\"分红\"支票，就是个撑爆预算的噱头。——总统在共和党中期选举大会上的这番话，恰恰说明了：为什么这么多在乎财政理智的美国人，如今在政治上感觉无处安身。 社会主义，是没法靠更多社会主义来阻止的。特朗普总统提议：只要共和党今秋守住参众两院，就给每个成年公民发放5000美元——这是一个孤注一掷的噱头，暴露出他对迫在眉睫的财政危机毫无严肃可言。\"共和党赢了，你们就跟我们一起赢，你们就能拿到5000美元，\"周三晚上，特朗普在达拉斯的共和党中期选举大会上如是说。他给这笔钱取名\"特朗普分红\"，并称领款人必须把钱花在美国境内——但对于这钱该怎么发，甚至国会要不要被请来批一下，他一个字也没提。 据无党派机构\"联邦预算责任委员会\"测算，这项提案明年将耗资约1.2万亿美元——粗算下来，就是5000美元乘以约2.45亿成年公民。而这笔钱只能靠借，还是要在已经高达40万亿美元的国债之上再借一笔——将来连本带利，一分都得还。更要命的是，就连政府内部，都还没能就\"这钱究竟从哪儿来\"说出个一致的答案。 特朗普说，关税收入足以覆盖这笔被他冠名为\"分红\"的钱。他对福克斯新闻的劳拉·英格拉汉姆宣称，我们靠关税\"进账21万亿美元\"。可财政部自己的账本上写着：本财年关税净收入约为1540亿。去年年初至今，关税与消费税的征收总额接近5000亿美元——但其中一千多亿已经退了回去，因为最高法院推翻了这届政府的大批关税；这意味着在某些月份，政府退出去的钱比收进来的还多。至于那些仍然有效的进口税，今年预计带来不到2000亿美元，而这笔钱早已被算进今年两万亿有余的赤字里。更何况，总统此前还说过，要把同一笔关税收入专门划出来，去填另外将近6万亿美元的开支。一笔钱，花不了两遍。 往经济里灌进这么多钱，只会让通胀雪上加霜——因为那意味着，更多的钱去追逐同样多的商品。米面油盐这些最基本的开销就会跟着涨，正如疫情期间两轮补贴支票发下去之后，美国人亲身领教过的那样。 不过，别指望支票真会寄到你手上。这至少是他本届任期内第四次承诺给美国人发钱、最后却不了了之。第一次是马斯克的\"DOGE分红\"：2025年2月放出风声，说要发5000美元，钱从\"政府效率部\"本该挖出来的那些节省里出。几个月后，马斯克离开华盛顿，找到的钱与那个数字相去甚远，这个想法就被悄悄束之高阁。第二次是去年11月，特朗普承诺2026年年中之前发放2000美元关税返还支票——这个时间点，恰好能让钱在中期选举前夕落进选民的口袋。那张支票，同样没有发出去。 如今，同一套选举季的逻辑又回来了：特朗普告诉英格拉汉姆，这5000美元的分红要等到中期选举之后，因为民主党现在\"通不过\"——尽管共和党眼下同时掌控着国会两院。（周四，他还另外宣布：近百万美国人将在选举之前收到500美元的返款支票，因为他们此前购买奥巴马医保计划时被多收了钱。） Palantir的联合创始人、特朗普的捐款人乔·朗斯代尔，在大会现场发了一条推特：\"我非常希望我们能赢下中期选举，这届政府身上我也有很多欣赏的地方——但我坚决反对这种'面包与马戏'式的收买。\" 特朗普演讲结束后，福克斯新闻主播布雷特·拜尔请副总统JD·万斯回应那些批评的声音——有人说这些支票就是贿选，会把国家拖垮。万斯的回答是：关税正在创造\"难以置信的财富\"，还\"帮我们还了债\"。这份财富，对那些专门替客户申请关税豁免的说客和律师而言，或许确实是真的——可对其他所有人来说，它只意味着物价在涨。 特朗普的这番讲话，正好说明了为什么这么多在意财政理智的美国人，如今在政治上无处安身。就在他于得州吹嘘政府入股芯片厂商英特尔、入股委内瑞拉油田的同时，在罗德岛的普罗维登斯，27岁的州众议员大卫·莫拉莱斯——一位获桑德斯背书、打着\"房租管制\"旗号参选的民主社会主义者，击败了现任民主党市长布雷特·斯迈利。同一周发布的昆尼皮亚克民调显示：59%的民主党人如今自认是\"美国民主社会主义者组织\"的支持者，而这个数字在全体选民中是26%。 一味逢迎的政客，总在大选前几个月抛出各种昂贵的主意。通常是新项目、新补贴，或者根本没有财源的减税。选民对这些\"免费\"的承诺早已习以为常——免费公交、免费托儿、免费医保，再加上免除学生贷款。特朗普这套\"支票换选票\"，不过是把话说得更直白罢了。"}};
